<body>
    <h1>Jared Humphreys Log</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p local-id=3D"56bc1d5d-ffdb-492c-a24a-b5ef34779ad9">2025-01-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"5d940b43-2be9-4c2f-8ff0-9285f1713ca2">Tested Keyence laser h=
eight gauge to measure flatness of a flat-top PXL single unit base before a=
nd after grinding with 800 grit sandpaper. The Keyence software can generat=
e a data set of height values recorded over a period of time, so you can st=
art recording data, move the part around underneath the laser, and stop rec=
ording the data, then export it into a CSV file that can be analyzed in Exc=
el, but the software doesn=E2=80=99t provide an easy way to do a flatness m=
easurement without external analysis.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8471760fb68d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b2e98f45-ad13-42a4-b6d4-8a0321a83f8e">Conducted REDRESS (rev=
erse investment casting) test. Printed a mold from Aquasys GP water-soluble=
 FDM filament, filled it with 17-4 powder that had been coated with PMMA, c=
ooked it in a heat treat furnace to 275F, impregnated the green part with p=
araffin, then washed it in warm water to remove the mold.</p>
<p local-id=3D"653d029c-f441-49c4-bc23-232cf4bcb8b9"></p>
<p local-id=3D"99e5db0c-8f95-4ddf-bd34-f2c2c9c5b1a5">2025-01-07</p>
<p local-id=3D"0e1feb71-256b-4a0f-8420-7b1696ce32a8">Bagel Tuesday</p>
<p local-id=3D"0518cac3-0367-4ff3-ab0a-8b9a5612e8d4">Tried to clean discolo=
ration from the bottom of the fully-soldered PXL plate using Lysol toilet b=
owl cleaner that contains 9% HCL, per Al=E2=80=99s suggestion. It did not s=
eem to affect the discoloration, however, the concept is worth trying on a =
production plate that may have a less durable discolored coating.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a4ab001-7e48-429d-856d-8383702ce70b">Two green parts were c=
reated following the previously documented REDRESS process. One was heated =
in the toaster oven to remove the paraffin but collapsed during this proces=
s, while the other was heated in the heat treat furnace and sagged but most=
ly kept its shape.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e66e17b-3941-4408-a3c5-7dda4a341736">Designed and started p=
rinting casting molds to make silicone molds that can be used to make stand=
ard 3 point bend testing coupons. These were printed on the Formlabs printe=
r using Clear Cast SLA material (this is an SLA material that can be used t=
o make ceramic molds; it can be burned off cleanly in an air furnace). This=
 material was used because it was originally purchased for the REDRESS proc=
ess but is no longer necessary for that project. It prints similarly to the=
 clear SLA material that I generally use for nonfunctional prototypes, so i=
t is a good material to use for this application. I also added a demo part =
to the print that Sean asked for to show the geometry they are considering =
for the Epson UV ink heat exchanger.</p>
<p local-id=3D"df1f965e-4e7c-4fde-ab45-71b5add509d8">Received a PXL assembl=
y back from AEM whose leaks had previously been epoxied. It was sent back t=
o have the tabs machined off, but this opened up two leaks: one in a new po=
sition on one of the pentagonal units that is adjacent to one of the remove=
d tabs, and one that is at a previously epoxied joint. To attempt to repair=
 these leaks, I used a Dremel tool with a carbide burr to excavate a groove=
 where the leak is present, and filled it with 3M DP420 epoxy.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0fec616a-027d-4598-a48b-f55345c69ec2">Meeting discussing PXL=
 project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a08164c2-2fc3-4977-b1da-bbb829237a62"></p>
<p local-id=3D"4e378e11-9841-43f5-a78a-94c2ba61b75c">2025-01-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"504c153d-8a9b-4c90-b769-efa6648c94ee">An issue was seen with=
 one of the Ultimaker S5s (Optimus Prime). It did not seem to be extruding =
filament properly and the print being attempted failed to start repeatedly =
(an Outer Bearing Ring Clamp - Lower for a rotary spray head). I first heat=
ed the print nozzle manually, made sure it was unclogged with a needle, the=
n tried to pull out the filament. It was stuck and took some force to pull =
it out with pliers. Upon inspection of the filament, the section of the fil=
ament that was present in the print head had expanded beyond its nominal di=
ameter, causing it to jam in the print head. This can be seen when the prin=
t head cooling fan fails, causing the region of the print head above the no=
zzle to heat above the melting point of the filament, but the fan seemed op=
erational. After cutting off the expanded portion of the filament and reins=
erting the filament into the print head, the print was restarted and comple=
ted successfully.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2fae4979-0b4f-4301-a20a-19e480120ed2">The 3D printed molds t=
hat were started yesterday were finished and post-processed this morning. T=
hey were coated with a dry film release agent and Smooth-On Mold Start 15 S=
low silicone was mixed and poured into the 3 molds. This silicone mold maki=
ng material was several years old and had to be mixed extensively; a white =
material had settled out of the silicone and fused to the bottom of both bu=
ckets. Even after mixing the silicone seemed slightly lumpy, but after mixi=
ng the two components together it looked OK. After mixing, the silicone was=
 degassed in the vacuum chamber and poured into the molds.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4fe0bf29-122f-4420-b662-d672f86d1241">Started a print of 4X =
X AXIS CABLE CARRIER TOWER HOSE FITTING V4s on the Formlabs with Tough 1500=
 material. The material currently being used (I believe Durable?) has broke=
n at least once. Hopefully the Tough 1500 material will hold up better over=
 time to the cyclical loading (NOTE 2025-11-04: it failed at least once mor=
e. Recommend remanufacturing from metal). The part is bent every time the s=
pindle is raised or lowered.</p>
<p local-id=3D"812607cc-b899-42bb-83f4-60757bb11591">A batch of pre-coated =
powder was retrieved after being sieved in the powder room. This batch is (=
I believe) coated with PVP-VA polymer backbone.</p>
<p local-id=3D"447e46b4-f00f-4e2b-b883-b4a961c596f5">The current process be=
ing used to coat the powder is:</p>
<p local-id=3D"3ea07eda-a650-4f9b-9879-da6d54900cf2" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Dissolve a polymer backbone in an appropriate solvent. For PVP-VA,=
 PVA, or PVP, this is water, and for PMMA, it is acetone. Pour an appropria=
te amount of solvent in a beaker (~200 mL/9g PVP-VA seems to be too much li=
quid). Use the mixing/heating plate in the Materials lab to dissolve it com=
pletely. PVA and PMMA seem to take about a day to dissolve at 80C and 1000 =
RPM, PVP-VA takes about an hour.</p>
<p local-id=3D"db3a2d34-03f4-4281-94ff-6a4ad9633555" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Pour this liquid binder into a mixing cup, then add the appropriat=
e amount of powder (the ratio I have been using is ~3% binder by weight, so=
 3g of binder per 97g of powder). Mix the powder and binder until they are =
uniform.</p>
<p local-id=3D"95493b7c-fba8-4bea-933c-d5e63bca5a0e" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Coat a pie tin with the same dry film release agent used for the 3=
D printed molds above. Fill it with the powder/liquid binder mix.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d1fa677a-bd16-4d8a-91f2-4895f108aac5" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Place this pie tin on a hot plate at 120C (if the binder is water =
based) or place it outside (if the binder is acetone based). Wait until the=
 mixture has solidified completely (overnight).</p>
<p local-id=3D"9cadd48e-e682-4403-93bc-c8da9334e0ef" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Remove the block of bound powder from the pie tin.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fd0a0bcc-2bd9-4af3-92df-f960d08ce555" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Break the block into pieces, then grind the pieces in a motorized =
spice grinder or equivalent. Be careful not to run the grinder for more tha=
n 30s or so; if the mixture heats up it will fuse to the sides of the grind=
er.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7e7e5a34-bec8-4730-bbb4-51009b8c3e04" style=3D"margin-left: =
30.0px;">Sift the ground powder using the powered sieve in the powder room.=
 This should return the powder to an acceptable particle size distribution =
(PSD)</p>
<p local-id=3D"3ec6dba1-50d4-47e5-a68a-65ec59449d2d">To fill the mold, a 3D=
 printed funnel is attached to the top of the mold, then the mold is filled=
 a little at a tap, and tapped repeatedly after adding powder each time to =
densify the powder. Once the mold has been filled completely, it may be dif=
ficult to densify the powder that is closer to the bottom of the mold becau=
se of the restriction at the nozzle throat. The mold is overfilled slightly=
, then the funnel is removed and the excess powder is pressed into the mold=
 to densify the powder in the mold further. As much excess powder on the ou=
tside of the mold is removed as possible using a small paintbrush.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f7389d59-709e-47d9-84ed-f00423cfae53">Blue Photon demo. This=
 is a system used to glue parts to a cylinder with a sapphire window at the=
 top. A thick UV-curable acrylic adhesive is applied to the sapphire window=
, the part is applied maintaining a gap of 0.040-0.060=E2=80=9D from the wi=
ndow (one customer has gone with a larger gap of 0.090 but it requires that=
 the cure time be increased from 60s to 120s) then a UV light is shone thro=
ugh the bottom of the cylinder, passing through the sapphire window and cur=
ing the adhesive. To remove the part after machining is complete, the joint=
 can be broken mechanically using a quarter-turn ejection mechanism build i=
nto the cylindrical =E2=80=9Cgripper,=E2=80=9D or the joint can be weakened=
 using heat or cold and the part removed. The acrylic adhesive is largely c=
hemically inert after UV curing, but residual adhesive on the part can be b=
urned off in a furnace at 900C. Adhesive left on the gripper is removed usi=
ng a steam gun or similar.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ddf05738-bc98-4c85-858f-90c219a1010c">A mold was filled with=
 the latest batch of PVP-VA coated 17-4 powder, heated to 275F, impregnated=
 with paraffin, and is currently being washed. The mold was damaged during =
filling; when the funnel was removed it snapped the inner shell at the thro=
at of the rocket nozzle. Because the powder generally holds its shape after=
 being tapped into the mold, the inner shell could be reinserted without ne=
eding to completely refill the upper part of the mold. Due to this and dama=
ge done to the green part while attempting to remove the bottom layer of th=
e mold to make impregnating with paraffin easier, the green part exposed af=
ter washing likely will not be functional, but it is still a useful test of=
 the overall process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"00d3e304-fc61-4bed-9f70-26174cc584ba">Another batch of PVP-V=
A powder was mixed up (300g) and is currently being dried on the hot plate.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"92f0a04f-48c8-476d-b6d1-237a8fac8ce1"></p>
<p local-id=3D"1c9fc9f7-7b32-48dc-8be5-c8febe7bd399">2025-01-09</p>
<p local-id=3D"71935008-9192-4bc4-8cb8-b95a133f0869">PXL meeting with AEM -=
 2X increase in heat transfer performance after flattening plate (~60 - ~12=
0 W/cm^2). Increasing interest from chip manufacturers in AEM=E2=80=99s tes=
ting system for testing wafers after semiconductor fabrication but before d=
icing. Craig raised concerns about the structural integrity of the brazed j=
oints currently, pointing out that they are not reliably sealing at the hor=
izontal flange surface and may only have marginal seals at the vertical sur=
face. He is worried that the assemblies may fail under the compressive forc=
e applied by probes to the wafer (several hundred pounds). He recommended t=
hat some process and design changes be made to yield a more robust product =
that also seals properly after one braze cycle with no rework. After the ca=
ll I again brought up the concept of epoxying the joints instead of brazing=
 them. If switching to epoxy provides the necessary seal and thermal and ch=
emical resistance (based on existing knowledge and documentation, it should=
, but this still needs to be tested) then this would have multiple benefits=
: elimination of the costs of braze cycles ($2500 each, assuming 1 round of=
 rework total is $5000), elimination of braze stop, paste, and wire applica=
tion (~1 day of operator time, $800), loosened part tolerance requirements =
and thus QC time (unknown), elimination of assembly distortion caused by hi=
gh temperature brazing (saves ~3 days machinist time, $2400)</p>
<p local-id=3D"bf902bae-7d81-4daa-a9f0-74fec9621aaa">B-Jetting Call - Prese=
ntation from Dan at B-Jetting on hopper and spreader systems for binder jet=
 printers, covering their doser and shaker systems as well as dual roller s=
preading systems. This is in preparation to design, fabrication, and testin=
g of an in-house developed hopper and spreader system intended to achieve v=
arious goals, including the reduction of overflow powder, the ability to us=
e less flowable powders, improvement in density uniformity of green parts, =
the ability to print with thinner layers, and the elimination of the powder=
 tank.</p>
<p local-id=3D"045d2ba8-9cb3-41db-9a21-13a0758f7495">PXL internal meeting -=
 Parts need to go to plating next Monday to meet the current schedule, whic=
h may not allow enough time for QC of the machined copper parts and any nec=
essary rework.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4ab1a41a-952e-4751-98d0-0f314cfc3396">PXL copper part machin=
ing fixturing meeting - discussed various options for fixturing copper part=
s for machining for the PXL assembly, including mechanical clamping, gluing=
 with Blue Photon=E2=80=99s system or cyanoacrylate, and vacuum chucks. Mec=
hanical clamping and gluing are to be tested in the near future.</p>
<p local-id=3D"82c501cd-4e58-4b5f-9ce8-c7c7e2e2c3a3">Metrology equipment me=
eting - discussed process of testing possible options for a metrology syste=
m. General plan is to select several representative parts and test measurin=
g them on each system, then ranking the systems based on various parameters=
 (ease of use, cost, accuracy, flexibility, fixturing required, etc.)</p>
<p local-id=3D"be33d642-f65d-4b55-87aa-5ce6f4010355">Re-epoxied PXL unit - =
PXL unit still had leaks at two epoxied joints after the latest round of ep=
oxying, some of which seemed to be coming from the vacuum channel, so I rem=
oved the epoxy at the leaks with a brass wire wheel in the Dremel tool and =
re epoxied them, also filling in the vacuum channel where it seemed to be l=
eaking. I also applied a vacuum to the plate while the epoxy was wet to hop=
efully pull it into the joints.</p>
<p local-id=3D"602f1e0e-c061-4a0e-a134-7610d1efe5da"></p>
<p local-id=3D"88221e34-e068-4ed2-bcba-bb400548ddd0">2025-01-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"2f8e11f9-15b9-4816-9d4a-1cb249fe3775">Hopper meeting - discu=
ssed hopper project. Immediate goal of the hopper project is to make a prot=
otype hopper derived from Dan=E2=80=99s design (B-Jetting) by Q1. Nominal d=
esign right now is a shaker-type hopper that has small channels on the bott=
om and two eccentric mass vibrator motors. When shaken, powder will flow th=
rough small channels at the bottom of the hopper, and when vibration is tur=
ned off, the powder will not be fluid enough to flow through the channels.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"c9d775e1-a16c-45fa-bbc5-8c6eca31b099">PXL repair - cleared o=
ut the partially occluded vacuum port on the PXL unit that AEM sent us afte=
r it had been lapped at Sealap. Process used was to drill out the channels =
by hand using a 2.5mm straight flute carbide drill bit designed for drillin=
g hardened materials. It was possible to blow air through all of the vacuum=
 channel ports before any rework, but the customer spec is that the holes b=
e visually clear and a toothpick can be inserted into the vertical and hori=
zontal holes and seen at the intersection of the two (I approximated a toot=
hpick with a 2mm drill bit shank). In one of the vertical holes that was oc=
cluded, it seemed that there were fragments of a broken drill bit, but in t=
he other partially occluded holes it seemed to be metal chips that had been=
 lodged in the holes at some point. There are two leaks in the unit that ap=
peared after lapping; one is at a previously grooved and brazed joint, whil=
e the other is at a joint that had not leaked previously. To repair these, =
3M DP420 epoxy will be applied to the leak points and a vacuum will be appl=
ied to the unit, hopefully pulling epoxy into the leaking opening to seal i=
t without any epoxy left on the surface.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d26a5daa-568d-4f72-abca-efb896d206aa">REDRESS - A water-solu=
ble mold that had been filled and heat-crosslinked yesterday was completely=
 immersed in melted paraffin and placed in a vacuum chamber to completely i=
mpregnate the green part with paraffin, then that part was placed in a wate=
r wash system to dissolve the mold. It was noted that in trying to remove t=
he mold more quickly by washing it in a sink with more water, the paraffin =
waterproofing the green part was softened and it was possible to distort th=
e green part. In the future, the paraffin impregnated green part should onl=
y be washed in cold water, or a wax with a higher melting point should be u=
sed, or a different hydrophobic treatment of the green part should be used,=
 or a non-water-soluble binder that is stronger than the PMMA tried previou=
sly should be used. Mahmood noted that Kymera is experimenting with pre-coa=
ting powder with PVA, which suggests that that is a binder I should potenti=
ally explore more. When I tried PVA recently I was unable to get it to cros=
slink at a low temperature, but this is likely a solvable problem. Gave two=
 17-4 green parts that had previously been impregnated with paraffin to Chr=
is Fontaine to sinter over the weekend. One was made with the REDRESS proce=
ss and one is a standard 3DEO printed green part. Both had the paraffin mel=
ted out in the toaster oven. Mixing up a new batch of powder that has 3% 35=
0K molecular weight PMMA dissolved in acetone mixed with 17-4PH powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cf4622f7-5c8f-4a5d-b348-df574cdd46de"></p>
<p local-id=3D"10352ad4-7f84-4566-9b1e-3dc6e74ff10a">2025-01-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"3571f72c-0b04-4ee8-b6be-b1790a1b6d66">Reamed the locating ho=
les in a Saffron spreader bar to 6mm. These holes tend to be undersize when=
 received, so the round locating pins tend to jam in the holes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"01ece3de-aad2-48f5-86a5-daa7fba89185">REDRESS - Filled a wat=
er soluble mold with PVP-VA powder and cooked it over the weekend. Today I =
impregnated the green part in the mold with PMMA dissolved in acetone. The =
goal is to hold the green part together initially with PVP-VA, then impregn=
ate it with PMMA to waterproof it for the mold removal cycle. Interestingly=
, the powder within the mold seemed to densify significantly while impregna=
ting it with the acetone/PMMA mixture; the level of powder that was initial=
ly near the top of the mold lowered ~10mm during the impregnation process. =
This may be a useful effect of impregnation; a denser green part is general=
ly better since it gives less shrinkage during sintering. However, a denser=
 green part means there is less space for vaporized binder to flow through =
the part while the part is debinding, which can lead to a longer debinding =
cycle being required, or the part may crack or collapse during the debindin=
g cycle from internal pressures.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c4d2bdd5-6efc-487c-b812-caaed02457f5">The first sintered RED=
RESS part came out of the sintering furnace this morning. It appears not to=
 have held its shape during the sintering process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"019076cb-8410-4141-82a1-eca90f16333c">AEM PXL design change =
meeting - 2 hour meeting on proposed design changes to the AEM PXL assembly=
 to improve reliability of sealing. Design is based on a brazing consultant=
=E2=80=99s suggestion to focus on sealing at the horizontal flange sealing =
surface, extend the sealing surface length from 1 to 3mm, and pre place BFM=
 wire in a groove in the lower sealing surface (in this case the stainless =
steel cavity plate). This design still requires the 50 micron flatness spec=
 on the contacting surfaces, as has been recommended by the previous 5 braz=
ing consultants. Any design changes will likely not be implemented until un=
it 8 since the copper parts are already printed up to unit 7. Before invest=
ing significant engineering time into the project, a proposal will be sent =
to AEM for them to pay for 3DEO=E2=80=99s work.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4ed35863-d4c0-4b9a-a384-3a55e9809c3f">HOPPER - began putting=
 together a SolidWorks assembly of the PILOT printer=E2=80=99s Y-axis gantr=
y to figure out how much space there is to fit a hopper. As expected there =
is not much space; it looks like ~1.5=E2=80=9D along the Y direction of tra=
vel, however, there is more space to extend the design in Y above the sprea=
der bar.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f752ffc0-3766-4c78-be93-05df5a9af30c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"01042714-f12b-4b71-bfd4-b3574669cfd7">2025-01-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"82b8a392-33af-437d-b8c9-2bc9eb3abc73">REDRESS: Removed mold =
packed with PMMA powder from heat treat furnace after curing. Green part se=
ems somewhat bound by testing with pressing a screw into the top of the par=
t, but I am concerned that it may break apart in the mold removal stage. In=
stead of directly washing the mold and green part as is, I will soak it in =
liquid paraffin to waterproof it by impregnating it with paraffin. To maint=
ain a controlled liquid paraffin bath, I have a beaker filled with paraffin=
 placed on top of a hot plate that is set to 100C. The bottom of the beaker=
 broke off when I was lifting it, spilling the liquid paraffin over the hot=
 plate and the surrounding area, so I instead filled a paper coffee cup wit=
h liquid paraffin heated in a wax melter, placed the filled mold in the par=
affin, and used a vacuum chamber to degass the green part and impregnate it=
 with paraffin.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b08d5e9f-8486-4226-9168-5ee874521047">Hopper Meeting - <a hr=
ef=3D"https://3deo.atlassian.net/wiki/pages/resumedraft.action?draftId=3D87=
1956484&amp;draftShareId=3D43ba9f64-7ad1-4f9a-9f2f-2f7c13f8d567" local-id=
=3D"2ea5115b-bf31-4a8f-8e96-b2a998a0d761" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" r=
el=3D"nofollow">https://3deo.atlassian.net/wiki/pages/resumedraft.action?dr=
aftId=3D871956484&amp;draftShareId=3D43ba9f64-7ad1-4f9a-9f2f-2f7c13f8d567</=
a> Generating want/need requirements and timeline for project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4a45cb44-d679-409d-a961-2ccf5b4f5755">Wax Dispensing Project=
 - Brainstorming session discussing the wax dispensing project with Payman =
and Pourya. The goal is to eliminate the need for depowdering green parts a=
fter printing by filling in cuts with wax during a print. To test the conce=
pt, ARM V2.0 (AUTOMATED REAMING MACHINE V2.0) will be retrofitted to have a=
 cutting spindle and a heated wax dispensing syringe.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a2315a79-3ed5-4840-8789-9839d97d90f8"></p>
<p local-id=3D"3c4f9e8b-40f1-4cad-8de5-6a72ce2ae112">2025-01-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"2296a547-db27-439b-8e58-b938b957465e">Set up print on Ultima=
ker S5 (Megatron) for an SVP bypass valve. The CAD is available in the 3DEO=
 SolidWorks PDM Vault =E2=80=9CAsm - minimalist svp bypass in-house print.S=
LDASM=E2=80=9D</p>
<p local-id=3D"4b250951-bff8-4113-83cd-61fe1a4c13c3">Set up print on Ultima=
ker S5 (Optimus Prime) for some rotary sprayer parts - a rotating plate sea=
l and 12 bearing clamps of the ROTSPRAY V6 design</p>
<p local-id=3D"be6086b5-1a19-4d82-ac7f-e9136261d7ec">REDRESS - Completed re=
moval of the water soluble mold from the green part that had been made yest=
erday by curing PMMA-coated powder in the heat treat furnace at 275F then i=
mpregnating the green part with paraffin to waterproof it. Cold water was u=
sed to dissolve the mold because the paraffin softens at higher temperature=
s. The green part seems to have come out well, the question at this point i=
s whether it is possible to melt out the paraffin and still have a sufficie=
ntly strong green part. If the paraffin impregnation process broke the PMMA=
 bonds then there will be nothing binding the metal particles together once=
 the paraffin is melted out. I am running that green part through the PMMA =
crosslinking thermal cycle in the lower Keith heat treat furnace to melt ou=
t any paraffin. At the same time, I have filled a new water-soluble mold wi=
th PMMA-coated powder and am curing it in the same heat treat furnace cycle=
. In theory PMMA binder should be waterproof, so it should be possible to s=
kip the paraffin impregnation step. It occurs to me that the melting point =
of paraffin is above the crosslinking temperature of PMMA, so even though P=
MMA is theoretically non-soluble in liquid paraffin, the impregnation proce=
ss is still likely to break the PMMA bonds formed when curing the PMMA-coat=
ed powder. Perhaps impregnating the part with a waterproofing agent with a =
melting point lower than room temperature would waterproof the part without=
 breaking the crosslinked bonds, or the PMMA part could be washed to remove=
 the mold without any impregnation. This was the original intent of switchi=
ng from PVP-VA/PVA/PVP to PMMA; it should create a waterproof green part bu=
t I have historically had issues with the green part breaking apart when wa=
shed in water. I am currently making a new batch of powder with 5% PMMA, in=
creased from the current 3% PMMA. This should create stronger green parts t=
hat may hold up better to washing. The part that had paraffin melted out of=
 it seemed to hold its shape, so it should be able to go through the sinter=
ing process. The other part that was cured at the same time was impregnated=
 with paraffin by soaking it in a beaker of liquid paraffin that was placed=
 in a vacuum chamber. It is currently being washed. Another water soluble m=
old has been filled with PMMA-coated powder and is currently being cured in=
 the Keith furnace. I have reduced the temperature ramp rate to minimize di=
stortion of the mold while it is being heated; it was previously 120 F/hr a=
nd is now 90 F/hr (to 275F). If distortion continues to be an issue then I =
will add reinforcement to the printed part so it holds its shape better dur=
ing the thermal crosslinking process, but I would prefer to avoid that for =
the sake of print time and material usage.</p>
<p local-id=3D"88542ed5-66e9-46ef-9f64-0669b0963759">HOPPER - Received vibr=
ator motors and installed them in Dan=E2=80=99s sample shaker-type hopper d=
esign. Benchtop testing shows that it can dispense stock 3DEO 17-4 powder w=
ith flowability additive (fumed silica) when vibrating and stop dispensing =
when vibration is stopped. The flow rate seems to be non-uniform across the=
 line of dispensing. It is able to dispense non-flowable Atmix powder, but =
the dispense rate is much lower and dispensing is even less uniform. The fl=
ow rate also appears to be highly dependent on vibration frequency and leve=
l of powder in the hopper. Dan noted in the meeting we had with him that he=
 prefers to use doser-type hoppers when possible; they don=E2=80=99t tend t=
o work well with flowable powders because the powder can flow around the do=
sing roller, but they work better with non-flowable powders than a shaker-t=
ype hopper. Next step in the project is for Pourya and Mohsen to build an e=
xperimental setup to test the flow rate of the hopper by weighing the powde=
r it dispenses onto a scale. Began working on a new hopper design that is b=
ased on Dan=E2=80=99s design; the channels have similar dimensions but are =
now adjustable because they are in a SolidWorks CAD model instead of an STL=
. I have also extended the length of the dispensing line to match the width=
 of the PILOT printer=E2=80=99s build plate. It will also have provisions f=
or being mounted to a vibration isolating mount. I have designed and am pri=
nting several cases to enclose the vibrating motors; these will make it eas=
y to swap out motors if necessary and will seal the motors from powder ingr=
ess. The disadvantage is that without air flow the motors may overheat and =
fail, but if that becomes a significant issue then the system can be redesi=
gned with motors that are already sealed and so are safe to use in open air=
 or the case can be redesigned so that it conducts heat away from the motor=
s.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e55bafd0-d221-4676-b96a-b2357e5abc7c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"07e0a7f3-8040-47ab-80be-fa13939543a2">2025-01-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"68166bf9-a775-4d1d-ba4c-310039aceb1d">PXL Meeting with AEM: =
AEM noted that there was epoxy blocking one of the vacuum ports on the plat=
e they have currently. Epoxy was initially applied to the area next to the =
area in question; a groove was machined from the copper and filled with 3M =
DP420 epoxy, but that failed to seal the leak. Epoxy was later applied to t=
he location in question in the vacuum groove to seal a leak through the vac=
uum groove itself where the groove may have been machined too close to the =
edge of the stainless steel cavity plate. Some of the epoxy wound up sealin=
g the vacuum port hole and I failed to clean it out later. The plate is bei=
ng brought back to 3DEO to remove epoxy from the vacuum port and make a gro=
ove in the epoxy that is in the vacuum groove. AEM recommended that we have=
 an inspection procedure and generate an inspection report before shipping =
a PXL unit to them. AEM is currently working with Electrolurgy to have a un=
it plated. We also discussed some of the possible changes to the braze join=
t to ensure that the joint is formed properly on the horizontal sealing sur=
face of the heat exchanger flange. The Japanese brazing vendor MTC suggeste=
d that we have a design that is 3mm wide, with a 1mm wide and 1mm deep groo=
ve in the middle of the flange surface that will have a wire preform instal=
led in it before the brazing cycle. Whether to place BFM outside of a joint=
 and have it flow in or to place it inside the joint seems to be a matter o=
f personal preference with brazing consultants. Extending the joint, howeve=
r, is a common rule of thumb; the rule of thumb is to have the overlap betw=
een two brazed surfaces be 3X the thickness. Since the flange thickness dur=
ing brazing is 1.5mm, we technically should have an overlap of 4.5mm. Since=
 the final flange thickness is 1mm, you could make a tortured argument that=
 the overlap should be 3mm. Currently, the overlap is close to 1mm. To incr=
ease the flange overlap would require significant design changes to the cop=
per parts and cavity plate, so we are trying to get AEM to pay us for the N=
RE.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c7f46193-7c33-42f4-aad1-9447fba633e4">HOPPER - Finished prin=
ting of the vibrating motor cases, which did not fit the motors properly an=
d curled up from the print bed. I have redesigned the case based on these r=
esults and applied Magigoo adhesive to the glass plate to improve adhesion.=
 I am currently running a print of the new case design on OG, one of the Ul=
timaker S3 printers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c423022f-e34f-4284-8615-5e836bba6a38">Designed and printing =
plate with same opening size and threaded insert spacing as the PILOT print=
er gantry to enable benchtop testing of hoppers that are designed to be ins=
talled in the PILOT printers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"da5cae89-a26d-4eed-82c4-cac9c7b587c5">After switching the Pr=
usa printer from Aquasys GP to TPU, running a test print, and switching bac=
k to Aquasys GP, the printer is having issues. It seems not to be dispensin=
g continuously, leaving large gaps in the line is trying to lay down. I am =
drying the Aquasys GP filament overnight in the dehydrator at 65C, and will=
 work on the issue further tomorrow.</p>
<p local-id=3D"01449f1c-4467-41c0-8dd9-a8ad87f0370d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"824bd9b9-d26d-4430-8a0d-7e62be26184c">2025-01-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"42ba5b59-7c17-4c5c-a6f7-8ac2f36b6ddd">The issue with the Pru=
sa seemed to be that the filament was not sufficiently dry; after drying th=
e Aquasys GP filament overnight at 65C in the dehydrator and starting the s=
ame print as yesterday, it seemed to adhere to the build plate properly and=
 did not have the same gaps in the lines being laid down as seen previously=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"af6d76d5-4feb-48dd-8448-1edfb92372a4">Cleaned out the vacuum=
 port hole that had been sealed on the plate that was brought back from AEM=
 this morning. The vacuum port hole had been blocked by braze filler materi=
al so I drilled that out using an uncoated HSS 2mm drill bit and checked th=
e vacuum port hole using a light shone in from the side as well as a toothp=
ick inserted into the hole. I also cut a small groove into the epoxy with a=
 scalpel, trying to keep away from the side of the vacuum channel that had =
leaked previously. This operation seems to have opened up a new leak that h=
ad previously been sealed by epoxy, but the leak is small enough that it ma=
y be acceptable to AEM.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b6ac61e2-0120-4242-b492-d37c44ffe133">Mixed up a new batch o=
f pre-coated powder. This one is 3DEO=E2=80=99s treated 17-4 powder coated =
with 5% PVP. I am trying PVP instead of PVA or PVP-VA in an attempt to incr=
ease the strength of green parts. I also removed the two green parts from t=
he heat treating oven where they were heated to a temperature of 275F at a =
rate of 90F/hr. The green part that had the mold dissolved off of it seemed=
 to crack and several pieces of it broke off. The other green part was one =
that was still in a water soluble mold. It had been made from 3% PMMA pre-c=
oated powder that was poured into the mold, the mold was tapped to densify =
it, and it was immersed in melted paraffin in an attempt to cure it without=
 needing to send it through an initial curing cycle. While immersed in para=
ffin, it was placed in a vacuum chamber to completely impregnate the green =
part with paraffin. It is currently being washed in the water wash chamber.=
 If the mold can be dissolved completely and the paraffin removed by heatin=
g it then it will go into tonight=E2=80=99s 17-4 sintering run, but it is u=
nlikely that that process will be completed in time. 2 parts are going into=
 the sintering run tonight. They are both made with 3% PMMA coated powder. =
A test was also conducted with powder pre-coated with 5% pure PVP. The powd=
er did not thermally crosslink and so stayed unbound after the curing cycle=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"84f19b87-177c-479a-8626-0c6a2b276699">The updated version of=
 the vibrator motor case has completed printing and seems to fit properly. =
I completed a design of side mounts that are attached to vibration isolatin=
g mounts as well as a central hopper. The vibrator motor cases are mounted =
to the side mounts, and the central hopper mounts to the side mounts with M=
5 thumb screws to make it easy to remove and replace the hopper without nee=
ding to switch the vibrator motors. The hoper has small channels to let out=
 powder on the bottom, so it is being printed on one of the Ultimaker S3s w=
ith a 0.25mm nozzle, which enables very small features to be reproduced acc=
urately but lengthens the print duration to ~2 1/2 days. I completely assem=
bled one of the vibrator motor cases. The motor is glued into one side, wir=
es are fed through the holes, and the other side of the case is glued on af=
ter a bead of Loctite Professional cyanoacrylate glue is applied to ensure =
that the case is sealed. The same glue is also applied around the holes whe=
re the wires pass through the case to seal them against powder ingress. Aft=
er gluing, the case is sprayed with super glue hardener to cure all of the =
super glue.</p>
