<p local-id=3D"08697550-596c-4ea0-9b8b-f75d93adfb4e">2025-01-20</p>
<p local-id=3D"67647e49-ca72-4d29-8427-21da8131ceb5">Ordering parts for a p=
ellet extruder style FDM print head for next-gen technologies. This print h=
ead can be used to extrude pure paraffin for filling in cut areas to elimin=
ate depowdering, or it can be used to print parts directly using a kind of =
feedstock that is derived from MIM powder. I don=E2=80=99t have an exact re=
cipe for the feedstock yet, but it should be similar to that used for metal=
 powder-bearing filament, with metal powder blended with a polymer backbone=
 that is thermally activated as it is passing through the hotend, as well a=
s whatever flow additives are necessary. I am hoping that by using an extru=
der screw instead of a standard filament feed mechanism, it will be possibl=
e to minimize the flow additive content to provide green parts with a highe=
r metal powder load.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a1d50716-acef-49b9-94c1-07a6fe891876">All the Hopper V1 part=
s are printed, so I will assemble those and review the design at today=E2=
=80=99s hopper meeting. Since the McMaster purchase request placed on Frida=
y has not been completed by morning today, the order will, at best, arrive =
tomorrow morning. Since I will be out tomorrow, I will not be able to assem=
ble the system until Wednesday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3879e828-91d2-4cd6-a1f6-41cf7152107a">The various wax meltin=
g experiments done so far have sometimes resulted in paraffin being heated =
beyond its smoke point and producing smoke that travels throughout the R&am=
p;D lab and attached office rooms. I have stopped using the toaster oven an=
d I am hoping to eliminate the solder pot that I=E2=80=99ve been using beca=
use it tends to overheat the wax. I have put in a purchase request for a pu=
rpose-built wax melter that will melt wax quickly but regulate the temperat=
ure so it does not go beyond the smoke point of paraffin.</p>
<p local-id=3D"249f6efc-ad65-4ca4-be4d-6bac1462e428">Filled a REDRESS mold =
that was printed from PolyMaker PolyCast with 3% PMMA 17-4 powder. I then p=
ut it in the lower Keith heat treat furnace and started it cooking with a c=
ycle ramping to 500F at 120F/hr. The goal here is to eliminate the need to =
impregnate the green part with paraffin and dissolve the mold. In theory, d=
uring this cycle the coating on the powder will first crosslink, creating a=
 green part, then the mold will soften and eventually vaporize as the tempe=
rature is ramped. This would simplify the workflow considerably: a mold wou=
ld be printed, filled with powder, and first run through a cycle in an air =
furnace to vaporize the mold and partially debind the part, then the part w=
ould be sintered as normal. If the PolyMaker PolyCast material could be rem=
oved in an inert atmosphere, then the workflow is even simpler: after filli=
ng the mold, it would be placed in the sintering furnace, and after an appr=
opriate cycle a completed part would come out.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6ae534a4-3c84-4232-829e-6f93734ee25e">Received the two parts=
 that had been sintered over the weekend. They seemed to hold their shape a=
nd did not develop more cracks (one of the parts had several cracks in the =
green state, but they did not seem to get much worse. Sliced a sample off o=
f the cracked part to have Adriana embed it in resin and polish it to get a=
n idea of the quality of the sintered part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"514ad0b0-c984-452b-b02c-19a1adaa9534">I have an idea for a c=
hange to the process I have been using. Polyethylene would be dissolved in =
liquid paraffin at elevated temperature, the binder would be blended with m=
etal powder, and the powder slurry would be poured into a water soluble mol=
d. This mold would then be dissolved with water, and the green part would e=
ither be thermally or chemically debound and sintered. I am in the process =
of dissolving Poly(ethylene-co-vinyl acetate) into liquid paraffin. PE is o=
nly semi-soluble in paraffin, so I have had to dilute it to a ratio of 85:1=
0 paraffin to PE and mix it at a temperature of 150C. I mixed 500g of 17-4 =
powder into the paraffin/PE-CVA binder, heated it until the mixture was liq=
uid, then poured some into a water soluble mold. It did not completely flow=
 into the mold; particularly in the reinforcing ribs there were bubbles lef=
t behind that are visible from the outside of the mold. I am currently rehe=
ating the mold along with the beaker of powder/binder mix in the toaster ov=
en with the temperature dial pointed straight left (9:00 position) which co=
rrelates to a temperature below 250F. The intent is to heat the mold until =
the powder mixture is liquid, then vibrate it to remove any bubbles, then t=
op it off with the mixture that has been kept liquid in the beaker. I incre=
ased the temperature to 250 because having the temperature set below 250 (i=
n a region of the dial labeled =E2=80=9Cwarm=E2=80=9D) did not seem to be h=
eating the mixture above its melting point. I was mistaken; the power strip=
 the toaster oven is connected to kept tripping and turning off the toaster=
 oven. I connected the toaster oven to the wall (bad practice) and it seeme=
d to keep running without blowing a circuit breaker, but the water soluble =
mold began to distort and I was not able to vibrate out the bubbles. I fill=
ed up the mold as much as I could with the powder/binder mix in the beaker,=
 but the ribs aren=E2=80=99t going to be formed when I demold the green par=
t.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8d92ddda-9bf3-415c-a9c5-5a9f6badd78e">HOPPER meeting: Marcus=
 populated the Hopper Jira page <a href=3D"https://3deo.atlassian.net/jira/=
core/projects/HOP/board?atlOrigin=3DeyJpIjoiNGI5M2NiZGVjNDk2NGQzYThhNjkxNjQ=
1YmJiNGU1OTYiLCJwIjoiaiJ9" local-id=3D"59dec747-f7cb-4d3c-8122-da421f5c15a4=
" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">=
https://3deo.atlassian.net/jira/core/projects/HOP/board?atlOrigin=3DeyJpIjo=
iNGI5M2NiZGVjNDk2NGQzYThhNjkxNjQ1YmJiNGU1OTYiLCJwIjoiaiJ9</a> with the curr=
ent progress on the Hopper project. The 3D printed parts for the HOPPER V1 =
prototype and base are done printing, I am just waiting on hardware to arri=
ve from McMaster to complete it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a2ecd356-29cd-4e85-9be3-aae289f380f2">AEM PXL Design Changes=
 Meeting: Discussed the process of testing possible changes to the design o=
f the AEM PXL assembly with the intent of improving sealing of the brazing =
process. There are various options regarding how to approach the experiment=
al protocol; one proposal is to do a series of single-unit tests first then=
 scale up to quarter units then scale up to full units. Another proposal, t=
he one that was ultimately preferred, is to test with quarter units. My pre=
ference is to pick one approach that is feasible to integrate into the fina=
l product (various changes to the sealing design have impacts on other aspe=
cts of the assembly design, and these impacts can be determined without hav=
ing run any experiments) and braze one full plate with the experimental des=
ign and one plate with the current design in the same brazing run. This app=
roach lets us eliminate untenable sealing concepts before investing in deta=
iled design and fabrication, and also increases confidence that the results=
 of the experiment (albeit only one data point) are more valid because we h=
ave previously seen that results with single unit tests don=E2=80=99t neces=
sarily correlate with results seen when brazing the full plate. Also, since=
 we are going to braze full plates with the current design anyway as part o=
f our current manufacturing schedule, this eliminates the need to make sepa=
rate samples just for the purpose of acting as a control.</p>
<p local-id=3D"435a3569-17f5-4b1b-adc4-bc7d2aa82712"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a41237f9-41d6-49d6-87a8-4ab2c0472f8e">2025-01-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"9fc72e35-3188-4e2d-9282-8c1000a0d5c4">HOPPER - Assembling HO=
PPER V1 prototype. Hopper V1 and benchtop test rig are assembled. The vibra=
tor motors need to be wired to a DC power supply for testing. Eventually th=
e vibrator motors will need to be controlled through software.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f3f6daf6-4046-4f73-bf7f-207d5f283751">REDRESS - Dissolving w=
ater-soluble mold off of the green part that was formed by pouring a mixtur=
e of 17-4 powder with Poly(ethylene-co-vinyl acetate) dissolve in paraffin =
into a water soluble mold. The pour left a number of large bubbles in the m=
old so it will not be a usable part, but for testing mold removal and disso=
lving the paraffin with N-Heptane and debinding and sintering the part it i=
s a useful sample.<strong> Mahmood inspected a sectioned sample from one of=
 the sintered PMMA parts, and noted that the porosity was comparable to 17-=
4 parts printed with 3DEO=E2=80=99s process, although he did note two voids=
 that were likely caused by a void present in the process of packing the po=
wder into the mold. This all suggests that in terms of part density and por=
osity at least, this is a viable process for making good quality parts. </s=
trong>Ground and sifted a batch of 5% coated PVP-VA powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"51483a38-2f09-4003-8bf3-dfcedc073e74"><strong>Abandoning app=
roach: </strong>Method is to dissolve a formulation of polyethylene in para=
ffin, combine this with metal powder, and pour it into a water soluble mold=
 when hot enough to pour well. (ratio used: 85g paraffin, 10g PE-CVA granul=
es, 500g pre-treated 17-4 powder). Note that the binder to powder ratio is =
~16% by weight, which would need to be reduced to 5% at most to have accept=
able green part density. The mold is then dissolved off with water, then th=
e green part is solvent debound in N-Heptane to remove the paraffin compone=
nt, then sintered. <strong>Reason for abandoning: </strong>difficulty of di=
ssolving PE-CVA in paraffin, difficulty with bubbles/voids forming in mold =
when pouring because the liquid cools below the melting point of paraffin a=
nd solidifies, need for a hazardous solvent debind step, open questions abo=
ut strength of green part after solvent debind.. I have greater confidence =
that a dry powder can be packed into a mold uniformly than pouring in a liq=
uid, particularly when the binder content percentage is reduced to the nece=
ssary level. That said, there are advantages to this process (ease of worki=
ng with a slurry compared with a powder, elimination of grinding/sieving st=
ep, protection from oxidation, solvent debind of paraffin opens up a networ=
k of pores for gas to escape from the part). The issues are potentially sol=
vable, but I think it is a better use of time to pursue the dry powder appr=
oach instead.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b63374c-43dc-4c46-8d47-1a43e464dde8">PXL Meeting - Philip a=
t meeting. Discussing the measurement protocol for measuring the copper par=
ts after machining. Copper drawings need to be updated with critical dimens=
ions and measurement protocol needs to be formalized for transfer to QC. In=
cluding visual inspection criteria (could be automated with Keyence VR-6000=
). Machining fixture (for OP 2) in progress.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9b20c505-8560-4526-a2b2-5668356c874d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"f68eaf3c-f6b4-44f4-b2ea-7e120802ac06">2025-01-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"9ceb462d-8fe1-4980-b948-dc7b5f22de78">REDRESS - Impregnated =
the mold filled with 5% PVP-VA coated powder with paraffin by placing the m=
old in a coffee cup, filling the cup to near the rim of the mold but not ov=
er it, placing the cup in the toaster oven set to a temperature that is sli=
ghtly above the melt temperature of paraffin for 30 minutes, then filling t=
he cup to 1cm above the top of the mold, placing the cup in a vacuum chambe=
r and slowly ramping the vacuum level to remove any air, holding the vacuum=
 at 25 inHg for 10 minutes, releasing the vacuum level, removing the mold f=
rom the paraffin cup, placing the mold on a paper towel in the toaster oven=
 for 10 mins to allow paraffin on the outside of the mold to flow away, rem=
oving the mold from the toaster oven and allowing it to cool, then placing =
it in the water wash station to dissolve the mold.</p>
<p local-id=3D"710f8f8b-9cbc-492b-8f60-e9b497b96b75">Dissolving PE-CVA into=
 paraffin at a 1:1 ratio in a beaker placed on the stirring hot plate set t=
o 110C. I have decided to try the PE-CVA/paraffin bound melted slurry pouri=
ng technique again; if I can make it work with a binder content of 5% with =
a binder that has a higher concentration of PE than previously tried, I thi=
nk it may be a viable technique.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a86ab1f4-30e5-4516-9883-fc2a76d886be">Mixed up a batch of po=
wder slurry using 10% binder that is 20g PE-CVA : 60g paraffin</p>
<p local-id=3D"afd5bd6f-019d-47cb-adfe-438b51b78809">Printing a new water-s=
oluble mold to try with the poured slurry technique. Tried it with the prev=
ious batch of PE-CVA/paraffin bound powder heated and poured into the mold =
a little at a time, with the mold stored in the toaster oven in between eve=
ry pour to give the slurry a chance to fill the mold. This heat was apparen=
tly too much for the mold to withstand and also hold its shape; it distorte=
d significantly and began to leak during this process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a5ec5ec7-241f-4efe-b576-842b2a22443f">AEM PXL Mtg - Discusse=
d chamfering the parts for rework and initial brazing to target the BFM at =
the joint, have a filled groove that seals the joint if BFM does not flow i=
nto the joint, and providing a way of locating BFM wire. Discussed possibil=
ity of DFM changes to the plate to increase reliability of the brazing proc=
ess. My opinion is that this is not necessary; our current process should w=
ork as long as our parts are sufficiently flat, but we will find out if thi=
s is the case with the next full plate we are brazing. AEM has installed th=
e sealed PXL they have right now into a wafer prober. They are also receivi=
ng a plated assembly from their plating vendor Electrolurgy.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a10fa6e-339c-4456-bf41-708541ab5a97">HOPPER - Decision was =
made yesterday to make HOPPER V2 a doser-style hopper instead of another sh=
aker-style hopper. Working on design of a prototype. I have advanced the de=
sign to the point that I am going to print out a test hopper and roller to =
test fit the components. The design is roughly based on the geometry of the=
 example hopper that Dan from B-Jetting provided us with an STL for.</p>
<p local-id=3D"84ca4d0c-b1de-4844-bc3b-9ebe57ff9ac5">PERPLEX - working on t=
he hardware design of a screw-driven pellet-fed print head for melting and =
depositing paraffin wax.</p>
<p local-id=3D"995cff96-5599-4b90-8ed7-1dfdedc2f362"></p>
<p local-id=3D"5def922c-0eae-4afa-83ec-69363efd2968">2025-01-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"827c6e1b-da2d-4799-b173-0fb302242df1">HOPPER - Test fit the =
roller into the hopper printed yesterday. The roller did not fit despite th=
e roller being printed at a diameter of 16mm and the hopper opening being p=
rinted at a diameter of 16.5mm. Continuing to develop design of doser-style=
 hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5131668b-c43d-45f2-8a37-ad3113cc0369">REDRESS - Experimentin=
g with pouring a slurry of PE-CVA/paraffin and 17-4 PH powder into several =
different styles of mold. Tried to dissolve SLA Formlabs Clear Cast in Dich=
loromethane and an acetone/xylene solution, neither of which it was soluble=
 in. PolyCast material was found to be soluble in dichloromethane. Melting =
paraffin out of a green part made using 5% PVP-VA powder cured and impregna=
ted with paraffin in preparation for a sintering run today. The part did no=
t seem to retain much strength as the paraffin was being melted out. In the=
 same heat treat furnace cycle, I am testing heat curing 3% and 5% PVP-VA p=
owder in ceramic crucibles. My current theory is that either the coated pow=
der is degraded by exposure to oxygen or atmospheric moisture and is theref=
ore not binding well during the curing process, or having 5% binder works l=
ess well than 3% binder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8eec6771-3d32-490c-844d-3b9ed93f9b2e">PERPLEX - Received NEM=
A 17 motor with 27:1 gearbox, which seems too large to be used for the hopp=
er or PERPLEX projects. I have put in an order request for a NEMA 11 and a =
NEMA 8 geared stepper motor.</p>
<p local-id=3D"399c5452-8f39-4c8c-ad4b-94287f15da0b">Meeting discussing thi=
s week=E2=80=99s progress on R&amp;D projects and next week=E2=80=99s proje=
cted work. Goal is to get HOPPER V2 designed, built, and start testing, sel=
ect parts to try with the NEXTGEN wax filling system, and have some sort of=
 demo of the NEXTGEN process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0efc09f5-f5bc-4235-9e6d-8481abb8711d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"7c1e085e-48c3-4404-a46f-5f31de0b97fb">2025-01-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"0b782cd7-81a3-4b28-89d1-007d0d4a09d2">REDRESS - Over the wee=
kend I made some more test parts using 5% 350K PMMA powder. The green parts=
 produced that way seem to be weak, so I am exploring some alternatives, su=
ch as using 15K PMMA as a binder, as well as using PE-CVA dissolved in para=
ffin. When I tried to melt the paraffin out of a part made this way it comp=
letely liquified, so I am going to try dissolving out the paraffin with hep=
tane</p>
<p local-id=3D"31afb9c2-d44f-44a8-893f-821a4d39b1ee">HOPPER - As expected, =
the current ordering system tends to delay McMaster and Amazon orders by 1-=
2 days as compared with the previous ordering process. Continuing design of=
 HOPPER V2. Moved bearings from the body to the side mounts so they won=E2=
=80=99t need to be replaced every time the body is switched out.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2580867-f039-4501-8245-11cbe2b238f5">NEXTGEN - Pellet extru=
der hot end needs to be powered up right now so I am setting up a Duet 2 bo=
ard for this purpose. Pellet extruder heat break was tested by energizing t=
he ceramic heater with a Duet 2 board and controlling the temperature with =
the installed thermistor. It was possible to control extrusion of wax by ro=
tating the screw using a cordless power drill. When the temperature was set=
 low enough (~50 degrees) it was seen that there was solid wax on the screw=
 after the screw was removed, which suggests that the extruder is functioni=
ng as desired. The amount of drag seen on the screw increased as the temper=
ature dropped and was noticeable by the amount of torque applied to the hea=
t break. It is unclear how much torque may be required in operation, so a g=
eared stepper motor is likely to be the best drive system to use for initia=
l testing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fcdb7634-f48e-4d3c-b1e2-99d334801d0e">AEM Design Changes Mee=
ting: Rajan talked with Christina about the design changes and the PXL proj=
ect in general, and it sounds like she doesn=E2=80=99t have confidence that=
 the design can be manufactured at all, much less cost-effectively. Rajan a=
sked us to answer a few questions about the design: design intent (assuming=
 he means brazing joint the answer is the drawings), what precision is nece=
ssary (assuming he means accuracy and not precision the answer is whatever =
is necessary to meet the drawing tolerances) and the confidence of the team=
 that this is achievable (100% confidence that we can make parts that demon=
strably meet the drawing tolerances, assuming that we change our process an=
d get the necessary equipment) and 70-90% confidence that if we make parts =
that meet those tolerances then the assembly will seal on the first braze.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"ac91a458-e6b3-4f81-95fb-f40523633549"></p>
<p local-id=3D"d8b17f04-152f-4d12-8991-9e59dbe6db88">2025-01-28</p>
<p local-id=3D"11453a46-13f3-47e4-8f73-a8a8e388f906">HOPPER - Finished init=
ial design and printing body, roller, and side mounts for initial testing.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"8c1d4c64-3920-4cfc-801b-b0c6bc3915ce">REDRESS - Dissolving m=
old off of paraffin-impregnated PVP-VA part. Melted out 2nd paraffin-impreg=
nated PMMA part. It held its shape, but two of the ribs were damaged by an =
edge of paper towel being blown against the part by the furnace=E2=80=99s i=
nternal blower.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e56ae406-7a8a-49a0-9c8e-aef73d984e9a">NEXTGEN - Continuing d=
esign work on the pellet style extrusion head.</p>
<p local-id=3D"07784b02-e1ae-4c08-b356-764cc4562b65">AEM PXL Meeting - inte=
rnal meeting discussing the AEM PXL project design changes. They are lookin=
g for a proposal for the proposed brazing joint design changes, including a=
 timeline, worker hours spent, and material costs.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dad20461-9dd0-4831-9cd9-cbe560b472fb"></p>
<p local-id=3D"bf5f165a-1b24-412d-9c85-34cfb86799a8">2025-01-29</p>
<p local-id=3D"e843b7bf-5538-4d73-9d42-40c1cf6768fe">HOPPER: The body, side=
 mounts, and one roller are printing on one of the Ultimaker S5s, and anoth=
er roller is printing on the Bambu printer. The various parts necessary hav=
e been ordered, so final assembly should be possible in a few days. Parts s=
howed up from McMaster, so I should be able to put it together with the NEM=
A 17 geared stepper motor tomorrow. The smaller stepper motors won=E2=80=99=
t show up until later, so I will have to wait to switch to using those.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c7d4ab0a-34c7-45bf-aa19-5b8596b9ed14">REDRESS: I have had is=
sues with making green parts using the thermal crosslink - paraffin impregn=
ation - mold dissolve - paraffin melt out process, so I am currently pursui=
ng two alternative processes: one is to fill a soluble mold with feedstock =
that is a blend of 17-4 powder with PE-CVA dissolved in paraffin. I am heat=
ing the mold and a syringe of feedstock in the toaster oven with the dial p=
ointed straight left; this keeps the temperature low enough that the mold d=
oesn=E2=80=99t soften from the heat but the feedstock is also liquid enough=
 to inject into the mold. At the same time, I am reexploring an older proce=
ss; this process entails filling a PolyCast mold with coated powder, therma=
lly crosslinking the powder, then burning out the mold. I had previously ha=
d some issues with the green part not keeping its shape, but I believe I ca=
n address that with newer formulations of powder coating.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ebe92460-ff74-4829-ae54-4847b7049ef4">PERPLEX - PERPLEX is t=
he name of the pellet-style extruder I am developing for the NEXTGEN projec=
t. I am still working on designing the mounting and structural components f=
or the extruder. Completed initial design of mount and printed from CF PLA =
on the Bambu printer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"98250b98-9207-4fbc-b6e5-4fd968c10d63"></p>
<p local-id=3D"c9ff79a0-ae77-4c67-bd65-8ada79a55dbb">2025-01-30</p>
<p local-id=3D"379b6769-220c-4755-877f-7e4cc9e780b1">HOPPER: Printed a PLA-=
CF roller. Cross drilled and tapped a hole in the shaft for a pin that will=
 rotationally lock the shaft to the roller while still being easy to remove=
 and replace by disassembling and sliding out the shaft. Received a NEMA 8 =
geared stepper motor. Installed threaded inserts for mounting vibrator and =
side mounts to the hopper body. I wired up connectors to the NEMA 8 stepper=
 motor and the two NEMA 17 stepper motors. I also fabricated two cables to =
connect the stepper motors to a Duet board (I used the standard Molex Micro=
-Fit 3.0 4-pin connectors we use on stepper motors around the company for t=
he steppers, and the cables adapt that to a standard Duet connector).</p>
<p local-id=3D"abe67fb7-9fdf-4178-8c9c-ee8ac95d9f28">REDRESS: Green part th=
at was soaked in N-Heptane overnight was cracked extensively and weakened, =
but it held up to heat that would reduce the unsoaked green part to a puddl=
e. It seems that there is still some paraffin in the middle ~1mm of the wal=
l of the part that can be seen to liquify when a broken piece of the green =
part is heated, but the outer 1mm of the wall seems to be unaffected by the=
 heat (the wall thickness is ~3mm). I am making a new batch of PVP-VA 3% co=
ated powder; that seems to produce the strongest green parts when thermally=
 crosslinking so that is the kind of powder I want to fill a PolyCast mold =
with and test heating to 500F tonight. I am also pursuing a new technique, =
using a slurry made with the same PVP-VA binder: I poured the slurry into a=
n AquaCast GP water-soluble mold. Historically I have had trouble with slur=
ries shrinking inside the mold as well as difficulties with removing the so=
lvent from the slurry before removing the mold, but in this case the water =
in the PVP-VA slurry should be absorbed into the mold and soften it, while =
at the same time drying out the slurry to form a solidified green part. At =
that point, I could either impregnate the green part with a non-water-solub=
le material (i.e. paraffin or mineral oil) or burn off the mold, although v=
aporizing AquaCast GP is an as-yet-untested technique.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7d4fd48d-6418-4031-b390-449a3e1be622">PERPLEX: Main structur=
e was printed yesterday, hopper and base mount was designed and printed tod=
ay.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ad2a7585-3157-416b-8672-946040c06c70">AEM PXL: The lapped PX=
L plate was returned from AEM by Karl, and was found to be leaking from thr=
ee unit cells, at points which had previously been repaired by rebrazing. W=
e received authorization to epoxy the joints to seal them, and I worked on =
my technique digging out trenches to epoxy the joint. The goal is to get a =
deep groove at the joint between the copper and stainless steel, but this i=
s more difficult than it sounds because there is a small distance of stainl=
ess steel to cut into before breaching the vacuum channels. I used a carbid=
e bit in a Dremel tool to cut a groove, while observing the joint under a m=
icroscope to cut as close to the vacuum channel as possible without cutting=
 into it. I applied 3M DP420 epoxy to the joint using a mixing nozzle with =
a needle on the end (the mixing nozzles are conveniently designed with a Lu=
er taper tip) and used a razor blade to scrape off as much excess epoxy as =
possible.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b0d12d00-2bfa-4621-b5f8-53428ddd49fe"></p>
<p local-id=3D"540b5968-6fb5-4346-9c9e-dab67cecf866">2025-01-31</p>
<p local-id=3D"feefab9a-3f8e-418c-b8c8-c13a5b09c3d2">PXL: Epoxied plate sea=
led when pressure tested in the morning, and Marcus is bringing it to AEM t=
oday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"403c0ef0-1b43-4fb6-80b8-54859dd9b9e1">HOPPER: Finished assem=
bling HOPPER V2. Had to drill and ream one of the timing belt pulleys to fi=
t the 8mm roller shaft. Tested stepper motor using a DUET 2 board, but it w=
ill eventually be powered using a DUET 3 board. I had to modify the benchto=
p test rig to get the new HOPPER design to fit: I had to cut a section out =
of the back of the test stand to provide clearance for the vibrator motor a=
nd cut a section out of the side (where one of the M5 threaded inserts is) =
to provide clearance for the timing belt pulley. There are Viton o-rings se=
aling the sides around the shaft support bearings, and the shaft support be=
arings are a solid polymer-filled type so it should take a while for powder=
 to make its way through them, but the fit between the shaft and the bearin=
gs is not as tight as I would like so I suspect some powder may make its wa=
y through there. For the next HOPPER, I will use felt or packing seals inbo=
ard of the bearings to act as a primary seal (the bearings will be retained=
 to support the shaft against the bending moment applied by the timing belt=
).</p>
<p local-id=3D"841573b8-996f-4745-bb7b-f4d9898c9b8b">PERPLEX: I assembled t=
he pellet-style extruder setup and tested it by running it with a DUET 2 bo=
ard. Initially there was some leakage between the ceramic heater and the he=
at break, as well as between the ceramic heater and the nozzle. I suspect t=
hat the pressure in the hotend is high enough that it leads to leaks at are=
as that are not pressure-tight. The heat break also started spinning after =
a minute of operation; the torque applied to it by the driving stepper moto=
r was too high for the 3D-printed clamping feature to hold.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9f43112f-0f5f-42c9-9f05-ac406f50002d">REDRESS: I have a new =
process that I think may work.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"cfb26caf-d279-4154-911e-91a5fb38afbf">
<li local-id=3D"b93a460c-3702-4117-9e16-2bf4dd5bdaa1">
<p local-id=3D"ae6d9d56-eb0b-438f-9435-dcfd178df0e5">Coat powder with PVP-V=
A at a concentration of 3%</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c663fd51-4495-47ba-b7b3-3d705d732334">
<p local-id=3D"6caa1479-7608-414e-ab39-c16a1c4563c7">Grind and sift powder<=
/p></li>
<li local-id=3D"00f2187e-7d1e-4160-ac1b-ae77818cf534">
<p local-id=3D"549d806a-0d5d-4d1e-a55b-8295064c2f71">Fill a water-soluble m=
old with powder and tap to maximize density</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"d1e6057d-1147-4f5f-a30e-b778c976a1a1">
<p local-id=3D"acda4a76-99dc-4404-a40f-2c08be5e633b">Heat mold and powder t=
o 250F at 60F/hour</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0350573a-ba42-41b3-b66c-e163efed052c">
<p local-id=3D"184069ba-ed65-4004-aad1-a759438cfc38">Remove bottom of mold<=
/p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b78e3d11-044f-4f82-bb14-2060d7d3cb96">
<p local-id=3D"98689ba5-472c-4cb4-8291-9f81127ef361">Place mold in a cup of=
 liquid paraffin where the level of paraffin is slightly below the top of t=
he mold</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0e4e5c26-7e75-48c8-b876-356ac2deffd6">
<p local-id=3D"4804bafb-527c-41b8-a546-fcd8f14a0db4">Place in oven at 220F =
for 30 minutes</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"7b3d48d7-a05c-477b-819b-4ea2aeb90a6c">
<p local-id=3D"8dea73ff-fd2f-456a-a043-6cd430958ec3">Add paraffin to the cu=
p to a level of 1cm above the top of the mold</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"92c01915-92e2-4d94-9d0e-6726a7e0bbbb">
<p local-id=3D"30cee8b5-397f-47f4-9182-ec99b48e2c67">Return to oven for 30 =
minutes</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"42c29efd-285c-4364-b400-98ab0c7b8c05">
<p local-id=3D"06f437d0-9e39-416f-8af3-45b4531c436f">Remove the mold from t=
he oven and place it on a paper towel that is on top of a ceramic plate</p>=
</li>
<li local-id=3D"59451dd3-28fb-430a-ac4a-aa4e738c17b6">
<p local-id=3D"a22bc393-f2c6-4b2a-ab9c-38004eb80ef5">Increase the oven temp=
erature to 250F and place the mold back into the oven for 1 hour</p>
<ul local-id=3D"400c377f-5253-490b-8955-6034ada95a82">
<li local-id=3D"21595d8a-37c7-4ed6-bcd5-af8f1aa28be3">
<p local-id=3D"a195e07d-6eab-47b5-b270-adcd6a9a1c0f">This will remove as mu=
ch paraffin as possible from the green part while leaving enough residue to=
 waterproof the green part. It will also serve to enhance crosslinking of t=
he part.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"a711b395-287a-4e12-be17-3bb84ad341c5">
<p local-id=3D"9969c8c2-2765-43cb-b340-bb360fd9201b">Remove the mold and al=
low it to cool to room temperature</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a04ec87f-84a3-45c9-9cc3-b95f47f2c996">
<p local-id=3D"fbd4a0e0-be3a-4211-be16-e7a9bf6e4714">Place the mold in a wa=
ter wash tank until the mold has been completely dissolved from the green p=
art</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"77292a74-b3e2-4e1e-b124-57d6ccd78732">
<p local-id=3D"c8de9b13-6619-47e5-86c1-618a9f64b3a8">Air debind the part to=
 500F at a rate of 60F/hour</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"eb8b1601-1067-460f-a029-dec95f2f5717">
<p local-id=3D"79cfac4b-7b4e-461c-8f40-24993a2acece">Sinter the part as usu=
al</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"e07339f8-9e1e-4c97-8679-10a4e5f80119">Note that the specific=
 process variables here are estimated and err on the conservative side; if =
the process works as written here there is extensive room for optimization.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2b3290d-6ee1-4d63-8c6d-372a4142a9c4"></p>
<p local-id=3D"7a4be144-9743-47cb-8003-a5f36153014e">2025-02-03</p>
<p local-id=3D"7bdd04cb-3422-4cd2-8682-2bd460e166cc">HOPPER: Assembled and =
wired HOPPER V2 to the Duet 3 board in the hopper testing area and ran a qu=
ick test by pouring powder into the hopper, running the vibrator at 7V, and=
 rotating the roller at 10 RPM. It seemed to dispense powder well, although=
 the spiral hopper caused the point of highest powder flow to sweep along t=
he line of dispensing. I am printing a new roller that has a larger number =
of straight grooves that are also somewhat shallower than the spiral groove=
s used on the current roller.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c0b2ff4b-5c01-4962-bba3-6a63e8ff2c3a">REDRESS: The sintered =
parts came out this morning; the two PMMA parts collapsed while the PVP-VA =
part mostly held its shape, although there is evidence of bubbling and the =
cracks that were present in the green part are still present in the sintere=
d part. I am making a new batch of coated powder using 9g PVP-VA and .5g St=
earic Acid dissolved in 99% isopropyl alcohol mixed with 300g of treated Sa=
ndvik 17-4 PH powder. Stearic acid is commonly used in MIM binder formulati=
ons as a lubricant; it may help here to get the powder particles to flow be=
tter when filling the mold. I also filled two molds with the PVP-VA powder =
dried over the weekend and ground and sieved today; one was cured rapidly i=
n the toaster oven at 250F for 10 minutes, the bottom of the mold was remov=
ed to allow paraffin to flow into the and impregnated with paraffin by plac=
ing it in a coffee cup filled with paraffin to slightly below the rim of th=
e mold, leaving it in the toaster oven for 10 minutes, filling the cup abov=
e the rim of the mold, leaving it in the toaster oven for 10 minutes, and l=
eaving it in a vacuum chamber at 25 inHg for 10 minutes to draw out any air=
. The other mold was placed in the heat treat furnace with air circulation =
running a cycle to heat it to 275F at 120F/hr (I think) with a dwell of 1 h=
our (I think). This more conservative heating approach is less likely to ca=
use green part cracking, but takes more time. Tomorrow I will impregnate th=
e part with paraffin using a more gentle procedure (using a vacuum chamber =
is fast and effective but I worry that it is aggressive enough that it may =
cause green part cracking).</p>
<p local-id=3D"2ccc12cb-6014-42cc-987c-cfd9378a0dd1">NEXTGEN:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b2d36cb2-f180-4ff4-9883-a8dffcd5d681">I am currently working=
 on an improved version of the pellet extruder-style wax dispenser. Some of=
 the changes are a hopper that clamps to the top of the heat break instead =
of screwing onto the mount, the heat break is clamped by a shaft collar wit=
h side tapped holes that the mount is screwed to to provide greater resista=
nce to spinning than the previous version, the hopper width is reduced in p=
reparation for designing it to fit within the PILOT printer, and I have eli=
minated the need for heat set inserts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8f1f0afa-7e1c-4db3-86d8-2034f0610109"></p>
<p local-id=3D"40c38360-cd30-4364-aa34-bdd0259c3fb4">2025-02-04</p>
<p local-id=3D"05aa2f99-ae1d-42f2-a0fa-212cdd53faa9">HOPPER: Replaced the h=
elical roller with one with straight grooves. It does not have the same iss=
ue with sweeping dispensing but it is still pulsing more than I would like.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"8a5b392f-24ed-4305-8cbc-af0d1fe80aae">REDRESS: Melting out w=
ax from the green part formed yesterday. The part seems to soften considera=
bly where the wax is liquid, but solidifies once the wax is removed (even a=
t high temperatures). I began impregnating the second PVP-VA green part in =
pure paraffin, but I decided to pull it out and mix up a custom formulation=
 of wax: 100g paraffin, 10g PE-CVA, 3g stearic acid. My hope is that this w=
ill flow into the part more easily and strengthen it while melting out the =
wax and during the debind process if any of the PE-CVA is left in the part =
after the wax is melted out.</p>
<p local-id=3D"74550618-b557-44be-80fb-474d1183be38">NEXTGEN: I have assemb=
led the V2 pellet style extruder. The ceramic heater I had cracked so I had=
 to replace it with a standard aluminum block-style heater. The mount snapp=
ed during testing; the torque being applied to the heat break was sufficien=
t to break the 3D printed part. I had printed it in a weaker orientation to=
 simplify the print, but now I will add some reinforcement to the part and =
print it in a stronger orientation. I will also add mounting features for t=
he fan; the heat sink on the heat break was warming up enough that I am con=
cerned it may cause issues with the hopper interface (which is a PLA part c=
lamped directly to the top of the heat break, yes, I know, but the temperat=
ures here are much lower than are usually used in FDM hot ends (&lt;100C vs=
. 200-300C)). Put in an order request for a replacement ceramic heater.</p>
<p local-id=3D"23d9f2ee-fb69-41cb-9dad-9c9e4929ec06"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a64e9b99-0bb6-47de-babe-2716ec0ffc52">2025-02-05</p>
<p local-id=3D"61c1d997-01fd-40ba-8289-c9f5415ee3b1">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"f9a53b3d-00cd-45a3-a799-d59858d9d1d2">REDRESS:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e8487f3a-410c-45b3-baca-80b1c8238f2a">NEXTGEN: Latest iterat=
ion of pellet extruder is operational. It seems to be able to dispense liqu=
id wax when the feed screw is rotating and not dispense it when the feed sc=
rew is not rotating. The feedstock being used is =E2=80=9Csand wax,=E2=80=
=9D which appears to be paraffin formed into small (~.5mm) balls so it flow=
s well in the hopper. Also, the stepper motor vibrates while operating, whi=
ch aids in flow of the feedstock.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e91e67e1-0433-41c0-9700-4e00289fd0a9"></p>
<p local-id=3D"5cb4b9c6-8a83-4603-b444-0629b26abaf6">2025-02-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"9d27dafc-e3cd-47c0-8b30-c31e7658f119">HOPPER: Testing is con=
tinuing. Mohsen plans to get the data collected together in preparation for=
 a discussion of the design tomorrow or Monday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c668623-fbae-48e1-89ff-8bd291aa5b79">REDRESS: Melting wax o=
ut of two green parts that had been crosslinked with 5% PVP-VA and demolded=
. I am still seeing issues with part cracking. I mixed up a batch of 3% PVP=
-VA that I am testing today as well as a batch with 3% PVP-VA and 2% Epsom =
Salts. The theory is that the Epsom Salts (magnesium sulfate) will act as a=
 metal binder that holds the particles in the green part together after the=
 polymer has been melted or vaporized but before sintering has started.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0ed347b0-b7f4-4841-9f14-8b550ab1d1ae">NEXTGEN: Replaced the =
aluminum hot end with a ceramic one that heats and cools faster and takes u=
p much less space, improving the packaging of the design. Tested the system=
 with paraffin granules that we have around; it does not dispense as reliab=
ly but it does not have the unmelted white particles that were visible in t=
he melted wax droplets with the previously used sand wax. I showed the syst=
em to Payman and Pourya and Payman=E2=80=99s suggestion was to improve the =
consistency of dispensing and increase the flow rate to the point that it c=
an dispense a steady stream instead of drops.</p>
<p local-id=3D"378cb487-8071-46d5-940e-b5368bf1a4bc"></p>
<p local-id=3D"34099b43-b21c-416b-8764-5c585537c610">2025-02-07</p>
<p local-id=3D"556674b6-9dc3-41fe-ae02-91cec9b9c81d">HOPPER: Meeting to go =
over results and discuss next design iteration. Hopper needs to be function=
al in machine at end of March (end of Q1). Functionality here is not offici=
ally defined; it may be dispensing only, dispensing and spreading, or dispe=
nsing, spreading, and printing.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"f0a95d86-af42-48cc-aa10-9761216796a3">
<li local-id=3D"a32c82a6-d019-4f3d-9920-712dcb352ebf">
<p local-id=3D"7fb1c05e-b7c8-4326-a139-fcd9fba9e0f4">Clear hopper to see po=
wder behavior in real time</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e9841e90-cde0-4353-b724-7bd1ed3cb418">
<p local-id=3D"6bea9b83-62c1-4f54-b7ce-58634988ea20">Connect vibrator motor=
 to Duet board</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"35a6c0ed-4b2b-4788-8f5a-cf752901689d">REDRESS: Dissolving th=
e mold from one of the paraffin-impregnated (soaked overnight) green parts =
made with 3% PVP-VA coated powder. There appear to be cracks in the green p=
art; I believe this is due to the shrinkage of the impregnating paraffin as=
 the part cools after impregnation. I am working on testing shrinkage of th=
e machinable wax we have as well as a custom-mixed wax comprising 80% paraf=
fin, 10% stearic acid, and 10% petrolatum.</p>
<p local-id=3D"af6e3602-f3c0-4e40-ae38-b307cbfe7667">NEXTGEN: Replaced the =
geared NEMA 17 stepper motor with a direct drive NEMA 23 motor that can run=
 considerably faster. It is actually able to extrude paraffin through the h=
eat break with the hot end removed and no heat, but the pressure generated =
when trying to force non-liquid paraffin through the hot end is high enough=
 that it leaks at the interface between the hot end and the heat break and =
between the hot end and the nozzle. This can be resolved by epoxying the ho=
t end to the heat break and the nozzle to the hot end and heat break, altho=
ugh this would make the assembly permanent.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"a1b0235d-454d-4a69-8bf5-3237fe080ff0">
<li local-id=3D"2d02992e-4017-4194-8400-08ddd207dd58">
<p local-id=3D"b58163f1-f3b3-4fac-bbb8-c1ef3dfb574a">To do: figure out how =
to dispense continuous streams of wax at different temperatures</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"22bc1b72-e7e5-4a52-8e68-38f32362ccbc">Slurry definition: <a =
href=3D"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slurry" local-id=3D"2e63704a-52c3-4c4=
8-9e55-0109adfd7ad2" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-link=
" rel=3D"nofollow">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slurry</a> solid particles=
 suspended in a liquid</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c1a5fa1-5244-40e9-af94-eb776c810117"></p>
<p local-id=3D"d7487f21-f1ea-4f99-986a-600c992d111d">2025-02-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"1c1968ce-a2c7-4ba6-8904-c488d2d822be">REDRESS: Had success o=
ver the weekend with making extremely strong green parts by adding Triethyl=
ene Glycol Dimethacrylate (MEHQ stabilized) to PMMA-coated powder and heati=
ng it to 300F. It turns out that PMMA is unnecessary; the TEG-DMA on its ow=
n will crosslink powder when heated above its thermal crosslinking temperat=
ure (~130-140C). The issue is with the water soluble molds; they weaken and=
 sag before the powder slurry hits its crosslinking temperature. I am worki=
ng on making higher-temperature molds, including reexploring using molds ma=
de with Formlabs Clear Cast resin and PolyMaker PolyCast filament. There ar=
e also higher-temperature water soluble FDM filaments made by Aquasys, but =
they require a printer with a heated build volume. It=E2=80=99s possible th=
e current Bambu printer would be able to print with it; it does not have a =
heated build chamber but the build chamber is enclosed. Filled 2 Aquasys GP=
 molds with slurry made by mixing 200g of pre-treated Sandvik 17-4 powder w=
ith 20g of TEG-DMA (I had to go with a 10% binder load to get the mixture f=
lowable enough). To fill the molds without bubbles forming, I first filled =
a syringe with the slurry and injected it into the mold while the mold was =
being vibrated vertically with a pneumatic linear vibrator.</p>
<p local-id=3D"98c94183-832e-47ea-8221-1eaff3691503">HOPPER: Reprinting the=
 current roller in FormLabs Tough 1500 Resin material to compare the perfor=
mance of a resin roller with an FDM printed roller.</p>
<p local-id=3D"56e92744-02be-4e05-a993-5b96f5ff3276">NEXTGEN: Epoxied the h=
eat break, ceramic heater, and 1.25mm nozzle together to hopefully withstan=
d the pressure applied when extruding non-liquid paraffin. I am also printi=
ng a new mount that is designed to use a NEMA 23 ungeared stepper motor ins=
tead of the previously used NEMA 17 geared stepper motor. This new motor is=
 much larger but can run at a much higher speed so it is more suited to dis=
pensing wax the way we need for this project. It may need to be reconsidere=
d when packaging the extruder to install in the PILOT printer, but for benc=
htop testing it is valuable.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6ca23239-50f2-4d08-ab94-e16eecfe52bf">Moved the tools and se=
veral tables out of the R&amp;D lab, including the AEM assembly and testing=
 tables.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e1268c66-a4c4-421e-abf3-f87f7bc8695c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"51867651-15ea-48f2-ab1e-4ef81a08be63">2025-02-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"4a46d613-702f-4a66-babe-c4dc2c227753">Bagel Tuesday meeting.=
 Key takeaways are that Rajan visited Epson and Atmix in Japan last week, a=
nd they are interested in integrating 3DEO=E2=80=99s technology into their =
prototype printers, receiving a PILOT printer, and integrating their powder=
 business with our process. There is a board meeting next Wednesday that ne=
eds to be prepared for. IHI wants to accelerate their Inconel thruster proj=
ect. There are IHI and Epson meetings on either side of next Wednesday=E2=
=80=99s board meeting, and some representatives from Epson will be present =
at the board meeting.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c72e12f2-5583-4228-92a5-15ca9db03b8b">Continuing to move the=
 R&amp;D lab over to the warehouse and organizing and setting up the new R&=
amp;D lab.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1432dd34-1a05-4ff3-92ea-c5fc714c0d06">REDRESS: Removed two m=
olds from the Keith furnace that had been filled with TEG-DMA/17-4 PH slurr=
y and cured with a cycle of 60 F/hr to 200F, dwell 1 hour, 30 F/hr to 300F,=
 dwell 3 hours, cool naturally. One part seems to have distorted slightly a=
nd the other look good. Th top of the green part is ~3mm below the top of t=
he mold, suggesting that either the slurry shrank while curing or the mold =
expanded outward slightly while the slurry was still liquid. Dissolved the =
mold off of the two green parts, and currently I am running the lower quali=
ty of the 2 through the =E2=80=9CGENBURNOUT=E2=80=9D cycle in the upper Kei=
th furnace. This is a cycle to 1000F at a ramp rate of 120 F/hr to pre-debi=
nd and pre-sinter the part before final debind and sinter. I am also runnin=
g two molds printed from FormLabs Clear Cast resin through the PMMACrosslin=
k cycle in the lower Keith furnace. One of them was filled with a slurry ma=
de using 200g of treated 17-4 PH Sandvik powder mixed with 20g of TEG-DMA a=
nd 1g of stearic acid as a flow aid. The other was half-filled with the sam=
e slurry and half-filled with the slurry from yesterday (it turns out that =
200g of powder and 21g of binder makes enough slurry to fill 1 1/2 SLA mold=
s).</p>
<p local-id=3D"2ad0492c-56e3-41a4-b1e3-f7f1c72d4ada"></p>
<p local-id=3D"e01744bc-21c8-44ad-b79a-1fcb42f8c3e6">2025-02-12</p>
<p local-id=3D"60882477-120b-447a-9e3d-ac69f3019060">REDRESS: The burned-ou=
t mold retained its shape but built up a rust-red crust on the outside, and=
 when the crust was removed the part was cratered with indentations ~1mm in=
 diameter. I am currently curing two parts with a lower binder load in wate=
r-soluble molds and burning out a part in a FormLabs Clear Cast mold as wel=
l as one that had been cured and had the water soluble mold dissolved off. =
I have set the ramp rate to 60F/hr in an attempt to reduce cratering of the=
 part. Making a new batch of PVP-VA 3% coated powder for a test Payman sugg=
ested. Filled 1 1/2 water-soluble molds with slurry made using a new recipe=
: 5g PVP-VA, 20g water, 5g TEG-DMA, 1g stearic acid, 5 drops T80. I will le=
ave the molds overnight to see if the water is absorbed into the mold, then=
 cure one of them using the standard cycle.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5ec7ee27-34a8-4818-bcbe-6a95faafc49e">HOPPER: Designing a ne=
w roller with rounded grooves. Mohsen is testing the SLA roller printed to =
match the geometry of the roller previously tested.</p>
<p local-id=3D"711dcfff-32a7-4fb6-a744-50c3771c9d2b">PXL: Meeting about fix=
tures for machining the copper parts. The earlier design did not have suffi=
cient throw to clamp parts that are as variable as our printed parts, so Ka=
i altered the design to use a different style of clamping component to give=
 more throw.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5dde1ca1-d729-48ec-bb1a-3ce36f2fd9dc">Continuing to organize=
 and set up new R&amp;D area.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9bf5a11c-e075-4bcb-9f22-ebfb8685728d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"39b1b91f-7353-445a-9e52-b7f4c1888b9f">2025-02-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"b5f828f8-c9bc-41b0-9293-9e3e046d68eb">PXL: Sean and Kai bega=
n test-fitting parts in PXL unit 4. The cavity plate pockets are undersize,=
 so the copper parts don=E2=80=99t fit as is. The decision was made to modi=
fy the copper parts to fit the current cavity plate rather than wait for th=
e next cavity plate to be completed (Next plate could be plated by next Wed=
nesday, so it would be a delay of 3 working days). Worked on sanding the ed=
ges of the square copper parts with a Dremel tool to make them fit in the p=
late. This may or may not effect the braze joint. It also means that we are=
 not making the assembly with any in-spec parts; the cavity plate was machi=
ned out of spec and the previously in-spec copper parts have now been modif=
ied so that they are no longer in spec.</p>
<p local-id=3D"37e35276-eb16-48e5-8d45-a3c22c8d2b65">REDRESS: Mixed up a ba=
tch of paraffin/PE-CVA/SA binder and filled a mold with that binder blended=
 with powder. Also made a batch of slurry with PVP-VA, TEG-DMA, SA, and TWE=
EN 80.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4fc2c066-7eda-4a22-b793-f2c03f17aa6b">HOPPER: In process of =
post-processing a new roller with rounded geometry that Payman suggested. A=
lso sanded down the previous SLA roller because it did not rotate freely in=
 the current hopper. Issues are being seen with the stepper motor getting h=
ot and skipping steps; I was able to get it working temporarily by reducing=
 the current and reducing the microstepping, but long-term it would be a go=
od idea to increase the motor size or gear ratio.</p>
<p local-id=3D"eeb5687b-be8d-4dd3-ba64-f91ec6296b81"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b93c707c-e4f4-47ac-bec8-e9bb7356a7bb">2025-02-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"6eda7433-0525-4dcd-bc07-9f8c916ab5c2">PXL: Continued sanding=
 parts. Began assembly of 4th full unit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a4f08787-873b-425a-ace5-1d855d9e85ed">REDRESS: Sent 2 brown =
parts to sintering (one debound to 600F, one debound to 1000F). Cured IN625=
 part in soluble mold, 8% TEG-DMA. Continuing issues with molds warping in =
heat; I ordered a higher-temperature water soluble 3D printing filament tha=
t should hold up better but is harder to print with and dissolve. I also th=
ink it is possible to heat-treat the molds to survive better.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9fe0c616-035a-4d96-b3e0-ea2e5adb0c1c">HOPPER: New roller wit=
h curved and filleted indentations is cured and needs to be post-processed =
and tested.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9eccbae5-5b34-4772-b325-2eab084fa5ff"></p>
<p local-id=3D"8ad97e02-63bc-40f7-89e5-9e1c0e808ac3">2025-02-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"534347b9-f97b-4a0b-b085-712951668163">PXL: Completed assembl=
y, including: applied BAg-8 braze paste to copper parts and installed them =
in the locations specified by Kai=E2=80=99s chart, applied NicroBraz Green =
braze stop around the perimeter of the cavity plate and on each copper part=
 to prevent BFM from flowing along the surface outside of the desired joint=
, applied braze paste to each corner of each copper part and placed BAg-8 B=
FM wires at each desired braze joint, with the braze paste holding the wire=
s in place, and placed the assembly on top of a hot plate set to 60C. A pap=
er towel was placed between the hot plate and the PXL assembly to keep the =
bottom of the assembly clean, which unfortunately also insulated the PXL as=
sembly from the desired applied heat. After noting that the braze paste had=
 not dried within an hour or so, I increased the hot plate temperature to 8=
0C. The hot plate will need to be turned off at 1:00 PM so that it has one =
hour to cool before Manju takes it over to Bodycote.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5de00b27-2ca2-4541-90e3-3f797f59280f">For the cavity plate f=
or unit 5, there were metal hanging chads left in the holes drilled for the=
 valve seats and the primary liquid feed/return channels for the copper hea=
t exchangers. These need to be removed, and I also need to try to fillet th=
e valve seat holes using some new radius forming end mills received today f=
rom Harvey Tool.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5021133e-5d2b-4db0-937c-2b3dd94c1bcf">REDRESS: Received 2 si=
ntered 17-4 parts that were sintered over the weekend, both bound with TEG-=
DMA, but one had been debound and pre-sintered to 600F while the other had =
been debound and pre-sintered to 1000F. The one that had been heated to 600=
F came out shinier, while the one that had been heated to 1000F came out mo=
re matte and with numerous cracks. There is also an interesting separation =
of a layer of the material within the combustion chamber internal side of t=
he part, which is not present on the part that was debound to 600F. I suspe=
ct that this is due to oxidation or other damage done to the powder by heat=
ing it to such a high temperature in air.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b5fd3db2-08db-40e5-a20b-324a44b7624e">NEXTGEN: Downloaded CA=
D file of Prusa I3 to design a pellet-feed print head for it. Took apart pr=
inthead to determine best way to retrofit printer to pellet extrusion.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8a2a4abe-e044-4766-889e-4be49b4bad6a"></p>
<p local-id=3D"7ccdca9c-ee73-4bb5-ac84-d028616aeb55">2025-02-18</p>
<p local-id=3D"92dfc90d-01bb-408f-988d-9e6d26328e97">NEXTGEN: Continued wor=
k on designing new Prusa-compatible pellet-feed printhead.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0927ac4a-903d-4aba-8900-d02f2dee23ee">Spent 1 hour on Bambu =
printer maintenance due to filament grinding when trying to print with an e=
xotic high-temperature water-soluble filament.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5abe6d05-ab3b-4a24-af53-c1f44c939cc7">REDRESS: Green part ma=
de using recent batch of PEG/PMMA powder was very weak and partly fell apar=
t during the mold dissolving process, but I think this is due to a weak bon=
d in general and not the water dissolving the binder used. I am making anot=
her batch of powder using a similar ratio of PEG to PMMA to powder but with=
 Stearic Acid added and mixed more thoroughly. I am also doing a 600F burno=
ut of the TEG-DMA CuCrZr part, a crucible filled with PMMA/PEG powder, and =
the partially broken green part. I am also curing another PMMA/PEG water so=
luble mold green part to a temperature of 300F instead of 275F.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bfd79d98-2cec-4235-810d-ee66c31834d3">PXL: Tested unit 4 and=
 found several leaks. Marked leaks, took pictures, then applied nickel powd=
er to all gaps and lift pin holes in the plate, then applied wire to the co=
rners of the parts and lift pin holes with paste tacking it in place. Becau=
se we ran out of BAg-8 BFM wire, I had to use braze paste instead at some j=
oints, but all leaking joints had wire applied.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8ffc55c7-2ed2-4c4c-9d52-79400e560858"></p>
<p local-id=3D"0b58faa9-22aa-41f0-9d95-b1d446083798">2025-02-19</p>
<p local-id=3D"2c498a95-a59e-4f5d-a59b-483a3dd8eb7c">PXL: Leaks found after=
 rebrazing. Plate was then machined, leak tested, and leaks were marked in =
preparation for cutting grooves and sealing. Decision was made to epoxy gro=
oves instead of attempting more braze repairs.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ad63fa96-033e-4194-b2aa-fd9e128aa69a">REDRESS: Tested new po=
wder with PEG/PMMA/SA coating in a small ceramic crucible (20g powder) in t=
he toaster oven to 300F. Filled a soluble mold with powder combined with TE=
G-DMA and EGME and cured in lower Keith heat treat furnace. The green part =
from the ceramic crucible is still very weak; the PEG/PMMA binder is waterp=
roof but too weak.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e0cbb18c-1fe3-49a6-87d2-c83903a574a6">HOPPER: Ordered piezo =
ultrasonic stack vibrator:</p>
