<p local-id=3D"18449ce6-9fb9-48c1-8757-b704dc78e329">NEXTGEN: Printhead des=
ign for Prusa completed and mount was printed, but direction of project was=
 changed by Payman to mount the pellet-style extruder to the former reaming=
 machine gantry that Pourya is using instead of mounting it to the Prusa. A=
 mount needs to be designed that can hold the pellet-style extruder, the sp=
indle, and Pourya=E2=80=99s syringe-type extruder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"38501438-e008-424a-b1a7-d5d355d6fa95"></p>
<p local-id=3D"1ad5ca46-3abf-45b1-86b3-ed50abff2946">2025-02-20</p>
<p local-id=3D"e6c740ae-f42a-46cb-af8a-cebf3d479f42">NEXTGEN: Removed suppo=
rts on Prusa pellet extruder mount.</p>
<p local-id=3D"84fc6c0c-ffc0-4a05-b7df-8da8d788d0a3">REDRESS: I remembered =
last night that PEG is water-soluble, so the binder I have been using that =
is a mix of PEG and PMMA shows that it is possible to make waterproof green=
 parts using a binder with a water-soluble component. Mixing new binder rec=
ipe that is 1% stearic acid (flow and wetting aid), 10% PEG 8000 (plasticiz=
er), 20% PMMA 350K (polymer binder that is waterproof but weak), 70% PVP-VA=
 (polymer binder that is strong but water-soluble), and 1% Aerosil R 972 if=
 it is available (hydrophobic fumed silica nanoparticles). Loaded a water s=
oluble mold and a ceramic crucible with tapped powder and am running them t=
hrough the TEG-DMA cycle on the lower Keith furnace.</p>
<p local-id=3D"56d783cc-2878-4085-9198-34d713f5176c">PXL: Cutting grooves i=
nto plate and applying epoxy to seal leaks. Cut grooves and applied epoxy. =
Left excess on surface to be removed during machining.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f2611c70-ffd7-4da4-a9a9-bd20ccac244f"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a484ebab-cc50-4e27-bf54-e05c3cfae3e2">2025-02-21</p>
<p local-id=3D"a361417a-404b-43dc-abdf-5da67c87ab64">NEXTGEN: Assembled pel=
let-style extruder printhead and installed to Prusa. Looking for granulated=
 paraffin to test it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c036ffae-5235-4c38-8827-d3e39289e566">REDRESS: The mold fill=
ed with yesterday=E2=80=99s batch of powder expanded and cracked in several=
 places, but the powder cured in a ceramic crucible seemed fairly strong an=
d waterproof.</p>
<p local-id=3D"27ed058e-8700-4c24-b0d1-f092e1758cd7">PXL: Unit 4 leaked aft=
er the epoxy repairs. It will be machined to the upper end of the final hei=
ght spec and have the vacuum grooves machined in, then it will be epoxy rep=
aired again. Received several new carbide burrs to use, including two 60 de=
gree conical burrs that should make it easier to machine a groove exposing =
the leaking joint while minimizing damage to the vacuum grooves.</p>
<p local-id=3D"71d35ab7-f498-4955-ae74-ebf51e7b6d91">HOPPER: Received piezo=
 vibrator and driver board from PiezoDrives. Connected waveform generator t=
o driver, power to driver, and driver to piezo stack. By running the piezo =
stack at audible frequencies and touching it to the table or various object=
s, it was possible to hear a tone, but the motion of the stack was not visi=
ble.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b7ef6ee3-651f-419e-a328-53f27cc4dcb0"></p>
<p local-id=3D"abf673d8-84b1-4886-96c8-e87c19ac4669">2025-02-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"e82a47fe-3d86-4551-a310-1569867d7317">NEXTGEN: Mixed up a fo=
rmulation of low-shrinkage wax over the weekend per a suggestion from Claud=
e: 80% paraffin, 10% PE-CVA, 10% Stearic Acid. I filled a small crucible wi=
th this formulation of wax to compare its shrinkage with the shrinkage of p=
ure paraffin poured into a crucible of the same size.</p>
<p local-id=3D"90efa260-6e52-4321-88ab-592708d682d4">REDRESS: After testing=
 a sample made by curing an earlier formulation of powder in a ceramic cruc=
ible (5% binder load, 70% PEG 8K, 30% PMMA 350K), I found that a green part=
 made with that formulation is fairly hard and and also quite waterproof. I=
 mixed up a new batch of powder by dissolving 17.5g of PEG 8K and 7.5g PMMA=
 15K in dichloromethane and combining that binder with 500g of treated Sand=
vik 17-4 PH powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"41703a70-4e57-4ee3-9782-f60e3c19b11b">PXL: Had a meeting inv=
estigating the root cause of leaks seen in unit 4, particularly focused on =
the differences between unit 3 and unit 4. Intro call with Vitesse Systems,=
 a company that makes various waveguides and heat exchangers with whom we a=
re talking about brazing. The cavity plate for unit 5 needs to have various=
 hanging chads removed at the 4 small holes and the 1 large hole in each va=
lve seat.</p>
<p local-id=3D"47a50a19-42b6-4532-ab6b-52223b3a6dcd">Vitesse Systems said t=
hat having two horizontal joints at the same time tends to fail.</p>
<p local-id=3D"69be447f-ea19-4e5e-9ebc-7e83e5bda1a0"></p>
<p local-id=3D"51e5a7b1-75d9-4af0-b5b3-34bfae685518">2025-02-25</p>
<p local-id=3D"20221d8c-a04e-482a-94d9-895cdb589978">PXL: After unit 4 was =
machined, I pressure tested it and found only one leak. I then cleaned the =
braze stop from the inside by injecting muriatic acid into all unit cells, =
then flushed out all the acid, then blew compressed air through all the ope=
nings to remove as much water as possible. After doing this, I found severa=
l more leaks (~8 in total) that I then marked, grooved, and epoxied.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6d6d084f-a83c-411e-9e70-0b37a9f9f148">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"83b7a636-e78a-459f-ab8b-efcdf8376bc1">REDRESS: I made a gree=
n part in one of the new high-temperature molds with the latest batch of po=
wder (70% PEG 8K 30% PMMA 15K) and it was not very flowable, after curing t=
here were multiple cracks visible through the translucent mold, and the gre=
en part was not waterproof; while trying to dissolve off the mold the part =
dissolved the same way PVP-VA bound parts tend to.</p>
<p local-id=3D"985057b4-70fb-4268-a660-203f32276d18">NEXTGEN: All the pelle=
t extruder components are now spliced into the Prusa 3D printer. Since it i=
s not possible to directly control the Prusa functions with a laptop, I am =
setting up OctoPrint on a Raspberry Pi to control the Prusa. This is necess=
ary because I need to print with the nozzle at a lower temperature than the=
 Prusa will allow (my target paraffin printing temp is ~45C, and the Prusa =
won=E2=80=99t extrude below 180C).</p>
<p local-id=3D"0c3e7e22-a788-4b69-b2dd-3b1275225035"></p>
<p local-id=3D"25b55dda-25f3-4644-b7f5-8a9181935c96">2025-02-26</p>
<p local-id=3D"2c9ae26a-ff97-4c1c-b2bc-05654923a09c">PXL: The unit was pres=
sure tested and was found to have three leaks. I ground out the leak points=
 (using a 60 degree conical burr to try and form an undercut in the stainle=
ss steel) and filled the grooves with epoxy.</p>
<p local-id=3D"219b4713-d5bb-4160-9e2c-e42de64bb100">REDRESS: Worked on pri=
nting more high-temperature water soluble molds. I am also going through an=
d retrying some earlier formulas that I had abandoned for what I now think =
are insufficient reasons. I am testing the PVP-VA/PMMA/PEG/SA/AS formula I =
made a while back in one of the high-temperature molds. I believe I abandon=
ed the formula because the parts were somewhat soft at elevated temperature=
, but I now think this is less important and this recipe yielded powder tha=
t is highly flowable and produces green parts that are both waterproof and =
stronger than the PMMA/PEG parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"da9cfb85-866a-4b2d-bc1e-8708f7f2f1aa">HOPPER: Made a new rol=
ler using Payman=E2=80=99s polygonal design suggestion. It was printed on t=
he Bambu from CF PLA as the Formlabs printer takes longer and its parts tak=
e more post-processing, and previous experiments suggested that there is no=
 functional difference between FDM and SLA printed parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"29e374e3-adc2-43fd-a8d8-1a657c7aa873">NEXTGEN: Got the wax p=
ellet extruder operating on the Prusa and started to run a few test prints.=
 So far I have had minimal success with getting a good print, but it does f=
ollow a tool path and extrude wax.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4660b2b3-4a30-457b-9922-020f8cea5676"></p>
<p local-id=3D"bef91b96-9320-420a-811f-fe9d2ef2c9a2">2025-02-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"ac86ecb0-c0b5-4fd7-8507-ab4db0ec0256">HOPPER: Discussing nex=
t week=E2=80=99s meeting with Dan at B-jetting next week. Goal is to figure=
 out how to present our current designs and results to get his advice on ho=
w to move forward with the project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4aca3f1e-fa96-45b6-a2b0-8912b68b414c">PXL: There is still on=
e leak in unit 4, so Steve did the final machining on unit 4 (adding vacuum=
 grooves, drilling vertical holes for the vacuum inlets, and removing plate=
 mounting tabs). Next I will grind out the leaking joint and epoxy it again=
. Received a new rotary tool to use for rework that has a remote motor and =
a pedal for turning the tool on and off and controlling its speed. It also =
has a three jaw chuck for holding burrs with different shank diameters and =
a more solid bearing setup than the cordless Dremel tool to reduce chatteri=
ng while using the tool. Also need to print a jig for aligning the flatteni=
ng punches that will be used to flatten and expand the parts after they are=
 installed in the plate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1c69f96c-f244-4905-a7ae-faedd38ca87f">NEXTGEN: Continued wor=
k on printing with the paraffin printing Prusa. I have received the heat br=
eak, extruder screw, and heater for the next pellet extruder printhead. I h=
ave not received the stepper motor yet. I still need to design and print a =
mount to interface with Pourya=E2=80=99s gantry.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a5f47be-8e3c-4206-87a2-e5fd972edd3a">REDRESS: The part made=
 yesterday with the elaborate formula powder was not completely; it didn=E2=
=80=99t completely dissolve but it broke apart into several pieces during t=
he mold dissolving process. I tested the sample cured in a ceramic cup and =
it was also not highly waterproof. The heat curing process only went to 250=
F; I think the PMMA needed to be heated more to melt and join the particles=
. I have filled up a high temperature water soluble mold with the same powd=
er and I am curing it to 300F. I have also made a green part by filling a c=
eramic cup with a slurry made from PMMA/PEG coated powder, some PVP-VA coat=
ed powder, and a wax mixture made with paraffin, stearic acid, and microcry=
stalline wax. Technically, based on how MIM works traditionally, it should =
fall apart once heated above the melt point of paraffin, but if it is possi=
ble to use paraffin as a binder in this way then it makes slurries more fea=
sible. I also tried to fill a low temperature water-soluble mold with the p=
araffin/powder slurry, but the slurry can only be poured when it is above t=
he melt point of paraffin, and pouring in a slurry at that temperature caus=
es the mold to soften and warp. It may be possible to fill one of the high-=
temperature water-soluble molds with the slurry, but that material doesn=E2=
=80=99t print reliably so I try to avoid using those molds whenever possibl=
e.</p>
<p local-id=3D"130e2522-a6a5-4319-87a4-81ba142ac2fe"></p>
<p local-id=3D"26b66ac3-d0ef-4db7-b147-e94df9f04be7">2025-02-28</p>
<p local-id=3D"ee88753a-7905-4fc6-8d46-613f07048cd0">NEXTGEN: Continued tes=
ting of paraffin pellet extruder on Prusa. Able to fill a channel in a gree=
n part with wax. Pellet extruder needs priming to build up pressure to extr=
ude and continues to extrude after the screw has stopped due to residual pr=
essure. This effect seems to be fairly reliable so it can in theory be comp=
ensated for in software, but it is something to keep in mind. Received two =
different geared NEMA 17 stepper motors for the printhead to be installed o=
n the test gantry. I still need to design and print components for the gant=
ry mounted printhead and assemble it. Pourya suggested using super glue to =
seal the nozzle threads; this apparently works well to seal them and is als=
o more removable than epoxy.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2a7786d1-3ac7-4d42-ad56-a11ad8746212">REDRESS: The green par=
t formed yesterday was not completely waterproof and fell apart during mold=
 dissolving. I also noted cracks on the top of the part after curing and be=
fore attempting to dissolve the mold. I am currently dissolving some Poly(e=
thylene-co-vinyl acetate) <a href=3D"https://www.sigmaaldrich.com/US/en/pro=
duct/aldrich/340502?srsltid=3DAfmBOoqpVP9ohAhJUe3CDdMzNdzFPgNhwRILnoty5lBl6=
z1NaKljKG_x" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.sigmaaldr=
ich.com/US/en/product/aldrich/340502?srsltid=3DAfmBOoqpVP9ohAhJUe3CDdMzNdzF=
PgNhwRILnoty5lBl6z1NaKljKG_x</a> in dichloromethane to use as a waterproof =
powder coating binder. I have historically been only able to dissolve P(ECV=
A) in paraffin, but it seems to be at least somewhat soluble in dichloromet=
hane, although it is taking a long time to dissolve. I have ordered some to=
luene that may be a more effective solvent.</p>
<p local-id=3D"65977cd6-70f3-40b1-966c-a0cd987c82a1">PXL: The plate was fou=
nd to leak at one point where it had not previously been epoxied. I will gr=
ind out the leak point and epoxy it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ac3fd319-379a-4557-abee-b0ab7ec03ffa"></p>
<p local-id=3D"94dbae8c-23e9-42b5-ab7f-0d8bfb9ecc25">2025-03-03</p>
<p local-id=3D"2f3b05d5-8f71-43ea-9b0b-a5c87f634f6d">NEXTGEN: Working on mo=
unt to install pellet extruder and filament extruder (Creality Sprite) on P=
ourya=E2=80=99s gantry.</p>
<p local-id=3D"906af421-2f60-41f9-83ca-2110b4f91615">HOPPER: Meeting with D=
an at B-Jetting to go over the results of our testing and the hopper design=
s we=E2=80=99ve tried so far.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fcd6932b-9980-4256-8b18-5b8c4ff21b16">PXL: A leak was found =
at one point that had been previously epoxied. The plate had to be pressuri=
zed for several hours before the leak appeared. Also ran a dye penetrant te=
st on the plate that showed several disbondments at the vertical seal that =
did not leak, suggesting that we are getting some successful seals at the h=
orizontal joint. I also reamed out the 5mm holes, but I am currently waitin=
g on the 4.5mm reamers to arrive to be able to ream those holes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"757b41b7-917b-4d2f-92d1-ff2a59ee2eb8">REDRESS: Ground and si=
eved a powder that was made with 500g of 17-4 powder, 15g of PVP-VA, 2g of =
PEG 8K, and 1g of Stearic Acid. I also tried coating a mold with the custom=
 wax I made (blend of paraffin, stearic acid, and P(ECVA)) and coating one =
with a PMMA/PEG blend. The idea is to waterproof the inside of the mold to =
protect the green part during mold dissolving and to improve flow of impreg=
nating wax along the mold between the green part and the mold. I am also di=
ssolving the mold off of a PVP-VA part that had been impregnated with my cu=
stom wax.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b07db0af-e530-459c-a5f8-ea5b8b952348"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b59d5215-7629-46c4-abc8-1659b09ab8c6">2025-03-04</p>
<p local-id=3D"66e12795-f17d-483b-a422-3342e94ad998">PXL: A test of the pla=
te did not show any leaks, so the plate is going to Steve to have the tabs =
machined off. If the plate passes another leak test after machining and cle=
aning, it should be ready to go to the customer. Discovered one leak after =
cleaning that expanded to two after several hours under pressure. Ground ou=
t the leaking area and epoxied it. AEM has issued an order to stop work on =
units 5, 6, and 7 and instead have use work on the changed design. I believ=
e unit 5 had a good shot at working if we used the punches to press the cop=
per parts into place with sufficient force. Alternatively, I believe the cu=
rrent design could work if epoxy was used instead of brazing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a8be24ad-f607-4da8-be98-e4ddd2f56717">REDRESS: I have the gr=
een part made with my custom PVP-VA/PEG/SA powder in a waxed mold and one m=
ade using PMMA/PEG/SA powder in a PMMA/PEG coated mold. The PVP-VA green pa=
rt will be impregnated with my custom low-shrinkage high-flow wax to waterp=
roof it, while the PMMA part will have some PMMA binder added to the top to=
 enhance waterproofing and have the mold dissolved off without wax impregna=
tion. Currently trying to dissolve the mold off of the PMMA/PEG part. I san=
ded the bottom off of the mold and the green part seemed to be broken into =
pieces at the bottom, possibly because of the sanding process or possibly b=
ecause it was like that during the curing process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"95fc2494-2399-45ae-9d4a-b498ab458b8d">HOPPER: Brainstorming =
meeting. Vibration applied to roller directly. Set up linear stage to move =
hopper along one axis. Quantify the uniformity required and develop a syste=
m for measuring it. System is currently being run with a Duet board so it s=
hould be easy to set it up to run a stepper motor with linear stage. <stron=
g>Adapt current doser design to PILOT printer. Try printing rollers from PP=
. Look for air knives.</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"28f5613b-a894-4ed3-913f-473049c72e15"></p>
<p local-id=3D"f3b0d7aa-3d16-4226-b768-2fd49ca828be">2025-03-05</p>
<p local-id=3D"de8bb753-aa2a-4398-be8a-9fa44e09b1e6">NEXTGEN: Designed moun=
t for Ender Sprite extruder. Will print to test fit and reprint if necessar=
y.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe32dea9-3d77-4934-9eda-4442d3c1174b">PXL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"394eda17-14dd-4678-9b18-5d81d171e3f6">REDRESS: Made a batch =
of PVP-VA (3%) coated powder. Made 1 kg of powder with 15g of PVP-VA dissol=
ved in ~150 mL of water. It is important that the powder slurry be well mix=
ed and as thick as possible before drying on a hot plate to prevent settlin=
g. I also added .1g of stearic acid as a flow aid. Also experimenting with =
dissolving paraffin in N-Heptane and soaking a green part with it to waterp=
roof it and enable the mold to be washed off without destroying the green p=
art.</p>
<p local-id=3D"27197b6c-7f14-40f7-8187-1f8cdb0ac594"></p>
<p local-id=3D"462ee296-f69d-49c2-b15f-ccb6e65de17b">2025-03-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a0b47ca-a81a-4eee-b554-b041265a4cf9">REDRESS: Left the 1kg =
batch of PVP-VA powder on the hot plate at 110F overnight. Ground it and si=
fted it, then filled 3X high-temperature water-soluble molds and 2 ceramic =
cups with 20g each and put in the lower Keith furnace to cure with a cycle =
heating from 70-275F at 30F/hour, with a 2 hour hold at peak.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9d901573-0654-4ac9-8e76-d637c9b0864f">PXL: Began assembling =
Unit 5 in preparation for brazing tomorrow, including: test-fitting parts t=
o select a set that fits as well as possible, applying braze stop to the ca=
vity plate and all copper parts, installing flow spreaders and copper parts=
, and pressing each copper part with a punch made from pre-hardened 4140 st=
eel in a hydraulic press.</p>
<p local-id=3D"34a643bc-5c67-48fa-a6a0-ca29008ca09d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a7a6a2d7-e988-4904-a33a-401d2062114a">2025-03-07</p>
<p local-id=3D"0b707b74-d26b-433d-aa37-c3ea8e6d5be8">PXL: The heights of th=
e copper parts in Unit 5 are currently being measured. NicroBraz green need=
s to be applied to each copper part and also around the edge of the plate t=
o prevent braze filler material from flowing down the edges of the plate or=
 flowing into the vacuum port holes. Wire preforms also need to be cut to t=
he correct length, bent if necessary, and held in place with braze paste.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"e1748d13-a17b-494f-b5a6-f8cc038a8aa5">NEXTGEN: Meeting with =
IVEK to discuss precision wax dispensing systems.</p>
<p local-id=3D"58a16e35-004b-4f68-95fd-4e4e9f958751">HOPPER: Continuing to =
work on design of HOPPER V3 for installation into the PILOT printers. One d=
esign goal is to make it compatible with doser-style dispensing or Payman=
=E2=80=99s powder valving system. Meeting discussing progress on hopper pro=
ject.</p>
<p local-id=3D"acc5c980-08f8-4cb4-94b2-328a5f0bbeaf"></p>
<p local-id=3D"4861f7a9-dac5-4953-9282-f3f52488a1f9">2025-03-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"60c957b9-d15f-4d85-b13f-96080b57ba55">PXL: Received Unit 5 a=
nd was leak tested by Sean. Multiple leaks were found. Next steps are TBD; =
Marcus has contacted Rajan to ask for permission to rebraze the assembly bu=
t as yet I have heard no reply.</p>
<p local-id=3D"242f2643-de2d-4a7c-b330-0d6d3f0d65bd">HOPPER: Continuing to =
work on design of HOPPER V3 to be installed in the PILOT printer. I have de=
signed the endcaps with the motor mounted on one side, but I am having diff=
iculty designing the overall system mounting; for vibration isolation it is=
 best that the assembly be resting on springs on top of the PILOT Y axis pl=
ate, but the HOPPER assembly must be installed from underneath due to the p=
rotruding motors.</p>
<p local-id=3D"478f67af-8034-4cff-81fc-d5587b4557ec">NEXTGEN: Working on as=
sembling the pellet extruder for Pourya=E2=80=99s gantry. I have designed a=
 new hopper for it and printed one out from ESD-safe PETG, but the print di=
d not come out as well as I=E2=80=99d like so I am going to print it again,=
 along with a test roller to see how powder sticks to it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5050f7ae-ab92-4799-b0b8-bdd5aab4ce5f">REDRESS: Removed a gre=
en part from the lower Keith furnace that had been filled and cured over th=
e weekend, then impregnated it with wax that is a blend of paraffin, PE-CVA=
, stearic acid, and mineral oil, to make a theoretically soft, low-shrinkag=
e wax. I first soaked it in melted wax on the large hot plate set to 150C f=
or 2 hours, then put it in a vacuum chamber to completely impregnate the pa=
rt. I then dissolved off the mold. The surface finish of the part looked go=
od but there were several longitudinal cracks that seemed to originate at t=
he castellation notches on the top of the part. The part did seem quite wat=
erproof. I also mixed up a batch of TEG-DMA/17-4 PH slurry and filled 2 mol=
ds with it, then put them in the lower Keith furnace to cure. The ratio I u=
sed is 500g metal powder to 50g TEG-DMA, which is a high binder load but I =
am trying to get some aesthetically good green parts even if it partially c=
ompromises debindability. I also mixed up a batch of coated powder that is =
currently drying made with 17-4PH powder and a binder liquid that comprises=
 PMMA, PEG 8K, and stearic acid dissolved in acetone. I used the same ratio=
 as I did TEG-DMA, although the actual binder load of the powder once it is=
 dried should be considerably lower because the PMMA/PEG/SA binder componen=
ts are a small percentage of the binder liquid. With both of these batches =
I made an effort to ensure that they were well-mixed with an electric hand =
mixer to ensure that the powder is completely coated with binder; I think t=
hat this is an important variable that I have so far been neglecting.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e9b9e5a2-ccd8-4767-942d-1862b34acdf4"></p>
<p local-id=3D"3d679e1e-a36e-42ec-af8c-c82f8f4fc7e6">2025-03-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e834fd4-e7ff-4fdc-9dc3-c58baeec8cae">Bagel Tuesday Company =
Meeting - 2X PILOT printers to Epson and 2X to IHI; undecided whether they =
will be made here or at Rajan=E2=80=99s machinery manufacturer. Discussed t=
he redesign of the PXL heat exchanger.</p>
<p local-id=3D"72bfc517-023d-4c2a-9cf6-ab3f357fbd65">Company Cleanup: Same =
as last time, I spent the hour cleaning up the fabrication R&amp;D lab.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e24075e-08cd-41fb-89d8-0be809ff17c7">PXL: PXL meeting was s=
cheduled but not canceled and not held.</p>
<p local-id=3D"261fe197-3a4b-46ed-970a-b1af606088ad">HOPPER: Completed prel=
iminary design of hopper body, side mounts, and upper mounts.</p><span clas=
s=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embed=
