<p local-id=3D"fe84d08f-78e2-44e9-9e29-cf5814023aa3">This recipe will, in t=
heory, be triggered to thermally crosslink when heated sufficiently, yieldi=
ng strong, waterproof green parts that can have their sacrificial molds dis=
solved without damage but will be easier to debind than a green part bound =
purely with TEG-DMA.</p>
<p local-id=3D"69a44412-b2f2-4c6e-b314-c1a13e65e510">HOPPER: Connected an a=
udio amplifier to the signal generator to drive the bare voice coil speaker=
 element and the PS5 controller vibrator. I was able to liquifact the powde=
r using either element; the voice coil was able to transmit more energy int=
o the powder but is considerably larger and noisier than the PS5 controller=
 vibrator. The most effective frequency seemed to be around 144 Hz, much lo=
wer than I had expected but in line with Claude=E2=80=99s suggestions. I al=
so connected one of the ultrasonic horns we have around to the 200V driver =
I got previously, and was able to generate a tone but not a significant amp=
litude of vibration. This may be due to the power capability of the driver =
I have or a limitation of the horn itself.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ca36cdf1-049a-4f03-ae1c-acf48ba5ff77"></p>
<p local-id=3D"6c5d81e2-e135-4ffb-9d87-848f91afd03c">2025-03-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"beda30c0-36c9-4765-846f-f825aa35d6e5">PXL: Assemble PXL unit=
 5 for first cycle of BAg-8 rebraze. Cleaned all joints with a stainless st=
eel wire wheel in a small rotary tool, the cleaned with acetone and air. Ap=
plied NicroBraz green braze stop around the edge of the plate and inside th=
e perimeter of each copper part. Applied braze stop to each corner (I used =
less braze stop than usual to conserve it; the only tube we have on hand is=
 running low). I straightened BAg-8 .032=E2=80=9D wire then cut it to the a=
ppropriate lengths. I upgraded my wire cutting tool with a glass bottle tha=
t screws onto the end; this catches cut wires so it isn=E2=80=99t necessary=
 to move the tool over a cup to drop the cut wires every time a wire is cut=
. I applied nickel powder to the lift pins and left a fillet-shaped pile of=
 nickel powder around the joint. Normally I try to confine the nickel powde=
r to the joint to be sealed, but this time I left a pile of powder to poten=
tially generate a brazed fillet that may provide better sealing. This techn=
ique may be applied to the chamfered joints as well.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6536ecd4-ba98-40ce-921a-7a8efa1b2b77">HOPPER: I printed out =
some of the smaller components required for HOPPER V3 from 3DXTECH ESD-PETG=
. In theory this material is slightly conductive so it should prevent stati=
c buildup and reduce the likelihood of powder sticking to it, but this rema=
ins to be tested.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0ff78db-69c5-4370-97c4-b5ff5efe553b">NEXTGEN: I finished wi=
ring up the pellet extruder for Pourya=E2=80=99s gantry. The only step rema=
ining is to test it before it can be mounted.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ceef09f7-9931-49fa-99c1-c91d48aa280b">I reassembled the Prus=
a and got it printing again; I think it is important to have at least two p=
rinters running 1.75mm filament because the Bambu printer may be booked in =
the near future for HOPPER parts. I want to be able to print water-soluble =
molds while the Bambu printer is being used for more critical projects.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d45617c5-a451-4de8-9f45-a019bf4689b5"></p>
<p local-id=3D"0928ead6-660c-4219-8b8d-e36e36573ac5">2025-03-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"af5a3ca5-c36f-457c-ab3e-ec90a49ff95f">HOPPER: I test-fit the=
 dovetail joints printed (I printed 2 of the actual side mounts and a small=
 section of HOPPER V3 that contains the dovetail negative) and they seem to=
 fit well; there is some drag on them but that is unlikely to be a critical=
 problem and will go away over time as the joints wear by being assembled a=
nd disassembled.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d118e3be-33f2-4b2b-bc4d-bbe9dc105716">REDRESS: Working on ge=
tting the Prusa to reliably print Aquasys 120 molds. I first had issues wit=
h stringing that I resolved by adjusting the scarf joint settings; now I am=
 have issues with the print cracking. This material is meant to be printed =
in an enclosed and heated build chamber, so it is unsurprising that it has =
issue with being printed in the open, particularly because the light R&amp;=
D lab is colder than usual today.</p>
<p local-id=3D"17f00231-85d4-4d94-accc-a160c8d00460">NEXTGEN: Order for a 3=
6=E2=80=9D fume hood for the heavy R&amp;D area was approved. This will be =
used to chemical mixing for binders and for wax melting experiments that ma=
y burn wax and release toxic fumes. I have the pellet extruder working well=
 on the benchtop; at 50C it is able to extruder through a 0.4mm nozzle. Per=
 Pourya=E2=80=99s suggestion I super glued the nozzle to the heater and the=
 nozzle/heater assembly to the heat break. This seems to provide an effecti=
ve seal,</p>
<p local-id=3D"084044ec-d8dc-441a-bd13-fa74263678de"></p>
<p local-id=3D"117df948-e683-4557-9f77-8da1f1c7d2dc">2025-03-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"23eeaf12-4caa-41e5-b568-2f615a6859b9">HOPPER: I have the bod=
y, side mounts, and prototype dovetail vibration isolation supports printed=
. I redesigned the dovetail mounts to have a screw passing through them so =
the will be held in place on the PILOT printer=E2=80=99s Y axis gantry wher=
e before they were just resting on springs. I have also designed the body a=
nd roller for Payman=E2=80=99s hopper design; it recycles the side mounts f=
rom the primary hopper design but flips them upside down so the roller is s=
lightly closer to the bottom of the hopper. Drilled 1X 3mm hole in each of =
2X 8mm shafts and installed a 3mm X 12mm L spring pin in each hole to rotat=
ionally couple the shaft to the roller. I assembled HOPPER V3.0 without a r=
oller and the components seem to fit properly.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a1008a39-34d3-4e4a-8dcb-3723939b5a95">NEXTGEN: The pellet-st=
yle extruder head is complete and tested and ready to be installed on Poury=
a=E2=80=99s gantry when the system is ready for it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fb889137-ec80-476b-be4f-1c5039058df6">REDRESS: I made a new =
formulation of powder over the weekend that has 15g of TEG-DMA and 5g of 3-=
(Trimethoxysilyl)propyl methacrylate (adhesion promoter) dissolved in aceto=
ne and mixed with 1 kg of 17-4 PH treated powder. After drying it over the =
weekend it still seemed damp and clumpy, and did not sift well through the =
sieve in the powder room. I have filled a water-soluble mold and a ceramic =
crucible with it as best I could and am passing it through the TEG-DMA cycl=
e on the lower Keith furnace (ramp to 200F, hold 1 hour, ramp to 275F, cool=
). I mixed up a new batch of coated powder with 10g PVP-VA and 20G TEG-DMA =
with the intent of making a reasonably strong =E2=80=9Ccake=E2=80=9D of pow=
der that can be ground and sieved cleanly (PVP-VA) while also being waterpr=
oof once it has been fully cured in a mold (TEG-DMA). Since this batch is a=
lready looking like it is going to be damp once fully dry, I am mixing up a=
nother batch. This new batch has 10g PVP-VA with 3g TEG-DMA and 420g of 17-=
4 PH powder, yielding a coated powder with a 3% binder load with binder tha=
t is 3:1 PVP-VA to TEG-DMA. This will hopefully yield a crispier powder tha=
t is easier to grind, sieve, and pour, while still giving the benefits of T=
EG-DMA binder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7a70f4fb-a76e-4c98-a75c-678cb6e9d43a">PXL: No word yet on th=
e success or failure of Unit 5 post rebraze, but I overheard that AEM is co=
nsidering having us solder the unit so presumably it leaked. This may give =
us the opportunity to try furnace soldering instead of manual soldering. Ma=
nual soldering had mixed success in the past, so I personally would like to=
 explore furnace soldering further. By soldering in a controlled atmosphere=
 with controlled thermal parameters, we can gain improved process control t=
hat may yield a more reliable soldered joint.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3d97f9cc-c158-4496-95b9-308d739d2c67"></p>
<p local-id=3D"1875a320-e996-4537-b096-e0b433ec6224">2025-03-18</p>
<p local-id=3D"f3e260f4-913b-4adc-9770-7dcbb97999d2">PXL: Unit 5 has 5 leak=
s after rebrazing and machining, which is considerably better than after th=
e first braze, but next steps are still to be determined. At the PXL meetin=
g we discussed various approaches for repair, including furnace soldering. =
I think the best option is flux soldering in the nitrogen furnace.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7f2cfdb3-c2bb-4f73-b6a5-7a176b3c934e">HOPPER: Redesigned hop=
per body to use square drop-in nuts instead of heat-set inserts and also ha=
ve a bolt-on dovetail receiver instead of printing the dovetail as part of =
the body (it was difficult to print the hopper body sideways with the dovet=
ail attached; since there is only a small part of the body attached directl=
y to the build plate it had a tendency to pull off of the build plate).</p>
<p local-id=3D"9939b6c9-a82e-4a75-bf83-c47a799bb4e2">REDRESS: The green par=
ts made with the 15g of TEG-DMA and 5g of 3-(Trimethoxysilyl)propyl methacr=
ylate (adhesion promoter) powder came out strong and waterproof, but it was=
 not possible to make a cohesive green part in the soluble mold because the=
 coated powder was damp and clumpy and did not flow well. Where I was able =
to pack the powder into the ceramic crucible by hand the green part came ou=
t waterproof and nearly indestructible (I couldn=E2=80=99t get it out of th=
e crucible so I smashed apart the crucible as much as I could, but where it=
 is glued to the green part I couldn=E2=80=99t affect it by hammering it wi=
thout applying a lot more impact than I was willing to). I am currently dry=
ing a new batch of powdering using 3% binder load (12g PMMA (8g 15K 4g 350K=
), 2.25g TEG-DMA, .75g Stearic Acid). The TEG-DMA content should be small e=
nough that the powder cake can still be ground and sieved and the powder wi=
ll flow reasonably well, but hopefully the TEG-DMA will act as a crosslinki=
ng initiator to cause the PMMA to crosslink when being cured. I also tested=
 the powder made using a 3:1 ratio of PVP-VA to TEG-DMA; the dried cake was=
 flexible and seemed damp after grinding, so I added about a tablespoon of =
fumed silica as a flow aid, which did allow it to flow well but the green p=
art produced was weak and not waterproof. This may be due to the PVP-VA con=
tent or the high flow aid content.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b6f4e2e-2919-4728-9731-183c7b7c47dd"></p>
<p local-id=3D"114909ab-68d1-4c48-ba1e-d7b244fc7a0a">2025-03-19</p>
<p local-id=3D"c100435e-6175-43d3-9ddb-9ab55fa458f9">PXL: Meeting with Raja=
n, Sean, Marcus, Manju, Payman, and me about design changes to the PXL. Spe=
cifically the near-term goal is to use a roughed plate that we have around =
and machine some copper parts as best we can to create a lap joint with mor=
e overlap. We are also going to do a brazing run to try and repair the last=
 leaks on Unit 5; I grooved the 4 side leaks and used a counterboring bit t=
o machine down the 2 leaking lift pin bosses. I then placed BAg-8 wire pref=
orms in all 4 grooves and the 2 lift pin bosses, tacked them in place with =
BAg-8 paste, and applied NicroBraz Green braze stop around all 6 repair poi=
nts. The grooving operation is intended to increase BFM access to the leak =
points, create a chamfer that if filled with BFM may act to seal the leak p=
oint even after machining, and provide a clean material surface for the BFM=
 to bond to.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b0860e9-328b-4629-92e2-447da3635a52">HOPPER: The HOPPER bod=
y V3.2 print last night was not successful and the two dovetail adapter par=
ts were warped because of poor bed adhesion, so I am currently reprinting t=
he dovetail adapters and will reprint the body tonight. I am also going to =
change the design of the body; I am rethinking the square nuts because heat=
-set inserts will provide a more reliable and reusable attachment point and=
 a cleaner design, and since we are likely not going to print very many HOP=
PER iterations the cost of using heat-set inserts is not a huge factor.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dbdf46bc-ca18-491d-94d0-fc7d5b0d0b5c">REDRESS: When mixing t=
he powder batch yesterday I forgot to add the TEG-DMA after dissolving the =
PMMA and Stearic Acid so I had to add 3.3g of TEG-DMA to 150 mL of acetone =
(I accidentally added too much TEG-DMA, so the recipe is going to have 3.3g=
 of TEG-DMA instead of 2.25g), then break up the dried cake and dissolve it=
 in the TEG-DMA/acetone solution. I mixed it in a cup first and poured it i=
nto a metal pie tin, but since there were still visible clumps I added more=
 acetone and mixed it further in the pie tin to uniformly coat the powder.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"3bbd27d5-0ef8-4946-bda1-0f6be04fe55d"></p>
<p local-id=3D"788ed17f-e189-4da1-85b7-2517cb3d27a1">2025-03-20</p>
<p local-id=3D"a9a1c86e-4ed8-44dc-a5b7-3db8e2d56723">HOPPER: Post-processed=
 hopper bodies for Payman=E2=80=99s design (HOPPER V3.1) and the latest HOP=
PER V3 design (HOPPER V3.4). Will install M3 and M5 heat set inserts and as=
semble. In process of printing rollers for HOPPER V3.4 and 4X different rol=
ler=E2=80=99s for Mohsen=E2=80=99s hopper design.</p>
<p local-id=3D"454b9cc2-0c0c-4a9e-90ef-e51de7b06d53">PXL: Meeting with AEM =
discussing the PXL. They plan to do a brazing experiment with machined copp=
er heat exchangers and a thick machined 304 SS base, without plating either=
. They also are increasing the thickness of the flange to increase the leng=
th of the vertical joint.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e6dbb3c4-4d99-4e26-9e14-b107812d2562">R&amp;D Meeting w/ Mic=
hele: 1.5hr meeting with me, Michele, Marcus, Mahmood, and Midhun discussin=
g R&amp;D projects in progress and the org structure of the R&amp;D departm=
ent.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1bd20754-bdbe-4400-ad6f-a8347e2508b9">REDRESS: Filled and cu=
red a mold and a ceramic crucible with the TEG-DMA/PMMA/SA powder, and the =
results were generally positive: the green part seemed reasonably strong an=
d highly waterproof (although it did have cracks when the mold was dissolve=
d, possibly from heating it too rapidly in the curing cycle or more likely =
from poor packing density). I am making a new batch tonight, with a 3% bind=
er load comprising 66% PMMA 15K, 15% TEG-DMA, 10% Dibutyl Phthalate (plasti=
cizer)</p>
<p local-id=3D"435bde6f-8a92-453b-9c49-a6aecfd67e0a"></p>
<p local-id=3D"f786db69-c103-4809-bfba-63e43812baf1">2025-03-21</p>
<p local-id=3D"97991dd0-9ee8-4847-ae65-a3ef277f6a1f">HOPPER: Assembled HOPP=
ER V3.1 (Payman=E2=80=99s idea) and HOPPER V3.4 (current design for install=
ation in PILOT). Still struggling with printing rollers from PETG on the Pr=
usa and Bambu printers. Issues generally revolve around base layer adhesion=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e327c9ce-40e2-494c-9ac5-a9ee2d42f535">PXL: PXL Unit 5 has so=
me leaks after the 2nd rebraze. There are some leaking brazed seams as well=
 as leaking from the bottom of 3 lift pin bosses. I removed the plate from =
the pressure test fixture and noted that one of the o-ring grooves seemed t=
o be filled with ash; I would guess that an o-ring was left inside that gro=
ove during brazing and the o-ring carbonized. I blew out the o-ring groove,=
 then noted that there was some dust or debris that came out of one of the =
nearby heat exchanger element holes, so I went though and blew them all out=
. There was noticeable dust, debris, or liquid that was blown out of each o=
ne. I suspect that this is residue of the NicroBraz Red braze stop that was=
 used previously and not removed with acid.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c1400ec-dba2-44d7-804e-b230465b8942">REDRESS: The green par=
t whose mold I started dissolving yesterday was fully exposed today. There =
are some noticeable cracks, but the part seems to have duplicated the surfa=
ce finish of the 3D printed mold well and seems to be uniformly porous (as =
desired) without macroscopic voids. I ground and sieved the powder made usi=
ng the advanced binder recipe</p>
<p local-id=3D"3cba205b-14d7-4df6-8886-b1dc91585cf9"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b31773b0-7154-4592-b015-91e1efeaf7d3">2025-03-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"8d4ea94f-ccc4-4f1d-a5e0-11381bfb598c">HOPPER: Installed dose=
r roller in HOPPER V3.4 prototype and installed on HOPPER test station for =
Sean to use to test vibrators of different styles.</p>
<p local-id=3D"11446ef3-eda6-4afe-8596-cc89cca8f61e">REDRESS: Trying new ad=
vanced recipe with both 1% and 3% binder load.</p>
