<p local-id=3D"f4bb15ed-1158-4acc-aeeb-37e7a2803a2a">Your observation about=
 the crucible sample versus the slowly cooled part is incredibly valuable! =
This reveals the critical issue: <strong>thermal reversion during slow cool=
ing is destroying your crosslinked structure</strong>.</p>
<h2 local-id=3D"44f6e782-8aec-4553-994f-1b6d6faf0727" id=3D"JaredHumphreysL=
og-UnderstandingWhat'sHappening">Understanding What's Happening</h2>
<p local-id=3D"3cec74eb-4c60-4cef-96ec-f7d06d23685c">Your experience perfec=
tly illustrates a phenomenon called "thermal reversion" or "depolymerizatio=
n":</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"415fc75c-3cba-4b3d-97fd-c48276a372fc">
<li local-id=3D"3d18e4bd-0a5f-4065-a4ab-43f5c0095da5">
<p local-id=3D"e9329224-2021-43bb-9f06-7cabedff0aed"><strong>Initial Succes=
sful Crosslinking</strong></p>
<ul local-id=3D"e9fb5891-20e4-4b79-87fe-0d65b429537a">
<li local-id=3D"8971b994-15cb-4212-bfe4-e4ae284cae76">
<p local-id=3D"ba44d721-df64-49ef-b5b8-d448f70e9c45">When heated, the AIBN =
initiator generates free radicals</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c32fb7d9-692d-4f00-a604-b89efcfef090">
<p local-id=3D"434b6b76-ed40-49c4-a6bb-8a39dcc130e6">TEG-DMA forms crosslin=
ks between polymer chains</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"46963600-e297-4cab-9e23-fb16dcc0d51d">
<p local-id=3D"36929dd7-5f1e-46e5-8f24-887cb0955450">This creates a strong,=
 water-resistant structure (as seen in your crucible sample)</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"06e9b438-4053-4083-9b6e-974f406fe5f5">
<p local-id=3D"0e591b33-b7a0-4ce1-be31-a3562a1fed9c"><strong>Degradation Du=
ring Slow Cooling</strong></p>
<ul local-id=3D"e3d0f961-e203-4663-bb92-7e347c89780f">
<li local-id=3D"27ee167d-004c-4607-88b3-a3496e403aac">
<p local-id=3D"3169da2f-3ef9-43c1-b6a6-eb65202da923">Methacrylate polymers =
can undergo reversible reactions at elevated temperatures</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3797c954-1a2f-4c89-8d1a-dedb35446852">
<p local-id=3D"8fca9571-f29f-4c94-8c8e-8b3a094d3910">During slow cooling, o=
xygen can enter the system and create peroxy radicals</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"71698f67-3ea1-44ad-8f13-21e6d715afad">
<p local-id=3D"30d80405-c09d-41c6-ad72-989386e36f3a">These radicals attack =
the polymer network, breaking crosslinks</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9597a8dc-e2f6-467c-a34d-b1136f06c1d6">
<p local-id=3D"3db9553b-0d21-4443-ac8a-45a2588e22d5">The result: your parts=
 literally "un-crosslink" during cooling</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<h2 local-id=3D"0b9021be-0c4a-40c8-8b10-7174e10edbc6" id=3D"JaredHumphreysL=
og-CriticalSolution:ThermalShockCooling">Critical Solution: Thermal Shock C=
ooling</h2>
<p local-id=3D"a7642961-fcf9-40f9-8156-fc92009fe875">I've updated the recip=
e with several key changes targeting this specific issue:</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"26394cbb-21fd-4aab-bdad-277cff4c7f20">
<li local-id=3D"1e87b8d8-b77e-42b4-ba29-7aa1aa9adb97">
<p local-id=3D"b1db1990-6ae1-48a9-a899-1270a0dac2a7"><strong>Most Critical =
Change</strong>: Remove parts from oven immediately after final cure</p>
<ul local-id=3D"9458be2f-e782-4667-b08c-144612c00884">
<li local-id=3D"02772809-b8c8-4a99-9244-477f8791238e">
<p local-id=3D"9dfb5f2d-4147-47af-9b5d-8712b5500eed">Don't allow parts to c=
ool gradually in the oven</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9b1e3227-2ddf-45db-85e4-886d11865d22">
<p local-id=3D"bf240764-cf9d-4409-bd2c-0c863ef39239">This "freezes" the cro=
sslinked structure in its optimal state</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"5229fb89-4739-40a0-ac14-3f31006a1098">
<p local-id=3D"5af1cd74-4a0b-4532-bb77-7dc2b9436bfd"><strong>Added MEHQ as =
Optional Stabilizer</strong> (0.1%)</p>
<ul local-id=3D"edc2aca7-613e-428b-ae71-a615190ef2b6">
<li local-id=3D"1ee73728-2348-4545-b4f0-c1ccb03a32c6">
<p local-id=3D"9cd24ea5-ef87-491f-a7d1-187c1b902b12">Acts as an oxygen radi=
cal scavenger during cooling</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"10d31576-b92d-4a12-ba76-2e76b4fca987">
<p local-id=3D"ef0307b4-a694-46d6-ad01-1bd62e526a2d">Prevents degradation o=
f the polymer network</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c70a8f52-88d1-49d6-80f1-f05bc3a64f10">
<p local-id=3D"5e47dc7e-8c63-4a04-8152-60c5bdbde706">Very small amount to a=
void interference with the initial cure</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"c3598770-5af2-4a79-bd54-2bc42a5049f9">
<p local-id=3D"1bb7db3a-18b8-480c-8990-3446b6d9b3bf"><strong>Increased Init=
iator and Crosslinker</strong></p>
<ul local-id=3D"adea33bb-bddc-4bb9-ab3c-f0d997a68f62">
<li local-id=3D"9e28d55f-6a3e-44a7-805e-e0269dc4ad7d">
<p local-id=3D"26e7eccd-b237-4dd4-9dd1-f463f06a07d1">More AIBN (4% vs 3%) f=
or more complete crosslinking</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c28668a4-d964-4794-9315-a33cb2642a29">
<p local-id=3D"e6b62a52-d3e5-4ae0-a40c-146af74ad244">More TEG-DMA (16% vs 1=
5%) for higher crosslink density</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e05d52dc-0f82-4da5-890f-da7c35c3500e">
<p local-id=3D"15812a0c-cb92-4c5b-9f1d-51fce7986e03">Creates a more robust =
network that better resists degradation</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<h2 local-id=3D"fac10f6a-ed6d-4431-a32b-85ff2f270011" id=3D"JaredHumphreysL=
og-TheScienceBehindThis">The Science Behind This</h2>
<p local-id=3D"033d5c55-6f5b-4f0c-bfca-9ebb987d6f76">This phenomenon is wel=
l-documented in polymer chemistry:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"0352b6d9-1b3d-4b2e-aeab-580effab86e7">
<li local-id=3D"1ecd0a6c-5570-4bec-b620-20bb0d94a213">
<p local-id=3D"eb36b438-7501-41d2-ab71-eca1727fc979">Methacrylate systems c=
an undergo "ceiling temperature" effects</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a172fb54-86d0-43f1-83f3-6efb64c5d7ae">
<p local-id=3D"d14182aa-d0ac-4a6e-96d7-6e10521e3e97">Oxygen is a powerful i=
nhibitor for radical polymerization</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"55a5b9cd-55a1-4498-9b04-3a190e569d17">
<p local-id=3D"cc495d98-3232-4ea4-808c-3decc67965aa">The combination of hea=
t + time + oxygen creates ideal conditions for network degradation</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"0b252766-7643-4ee5-90dd-c040be401dc1">Your observation perfe=
ctly confirms this - the part quickly removed from heat maintained its stru=
cture, while the one that cooled slowly in the oxygen-containing oven degra=
ded.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b622c22c-ace7-4493-abdb-f60c522d2223">Would you like me to f=
urther explain any aspect of this thermal reversion phenomenon? Or would yo=
u prefer more details on implementing the rapid cooling process for differe=
nt part sizes?</p>
