</script></span> . In Japan, it is optional to use either red or black for =
single phase line voltage, but it would be better for use to use black beca=
use the cables sold by McMaster use the black-white-green American color co=
de.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e1f3f860-26d7-4b25-81bd-704d67ddab6a">HOPPER: Printed new Ed=
munds stage to Y axis mounts for the bladed hopper V3.4.0 because the older=
 ones did not allow the hopper to be raised high enough that the spreader b=
ar was clear of the base plate. Continuing to work on installing a limit sw=
itch to detect whether the gate has completely opened or closed. It is chal=
lenging because of the space limitations, mainly the fact that the opening =
in the Y axis gantry is so small, and the hopper needs to fit within that s=
pace to be able to be dropped in from above.</p>
<p local-id=3D"488f9c4a-47c1-45fa-b673-1b5780331129">NEXTGEN: Played with s=
ome different end mill options to see if changing geometry or wax formulati=
on can reduce wax buildup. I tried a stock end mill (.020=E2=80=9D cutting =
diameter, .060=E2=80=9D LOC, 4 flute, coated but exact coating formulation =
is unknown), an end mill from McMaster that has 2 flutes but has a 1/8=E2=
=80=9D shank diameter and so it incompatible with the .09375=E2=80=9D diame=
ter collets in the spindles currently being used in the Pilot printers,</p>
<p local-id=3D"85588e04-1e3a-4882-93e0-0f8f8d89c3b9">2025-07-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"589976fb-52cb-4e8c-ab45-f053ca64dc64">Bagel Tuesday: Emily d=
iscussed hiring for various positions, including several high-level positio=
ns for developing next-generation printers. There were several anonymous qu=
estions asked. One was asking why the pay scales for the recent job posting=
s were much higher than what current engineers are getting, and their respo=
nse was that the pay scales match what people are getting paid right now. S=
ince the job postings are for positions that are level V and VI, so this su=
ggests that at least Midhun and I are essentially junior-level engineers, s=
ince our pay is below the lowest pay of the pay scale. Another question ask=
ed was about 3DEO=E2=80=99s 1 year and 5 year plans. The 1 year plan is to =
stabilize the company, sell parts made with the laser and wax deposition te=
chnologies, sell parts made with inconel, prototype parts profitably, reduc=
e our cash burn by 1/2 by adding commercial revenue. In roughly 2 years we =
expect to be selling next-generation printers. Within 5 years, the plan is =
to sell the company. The third question asked was what exactly Epson expect=
s for their investment in 3DEO; the answer was that they expect to be first=
 in line for acquiring us if and when that makes sense.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8908960a-2c10-4614-a999-0e158f285621">HOPPER: Gave overview =
of hopper history and purpose to Mason Gunn, a new R&amp;D ME intern who st=
arted today. Modified design of bladed hopper as a result of some issues en=
countered with the blade riding up the hopper edge and motor mount breaking=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"62c9b588-d95f-4f86-94c8-909d2259e750">PILOT: Meeting reviewi=
ng machined part drawings. I raised a concern about bowing of the plate due=
 to weight of the powder and build tank and suggested we increase the base =
plate from 1/2=E2=80=9D to 3/4=E2=80=9D, but the decision was ultimately ma=
de to keep it the same.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0ddd38a-d665-45da-a9cf-7ef35b7a131d">NEXTGEN: Placed wax fi=
ltering setup in lower Keith furnace set to dwell for 2 hours at 220F.</p>
<p local-id=3D"40d9548e-753d-412b-b5fb-9fc1c7d5ff29">2025-07-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"aeb1df24-0db7-494e-963f-a1ade51582ee">NEXTGEN: Experimenting=
 with reaming the collets in the dental spindles we are using to fit end mi=
lls with a standard 1/8=E2=80=9D shank. It is difficult to find end mills w=
ith the smaller stock shank size that the spindles use; I have so far had t=
wo different tool makers no-quote my request for a tool bit with a .09375=
=E2=80=9D shank because they can=E2=80=99t grind something that small. Ream=
ing the collet will cause some loss of precision and increased runout, but =
that may be acceptable for basic proof-of-concept testing. I have ordered s=
everal end mill options to try from McMaster and placed an order request to=
 order an end mill from Harvey Tool for a single-flute end mill option.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c62a9f25-0e73-44d3-a5be-ffde7cb9670c">PILOT: Meeting with Ma=
njun to discuss the updated electrical BOM with components that are specifi=
ed by Novo. Steve agreed with me that the base plate should be made thicker=
. We are both concerned about distortion from weight, distortion from insta=
llation, and distortion during machining from the original ground state. Th=
ere also is no real disadvantage to a thicker plate; it would cost slightly=
 more and would be slightly heavier if we bumped the thickness up to 3/4=E2=
=80=9D (20 Kg to 30 Kg), but those are largely irrelevant concerns within t=
he scope of this project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bee3fba9-32d2-4237-a402-cb34849b2e50">Meeting with Will and =
Sean to go over the part machining drawings for the Epson Pilot printers.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"f1eaefc8-db95-4193-a57e-5b3bda4f088b">HOPPER: Printed a new =
V3.4.0 bladed hopper with some strength upgrades and mounting points for ha=
rd stops/limit switches to prevent overtravel of the blade. It also has mou=
nting points for a funnel to expand the capacity of the hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f653508e-6dea-4858-a496-fddd66ae1803">2025-07-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"dbcd68cf-b2d4-4cd5-8e71-5d1a9dbce8be">NEXTGEN: Bored out the=
 collet on a Beta dental spindle to fit a 1/8=E2=80=9D end mill. I bored it=
 out in the closed state so that if I bore it out to slightly less than the=
 end mill shank diameter (in theory) it will fit the end mill when open. I =
first reamed it to .122=E2=80=9D, which was too small to fit the end mill e=
ven when the collet was open. I then drilled it to .125=E2=80=9D, which was=
 still too small. I then drilled it to .1285=E2=80=9D (#30 drill bit) and t=
he end mill could fit when the collet was open but was loose even when the =
collet was closed. For now I super glued the most promising end mill (1/16=
=E2=80=9D diameter, 1 flute, ceramic coated carbide) into the collet, which=
 means the spindle will be essentially scrap once the end mill testing is c=
omplete, but it is the fastest way to test a single flute end mill. I will =
order a .126=E2=80=9D reamer and try that, which hopefully will be the swee=
t spot where the end mill will fit when the collet is open but be held in p=
lace when the collet is closed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5c5aec88-c7cd-4e3a-82ae-1f160a45e43a">Made a new formulation=
 of wax that is 90% paraffin and 10% stearic acid, with the intent of makin=
g a harder formulation of wax that is still possible to debind cleanly.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fdb75e63-46da-4719-b2f4-d07fad35cb68">The paraffin burnout t=
est done last night heating a ceramic crucible with .5g of paraplast paraff=
in to 1000F yielded the expected result: it burned out completely, with a f=
ew particles left in the crucible that I believe are ceramic fragments that=
 fell from the top of the furnace.</p>
<p local-id=3D"33efdc1f-649b-41e8-9fb2-b0893ff055cf">HOPPER: Designed and p=
rinting limit switch mounts/hardstops for zero-waste hopper V3.4.0. Designe=
