 VulcanJet wax drop dispensing head.</p>
<p local-id=3D"24b76fce-eb5e-4995-a8df-06cd5cf6c2ed">2025-07-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"0dae7ef0-e6c4-4755-a056-558dfe3b5f2d">NEXTGEN: Did a burnout=
 test of 1g of the 90/10 paraffin/stearic acid wax. There was a very small =
amount of black particles left behind, which is worse than any of the other=
 burnout tests (other than the first one, which left a large amount of blac=
k sludge-like residue).</p>
<p local-id=3D"1ef2906b-5de1-4d3e-ab48-44e3a4df1ed6">Working on modifying a=
 Beta spindle to accept 1/8=E2=80=9D shank diameter end mills. My procedure=
 is to remove the spindle and collet assembly, which is naturally closed wh=
en removed. I chuck it in the lathe and align it, then use a reamer to expa=
nd the hole. I started with a .122=E2=80=9D reamer, which did not allow the=
 end mill to be fully inserted when the collet was open (after the collet/s=
pindle assembly was reinstalled in the tool). I then tried a .1285=E2=80=9D=
 diameter #30 drill bit, which allowed the tool to slip even when the colle=
t was closed. I then used a .1245=E2=80=9D reamer, which did not allow the =
end mill to be inserted fully, much like the .122=E2=80=9D end mill. I susp=
ect that there is a diameter between .1245=E2=80=9D and .1285=E2=80=9D that=
 would work, but for the moment the runout with a tool inserted in the coll=
et reamed out to .1245=E2=80=9D seems to not have visible runout, so I deem=
ed it good enough for Mason to start testing with it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"53ad14b7-5451-467b-b2d7-e3b2cf86bb11">HOPPER: Removed limit =
switch from the zero-waste gated hopper in pilot 3 so Pourya could rewire i=
t; I wired it to be normally closed originally, which is generally preferre=
d because if the wire is cut it reads as the switch being pressed, but appa=
rently the rest of the limit switches on the pilot printers are wired to be=
 normally open. I believe I standardized that originally because only norma=
lly open Metrol limit switches were available from Automation Direct when I=
 was first ordering parts for pilot 1, and it became a de facto standard fr=
om then on. This is an interesting case of how an engineering decision can =
be propagated through design iterations even though the original motivation=
 is not there, even when the engineering decision is sub-optimal for the ap=
plication. Anyway, I removed the limit switch, Pourya rewired it, and I ins=
talled it. It is possible to remove and reinstall the limit switch while th=
e hopper is installed, but it is unergonomic enough that I would not want a=
nyone else to do it that way.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4a105bb7-c7fb-44ce-a84f-baf8c88f8a25">Biweekly cleanup in pr=
eparation for IHI/FusionX/someone tour on Thursday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2018718e-616b-4742-85ad-6c87420b5a5e">2025-07-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"1c079ff0-3f50-4fba-8435-a2c54e237f15">HOPPER: Post-processed=
 and populated with heat set inserts the hopper Payman asked me to print th=
at is a modified version of V3.3.5 (spring steel gated, extended shaft supp=
orted by two bearings). He modified it so that it uses a feature on the 3D =
printed body as the spreader bar instead of a metal part and reduced the ou=
tlet gap width, both presumably to reduce waste. I also populated a new ver=
sion of the linear-motion zero-waste hopper with threaded inserts. This is =
a version modified to accept a sharper blade (20=C2=B0 angle instead of the=
 previous 45=C2=B0 angle blade). There are also some minor dimensional chan=
ges I made to increase strength and improve rigidity and alignment of the l=
inear adjustment stages.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e9b6095-2fbb-4dd0-a8c1-434a548beb55">Payman gave Al the dra=
wing and stock for making the sharper blade, since Philip has not responded=
 to my email or Slack message. Al will need to order a chamfering bit to be=
 able to make it</p>
<p local-id=3D"fd9c0e49-2aa3-4d0f-9021-cad2a28aef0f">Keyence Demo:</p>
<p local-id=3D"3f50c1b6-9241-4393-a2a8-4aaf8cf6824f">Keyence LM-X system wa=
s demoed with sample parts and with a copper heatsink that we had printed. =
It is a metrology system similar to the IM, except it has a standard touch =
probe (the physical probes they buy from Renishaw) and confocal laser heigh=
t gage along with the optical system the IM has. It also has greater edge d=
istinguishing ability with its optical system because it uses higher magnif=
ication than the IM as well as an =E2=80=9Copen aperture=E2=80=9D (meaning =
large aperture) camera, yielding a smaller depth of field that enables any =
features other than the target feature to be out of focus. It can measure u=
p to 1000 parts at a time, can conduct 5000 measurements in one cycle, and =
has a total system cost of ~$94K. This includes a PC and a calibration targ=
et. It can generate a heat map from multiple discrete height measurements, =
and the probe enables it to measure vertical or angled surfaces. It is bill=
ed as being a system somewhere between a CMM and a pure optical system like=
 the IM. Apparently the IM has a =E2=80=9Clight probe,=E2=80=9D an illumina=
ted ball that is touched off of parts being measured. I have heard anecdota=
l evidence from coworkers that it is not accurate, but I have not verified =
that independently.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c090aa48-d710-431a-a427-c7374e59f8e3">PILOT: The StoneVac AM=
 vacuum cleaner from Vaniman showed up and I have begun assembling it in pr=
eparation for testing with powder. It is a simple design but it seems fairl=
y robust, and is surprisingly quiet in operation (other than its vibrating =
filter cleaner, which is quite loud). It came with an ESD-safe cylone separ=
ator that is screwed to the side of the vacuum unit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d822f6ce-93af-4e82-93ed-a9debe165eea">2025-07-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"b1799783-8953-488f-b866-3082f6062740">PILOT: Started work on=
