<p local-id=3D"fd034c7e-9335-4cd0-b6ac-8527871e2d36">HOPPER: Changed layer =
target temperature in an attempt to reduce layer time. Baseline temperature=
 is 24.5C pre-spread, 25.5 pre-cut. Changed to 27/28 for one test and 30/29=
 <img class=3D"emoticon emoticon-question" data-emoji-id=3D"atlassian-quest=
ion_mark" data-emoji-shortname=3D":question_mark:" data-emoji-fallback=3D":=
question_mark:" src=3D"15980ee8a50fc4b98598ecdf37537f3205171de7e65591152d4b=
c4e8fa954879" width=3D"16" height=3D"16" data-emoticon-name=3D"question" al=
t=3D"(question)"> on glass plate and 27/28 for two experiments on an alumin=
um base plate. Print time was &gt;12 hours for the baseline test, ~7.5 hrs =
for the glass plate, ~6.5 hrs for the aluminum base plate. Layer time was g=
enerally shorter with the aluminum build plate. Measured density of parts s=
eemed good, generally 57.5-58.5%. This improvement as compared with previou=
s results is likely due to the addition of vibration directly applied to th=
e powder in the hopper by a vibrated steel sheet. After the spread, there w=
as powder dropped on the build surface by the hopper going back over the bu=
ild surface. These had to be manually removed and resulted in defects in th=
e parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f9a0189e-3001-4c63-ae81-ee7ec46b3bbc">Vibrating plate added =
to zero-waste hopper. A screw came loose from one of the side mounts, causi=
ng the side mount to break.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5d0f4876-06f5-4697-a48d-cf9bf5d77a1c">NEXTGEN: Mason worked =
on the issue of wax buildup on the end mill. He tested various end mills an=
d cutting parameters (feed rates, vacuum timing) to machine slots in cast b=
locks of pure paraffin. Microcrystalline wax machined worse than pure paraf=
fin, leaving longer strings of wax on the end mill. The wax seems to stick =
to the end mill on pilot 1 when machining cuts filled with wax more than it=
 does on the open air gantry machining cast blocks of wax. It is possible t=
hat this is due to wax temperature when cutting; the wax on the pilot print=
er will be at a higher temperature during the print.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ddaac822-06db-445b-86e4-4874f7443fb3">Pourya ran more experi=
ments with filling cut channels with wax and machining the top. Order was p=
laced for MEST wax dispensing unit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a1c2aa2e-d4b0-4b0c-8dbc-32ad4814df85">Mahmood raised concern=
s about the CTE mismatch between metal powder and waxes; pure paraffin wax =
expands at a rate ~30X that of green parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"30ffd284-1b52-4b69-a214-526f36a9b7ae">2025-07-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"a631d860-f1e1-4759-837a-b1915030730e">PILOT: Working on CAD =
for electrical control cabinet for pilot printers to send to Epson. Meeting=
 with Manjun and Will to go over panels to have laser cut and bent sheet me=
tal parts for the new heater design. Marked up the latest electrical schema=
tic from the EE at Novo. I would like to start making the subpanels, but Ra=
jan wants to hold off on that until the CAD is 100% complete, and he doesn=
=E2=80=99t want that to be done until the schematic is 100% complete, which=
 is dependent on the Novo EE.</p>
<p local-id=3D"286952c7-ae2c-4eb0-8704-3d82d4184624">HOPPER: Printed two ne=
w Y gantry mounts to replace the ones that had been broken for the zero-was=
te hopper on Pilot 3. Showed Mason how to put together the zero-waste hoppe=
r with the new, sharper, 20=C2=B0 blade.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bcdea1cd-0ac5-4bd7-bf85-102063e36150">2025-07-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"ac218479-dea2-4653-888c-ef3bf080c398">HOPPER: Working on a r=
edesign of the zero-waste linear gated hopper. The spreader bar is changed =
to a larger diameter bar stock that has been machined down to be 10mm thick=
 between flats and has holes added for mounting to the hopper. This will le=
t us use a larger diameter spreader bar surface while also letting the gate=
 close as close to the spreading surface as possible. The concept of the ne=
w blade is to start with a thicker bar of material with a section of it mac=
hined down into a thin blade. This should both increase the strength of the=
 blade while decreasing the force required to close the blade. The difficul=
ty with the design, other than machining the part, is designing it such tha=
t it can close without powder building up in front of the thicker portion o=
