<p local-id=3D"966c674e-5fa3-475e-8631-9f2b5cd68721">PILOT: Finished the N1=
/N2/L1/L2/E labeling of the first Epson printer, then moved it to the R&amp=
;D lab. It was too large to fit through the standard door, so we had to rol=
l it through the double doors to the R&amp;D printing pod. The vacuum clean=
er support plate was grinding on the drawer slides that it is riding on, pa=
rtly because the plate was flexing from the force of the clamping straps an=
d partly because the vacuums were shifted too far back and were causing the=
 plate to tilt backwards. The solution is to design a new sheet metal plate=
 with folded sides for increased strength along with mounting holes for the=
 center lifting eyes, cutouts for the strap carabiners to grab so lifting e=
yes will only be necessary in the center, and holes premade for bolting on =
the slides. Started printing the left side of the powder tank catch box.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"d8fe2c0a-5747-40d6-8927-ea84fc6930a7">2025-09-25</p>
<p local-id=3D"e2773b9b-3ee9-4100-9292-15d4cb4e143b">PILOT: The spindle vac=
uum on Epson 1 is not turning on when the relay is energized, so I need to =
figure that out. Apparently it is an issue with the Festool vacuum; when th=
e Bosch vacuum is plugged into the spindle vacuum port it works fine, and w=
hen the Festool is plugged into the mist vacuum port it still does not work=
. There has been flex noted in the X endstop mount, which really needs to b=
e machined from aluminum so I am planning on redesigning that for machinabi=
lity today. I also need to update the model of the control cabinet with cha=
nges that need to be made based on lessons learned from Epson 1 and 2, incl=
uding the safety reset button cutout and new cable pass-through connections=
. The Roxtec block used currently does not have enough openings for all the=
 cables used, so it needs to be redesigned. An issue was encountered with t=
he Festool vacuums; they are a newer version than the ones used on Pilots 1=
-3 that does not turn on automatically when power is connected. The short t=
erm workaround was to connect a dummy load to the power tool power outlet o=
n the vacuum (in this case we used a solid-state Sprite refrigerator). The =
vacuum is designed so that it can only be switched on by either pressing th=
e =E2=80=9CMAN=E2=80=9D membrane button on the front or when it detects cur=
rent draw through a 120 VAC outlet on the vacuum. I am working on installin=
g a cooling fan in the vacuum cleaner chamber that will be plugged in to th=
e Festool; this will both act as a load that triggers the vacuum and will r=
ecirculate air in the vacuum cleaner chamber while the Festool vacuum is ru=
nning to prevent heat buildup. It was noted in testing Epson 1 that the hea=
ter exceeded 100C when it was run without the cooling fans, which caused th=
e safety system to trip and shut down the Duet. Talking with ZZ the setpoin=
t is currently set at 100C. Made new laser engraved plaques for EPSON 1 and=
 EPSON 2 that will need to be covered with protective film until the printe=
rs have been delivered.</p>
<p local-id=3D"72c52bda-0d85-4648-9dde-ed3ac8ac7a4b">2025-09-26</p>
<p local-id=3D"b1ea0a2e-21dd-43cd-a7ef-081514e57bea">PILOT: Printing more V=
ention zip tie mounts. Redesigned and printing new overflow chute parts; I =
modified the design of the chute and blanking plates to fit the machine mai=
n plate more tightly. I eliminated the mounting area for the brush. I also =
matched the bolted thickness of the overflow chute to the blanking plate so=
 they will use the same screws. I updated the schematic with new lengths of=
 cables and corrected the board side connector callouts. I designed and gen=
erated a drawing for a new machined X axis endstop mount. I sent the part n=
umber for M3X10 screws to Manju to order.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6e619e04-ce6b-4e20-a3d3-0c1c65cba4d1">NEXTGEN: Weekly R&amp;=
D meeting discussing the wax dispensing no-depowdering project. I feel like=
 the project has not really advanced in the last few months, and I am unsur=
e that the approach being taken is correct. Dispensing wax using the variou=
s wax dispensing heads does not seem to be controllable enough for our purp=
oses and there are many unknowns around how dewaxing and sintering the dewa=
xed part will result, and how the internal surface finish will be in fully =
enclosed parts. If the goal is to make it easier to depowder internal chann=
els that have a connection to the outside of the part, I would focus on inc=
reasing green part strength and transitioning to using a binder jetting hea=
d to enable the use of stronger binders and more selective binder applicati=
on. A layer-by-layer hybrid LPBF/machining process also would make internal=
 features easier to depowder while maintaining the surface finish advantage=
s of 3DEO=E2=80=99s printing process, although this carries with it increas=
ed risk. If the goal is to make fully enclosed features, I would first ques=
tion why that is necessary. Filling channels with wax (or preferably a wate=
r-soluble polymer such as PVP-VA) is not necessarily a bad approach, but it=
 is clearly more difficult to implement in a production process than we ant=
icipated. Either the timeline to production implementation should be extend=
ed, or resources should be added to the project (this would necessitate hir=
ing more engineers, I think). A hybrid DED process might make fully enclose=
d features possible, as would a paste-based or FDM-based sinter-based syste=
m, such as Mantle=E2=80=99s process or running metal-powder-bearing filamen=
t in an FDM printer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2fb92639-29cf-424f-93dc-b7e8e680ebe6">2025-09-29</p>
<p local-id=3D"a79cc474-a044-449f-801b-3edf36f2601e">PILOT: Installed solen=
oid valve on Epson 2 and connected it to the air line that runs to the muff=
ler in the printing chamber. Working on a mount for the vacuum chamber air =
recirculation fan. Printing another handle for the vacuum plate. Filled the=
 =E2=80=9CPULL=E2=80=9D letter debossing with gray SLA resin and currently =
curing it in the Formlabs curing chamber with the same settings as ESD resi=
n. An increased layer time has been noted on Epson 1; this may be due to a =
lower cooling rate because the layer cooling fan is mounted higher on Epson=
 1 and 2 than it is on PILOT 1-3. The easiest approach to try improving thi=
s is to print a duct to make the fan flow more laminar and provide more tar=
geted flow at the build plate, so I have designed such a duct and it is pri=
nting now on the X1E. Printed the duct and gave it to Ben to install on Eps=
on 1 once it is not busy with printing. I have also designed a mounting sch=
eme for the vacuum cleaner chamber air recirculation fan. It will be bolted=
 directly to the left rear louver (on the side of the Festool dust collecto=
r) on the inside of the vacuum cleaner chamber, blowing air outwards. Holes=
 will need to be drilled through the louver and the sheet metal panel to mo=
unt the fan. In the future, the sheet metal will have its holes laser cut (=
I have already modified the sheet metal design accordingly) but the louvers=
 will likely need to be modified, unless we are able to form the louvers in=
to the side sheet metal panels in the future. Drilled holes through 2X louv=
ers matching the mounting holes of the fans being used, then riveted them t=
o the sheet metal panels, then used the drilled louver holes to drill holes=
 through the sheet metal panels. Received the sheet metal panels and earthq=
uake mounting brackets.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c118ea0c-9427-4281-874a-3cf63ad0cf53">2025-09-30</p>
<p local-id=3D"b0a77fe5-efe9-4ad2-aeda-6d6aedb3a02a">PILOT: TS3. Need to de=
sign a printed fan guard for the vacuum cleaner chamber cooling fan. The li=
ght mounting clips are printed but not installed. The left side panel with =
louvers riveted and fan installed has been mounted to Epson 1. The cooling =
fan for Epson 2 needs to be wired and installed. Selecting new terminal blo=
cks for the next machines that are screwless (spring loaded), have the wire=
s coming out the top so it is easier to install/remove wires out of order, =
and hopefully have labels that clip in better than the current screw-based =
terminal blocks. Designed and printed the fan guards and installed them on =
Epson 2 along with the fan.</p>
