<p local-id=3D"f10a5176-2e53-40a7-b716-209b7b131a0a">2025-10-01</p>
<p local-id=3D"c6447884-4fe8-4b63-b0c7-1c4657829bb9">PILOT: Installed vacuu=
m chamber fan guard on Epson 1. Installed spiral wrap on some of the hoses =
and wires on Epson 1 to show others the general approach. The Epson enginee=
rs signed off on the use of spiral wrap instead of teflon sheets. Installed=
 light mounts on Epson 1 and switched to a different type of barrel electri=
cal splitter instead of having the lights wired in series; since the maximu=
m run of the lights we=E2=80=99re using is 2, having 3 in series either cau=
ses them to be dimmer or causes a risk of fire. With me and Manju present, =
the Epson engineers approved putting the doors on the front of the machine =
instead of the back and were informed by Manju that the rear panel would ne=
ed to be removed to access the vacuum hoses, the control panels of the vacu=
ums, and the vacuum chamber exhaust fan. They also confirmed that we will b=
e adding the same door latches to the lower doors as are present on the upp=
er doors. I am currently printing the door latch mounts on the Bambu X1E. I=
 am currently printing a layer fan duct and layer fan guard on the X1C. The=
 layer fan duct and guard are printed and ready for installation. I need to=
 print another fan guard for Epson 2. Received a batch of 5000K LED light b=
ars (the ones I have been using are 4000K, because the 5000K were out of st=
ock at some point). I tried wiring up 2X 5000K and 2X 4000K light bars on E=
pson 2, because I think the combination of lights with two color temperatur=
es is cool and because it lets us use up the 4000K light bars. Decision was=
 made to standardize to 5000K LED lights across the machines.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ba950e5c-5974-4bf9-a782-b42fa1030871">2025-10-02</p>
<p local-id=3D"098861c8-8414-4376-9b0f-c2f7c0545aba">PILOT: Went over wirin=
g in Epson 2 with Cisco. Some of the wires are connected to points that do =
not match the schematic, the circuit breaker for the 48V TEC power supply w=
as unconnected, and the safety system wiring is undocumented because it is =
wiring done by ZZ, but electrically the system is functional. Combined the =
two overflow chute components that are currently super glued together into =
one component, and modified the dimensions so that it will provide a better=
 powder seal to prevent the powder from leaking through the plate. Currentl=
y printing one on the X1E. Since the Festool on Epson 1 is still loud even =
after reducing the flow, I am working on adding an intake filter to further=
 muffle the sound.</p>
<p local-id=3D"45934bee-44c0-4007-a503-4d22b61bc75b">2025-10-03</p>
<p local-id=3D"eb95fadc-de33-4b91-986d-c167bca38934">PILOT: Attached 3X Cau=
tion Hot labels to the heater on Epson 1. I ran the heater until the heatsi=
nk temperature hit 75C, and the labels seemed to still be adhering, but it =
will definitely take more testing to confirm that it is a reliable solution=
. I was informed by Marcus at the end of the day yesterday that we have hir=
ed a new EE, and he has set up a meeting with him, me, Pourya, and ZZ next =
week to transfer knowledge of the pilot printers so that he can design the =
electrical systems for the next generation pilot printers, which makes me v=
ery sad because I had hoped to be working on that at some point. It is also=
 interesting to note that the whole interview process was conducted entirel=
y by senior management, with no involvement of any engineers or people who =
would be working with this new guy. I am having difficulty spinning that in=
 a positive way; the best I have come up with so far is that maybe they wer=
e trying to minimize our extra workload because we are busy with the printe=
rs to be sent to Epson, but there are many possible negative interpretation=
s of it. I entered the situation into Claude to get its analysis, and it wa=
s uncharacteristically negative about the situation. Usually it puts a posi=
tive spin on almost everything it says, but in this case it found the event=
s worrisome. I learned at 2:00 that they want the lower doors installed on =
the printers today so that they can show them to some higher-up at Epson on=
 Monday, so I cut 8X 720mm Vention extrusion pieces so that Cisco can get t=
he doors put together and installed today. Since the X axis cable carrier t=
ower hose fitting on Epson 1 broke during printing, I am doing a complete r=
edesign to make it more robust. The general design concept is to use a bras=
s barbed elbow fitting that is clamped between two 3D printed blocks to hol=
d it in the right orientation and hold the hose onto the barbed fitting.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"ff3c9462-d31c-4c1c-ab63-025c9da889c6">2025-10-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"d4310d9c-d5ff-40d5-81ef-45b28acfeb77">PILOT: I confirmed wit=
h Manju twice and Marcus once that the plan is to not test Epson 1 printing=
 any further, and move it back to the assembly area for wiring of the light=
 stack and cleaning in preparation for shipping. I have concerns about this=
; I would rather benchmark it as much as possible and run it to see if any =
other issues show up, such as the X axis cable carrier tower hose fitting b=
reaking. I am working on a comparable part made from all metal pipe and hos=
e fittings.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1031de66-d164-4910-a819-db7cc42165fb">2025-10-07</p>
<p local-id=3D"ff1f3067-5b0c-4c8f-a9c8-70c1670832d5">PILOT: The printed box=
 parts don=E2=80=99t really make sense; the crenellated should be on the re=
ar door instead of the body so I will redesign it today and reprint it toni=
ght. I am also working on updating the electrical schematic with the update=
s given to me by ZZ; this will be done by the end of the day so that Cisco =
and/or Ning can double check the panels to see if the actual wiring matches=
 the schematic. The sheet metal vacuum plate has still not been ordered, de=
spite Manju saying in this morning=E2=80=99s meeting that Philip had confir=
med that it had been ordered last Friday. After Jasmine got authorization f=
rom Marcus and completed the PO, I entered the link to place the order in t=
he Jira page yesterday. I have completed the latest revision of the electri=
cal schematic and sent it out to the team. I need to update the design of t=
he X axis cable carrier tower hose fitting.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4ffb3fc0-2c5b-4512-b322-03790b004687">2025-10-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"5d44193b-9dfc-43db-96e2-2673e7500dc1">PILOT: Spoke with Marc=
us about the plan for the next two Pilot printers. It sounds like we=E2=80=
=99re putting them on the back burner for now because we don=E2=80=99t have=
 any technicians to dedicate to assembling them. Presumably Cisco will retu=
rn to mainly working on inconel development once the two Epson printers hav=
e shipped, and the implication is that I will return to R&amp;D efforts. Me=
eting about spare parts to send to Epson; I need to print a set of all the =
3D printed parts that are used one printer, as a well as a tool for measuri=
ng the static pressure of the Festool vacuum applied at the tip of the spin=
dle mount.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6b44a46d-3276-406a-bcd1-1abfff47d9f4">2025-10-09</p>
<p local-id=3D"14c2af4d-0584-404d-a09e-dab84f4b3f9f">PILOT: Rajan spoke to =
Ning yesterday and the plan as it is right now is to move forward with buil=
ding the next two Pilot printers and have Ning make the next two electrical=
 subpanels matched to the updated Epson schematic design. I will be moved b=
ack to R&amp;D process development (no depowdering, hopper). This makes me =
very sad for several reasons: first, I had hoped to create a new electrical=
 panel design that had some significant improvements from the current one, =
including cleaning up the location of circuit breakers and power supplies, =
reducing the number of power supplies, switching to spring-loaded terminal =
blocks, cleaning up the arduino to sensor wiring, etc. I also was not invol=
ved in the hiring process, and the work I was doing that I had really enjoy=
ed (particularly learning KiCAD) will be taken over by someone who I was no=
t involved in selecting. I also was interested in learning about SolidWorks=
 electrical, which now will no longer be necessary. I was also not consulte=
d about what projects I would like to work on; I would prefer to focus on m=
anufacturing of the current PILOT design and development of the next indust=
rialized version. This lack of transparency and collaboration in company di=
rection and roles is frustrating. I also have concerns about the viability =
and necessity of the no-depowdering, laser, and hopper processes. I would b=
e more interested in developing a machine that uses the traditional 3DEO ha=
rdware stack with tighter environmental and motion controls as well as in-p=
rinter measurement to achieve tighter process controls, as well as developi=
ng more controlled processes around printing itself, such as pre-processing=
 the powder and recycling overflow powder to reduce waste in a way that is =
more easily industrialized than a hopper design. Pilot documentation meetin=
g. Designed and printed new angled part for holding the all-metal X axis ca=
ble carrier tower hose adapter. Working on post-processing the first batch =
of resin 3DP parts and preparing the second batch for printing; it seems th=
at I can print all the parts required for one printer in two Form 3 prints.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"4cdbb00a-f257-4345-86df-d221c4d1b146">2025-10-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"312f2ec3-5ad8-405f-9b4e-40572bf207cd">Went over the schemati=
