</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"82b4637a-527f-4ccb-97d5-e425a8cdd2f6">
<p local-id=3D"b0259734-891d-4882-b840-ba5dc1d079f4">IN parallel to all thi=
s, Ning owns building the electrical cabinets, with Abe's help as needed. (=
we also potentially can bring in an intern Philipp knows if that would be h=
elpful).</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"5a41b262-97b4-44ed-801b-dc5f90e09f02">
<p local-id=3D"dd9e72f8-bf6a-4f8f-a000-ee0c91d24be3">Any other resources (S=
ean, Mason, Senai etc) should only be tapped through me, with my explicit a=
pproval.&nbsp;</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"4e3dfc1b-9f16-4ea1-aa1b-811d8d9a0d76">This is intentionally =
not the fastest possible way of getting these units built, but should be re=
asonable. We're going for minimally intrusive, so we can get back to some t=
hings that got back-burnered during the Epson sprint.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bd52b83d-8879-4445-a096-c77d08c9d118">Questions, comments, c=
oncerns? Let me know! Otherwise, we'll officially kick this off next week w=
hen Cisco's help is no longer needed on the Epson units.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dd883c3d-25b5-42f4-9a3f-bc55c053fc18">Thanks,</p>
<p local-id=3D"cd839689-c7d2-40e0-9de4-3758f72b4d08">Marcus</p>
<p local-id=3D"10ddbb9a-4a6e-4550-936f-53cc04da2442">Sounds like I=E2=80=99=
m off the pilots for the foreseeable future. There are also no immediate pl=
ans to begin work on the industrialized version of the pilot printers. I as=
ked Marcus about it, and he said that the plans are contingent on decisions=
 by the board and decisions driven by future funding received (or not).</p>
<p local-id=3D"07942bfa-e339-441f-8cb4-2f9c96dbc4a5">2025-10-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"9da5031a-c838-49e6-bd7e-03bf4ffceae8">PILOT: The latest desi=
gn of the powder catch was printed over the weekend and fits well; now I ne=
ed to print 3 more. The powder tank module box parts were also printed, so =
I joined those together with VHB tape in preparation for installing them in=
to Epson 2. The design of the custom electrical control cabinet from NEMA E=
nclosures Mfg. is complete, and I signed off on the design for ordering. Ma=
nju talked about the need to customize the COTS electrical cabinets we have=
 in stock to match the custom design we ordered. I also pointed out that we=
 will need a custom subpanel designed and fabricated, which will require Ni=
ng to complete the schematic, create the SolidWorks assembly of the full el=
ectrical panel, design the sheet metal part, and have it fabricated. I woul=
d prefer to create an improved schematic with various upgrades (fewer power=
 supplies, spring loaded terminal blocks instead of screw terminal, better =
organized circuit breakers, terminal blocks, and power supplies, more organ=
ized wiring (possibly using SolidWorks Electrical), etc., but that is out o=
f my hands now). Sometimes you can=E2=80=99t do what you want to do, or eve=
n what you=E2=80=99re best at. Sometimes you have to do what only you can d=
o. This is infuriating, obviously, but I understand the motivation. It is i=
mportant to have redundancy in employee skills, and it would be unwise for =
the company to only have me who can do electrical design and mechanical des=
ign for the PILOT printers as well as certain R&amp;D activities. On the ot=
her hand, what I would prefer in this situation is to be heavily involved i=
n the selection of new employees to develop new electrical and mechanical s=
ystems, as well as have a say in how my time will be split between developi=
ng next-generation printers vs. experimental subsystems. Also, I would like=
 clarity in whether we will even be developing new printers or just duplica=
ting the current PILOT printers. I feel like the party line with the PILOT =
printers (for years) has been that we need to crank out something quickly t=
o show board members, get funding, and run some experiments, then for the n=
ext generation we will take our time to design something right. That wasn=
=E2=80=99t true when going from PILOT 1 to 2 and 3, it wasn=E2=80=99t true =
when going from PILOTs 1-3 to Epson 1 and 2, and it wasn=E2=80=99t true whe=
n going from Epson 1 and 2 to PILOTs 6 and 7. For some reason we were alway=
s rushed when moving from generation to generation.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5451b9d2-43e5-4c6b-90b5-ae7194d8d8c5">R&amp;D To Do This Wee=
k:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"a1ecde03-0c03-4bdb-bc6e-8878c37663c5">
<li local-id=3D"bae38b4e-a825-410b-8954-df0acf98940b">
<p local-id=3D"f8775319-2020-462d-83d3-86ca65b214c7">Spindle bit cleaning</=
p></li>
<li local-id=3D"07d3114e-ec4f-400b-b91e-e724defc3589">
<p local-id=3D"62d5ac67-fd82-40c9-acd6-1f806abba714">New wax recipe</p></li=
>
<li local-id=3D"472c7425-15c9-4976-9be7-58d6442592ac">
<p local-id=3D"67a0ef6d-78d8-4b8d-8fa1-799fa2e4d6b7">Burnout comparison bet=
ween paraffin and microcrystalline</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"1c4460ca-d502-4f14-996d-3fcde89f3366">Learning how to set up=
 an ESP32-based touchscreen to apply to an upgraded version of the THC. Wor=
king on developing a cleaner version of the electrical system for the PILOT=
 printers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"af2128af-2f93-4846-a518-b363f28103eb">2025-10-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"386f3d21-a6d2-4f78-9f94-009811890b0d">PILOT: Preparing print=
ers for shipping. Designing and printing a plate to replace the Roxtec bloc=
k on the bottom of the control cabinet with three bulkhead electrical pass-=
throughs (2X USB, 1X HDMI).</p>
<p local-id=3D"ca906165-14a9-48b4-a5c4-5928b10f45d2">NEXTGEN: Ordered some =
polyurethane elastomer material from McMaster in three different hardnesses=
. The goal is to clean wax residue off of the spindle bit by inserting the =
spindle bit into the rubber, either with the the spindle motor on or off, d=
epending on what seems to work better. I will design and print an insert fo=
r the non-overflow chute side slot with three rectangles of rubber inserted=
 (40A, 60A, and 80A durometer). I did a crude benchtop test where I dipped =
a spindle bit into some pure paraffin and jabbed it into the different hard=
nesses of rubber, and it wiped off most of the wax, although the bit had to=
 be rotated to clear out the flutes completely. I am unsure if it will be a=
dequate to clean off the outside of the end mill cutting length without cle=
aring out the flutes entirely.</p>
<p local-id=3D"38aecba9-c97c-4800-a727-19a59a58024f">2025-10-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"b16c6645-8895-4f5b-a2ed-076bd0f70dbd">PILOT: Printed the fir=
st Roxtec block blanking plate with bulkhead electrical pass-throughs and i=
nstalled the pass-throughs. There were connectivity issues with the cable g=
oing through the HDMI bulkhead connection point.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d4056395-7483-4000-a445-34a355dee1b1">NEXTGEN: I have been s=
teadily ramping up the temperature of a paraffin/EVA (ethylene vinyl acetat=
e copolymer) mix on the hot plate in the R&amp;D fume hood. The EVA seems v=
ery difficult to dissolve; I am up to 180C now and the EVA has softened int=
o a floating mass but it is not completely dissolved yet. I am continuing t=
o work on the design of a rubber block holder for cleaning end mills on one=
 of the Pilots. Finally completed the design and it is printing right now.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"72797d10-2e12-4e64-9552-421a4106e183">2025-10-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"5198377a-8bb4-469c-a043-13d327b6fed7">PILOT: Completed the s=
econd Roxblock blanking plate. There were some more connectivity issues tha=
t seemed to be alleviated by replacing the micro HDMI to HDMI cable with a =
higher quality one, then when Epson 2 was moved from the R&amp;D lab to the=
 warehouse the RPi power supply breaker was tripped for some reason. I swit=
ched it back on and the system seems OK so far, but that is a worrisome fai=
lure mode because I am not sure why the Pi breaker might trip, unless there=
 was a short in the system or the Pi was overloading its power supply for s=
ome reason (it is a 4A power supply so it should be good, but it is still a=
 possibility). Printed TEC terminal covers on the Bambu using Senai=E2=80=
=99s design. Tapped holes for, and installed, the powder and build tank mod=
ule lower cover, and slid the two powder catches into place. Completed the =
design of the custom electrical subpanel and made the order request in Jira=
. I designed the plate out of 1/8=E2=80=9D thick aluminum instead of the or=
iginal .09375=E2=80=9D thick mild steel to reduce cost and eliminate the ne=
ed for powder coating.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f67baf0e-5c32-4e9f-951b-069748c00daf">NEXTGEN: I cut out blo=
cks of rubber of the three hardnesses being tested and glued them into the =
appropriate slots, then gave the assembly to Pourya to test whenever he has=
 a chance.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5ba6b851-a3a7-44eb-a816-7115e7229742">2025-10-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"c6a7356a-9300-48e2-ae93-7ee92698483d">NEXTGEN: I ran across =
a very interesting solvent that <a href=3D"http://machinablewax.com" class=
=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">machinablewax.com</a> sells for dewaxin=
g:</p>
<p local-id=3D"dbba32ca-a19d-4d0c-b7df-3458482c40ed"><a href=3D"https://mac=
hinablewax.com/sp-28-wax-remover/?searchid=3D7965&amp;search_query=3DF-99+%=
28Arctic%29+Machinable+Wax%3A" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">htt=
ps://machinablewax.com/sp-28-wax-remover/?searchid=3D7965&amp;search_query=
=3DF-99+%28Arctic%29+Machinable+Wax%3A</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"055f9621-7390-461c-b3ce-9c2ce0ae82d5">It is reputed to be ab=
le to dissolve both paraffinic and microcrystalline waxes, and is also alle=
gedly non-hazardous.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cb4c5607-c08d-44d5-987f-927fed6a90bb">Ordered a couple of ne=
w spindle motors that use 1/8=E2=80=9D collets, so they should be compatibl=
e with a wider variety of end mills:</p>
<p local-id=3D"776c424c-51d2-4aaf-8818-eec718f80694"><a href=3D"https://www=
.ramprodinc.com/45000-rpm-handpiece-with-1-8-collet/" class=3D"external-lin=
k" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.ramprodinc.com/45000-rpm-handpiece-with-1-8=
-collet/</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"34ef6004-2248-44ee-8da6-e7283abe6ae7">PILOT: Still preparing=
 Epson printers for final inspection and crating on Monday. Some of the ESD=
 safe resin prints recently have been failing on the Formlab, so I greased =
the leadscrews and linear guides, wiped off the bars on the optical head, a=
nd replaced the tank. I then restarted the spindle holder print that I had =
tried to run last night. I wired up 4X Kamoer water pumps with the necessar=
y connector and the red and white wires connected. One of the vacuum suppor=
t plates was warped from brazing on the lifting eyes, so I laid it down fla=
t on the welding table and welded the rear corners where the sheet metal is=
 bent to meet. Hopefully this will hold it flat for installation. The warpa=
ge meant that it would flex between two stable shapes depending on forces a=
pplied, so hopefully the welds will hold it in the more flat condition.</p>
<p local-id=3D"039b2eb0-49cb-49c9-9efe-b5d9a1aecc5d">2025-10-20</p>
<p local-id=3D"43c16ff7-e152-4658-b711-5dd9b026a480">PILOT: Final inspectio=
n and preparation of Epson 1 and 2 for shipping. The new reinforced vacuum =
plate handles have been printed and were given to Cisco to install.</p>
<p local-id=3D"eb2d9631-6a41-42cd-8307-10aa618ccc1f">NEXTGEN: Experimenting=
 with making a recipe of machinable wax that is 15% PE-CVA (12% VA by weigh=
t), 1% Stearic Acid, and 84% paraplast paraffin.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a57b7562-f5d5-4ed4-8e59-225a3497e0a5">2025-10-21</p>
<p local-id=3D"9b17a710-fcfb-4b97-8a71-1cac94a28265">NEXTGEN: Making a sili=
cone mold for casting blanks with machinable wax; the silicone I poured yes=
terday did not harden at all (it was several years old so that is not too s=
urprising) so I am making a new one with the Mold Star 30 I got for making =
the Scientific Drilling resin test part casting molds.</p>
<p local-id=3D"934b3bf5-f6fa-4912-93e8-3c7ae73e5faa">PILOT: Epson printers =
are being crated up in preparation for shipping. Printing 4X more small bot=
tle caps labeled with =E2=80=9CFLUSH=E2=80=9D because initially all the cap=
s were labeled with =E2=80=9CBINDER.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p local-id=3D"5d9dabdf-0d86-4a8e-9455-516fdcbc9ace">PRODUCTION: Made silic=
one molds for casting duplicates of the Scientific Drilling parts. The mold=
s should be ready to be removed and used for casting tomorrow.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2ca089c-36dc-4b25-a20e-cbfc32895c67">2025-10-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"46b5993c-ad50-4bae-8d5a-4ab82bea9c85">NEXTGEN: Poured 9 bloc=
ks of filtered machinable wax using the recipe above. The filtered blocks s=
eemed to shrink significantly after pouring, but when I later poured an unf=
iltered block it did not seem to shrink the same amount. This could be due =
to any number of different factors, but the filter I am using is one that i=
s designed to be used with SLA resin at room temperature, so it is possible=
 that the high temperature liquid wax I am pouring into it is damaging it, =
or the paraffin itself may be chemically attacking it and contaminating the=
 batch. On the other hand, I had poured most of the wax through a filter in=
to the silicone block casting mold yesterday then remelted it, so if it was=
 going to be contaminated by the filter it should have been contaminated th=
en as well. I am working on a new formulation of machinable wax that has:</=
p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper conf=
luence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-=
center" alt=3D"image-20251022-215036.png" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" sr=
