<p local-id=3D"61789ca9-de37-43ee-a44b-5b626e18f34b"></p>
<p local-id=3D"36102edc-6332-46f7-aed1-aa500612c6da">PRODUCTION: Removed th=
e silicone molds from the Scientific Drilling parts and mixed up and poured=
 the mineral-filled casting resin into the larger threaded mold and tried t=
o pour it into the smaller mold with the curved features, but the resin had=
 begun to harden at that point and did not fill the mold well, so I will ha=
ve to mix up a new batch at some point. The resin is mixed with a 1:5 ratio=
, so I initially mixed 50g:250g. I think the volume is correct to fill both=
 molds (although barely) but the pot life seems considerably shorter than t=
he specified 10 minutes, so it needs to be poured quickly.</p>
<p local-id=3D"867074f3-c5a3-4db7-8f94-49fd92839f15">PILOT: Scheduling meet=
ing about PILOTS 6 and 7. Manju=E2=80=99s estimate for timeline to make the=
 two machines is 1 month, which sounds preposterously overoptimistic to me.=
 Epsons 1 and 2 took about 2.5 months calendar time, with me, Cisco, Fernan=
do, Mohsen Mason, Sean, albeit with different percentages of time breakdown=
. Marcus rejected my order request for the custom electrical subpanels; sin=
ce the goal of these next two PILOT printers (as I am defining it) is to ha=
ndcraft them the same way the last two were handcrafted, laboriously and im=
precisely but quickly and cheaply, it does make sense to make the subpanels=
 the same way they were made last time. Ning said that he was going to star=
t work on them today, although I am unsure if that includes the mechanical =
fabrication of the subpanel as well as wiring. Finished design of the new I=
R camera enclosure that allows stacking of the terminator board on top of t=
he IR camera. I tried soldering the terminator board to the IR camera using=
 header pins, but I realized that they use different pinouts for some reaso=
n, so I need to desolder them. It should still be safe to stack the boards =
on top of each other, they will just need to be connected using QWIIC cable=
s instead of the header pins.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"a74e2857-c64e-47d9-a6c6-90e4af55d124">
<li local-id=3D"61fe3ff4-5db7-4e52-99e8-83cd26ce8e8b">
<p local-id=3D"c5ab9eb2-2646-4045-a8e8-37165fed6ff0">Assemble powder and bu=
ild tank modules before 10/30</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"b0b5169e-9515-4f11-ba21-40c2b92bbf7a">2025-10-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"ae4e86f2-f33c-4ab3-8905-4b042de8e925">PILOT: Discussed the d=
esign of the subpanel with Ning. He wants to match the cutout on the subpan=
el to the cutout size of the cooling fans on the control cabinet. This woul=
d increase the height of the cutout by .2=E2=80=9D but decrease the width b=
y 1.1=E2=80=9D. I couldn=E2=80=99t get a clear answer for why he wants to m=
ake the change, and I explained that it doesn=E2=80=99t affect the work Al =
is going to do on it, but I=E2=80=99m happy to hand off control of the desi=
gn to Ning if he prefers to standardize the cutout size.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cc1f6426-e5f1-4044-8e34-a675fabdc7f3">PRODUCTION: The resin =
part I cast for duplicating the machined bronze component seems OK, althoug=
h it has some bubbles that I believe were cause by pouring too quickly. The=
 mold to duplicate the stainless steel part doesn=E2=80=99t replicate the c=
enter opening that is necessary to fully insert the bronze part, so I am go=
ing to 3D print a part to act as a mold that slides over the current mold s=
o I can pour some silicone that will attach to the current mold to create t=
he necessary opening and make the mold deeper so that when I pour resin int=
o it the resin will fill the internal features better than it is right now.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"b56d4c17-5106-418d-b63a-0c8edab9f110">NEXTGEN: I am having d=
ifficulty getting the PE-CVA to fully dissolve, even with the hot plate set=
 to 190C. I believe this is because of heat lost from the upper portions of=
 the wax in the beaker, so I have poured out about half of the wax into the=
 silicone mold for making wax blanks. Hopefully this will enable the PE-CVA=
 to fully dissolve, at which point I can pour that fully mixed wax through =
a filter into the silicone mold, then remelt the wax blanks in the beaker. =
I tried manually carving the blocks with a Leatherman Arc Magnacut blade, a=
nd the feel was noticeably different. The V1 wax (84% paraffin, 15% PE-CVA,=
 1% SA) was smooth to cut and made long shavings, while the V2 wax (50% par=
affin, 25% hard microcrystalline wax, 20% PE-CVA, 5% SA) was more brittle a=
nd jumpy to cut. Doing some hand milling with one of our .020=E2=80=9D diam=
eter 4-flute end mills, it seemed that there was more of a tendency of the =
V1 wax to gum up the end mill than the V2 wax, but both of these tests are =
subjective . I also have yet to compare these two wax versions against pure=
 paraffin or pure microcrystalline wax.</p>
<p local-id=3D"107b90de-e519-4e7b-9f57-058297f77889">2025-10-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"97d1da5b-59c8-4c16-aa8d-bfca878f73a1">PILOT: Setting up a pr=
int for all the FDM parts necessary for one powder and build tank module. W=
orking on assembly drawings for the powder and build tank module to make it=
 easier for someone else to assemble it. Installed locating pins in 5X fixe=
d build plates and catches in 2X removable build plates (there seem to be a=
 total of 3 removable build plates in stock, so more will need to be made f=
or these two printers. Assembled and aligned two fixed base plate assemblie=
s. I had to shim the back plate to get it properly aligned; I was measuring=
 off of the machined surface where the linear rail will mount. The other si=
de, where the aluminum had been ground but not machined here, was aligned w=
ith the plate properly. That suggests there was some error in machining the=
 slot for the linear rail, so it was not properly aligned with the other si=
de of the part, but the surface that contacts the build plate was machined =
properly relative to the ground side. I did not shim the assemblies for the=
 Epson printers, but that could be because I may have been measuring agains=
t the ground face that was on the back side of the part relative to the mac=
hined groove for the linear rail.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a8ba90d8-eb1d-484f-acd6-5f28625a1910">2025-10-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e4ddaab-282c-43ca-8935-7f4dd30ea42c">PILOT: There is a new =
BOM Manju made for PILOTs 6 and 7, despite the design intent being for thes=
e to be identical to the ones sent to Epson. Additionally, the Epson printe=
r BOM is incomplete (missing some parts, some parts added that are not nece=
ssary). Some of the components being installed in the electrical control ca=
binet for 6 and 7 are not the same as the ones used in the Epsons; the term=
inal blocks are a mix of spring-loaded ones that I had ordered to test and =
spring-loaded ones that Ning specified that have 6 contact points but as-de=
livered have 3 separate pass through circuits, and will therefore need to h=
ave extra components added to connect the 3 circuits. Ning is also working =
on making new nebulizer cables so we don=E2=80=99t need to buy them from Tr=
ak; the Trak cable cost is $120 and the cable material cost is $20, plus ha=
lf an hour of Ning=E2=80=99s time, which costs the company ~$50 and delays =
other work he could be doing. Ning also thinks that the point of failure of=
 the cables is the solder joint, when my understanding historically from di=
scussing it with Chin is that it was the cables failing, but I may be mista=
ken about that. Working on a list of tools and supplies that will need to b=
e brought to the Epson facility for the Japan visit. Assisting Abe and Cisc=
o in installing the main plate on Pilot 6. I showed them how to install the=
 keensert inserts and also made a judgement call that only some of the inse=
rts be installed because there are not enough in stock to fully populate th=
e main plate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a13b1176-6ae1-4d8f-8874-19d8150f94bc">GENERAL R&amp;D: Marcu=
s talked to Devin about the part casting project I have been spending a lit=
tle time on and said that any production requests should go through him. Ma=
rcus said that Payman is particularly sensitive to my time being drawn away=
. This strongly suggests that they do not believe that I can manage my time=
 myself. It also raises the question of whether or not I have been delayed =
on any tasks to which I have committed; to the best of my knowledge everyth=
ing I have been tasked with has been completed on schedule, but if this wer=
e the case then it would not make sense to doubt my time management capabil=
ities. Assisted Sean with an experiment for a non-AEM customer who is inter=
ested in copper heat exchangers. He wanted to try epoxying some leftover PX=
L nickel-plated heat exchangers into a new stainless steel base. The base i=
s machined from 304 stainless steel that was sandblasted several days befor=
e epoxying. The parts were acetone cleaned then placed in non-airtight pack=
aging over the weekend. I applied epoxy along the step on the stainless ste=
el base, and on one of the parts I applied a bead perpendicular to an imagi=
nary line drawn between the two pressure test ports and on the other I forg=
ot to apply a bead. We only glued two heat exchangers down so that if there=
 are leaks or mechanical failures we can try altering the method. The white=
 papers I found for 3M structural adhesives recommend various chemical etch=
ing processes for gluing nickel and stainless steel; nickel was a 5 second =
etch in concentrated nitric acid, while stainless steel was a 10 minute imm=
ersion in a mixture of oxalic acid, sulfuric acid, and water, followed by a=
 cold water rinse, then a hot water rinse, then an air dry, then a mechanic=
al removal of black residue. Neither of these processes really seem suitabl=
e for production; I would be worried about the nickel etching process possi=
bly damaging the nickel plating, and the stainless steel etching process is=
 too complex and would create too much FOD for my tastes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"91ad8765-efef-41f6-9cb5-4856ecad315b">2025-10-28</p>
<p local-id=3D"92bec2ab-203a-411a-b21b-9e8fedd63a22">PILOT: Showed Ning how=
 to install the cabinet side window and the cabinet. Modified 2X cabinet si=
de windows to accommodate the 2X Icotek blocks now used as cable pass-throu=
ghs. Reprinting the drilling jig for the M3 holes at the bottom of the powd=
er tank module linear rail mount components; the bushings that came in have=
 an OD of 4mm, but I designed the original jig for .250=E2=80=9D OD bushing=
s. I assembled the reprinted jig; I had to ream out the 4mm holes because I=
 intentionally undersized them. Even after super gluing the drill bushings,=
 I had chronic issues with the bushings pulling out. In hindsight, I should=
 have designed it so that the drill bushings are inserted into the jig from=
 the internal side of the 3D printed part, the side that contacts the alumi=
num part I am drilling. That way the drill bushing would need to rip throug=
h the entire part to pull out. Even with the dodgy drill jig, I was able to=
 drill and tap the two aluminum parts that have already been bolted to fixe=
d base plates and one of the two for Pilot 7. For the other one, I would li=
ke to hand if off to Steve or Al to have them add the holes properly. I red=
esigned the carriage bellows clip so that it has a feature that will give m=
ore surface area for the VHB tape to attach to when connecting the bellows =
to the carriage bellows clip and will also force alignment with the carriag=
e so that it cannot slide off the carriage in the direction of travel of th=
e carriage. This would only be a problem during installation, but adding th=
e feature will make installation easier.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe06556d-9471-4d61-8278-04c862f63721">Got a formal quote for=
 5X PSU sheet metal mounting parts because I noticed that Ning had not made=
 holes in the subpanel for the rivet nuts used to mount the PSUs. I entered=
 a Jira ticket to place the order and it appears that Philip has placed it.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"12c9cdbd-414c-4a7e-a231-26ca0ef8456e">Continuing to put toge=
ther a list of tools to bring to Japan for the Epson printer visit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8cc73620-4c3b-47f8-9bd6-cdb3a1e93986">Meeting about Epson pr=
inter documentation, mainly focused on the preventive maintenance manual.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"170b0304-3525-447a-a493-be89122d6b37">COATED POWDER: Running=
 some coated powder through the heat treat furnace to 300F, the run I did l=
ast night to 250F did not completely bond the particles. Mohsen ran a test =
on Pilot 2, first with heat curing only, which did not work at the upper te=
mperature limit of the Pilot=E2=80=99s capabilities (~250F), the with spray=
ing only water and the normal print settings. This did manage to bind the l=
ayers, but the cuts did not form completely so it was difficult to see wher=
e the TRS coupons would be in the cake, and the lines that I think are the =
outlines of the parts are distorted and duplicated. I suspect that the wate=
r penetrated into the cake too well and bound the powder that was spread in=
to the cuts, yielding a mostly solid cake.</p>
<p local-id=3D"49c90de2-93cc-4773-86ad-235f0a1e3ff2">2025-10-29</p>
<p local-id=3D"90d573b8-bbeb-497e-b979-a1dbe6302ac6">PILOT: I assisted Cisc=
o and Abe with installing and aligning the Y stages, Y gantry plate, X moun=
ting plate, X stage, and the Z stage mounting plate. When initially alignin=
g the Y stages, after bolting down the master Y stage, I forgot to connect =
the stepper motor driver to the floating stage, but was still able to go th=
rough the whole alignment procedure. Apparently the setup is rigid enough t=
hat the master stage can force the floating stage to move when the floating=
 stage is unpowered. When I connected the floating stage (now bolted down) =
to the stepper driver and jogged the gantry back and forth, the motion seem=
ed fairly smooth but I could hear a slight rubbing noise from the floating =
stage at one point in the travel, and only when traveling in one direction.=
 I unbolted the floating stage, jogged the stages back and forth a few time=
s, and went through the procedure to bolt down the floating stage, and the =
noise went away. The dowel pin holes on the Z stage mounting plate were sli=
ghtly undersized, so I expanded them by hand using a 4mm reamer. I have pri=
nted out the latest drawing detailing the components in the powder and buil=
d tank module as well as the hardware used, however, I have not yet created=
 a detailed guide for assembling and aligning the module.</p>
<p local-id=3D"59d0cbaf-06df-498e-9f64-1e05e3fef91f">COATED POWDER: The cru=
cible of pre-coated powder that I put in the furnace last night seemed comp=
letely unbound when I pulled it out today; I believe that the furnace did n=
ot heat up overnight because the door was not fully closed. I put it back i=
n and restarted the cycle, so it should be ready to pull out tomorrow, alth=
ough I will not be able to check it until 11/11. I went over the cake from =
yesterday with Mohsen. My main takeaway there is that spraying pre-coated p=
owder with water will take some finagling to get it to work with our curren=
t process.</p>
