<p local-id=3D"302feccb-996d-4892-9fba-7c1cd076f1a3">I put one mold in the =
upper Keith furnace to heat to 250F at 200 F/hr and dwell for 30 mins. When=
 it had heated and cooled down to 200F I removed it, and the part did not s=
eem fully cured so I put it back in at a new cycle to heat to 500F at the s=
ame rate with a dwell of 2 hours. I put another mold in the lower Keith fur=
nace, with a cycle to heat to 250F at 60 F/hr and dwell for 1 hour (I think=
). When it was at 180F Mahmood accidentally opened the furnace, and I ended=
 up ending the cycle and restarting it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d33e5859-0cfa-4a7b-858a-f7690e6e7762">2025-12-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"e7913380-5312-44e9-9785-1ba1447d0f28">FLIP: Received the spl=
ined shaft yesterday, so I redesigned the doser ball based on fit tests wit=
h the previous doser ball design, and am currently printing the new design.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"66fbb727-615b-4c05-8b1d-741591ac9dd4">BINDER: The samples I =
cured yesterday did not come out hard, so I tried a 1:1 blend of TEG-DMA wi=
th PEG-400 and heated it in a ceramic crucible on a hot plate set to 200C, =
and it seemed to cure well. I have mixed up a new batch of slurry with 10g =
TEG-DMA, 10g PEG-400, and</p>
<p local-id=3D"be30a634-87b5-4bf4-8e0b-da9b4ff96644">PILOT: The multi-Z-sta=
ge Mest mount project has been bumped up in priority, so I am going to focu=
s more on that than FLIP.</p>
<p local-id=3D"01dba35f-6aa8-48d3-9bef-4639f3f707ad">GENERAL R&amp;D: In a =
discussion with Marcus and Cisco about using the Beta printers as a hopper =
testbed, Payman mentioned that the Pilot printers were meant to be a tempor=
ary solution before developing the Pilot Ultra, a simpler machine, and that=
 replicating the Pilots is not what he wanted to do. The current version of=
 the Pilot Ultra is an ultimaker that has been retrofitted with a small hop=
per and a traveling mist box with an oscillating head that replaces the cut=
ting spindle with a laser. It actually has made some parts with impressivel=
y small features, although I don=E2=80=99t believe it has gone over 3mm bui=
ld height yet. I get that he wants to simplify and reduce the cost of the p=
latform, although I am unsure of the value of a very low-cost indirect AM p=
latform. A good sintering furnace will likely cost 100-250K depending on si=
zing and atmosphere requirements and accessories etc., plus we would need t=
o sell them a curing furnace and a depowdering station, so if we were selli=
ng a customer a full system then it won=E2=80=99t really matter if the prin=
ter is 100K or 50K or 1K since the total system cost is so high.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d6ec3290-2022-43d4-a83a-b7ff6609b1d8">2025-12-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"33a7b8bc-b02a-48a1-a54b-47162e94ed11">PILOT: Continuing to w=
ork on the design of the three-stage X axis mount for Pilots 6 and 7. I con=
firmed with Mason that the size of the mounting screws are 4mm. For the Mes=
t unit - z stage connection I am working on a two-piece design where one al=
uminum part mounted to the Mest unit slides into a slot in the aluminum par=
t mounted to the Z stage, with two quick-release pins that slide through bo=
th parts to lock them together. This should be adequately rigid and locate =
the Mest unit well while also being fairly easy to disassemble if necessary=
. I am also considering using should screws instead of quick release pins; =
this would likely be more secure, but it would be more difficult to disasse=
mble and reassemble, and it would require threads to be machined into one o=
f the aluminum parts, which would then need threaded inserts because the al=
uminum threads would wear out over time with the shoulder screws being repe=
atedly uninstalled and installed. It is also possible to use nuts on the ou=
tside of the aluminum part, but this would further increase the difficulty =
of disassembling/reassembling the system and would require an additional to=
ol.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4522163c-001f-4331-a885-cf74bddd8974">2025-12-12</p>
<p local-id=3D"c7556d65-4bdc-45c8-baf4-b792f98fbe65">PILOT: Working on the =
3 stage Z axis mount assembly, intended to have two spindles and one Mest u=
nit installed. I have modified the mount that connects the Mest unit to the=
 linear stage to use a dovetail instead of the previous slot and groove des=
ign with shoulder screws. The dovetail should provide improved repeatabilit=
y, robustness, and rigidity than the slot and groove, albeit with a few dra=
wbacks: machining is more complex, and locking it in place with screws will=
 be more sensitive to tightening torque.</p>
<p local-id=3D"13a0b059-ba88-4b99-ab3a-f5f29d386bde">BINDER: Trying to prin=
t with the same wet roll of Aquasys 120, with expected results. Designed a =
new clear cast mold with much thinner walls (~.5mm and 4 instead of 6 reinf=
orcing ribs) and printed 2X molds. Mixed up a slurry with 10g TEG-DMA, 10g =
PEG-400, and 190g Inconel 625 waste powder and filled both molds with the s=
lurry. I have placed them in the recirculating air Keith furnace set to hea=
t them to 250F at 60F/hour and dwell for 2 (or 3?) hours. The upper Keith f=
urnace has a mold that I had filled with the most recent batch of copper sl=
urry, then cured using the lower furnace set to 250, then ran in the cure a=
nd burn cycle that heated to 250 at 60F/hour, dwelled an hour or two, then =
heated to 400F at 200F/hr, then dwelled an hour or two. After cooling, I in=
spected it this morning, and the mold had cracked and curled but the green =
part within seemed largely unaffected. I started a cycle that has the same =
ramp to 250 to cure but heats to 500F to burn off the mold. It is unlikely =
that 500F will be sufficient to vaporize the mold, but it will be interesti=
ng to see how close it gets.</p>
<p local-id=3D"60188e09-187b-4632-9beb-242f73bfed59">2025-12-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"becbc056-1fbe-45bc-b154-3e41f4f098b6">PILOT: The design of t=
he new 3 stage Mest/spindle mount is complete enough that I feel comfortabl=
e sending it out for review with the team; it=E2=80=99s still missing an en=
dstop flag for the Mest unit but that is in progress. Since this week is lo=
oking like it will be fairly slow I am planning to machine the aluminum com=
ponents manually here; it would be good to become a little more familiar wi=
th the Trak mills.</p>
<p local-id=3D"be074421-51de-4da4-b627-a7759ce85537">MOSP: Payman set up a =
meeting to discuss installing a Mest unit on one of the Saffron printers to=
 investigate whether the process is compatible with copper. I am currently =
collaborating with Dong to design a 3D printed mount to install on one of t=
he copper Saffron printers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e84d5b5c-efc5-4786-af37-132c200b5aab">2025-12-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"7da62685-56c8-4766-913e-6cfc36b9f325">PILOT: Ben noticed tha=
t the mist hose cleaning pump was not operating, and upon inspecting the co=
nnector I found that the negative pin had come out and did not click back i=
n properly, so I cut it off and crimped on a new pin. The pump seems to be =
operational now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"166bbb62-99e2-453b-b813-e0dcc194a525">BINDER: Brought in a r=
oll of 2.85mm Aquasys 120 that I bought off of Amazon. Printing a test mold=
 on the Ultimaker S5 that is still operational (the other is down for maint=
enance at the moment). I also cooked the roll of 1.75mm Aquasys filament ov=
ernight in the filament dryer set to its highest setting and printed a test=
 ring cup on the Bambu X1C, and it seemed to come out very well when printe=
d with PLA settings (no bubbling in layers, no stringing) so I am running a=
 test mold print with that material. For reference, here is the TGA of a TE=
G-DMA variant:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-cen=
