<p local-id=3D"05ec7043-c484-4344-981b-dc8518202d76">2025-12-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"f48d40b2-12e2-4af5-b067-1eefaa6cc1fd">PILOT: Looked at the t=
hermocouple reader on Pilot 7. The temperature value seems to jump around a=
s the heater heats up; it starts out more or less correctly but jumps up th=
en jumps negative (I=E2=80=99ve seen it go down to -150C). It doesn=E2=80=
=99t seem like a smooth drop, which would suggest that the sensor reader is=
 set incorrectly, but seems more like a noise issue to me. Ning is currentl=
y troubleshooting it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b38babee-6d42-45e4-b637-4905b9ecdac9">BINDER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b403ca81-3570-433f-9292-d5c87da7f8ce">MOSP: Printing an upda=
ted design of the Mest mount for the Saffron printer. It has slots instead =
of holes for accessing the dovetail set screws at different mount positions=
 and the hard stops on top of the MOSP mount have been removed because they=
 interfere with screws that are present on the spindle stage carriages that=
 were not in the CAD model (these screws were added to mount the positively=
 pressurized bellows to the carriage later than the CAD model I have was ge=
nerated).</p>
<p local-id=3D"54c822e5-e28a-4809-be13-34bd711fb7cb">2025-12-18</p>
<p local-id=3D"66acfb64-c8a9-4e9f-a768-505cd9333612">PILOT: In process of m=
achining the main mounting plate for the three stage X axis mount. I printe=
d a mount for a Mitutoyo height gage on the now-repaired Ultimaker S5 Thano=
s, but it did not some out super well so I will reprint it on one of the Ba=
mbus.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0bc519c2-8336-44fd-aadd-d0931693f7e4">HOPPER: Since Mohsen i=
s out for a month, Mason has taken over testing and designing the hopper vi=
bration system, with Senai also working on designing a new linear motion sy=
stem for the linear gate style hopper. I need to spend some time working on=
 the automated hopper refilling system at some point.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a667448e-e85d-46d5-b380-d2133c5777d1">MOSP: The Mest unit ha=
s been removed from Pilot 3 and is in the process of being cleaned and inst=
alled on the copper Saffron printer in preparation for test printing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5910d9bf-ca55-484f-a171-b3dea9352624">MACHINING: I noticed t=
hat the tanks for the automated oiling systems on both ProtoTrak CNC mills =
were empty and did not seem to be pressurized.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3d94f77d-2cdb-47dc-8679-e7b8326534b8">2025-12-19</p>
<p local-id=3D"47e73a3b-5907-45a7-9444-c6a74bc62137">PILOT: Finished machin=
ing 2X aluminum mounts for the two spindle and Mest unit setup for polymer =
cut filling on Pilots 6 and 7. I machined the holes for the stage mounting =
points and tapped them for an M6 thread, so they are compatible with the M3=
 threaded inserts that were used on the single spindle mounts for the Pilot=
 printers previously, but I found when machining the M3 tap drill holes on =
the top that it is not very difficult to machine holes in aluminum using a =
1/16=E2=80=9D end mill, so in the future I plan to machine and tap those ho=
les for M3 threads instead of the larger M6 threads so that it is not neces=
sary to use threaded inserts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3a77f74f-d574-42d1-bf5a-bffbb1f96f63">2025-12-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"7ec5b339-9c27-47f7-843e-be70f7ab707b">PILOT: Installed threa=
ded inserts on the aluminum mounts for screwing the stages to the mount. I =
am in the process of redesigning the endstop mount to be 3D printed instead=
 of made from sheet metal because SendCutSend is having some issue with mak=
ing it and we also need the mount to be installed sooner than we would get =
the mount from SendCutSend. Finished the design and printed one from PAHT-C=
F, but it came out really rough so I am reprinting it from PETG-CF.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3fbea1f1-1952-4fdf-8803-a2380057d73e">2025-12-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"cbae4b37-bedb-4e6a-97f7-c731a3e52cea">PILOT: Set up program =
for roughing out the Mest-side Mest unit dovetail mounts. I scrapped out on=
e setup piece but roughed out two good parts. The current program uses a 1/=
2=E2=80=9D end mill to face the top and machine the outlines of the part, t=
hen I need to switch to a 1/8=E2=80=9D end mill to machine the counterbores=
, then switch to a dovetail cutter to machine the dovetail profile, and fin=
ally flip the part over and face the back side.</p>
<p local-id=3D"75b88d9b-4528-4856-a4f5-c1e528ce344c">2026-01-05</p>
<p local-id=3D"bc21676b-ff6a-465b-847c-c00458f1441a">PILOT: Continuing to w=
ork on machining the Mest dovetail mounts. I had to create new blanks becau=
se all 3 that I machined last week had some dimensional issues, but I set u=
p programs for, and complete, 3 machining ops for 3X Mest side dovetail mou=
nts: Machining the general shape, machining the dovetail with a 32=C2=B0 in=
cluded angle dovetail cutter, and machining 2X M4 counterbores. The only op=
 left is to flip the parts over and face off the back. Completed the facing=
 op. I found that the first op that machined the outer perimeter of the par=
t did not quite cut down as deeply as I needed it to, so when I faced off t=
he back there was some material left attached that I had to remove with a b=
ench sander. Fortunately this is not a function critical surface, but it is=
 a good lesson for the next parts I make that when first machining the peri=
meter of the part it is best to overshoot the height somewhat so that when =
facing the back surface there is no chance of material being left behind by=
 both ops.</p>
<p local-id=3D"371ad4d1-841a-4051-b32e-58f0432d6665">MOSP: Dong requested a=
 new Mest mount for the Saffron printer that only engages with the center t=
wo spindle stages, in case one of the outer two stages faults out and fails=
 to move with the center stages. I will make a new configuration of the CAD=
 where the outer two dovetail mounts are eliminated and print the new model=
 on the Bambu X1C.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8c3fc075-3773-422f-b796-54a1145682e3">Alignment/2026 Priorit=
ies Meeting w/ Marcus and Payman: Payman and Marcus are meeting with the va=
rious core R&amp;D people to discuss the priorities for tasks in 2026. Soun=
ds like my primary project is to work on the hopper, with the intent of hav=
ing hoppers on all pilot printers by the end of Q2 that are as robust as th=
e various other printer systems. Requirements are:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"c9695833-11ae-480b-9074-b43deed5a2ac">
<li local-id=3D"1b2715ed-d965-4937-84a7-05f8ab5e40b7">
<p local-id=3D"2465b34f-71ac-4b6d-ad50-e172349aa46b">Hoppers installed on a=
ll pilot printers</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"d9de8351-e5be-4273-8cd6-60de09d5e321">
<p local-id=3D"5bcb542e-2704-44d9-bb64-efc6939f4771">Spreader bar integrate=
d into hopper unit</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3301bc61-dfa0-4f2b-a873-2e54d7283a03">
<p local-id=3D"031b16c0-5596-4a5b-9609-5cffa89ae1ad">Able to print indefini=
tely without direct manual reloading</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b768a80d-6fb0-484b-8b33-9ed89a387e31">
<p local-id=3D"ed0e1f3b-9783-439f-9ba1-0134eef93cee">Able to operate indefi=
nitely without gate failure to open or close</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"813ba7b3-99ba-4afa-b51a-9a9eb8795cbb">
<p local-id=3D"553f7e32-b936-4686-a7d5-c3ba78018d8b">Fault detection that w=
ill stop print if gate fails to open or close</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"862e48d2-fa24-410c-b006-00499a0a0194">
<p local-id=3D"e3b87aa9-098e-479a-bb11-7fcf61129f3a">Hopper must use linear=
 gate</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"58634faf-43bc-4acc-b88e-b4254274d98a">
<p local-id=3D"42fff0f7-b505-490f-aa3b-406ab5ba9c7a">Reduced powder wastage=
</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e1893dcc-0340-4da6-a90b-c9af91882a8c">
<p local-id=3D"33d90589-5c37-43fe-b29f-58feefd18d25">Equivalent density to =
tank-to-tank system</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"80ab3021-adbc-4853-a0d1-b5e24760114c">
<p local-id=3D"19587be7-6e69-422c-9031-44bf315f5625">Part geometry limitati=
ons are OK (i.e. cannot print features below a certain size, etc.)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"256cb764-eacc-4c14-bb56-1de50b67c6e6">
<p local-id=3D"e0a9174d-75c7-428d-be4a-02d8b0c4f00f">Pre-treated 17-4 PH on=
ly</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"f74d7aaa-1e58-4810-9780-fef612dcd325">Also of note, Marcus a=
nd Payman mentioned that Rajan and Alex are not convinced about the Pilot U=
ltra concept. Also included under my primary project category is any modifi=
cations that need to be made to the pilot printers, such as the three stage=
 spindle/Mest mount. My secondary project is to work on next generation pri=
nter architecture (haha).</p>
<p local-id=3D"69ef0304-d638-498d-a499-49f82efd9d24">2026-01-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"833d6486-b99f-45d1-b518-f59b8cf38af3">PILOT: Modified design=
 of the endstop mount for the two spindle / Mest unit mount on Pilot 6; the=
 mount interfered with the X axis mount and I also made it slightly thicker=
 (3mm - 5mm) to be stiffer. Working on machining the stage side Mest mount.=
 I have the first op complete on all three parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dc640d61-6a38-4657-85ef-bd31b5f0f83f">Informed by Mahmood th=
at he will be leaving for Divergent at the end of next week.</p>
<p local-id=3D"29c9723b-5be5-4899-bb8a-bdd23ffccbdf">Also, Creighton sent o=
ut a Google calendar invitation for a meeting on Thursday: =E2=80=9CJoin us=
 for lunch from Aristo Cafe - Alex will be making announcements and discuss=
ing current matters.=E2=80=9D Sounds ominous to me, but Devin asked Creight=
on if there were further layoffs planned and he said that there were not. M=
ason=E2=80=99s theory is that it is a meeting for Alex to convince people t=
o stay at 3DEO; apparently he is planning to go to Japan to talk to someone=
 who may give us some money so he may use that as an argument that there is=
 hope for continued funding. Some questions I would like to know the answer=
 to:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"bfa73c10-da31-4f0e-bd3e-b09971777439">
<li local-id=3D"9ff295aa-b671-42ba-adb5-e11a68f17806">
<p local-id=3D"9c5f0145-f517-4e85-bafd-b4b5a9539c3d">How much runway do we =
currently have in the bank?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"727fb19a-4c85-4a75-82fc-1f50e7ad1d36">
<p local-id=3D"f1d40945-3503-43fc-a466-6753f86175d6">Do we have investors w=
ho have promised more funding if we show some specific results (i.e. no-dep=
owdering, laser, etc.)? If so, what results precisely do they want to see?<=
/p></li>
<li local-id=3D"352193d8-f3a4-46c8-8623-11dc607b2b4a">
<p local-id=3D"2bcf84bf-564b-495b-ada8-058c23d9aede">What is the proposed 1=
 year and 5 year plan for 3DEO? Is it centered around selling next-generati=
on printers with the new subsystems being developed? What if those subsyste=
ms do not pan out? Can we sell upgraded printers with our current technolog=
y stack (i.e. similar to Saffrons)?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"ec3bc2a4-c69f-4cbf-b238-e15ae434ed1a">
<p local-id=3D"98b71a0a-94b7-4023-8d65-b1b1b5446ec4">How production-focused=
 is the company expected to be? Can we invest in some alternative technolog=
ies to use as a =E2=80=9Cside hustle=E2=80=9D to get some revenue in while =
we update and sell the 3DEO hardware stack printers?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"4d6335af-667f-4271-9e6e-8e734af50dd8">
<p local-id=3D"3458e76a-fab6-45fa-b01a-31cd31efc6a9">Do we plan to hire mor=
e people to replace those who are leaving? If so, what is the expected time=
line?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"5d9d5ac7-7394-4231-8d9f-67d6abb1b25f">
<p local-id=3D"3ccf53dd-eb7d-4594-842a-de7d2338a67f">Are raises/incentives =
planned to retain current employees (i.e. raises, bonuses, benefits such as=
 free food and snacks, access to use tools for personal projects on the wee=
kends, return authority to order from McMaster directly)?</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"4dbbd91c-bd16-4379-82a0-ff6741c739bf"></p>
<p local-id=3D"9bc6e685-ecc0-44af-a47f-e197916e2d7f">2026-01-07</p>
<p local-id=3D"b38b5011-301d-44b5-b49a-0294498e083d">PILOT: Continuing to m=
achine the stage-side Mest unit dovetail mounts. I need to machine the dove=
tails and the counterbores, then flip the part over to face off the back, t=
hen drill and tap the holes that one one side clamp the dovetail in place a=
nd on the other side hold the Mest unit endstop contact point. Completed ma=
chining the stage side Mest unit mount, including tapping the 6X M3 holes o=
n each part. Completed design of an endstop flag for the Mest mount (it is =
a 3D printed part that directly contacts the endstop; the unit is close eno=
ugh to the adjacent spindle mount that I could not design a mount that hold=
s one of the standard hardened steel contact points I usually like to use. =
This is an untested approach, but I think it should work OK for the Mest un=
it).</p>
<p local-id=3D"d9ae75d7-91d9-47a6-8d15-10e0bc76e427">HOPPER: I plan to look=
 at the latest hopper design today. I have been following the development o=
f the latest model and advising where helpful, so I am fairly up to date wi=
th the current design, but it is always good to refamiliarize myself with t=
he details of the design. Mason=E2=80=99s CAD assembly on the Vault is miss=
ing several components; the file path of the files points to Mason=E2=80=99=
s desktop so they are presumably stored locally on his computer. Fortunatel=
y I had planned to redesign much of the hopper anyway, and this gives me an=
 excuse to do so. <br></p>
<p local-id=3D"98f089a1-3b32-4617-ac85-ae558a9d5589">2026-01-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"df739e19-a96d-4beb-919b-2b6a69a82d66">Discussed the design o=
f the Epson heat exchanger currently being developed that will have a thin =
plate brazed on to it. Since it is impossible to depowder the gyroid struct=
ure being used when the heat exchanger is printed in the fully enclosed con=
figuration, the part must be printed with the gyroid structure fully expose=
d then sealed with an attached plate. I discussed several joint and braze p=
lacement strategies with Senai, and the two being currently pursued both ha=
ve BAg-7 foil pre placed in the joint, with one joint having the gyroid str=
ucture flush with the sealing plate while the other has the gyroid structur=
e recessed by 0.5mm to act as a braze stop to retain the braze filler mater=
ial within the lap joint. This recessed gyroid structure design also has se=
veral issues: there will be some performance loss because there is a flow p=
ath outside of the gyroid structure, and the thin sealing plate may distort=
 when the heat exchanger is pressurized because it is only attached around =
the edges instead of being attached to the whole area of the gyroid structu=
re.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c0df9816-6bea-4e3f-8648-79929b8b8d94">HOPPER: Working on adj=
usting the design of the hopper. Senai is working on installing his new rod=
 guide design, while I am working on learning from the current design and d=
eveloping one that is simpler and more robust. I am working on switching th=
e gate from being belt driven to cam driven, with the larger stepper motor =
oriented vertically instead of horizontally and a small track roller riding=
 in a slot machined into the gate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"df85f9f2-4b88-4432-bb7a-7f70ed0b8a56">We had a free company =
lunch today so that Alex could make some announcements. His statements abou=
t company strategy and fundraising were largely unchanged from what we have=
 heard over the past few years; we want to sell machines eventually, we are=
 trying to only do production of parts that make sense for our process with=
 customers who are willing to be strategic partners (that last part is new-=
ish, maybe only a year old), and we are trying to drum up interest in buyin=
g our machines (that part might actually be new). He, Scott, and Rajan ( ? =
) are going to Japan soon to visit Epson, IHI, Honda, and some of our bank =
investors to ask for more money. We have apparently closed a $3M funding ro=
und at some point (not sure if that is new or not), mostly from previous in=
vestors, and are looking to get another $3M from outside investors (the $6M=
 goal is chosen because that would give us enough capital to run until the =
end of the year. Not really anything particularly new there. He also announ=
ced that 4 people are leaving: Mahmood (which I already knew), Chris Fontai=
ne (which I did not know), and the two bean counters Creighton and Jasmine,=
 which I did not know but also don=E2=80=99t really care about. There were =
no questions.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bce45458-546d-4230-9398-78657d3a851d">2026-01-09</p>
<p local-id=3D"1fea5a81-f2a1-4a6a-8aff-9c413e371f84">PILOT: Working on a mo=
dified version of the linear gate hopper, with more standardized parts and =
a reduced part count. By eliminating the belt drive system in favor of a ca=
m drive, I clear up the space on the side previously taken up by a pair of =
belt sprockets and the belt itself along with the guide rails so that I onl=
y need to fit in guide rods; this lets me use some really beefy guide rods =
that should provide greater robustness and controlled movement than the pre=
vious guide rail carriages. Payman suggested adding some guards over the gu=
ide rods to give the linear bearings some protection from powder; along wit=
h the rubber seals this should allow the linear bearing system to survive f=
or an extended period of time in the hostile environment of the printers. T=
hey will eventually ingest powder and fail, but depending on the time this =
takes, we can either: replace them with bushings that are more powder toler=
ant but provide less precise alignment, tolerate the limited lifespan of th=
e linear bearings, or add positively pressurized and sealed bellows. This w=
ould be the most complex, expensive, and space-consuming solution, but it w=
ould also yield the longest operational lifespan of any of the available op=
tions.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cd660804-bdd3-44ca-8e7b-a08b4f6a5174">2026-01-12</p>
<p local-id=3D"a80cb4a7-8ece-41a5-9173-a0c17923a177">GENERAL: Chris Fontain=
e spent two hours training people on the operation of the Elnik furnace and=
 miscellaneous procedures around the sintering process. I recorded most of =
it using the GoPro.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a0e423cf-8042-4707-ad3a-534ba63cf9d9">HOPPER: Continuing to =
work on the design of HOPPER V5 (the linear gate design derived from Mason=
=E2=80=99s design).</p>
<p local-id=3D"0bacd922-71d9-4810-ba4f-8bc65ebdb82e">2026-01-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"48a56c0f-cd12-4cf8-839a-e25ad5b862c9">HOPPER: Working on fin=
alizing the new hopper design. I had to add some 3mm spacers to raise the h=
opper by 3mm; with the Edmund stages centered the hopper was too low. Techn=
ically the stages have enough travel that they could be adjusted to the cor=
rect position, but it would be close to the end of their travel. I would li=
ke the hopper to theoretically be at the correct position when the stages a=
re centered so that there is as much adjustment as possible in either direc=
tion to compensate for manufacturing variation and any component changes th=
at might need to be made in the future. The remaining feature that needs to=
 be added is some detection of the position of the gate, either a continuou=
s ranging sensor or a limit switch. I am leaning towards a range sensor bec=
ause the powder feed control system will need them for measuring powder hei=
ght in the hopper anyway, and it gives closed-loop software control of the =
gate position so it is easier to adjust than a hardware adjustment solution=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fded0b4c-e44d-4484-afbb-ed85b832d706">The precision of avail=
able inexpensive sensors is fairly poor for measuring gate position (about =
3mm) so I am going to use mechanical limit switches. Inductive proximity sw=
itches would also work, but may be more difficult to interface with our cur=
rent control system so I am going to stick with the limit switches we gener=
ally use.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b95ba2a2-4e85-47ed-94d0-50c207071dec">I am instead going to =
use ultra-miniature limit switches because, in this particular design, the =
smaller the limit switch, the smaller the machined flag can be.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2605b0fa-7c2e-4272-af94-87d643734c1c">I completed the design=
, got the machined parts quoted on Xometry, and sent the quote into the pur=
chasing system. The total cost is ~$1500 using the slowest and cheapest int=
ernational purchasing option. I forgot to add the stepper motor cam to the =
first order, so I got it quoted separately and submitted the order request.=
 The cost is ~$150. Interestingly, the cost part part varied within a prett=
y narrow range, ~$150-$250, regardless of part size or complexity, and incr=
easing the quantity from 1 to 2 usually only added ~$30 or so. This makes s=
ense, since material costs are a small fraction of the total cost and most =
of the cost is taken up by initial setup, so 2 parts don=E2=80=99t cost muc=
h more to make than 1, and part size is largely irrelevant when it comes to=
 cost. However, they may come back and say that some of the parts are not m=
achinable, so it remains to be seen how accurate their automated quoting sy=
stem is.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3390980e-62ad-4da1-b93d-ee6dcd3c4d19">2026-01-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"818c5eff-becd-4f6f-aa36-349366b22426">Training in the materi=
als lab on tap testing powder, sintered sample preparation (slicing, deburr=
ing, embedding in resin, grinding and polishing in Buehler polishing machin=
e), and sample inspection with the Keyence digital microscope. Videos are s=
tored in the =E2=80=9CJared=E2=80=9D folder under =E2=80=9CPersonal=E2=80=
=9D in the Z drive.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bf05cac9-2e1f-4dba-8ac6-20f21723f00c">HOPPER: All machined p=
arts have now been ordered from Xometry. In theory they will be here within=
 11 business days, since they were ordered with the super-cheap economy int=
ernational option (ordering them faster would roughly quadruple the cost). =
I have selected the ESP32 architecture for the control system because of it=
s ease of programming (Arduino IDE), low cost, high processing speed, and w=
ireless connectivity features.</p>
<p local-id=3D"12d39474-a0b5-4f37-93e4-800fc64c5227">ULTRA: Spoke with Paym=
an about his ideas for an updated Pilot Ultra platform. Essentially the sam=
e functionality as the Ultimaker setup he has, with an axis for the spread =
and spray module, a z axis for raising and lowering the build, and a laser =
mounted on top for doing the cuts, but more robust and purpose-built for th=
e application. Target BOM cost is &lt;$10K (~$4K for the Mest unit, $2K for=
 the laser unit, $4K for structure, motion components, and control system).=
 This obviously makes precision stages not an option, but as long as precis=
ion requirements are loose (say, .05mm) then it should be doable with inexp=
ensive linear carriages and timing belts. For the Z axis, the stepper leads=
crew system can be recycled from the Pilot machines as it seems adequate fo=
r our application. It is important to note that if we want to position the =
Mest unit or cut with a spindle with comparable accuracy to the current pri=
nters, it will (likely) not be possible to use very low cost components.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"b2296849-4fbe-40e4-9bb4-3f815e856057">2026-01-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"4128d2c4-3d65-4c66-a60b-cd28c1b56f44">More materials lab tra=
ining, this time on sample preparation for the tensile tester, operating th=
e Shimadzu tensile tenser, and entering and recording corresponding data. A=
lso, Adriana went over the procedure for measuring part density using the A=
rchimedes density testing process. Chris also went over the procedure for u=
sing the hardness tester. I documented all of this with the GoPro, and will=
 put the videos in the same folder as all the other GoPro videos.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ba685e4e-d0d7-4122-b584-00ccec9cae22">Printing a demo of a h=
eat exchanger model from Alloyed for Marcus on the Formlabs printer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ca06d237-30d7-4c35-8d40-5f755d2f9be4">HOPPER: Completed desi=
gn of the rubber vibration isolator and a mold for casting it. I will print=
 this on the Bambu X1E that I moved into the R&amp;D lab today. I have also=
 continued to work on the combined powder feed hat/vibrator. As designed ri=
ght now, it will have a metal sheet attached to it running into the powder,=
 have a vibrator motor on the outside, and have two ultrasonic ranging sens=
ors installed to measure the height of the powder.</p>
