<p local-id=3D"f8092eaa-54dc-4cea-a302-912dd1c3797b">No Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"a3be64fc-513e-4402-83d1-19bd2ae941a7">Pourya printing AEM pa=
rts. Working on increasing speed. Mason preparing to run a print on the cop=
per Saffron. Collision detection macro tripping erroneously.</p>
<p local-id=3D"928b09f0-d98c-4ce0-86d8-f27beab6862a">Laser:</p>
<p local-id=3D"daf2e220-be0f-42b3-87d9-dcc063a7fd24">Mason reinstalled Xtoo=
l on Pilot 3 and reinstalled protective doors. Mason experimenting with new=
 MOPA laser on some spread/spray-made layers. Adjusting scan speed, laser p=
ower, pulse width, frequency. Having more water in the powder seems to help=
 with ejecting powder. The MOPA laser seems to yield cleaner cuts than the =
Xtool, theoretically because of the greater parameter control (more, shorte=
r, higher power pulses). Removing the Xtool and installing the MOPA laser o=
n Pilot 3. Designing a box to inject crossflow air on one side and vacuum o=
ut the air from the other side to collect ejected powder during laser proce=
ssing. Payman working on software for generating slices for the laser.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6e6f47ba-273f-48b2-b1fb-3a0259588292">Spread/Spray:</p>
<p local-id=3D"da645bc6-24aa-491e-979d-1b58e4f30ff1">Sean working on Ultima=
ker with a traveling mist box and a rotary gate hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"84c5451e-fad8-466f-b620-221220a58e8f">2026-01-19</p>
<p local-id=3D"41092275-e9ce-438b-8ddd-03d72beb3b0d">Mohsen is back today. =
First day without Mahmood or Chris.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3fb33681-8fff-41cc-8974-465dc140174f">HOPPER: Continuing to =
work on vibrating hat and beginning to work on control system. I am switchi=
ng to infrared distance switches; in theory they will be able to detect the=
 powder level at a distance, are easy to mount, and can be adjusted to cont=
rol the detection distance at which they trip from the outside of the vibra=
ting hat, so they can easily be adjusted to set the powder height. I plan t=
o install two sensors for redundancy. I changed my mind, the 18mm diameter =
(with 23mm diameter nuts) IR switch is too large to easily integrate into t=
he hat, so I am instead going to use 2X TOF sensors: <a href=3D"https://www=
.adafruit.com/product/5425" local-id=3D"7c092161-7c84-4077-be5f-b35104e1956=
e" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow"=
>https://www.adafruit.com/product/5425</a> . This particular model is capab=
le of multi-object detection, which is important here because the powder ta=
kes up a very small section of the FOV when the sensor is mounted to the vi=
brating hat. Now I=E2=80=99m leaning towards using my first idea: two condu=
ctive probes inserted into the hopper that will conduct electricity when th=
ey both contact powder. This needs to be tested, and will only work on cond=
uctive powders (for example, certain ceramic powders will not work with thi=
s system), but it is very simple, will work in the narrow viewing window of=
 this hopper design, and will also be easy to integrate into a hopper this =
small (after making cutouts for installing the TOF boards, I see that the p=
acking will be challenging due mainly to the need for a vibrating metal she=
et. I checked the conductivity of our powder, and my Fluke multimeter canno=
t detect it, even if the probes are ~3mm apart. I also tried measuring the =
capacitance, and did not detect anything. Granted, I=E2=80=99m sure it is p=
ossible to detect capacitance and resistance changes with probes with the r=
ight circuitry, but that would probably be more trouble than it=E2=80=99s w=
orth.</p>
<p local-id=3D"72f2a4c2-d105-40b6-b28e-f392ba0479b8">I asked Mason how high=
 he generally fills the hopper, and he said that he usually fills it pretty=
 much to the top, so that is what I will target with the installed sensors.=
 This actually makes sensor installation much easier, because instead of tr=
ying to measure the height of the powder when it is more recessed in the ho=
pper I can measure it at the top of the hopper, where more of the the full =
width of the hopper is available for the sensor FOV (even at the top of the=
 aluminum hopper component the sheet metal mount takes up some of the width=
).</p>
<p local-id=3D"9f48fe81-020c-4d13-9f29-3601a950f247">Marcus recommended hav=
ing Mohsen explore the impact of powder height on the print (I recommended =
print density and print uniformity as output variables).</p>
<p local-id=3D"944c86dd-187a-41aa-a07d-2c7367eebe42">2026-01-20</p>
<p local-id=3D"51d67750-932d-4018-9f0d-579fc9ef8eb4">HOPPER: Discussed test=
ing of currently installed hopper design with Mohsen and Senai; I think it =
will be useful to get some data on the effect of powder height on print qua=
lity and general data on the quality of prints with the linear gate hopper =
design.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ac80c6e8-40b9-4927-9214-b3b09cbe2b57">I have what I think is=
 a fairly solid vibrating motor mount design, so today I am going to focus =
on sensor integration with the vibrating hat. The three sensors I am explor=
ing are an IR ranging switch, an inductive proximity switch, and a TOF sens=
or (<a href=3D"https://www.adafruit.com/product/3967" local-id=3D"9650fd3f-=
3a5a-417d-af28-601bd16c3a05" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"exter=
nal-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.adafruit.com/product/3967</a> ). All=
 three have unique advantages and disadvantages. They are all potentially s=
ensitive to metal powder buildup (they each need to have their sensing elem=
ent inserted through the vibrating hat, directly exposing them to powder. T=
he IR sensor and TOF sensor both require light to be transmitted from and r=
eceived through transparent windows, so covering them with powder may yield=
 false readings. The same is possible with the inductive proximity switch, =
but depending on its sensitivity it should be more tolerant to detecting po=
wder buildup; even caked in powder, if there is enough of a difference betw=
een the inductance of the powder caking and the powder in the hopper, it sh=
ould still be able to detect the powder height. The inductive and IR switch=
es are easy to interface to the ESP32 board because they only send a digita=
l on/off signal, and the set powder height can be adjusted mechanically by =
physically adjusting the sensor height, but they also are only able to dete=
ct the powder at a single height. The TOF sensor is slightly more difficult=
 to interface (I2C, so not super hard), and it has a very small light apert=
ure that may be more sensitive to powder buildup, but it also enables actua=
l measurement of the height of the powder instead of only detecting if it h=
as reached a single point. However, the accuracy of the TOF sensor is unkno=
wn, and may be less than the triggering range band of the IR and inductive =
proximity switches.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b2e5da33-f63d-4957-8af3-53bbb881d1f5">I completed the design=
 of the integrated vibration application, powder feed, and height measureme=
nt hat, along with the vibratory motor mount and 2X bayonet mounts for diff=
erent diameters of proximity switch (the 18mm OD IR switch and the 12mm OD =
inductive switch that I have around). I am printing those parts overnight t=
o test the fit and begin assembly of the vibratory hat. I have the design o=
f the vibrating sheet complete but I will need to order some material to ma=
ke it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5d7b272f-250e-4dc4-8366-d41214e3fc06">I forgot to add the ho=
les for wires in the vibrator motor mount case, and I also realized that th=
e motor is not well located in the case when the case is pulled off of the =
vibrating hat. I plan to super glue it to the case to hold it in position, =
then when the case is bolted on it will be clamped between the case and the=
 vibrating hat body, but that=E2=80=99s a bit of a janky solution. It makes=
 it difficult to transfer the motor to a new mount if it is glued to a 3D p=
rinted part. Of course, I could just break it off, but that=E2=80=99s not a=
n approach that others generally like to take so I am trying not to design =
things that way anymore.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe2b2805-0fba-4906-a379-d70a43e8cf80">2026-01-21</p>
<p local-id=3D"b9dade58-d3ac-4f6a-83f2-2bee02a320d3">HOPPER: Removed the pr=
inted vibrating hat, motor case, and 2X proximity switch sensors from the B=
ambu X1E. The bayonet lugs on the switch sensor mounts don=E2=80=99t engage=
 with the vibrating hat bayonet slots, but that=E2=80=99s to be expected; g=
etting that functionality from FDM parts usually takes some iteration becau=
se the functionality is highly sensitive to lug-slot spacing. I trimmed off=
 a bit of the material on the bayonet lugs and they seem to engage well now=
, so maybe the design is OK. The material I trimmed off was some filament t=
hat had sagged during printing. The height of the IR switch looks OK, but t=
he inductive proximity switch is a little higher than I=E2=80=99d like, eve=
n with its mounting nuts positioned as high as possible, so I am going to r=
eprint its bayonet mount so that it can be adjusted lower in the hopper. It=
 is also difficult to tighten the nuts onto the 18mm OD IR proximity switch=
, so I am going to redesign that as well so at least one of the nuts is mor=
e accessible.</p>
<p local-id=3D"eb494db3-332b-416d-be25-c0de164616a3">I placed an order requ=
est for some 70A urethane rubber for making stiffer vibration isolation pad=
s: <a href=3D"https://www.reynoldsam.com/product/pmc-770" local-id=3D"2e63e=
ea9-1821-41fb-b32f-48a5a33c3bf5" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"e=
xternal-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.reynoldsam.com/product/pmc-770</=
a> . Softer materials provide better vibration isolation, but stiffer rubbe=
rs provide better material properties for withstanding the stress of the fi=
ll duct pulling on the vibrating hat while the gantry is moving. I am not s=
ure quite what the optimal balance is, so I am going to try making vibratio=
n isolation pads from a variety of materials and see what works best.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3a534fa3-9601-48b0-ad5e-6d99dcf788d9">I am restarting work o=
n FLIP - FINE POWDER LOADING INVERSION PROTOCOL, the automated cartridge-fe=
d powder loading system for the hopper. I have added a toggle clamp to lock=
 the cartridge in place once it has been installed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"df18984d-32f5-4b40-933e-3e5be106bc54">I went over the 3 sens=
or types I am exploring at the moment with Devin, and he recommended switch=
ing to load cells to measure the weight of the hopper instead of height sen=
sors. This has the advantage of measuring powder height without the sensors=
 being exposed to the powder directly, which is the case with all 3 of the =
other sensor types being tested (inductive proximity switch, IR ranging swi=
tch, laser TOF sensor).</p>
<p local-id=3D"21ede8f0-7b39-4fbd-aa66-9ca9ceac06a3">GENERAL R&amp;D: I did=
 some maintenance on ABSAM, the automated band saw cutting machine. I empti=
ed out and cleaned the coolant reservoir, and refilled it with a mix of DI =
water and the MicroSol coolant used in the machine shop. I also replaced th=
e blade with one for cutting nickel alloys and stainless steel, which I=E2=
=80=99m pretty sure it already had because I had ordered one of those blade=
s before and the blade on the band saw looked like it had the same dark ban=
d of coloration where the teeth are present, but the new blade is at least =
probably sharper (although the old one seemed sharp enough, so). Dong had a=
sked me to replace the blade with one spec=E2=80=99d for Inconel (i.e. nick=
el alloys) because there was some vaporization of coolant with the previous=
 blade. I saw some vaporization with the new blade, but I increased the coo=
lant flow and decreased the cutting speed to eliminate it. Dong did not see=
 the vaporization, so he is happy with the new blade. The sample we cut did=
 seem to cut quite cleanly, so I would guess that the new blade does perfor=
m better than the old one, even if it is the same model of blade and replac=
ement was probably unnecessary.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8fc18e2c-af89-4b72-a81d-f94ccd62833d">2026-01-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"e886b400-c8b4-485f-b54a-80dbbea3b2e1">Printed a new hopper d=
esign of Payman=E2=80=99s that is designed so that it can be printed withou=
t supports on the Bambu printer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a5791466-e670-4992-94e5-e58d809f1310">HOPPER: Continuing to =
work on the cartridge-based powder feed system. Designed a vibration isolat=
ing mount that has two 3D printed parts with a cast pad sandwiched between =
them. A mold will be installed around the two sandwiching plates, which hav=
e locking features that will hold the vibration isolation pad material afte=
r it has been poured into the mold and cured. After casting, the mold will =
be removed (in theory) and the result should be two 3D printed parts that a=
ct as mounts that are attached to each other only with a cast rubber vibrat=
ion isolating pad. I mixed and poured some of the urethane rubber received =
today (Smooth-On PMC-770) and 1X mold described above and 2X molds that I h=
ad previously prepared for making vibration isolating pads for the vibratin=
g hat. The rubber has a color I have seen in urethane sheets previously, si=
milar to a slightly used motor oil, an amber or dark honey color. I am not =
sure how strongly the urethane will adhere to the 3D printed mold parts (I =
chose not to use mold release as an experiment to test adhesion). The mold =
for the vibration isolator for the powder cartridge dock was printed from P=
ETG-CF, while the molds for the vibration isolators for the vibrating hat a=
re PLA-CF. I also poured some excess rubber into a platinum cure silicone m=
old I had around (also without applying any fresh mold release, although mo=
ld release had been applied to that silicone mold in the past).</p>
<p local-id=3D"1a0be595-6b49-4753-8974-c40596ea1adb">2026-01-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"aad6d91f-de36-4ba5-b652-67b2b17377ee">HOPPER: The molded ure=
thane came out of the silicone mold easily, but adhered strongly to the PLA=
 and PETG parts. I was able to cut off the molding walls from the powder ca=
rtridge vibration isolator, but the vibrating hat vibration isolators were =
difficult enough to remove that it is not worth my time to tear them apart.=
 I am considering either making a silicone mold to cast that part or making=
 a two-part water soluble mold from Aquasys material that can be dissolved =
off once the urethane part is solidified.</p>
<p local-id=3D"61818d28-cf98-40a8-a34f-987ee7b1b29b">I am currently making =
a PLA-CF master to make a silicone mold that can be used to cast a vibratio=
n isolator from various urethanes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9f354481-f6b2-4d19-a2bd-18d9b1050a8b">Weekly R&amp;D Meeting=
:</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe9b0abd-7610-4558-be91-3e7292d76a02">No Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"ec01afed-cce6-4a54-aae5-b4fb961ecdc9">Mason printed a 20 lay=
er square on the copper Saffron, but it required extensive manual intervent=
ion. His theory for why they were having difficulties is that the (colder) =
environment in the room was causing issues by cooling down the heated nozzl=
e. The print is now in the desiccant box. To resolve the issue he plans to =
implement a shield to protect the Mest dispenser from the environment.</p>
<p local-id=3D"79858b5a-8e24-4585-8c8d-fbbebe9e1cb3">Pourya was running pri=
nts on Pilot 1. Ran into issues with hatched areas being overfilled and iss=
ues with poor spreading. Did another print with unscaled parts and no hatch=
ing at a higher speed, which did not encounter the same issues (400 mm/min =
vs 100 mm/min previously). Part itself was good but was difficult to break =
out because it was stuck to the base plate. Printed a part from layer 58-71=
 with hatch but no scale factor at the higher speed. He plans to use 400 mm=
/min moving forward for the dispense step. RPi issues (USB connectivity iss=
ues, changing RPi did not resolve issue), now trying replacing USB hub. Usu=
ally facing is done by following the same XY G code, but this time he tried=
 facing the entire part, which made the surface more flat and did not have =
any issues with spreading. Marcus suggests he rerun the same test on a diff=
erent pilot printer to try to diagnose the issue. 2 new Mest units ordered.=
 Next week plans to fix the Raspberry Pi issues.</p>
<p local-id=3D"abdf0d68-eea9-4918-81f6-92c0f3189f31">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe23ebfd-09c1-4ee2-89a1-9a1bbafdf1af">Rotating joint from po=
wder dispenser</p>
<ul local-id=3D"5fbc7c40-0248-4f48-bd78-7000e11c7af3">
<li local-id=3D"6ae5baec-03aa-4209-abeb-bada240621fa">
<p local-id=3D"109e8fb4-76ee-4c1a-ac01-ba76a6c1a8ad">Bring up control syste=
m</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"55285a29-39cd-4a78-9040-ae7a63f08aee">
<p local-id=3D"990c2e06-b8e1-468a-9f25-997f96d18850">Complete design and st=
art prototype of automated powder feeder system</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"dc7d74fb-5d81-4d70-a292-6a18137bcf07">Spread and Spray:</p>
<p local-id=3D"26697db5-0177-49e0-bf9e-36034015eb53">Made new spray box. Ad=
ding and testing vacuum attachments to reduce mist that escapes from the to=
p of the (open) mist box and around the edges of the mist outlet. Working o=
n preventing droplet formation due to mist collecting on the edge of the mi=
st box.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2cafbdc4-400a-4d0f-9d0f-782734629133">Laser:</p>
<p local-id=3D"44419230-f49c-4046-96bd-bda4e29bc671">MOPA laser installed o=
n Pilot 3. Finished writing macros for making layers and turning the LVP on=
 and off (Laser Vacuum [Part?], air crossflow/vacuum box that goes around t=
he laser area). Poor suction with current vacuum setup, but had success usi=
ng shop vac. Ready for testing the system next week. No printing on Pilot 3=
 yet. Payman working on generating software to create laser compatible file=
s from slices and send them to Lightburn to operate the laser. Mason out ne=
xt Tuesday-Friday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b9cd42b1-558b-4a4c-967a-82481768721e">Layer drying with comp=
ressed air:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1a68f7b7-be13-46ea-b03e-1835b860c3d8">Heater is not compatib=
le with the PEG being used for the no depowdering process, so the goal is t=
o try using dry compressed air to dry the layer instead of letting it dry i=
n ambient air (which currently takes ~5 mins). Payman pointed out that prev=
ious tests had the most success with blowing dry air into an enclosed box a=
round the layer (I am assuming that there is still some opening in the cham=
ber) but Mohsen plans to use a custom-made box that is half the size of the=
 build and have it travel along the build to dry out the layer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0c972383-9113-404a-a207-d1697e35b010">I would recommend impl=
ementing a crossflow system similar to that used in LPBF printers, essentia=
lly a low-velocity air knife mounted at the edge of the build blowing air a=
long the build.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3915e4f0-9402-4498-b0fe-74b6c47829e5">Patent files for no de=
powdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"5614aab8-447a-4862-9983-cba0fc2e6cf9">Initial disclosure Mar=
 last year, need to formalize by this March. Marcus stated that he is going=
 to set up meetings with everyone involved in R&amp;D in the next week.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e4b4d0c2-e217-4d52-b1a7-3729526fb87e">2026-01-26</p>
<p local-id=3D"82b90156-9715-4fe7-8f6f-00fa4e52f000">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8584dab3-5b43-40aa-b98c-0b9e09f32ee8">I messed up removing t=
he mold positive for the vibrating hat vibration isolator pad mold; there a=
re sections of the mold that form the counterbored in the isolator pad, and=
 they need to be cut at the smaller diameter so that the mold counterbore p=
ositive can be removed when demolding the part, but the first two I cut at =
the larger diameter counterbore end, which makes it impossible to demold th=
e part. I am going to print another mold positive and pour silicone into it=
 at the same time as I am filling a mold positive for making a silicone mol=
d to cast the powder dispenser vibration isolation pad.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cbae2e43-734f-48dc-aed1-3774b255c552">Completed the pour of =
the mold for making the powder dispensing dock vibration isolator, but was =
only able to half finish the pour of the mold for the vibrating hat vibrati=
on isolator, even with mixing up the last of the Mold Star 15 Slow and the =
Mold Star 31 that we had around. I have been using up older silicones as mu=
ch as possible, so there is more Mold Star 30 that is fairly fresh that I d=
id not use, so I can finish the pour tomorrow and the various layers should=
 fuse together chemically (as long as the older silicones cure OK).</p>
<p local-id=3D"12ea6207-c25e-420a-8683-2ed2514ec09c">I have begun working o=
n the system that will enable the duct hose to rotate freely as the gantry =
moves while minimizing exposure of the bearing to powder. It will eventuall=
y ingest powder and seize, but between its rubber seals and the 3D printed =
guards/labyrinth seal I am making it should last for quite a while.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c6e4bc3-9804-4e0b-b076-7454014daf83">2026-01-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"28f2c46a-ee3d-4e70-bc65-012158a7a20e">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2fa06fd0-9750-42c5-ba6c-7d195215474e">I printed a Nalgene bo=
ttle interface for FLIP last night, but the opening for the splined shaft c=
lamps is a little too small so I am going to expand that and reprint the pa=
rt. The sides of the opening do not seal to the splined shaft clamp, it is =
the contacting face that seals with some wool, so it is not critical that t=
he counterbore in the Nalgene bottle holder be very close to the OD of the =
splined shaft clamp. I have modified the model by expanding the counterbore=
 from 25mm to 26mm.</p>
<p local-id=3D"28c8f95c-8a5e-47f7-85bc-4486ed8f2f51">The mold for the powde=
r dispenser vibration isolator is fully cured so I am going to try breaking=
 that out. The mold for the vibrating hat vibration isolator needs a third =
silicone pour to fill the mold to the top, so I will do that as long as I a=
m mixing up various casting compounds.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0852b801-596b-4993-b131-52afa83f3129">I removed the silicone=
 mold for the FLIP vibration isolator and installed 8X M4 screws holding 8X=
 M4 threaded inserts in place. I then mixed up some of the industrial grade=
 urethane and started a pour, but it reached a point where the urethane sto=
pped flowing through the funnel feature, so I filled up a syringe with uret=
hane and injected it under pressure, which worked for a while until the ure=
thane began leaking out the sides of the mold. I plan to let the urethane c=
ure then cut some more funnel features into the mold and try to finish the =
pour that way. Since there was some urethane left over at that point, I pou=
red it into the first mold I had made for the vibrating hat vibration isola=
tor as a test. Since that mold is missing two of its counterbore features i=
t will not be possible to use the part that is cast unless I freeze it in l=
iquid nitrogen and post-machine the counterbores, but it will give me a bet=
ter sense of how to do a successful cast with this urethane. I have the des=
ign of FLIP reasonably complete:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file=
-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=
=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260127-200315.pn=
g" width=3D"468" loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"21bca772f79f70eeb14a437fe9f27e70f1=
