<p local-id=3D"af0fc1923d27">Began working on assembling the machined compo=
nents of the hopper. I had to ream out the dowel pin holes by hand, grind d=
own the aluminum side plates where they were interfering with the gate clos=
ing fully, add in the screws to act as hard stops for the side bushings, st=
art alignment work and shimming of the gate and bushing mounts to get the g=
ate to move freely. I tried installing the cam holder on a stepper motor an=
d wound up destroying the stepper gear box shaft. The opening in the cam ho=
lder is too small, so I ground it out a bit, heated it up with an acetylene=
 torch, and tried to press it on to the shaft using a vise. I think the cam=
 holder cooled down as I was pressing it on, and I applied enough force wit=
h the vise that the gear box shaft ended up buckling.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5b220641ffcc">2026-02-04</p>
<p local-id=3D"1645c7eba0e5">At the morning standup Marcus announced that a=
t the long meeting yesterday that he had with Payman, Rahjan, and Alex, the=
y decided, among other things, that they want 2 more pilots built, at least=
 one of which will be dedicated to copper printing. I discussed with Senai =
what kinds of resources will be necessary to build these printers, and the =
rough estimate we came up with is that it will take about 4 people 3 months=
 to build them after the design is complete (Marcus also wants some design =
upgrades). Between Ning (full time Pilot), Cisco, Ben, me, Sean, and Senai,=
 we can probably scrape together 1 1/2 to 2 people equivalent without too m=
uch disruption to other projects, so optimistically it would take 6 months.=
 I think a realistic estimate is that without additional resources it would=
 take about 9 months to complete the design upgrades, order all the parts, =
and build and commission the printers. To cut down that time we would eithe=
r need to hire some additional people (which may or may not accelerate the =
project because they would have to be trained and I have failed to document=
 the printers well), or stop work on most R&amp;D efforts and production pr=
ojects (I suspect Accretech and other customer projects will take up all of=
 Sean=E2=80=99s time and some or all of Senai=E2=80=99s time), or we could =
contract the machine building to an external machine manufacturer (which wo=
uld have the added advantage of familiarizing them with our process so we c=
ould then contract them to make a productized pilot).</p>
<p local-id=3D"a095602d21e7">Senai and I also have questions about the nece=
ssity of this project. Having two more pilots would certainly be nice, but =
we don=E2=80=99t have resources to dedicate to it at moment and it is also =
an open question what exactly we are trying to accomplish as a company. Her=
e are some company objectives I can imagine:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"f40a9b072b70">
<li local-id=3D"03a16bda854a">
<p local-id=3D"1926156b7b3d">Make and sell more pilot printers more or less=
 of the current design</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"f9e828888e6b">
<p local-id=3D"73461d415358">Complete the R&amp;D projects and integrate a =
refined version of them with the pilots</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"f14db48d2ca9">
<p local-id=3D"63625e09531e">Complete the R&amp;D projects and create a new=
 printer platform with them designed into it</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a111d32f4332">
<p local-id=3D"43f44efaad01">Design and make a productized printer and slic=
er that we can sell to any customer</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"96be4f096757">
<p local-id=3D"2b61f317796c">Make printers to operate in-house to work as a=
 part and assembly manufacturer</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0e60f98565be">
<p local-id=3D"69568511041c">Focus fully on providing part and assembly ser=
vices using any technology that is most appropriate to the design; we would=
 purchase printers using other processes and continue to operate and refine=
 our printers. This would open up new market opportunities (such as printin=
g larger parts or printing in materials such as ceramics and aluminum or ti=
tanium that we cannot currently print on our platform) which would provide =
a more reliable revenue stream to help support the development of our print=
ers.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"500a685a9851">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e1db6f499e6">I plan to try to remove the cam holder from th=
e shaft and grind down its opening to enable it to fit on the second motor =
I have, but since I have destroyed one of the motors I need to either order=
 a new one or borrow one from Senai (I think I ordered the same model for t=
his hopper design that I had previously ordered for Mason=E2=80=99s that Se=
nai installed, and I think I ordered him two motors).</p>
<p local-id=3D"bb02b58e3c94">I finished post-processing the 3D printed wedg=
e, glued some wool to the bottom to act as a seal/wiper for the blade, and =
tried clamping the blade back on, but the gap for the wool seal is too smal=
l. When I tried clamping the blade back on, it was not possible to move it =
because the friction against the wool was too high. I have redesigned the 3=
D printed wedge and will print the new version now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"acd95ead7c17">The newly printed version will probably work b=
ut may be a little too tight, so just in case I am printing a new version t=
hat is .3mm looser. I have also wired both stepper motors to TMC2209 steppe=
r motor driver boards. I also modified a stepper motor shaft and the stainl=
ess steel cam holder to fit each other. This should enable me to begin cycl=
e testing tomorrow as soon as I wire up the ESP32 board to the stepper moto=
r drivers and the two endstop switches that detect whether the gate is full=
y open or closed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"42394f537551">2026-02-05</p>
<p local-id=3D"8f3a693aa1b5">Troubleshot the binder making machine with Ben=
 and Ning. The lower limit switch that the linear stage to which the mixer =
is mounted uses to detect when the mixer has reached the binder pitcher was=
 not tripping correctly. We first tested the limit switch function directly=
 and it seemed fine, so we then inspected the Tango board that the machine =
is using as its control system and ben found that connector for that limit =
switch had come loose. Ben reconnected it, and the machine seems functional=
 now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"99e18f9ec83e">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"63b31648773b">Wired up the TMC2209 stepper driver boards to =
the ESP32 and the two stepper motors and got them to work with the AccelSte=
pper library. The motors are both spinning now, although they are fairly sp=
eed limited. Part of that is that the AccelStepper library itself is speed =
limited, but it is also because the motors are geared down with a 100:1 gea=
rbox.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5dc15c48762a">Got some sample code working reading both limi=
t switches with the ezButton library. This has a built in debouncing functi=
on that I think may be necessary for the limit switches I am using.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f431b899672e">2026-02-06</p>
<p local-id=3D"b11f95b386f4">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"eac1d718251d">I finally have code opening and closing the ho=
pper, so I can start a cycle life test. One issue I noted is that due to th=
e design of the cam and the slot, it is possible for the motor to rotate co=
ntinuously, and if the cam travels to the other side of the slot then the d=
irection of rotation of the motor will cause the inverse of the gate open/c=
lose function; rotating clockwise will cause the gate to open if it is on o=
ne side of the slot but will cause it to close if the cam is on the other s=
ide of the slot. For the next iteration I would like the slot narrower and =
the motor installed off center so that the force applied is very close to t=
he center of the gate and the cam can only engage in one orientation so tha=
t the direction of rotation of the motor will always correlate with the dir=
ection of motion of the gate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"70460d69ce5c">I have the code running a cycle test up to 281=
 cycles now. The cycle time is pretty slow, about 10 seconds per full open-=
close cycle. I plan to leave it running over the weekend to see how it hold=
s up. The force the motor applies is pretty good though; it takes some effo=
rt to get the motor to stall.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1b28bb9277fc">After lunch the hopper is up to 810 cycles and=
 seems to be running fine.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d4454b028cf8">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"61612b45ac3f">I machined out a section of the Mest mount for=
 the three-stage mount for Pilot 6. That section could interfere with a par=
t of the Parker stage so it would limit the travel of the Mest unit. I also=
 mounted the 3D printed endstop contact part and installed 3X SHCS to act a=
s set screws to hold the Mest mount in place once it is installed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"70f85bda73bd">R&amp;D Weekly Meeting</p>
<p local-id=3D"58a832cab955">No Depowdering:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"d567af7c8b58">
<li local-id=3D"c8a2ed3f1343">
<p local-id=3D"77a25a14ba78">Used desiccant box, but it was still difficult=
 to separate the build from the build plate</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3eb6ead4ead5">
