tainless steel to make the vibrating sheet, and I will next install the vib=
rator motor so that the vibrating hat will be fully functional once I have =
the motor installed and bolt it all up.</p>
<p local-id=3D"859611abc83d"><strong>DRREDFATI:</strong></p>
<ul local-id=3D"e751a193ea13">
<li local-id=3D"e28907e7f446">
<p local-id=3D"ea7b02649ccc"><strong>Does this need to be done?</strong> Is=
 there a better way to achieve the same goals? Do the goals actually need t=
o be achieved at all? Are there commercially available options that seem ex=
pensive but would actually be cheaper when you factor in the time spent to =
make something in-house?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"452c4adfc006">
<p local-id=3D"f1b370362671"><strong>Requirements:</strong> What are the re=
quirements for the project? Is it an open-ended problem to solve, or is the=
re a specific approach baked into the project requirements? What are the qu=
antifiable metrics for success that, if achieved, will make the project con=
sidered to be a success? Is the intent to start the project without these r=
equirements, and generate formal requirements along the way? What is the ex=
act plan for doing so?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0a3c3bb9fa0e">
<p local-id=3D"ecfa24da5087"><strong>Resources:</strong> What are the resou=
rces available for the project (money, people, time, tools). Is it feasible=
 that the requirements can be met with the resources available? If not, wha=
t resources would be necessary to have a reasonable chance of meeting the r=
equirements?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"238fb9226e9e">
<p local-id=3D"e1798e01b274"><strong>Engineering:</strong> The high-level i=
deation and engineering (math, process selection, etc.) of a solution or ap=
proach, as well as determining how any solution or approach will meet the r=
equirements outlined above. If there is no way to quantifiably measure whet=
her an approach will meet the requirements, either the requirements or appr=
oach need to be refined.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0c8e521dd7f9">
<p local-id=3D"306da6069387"><strong>Design:</strong> The low-level design =
(CAD, drawings, numbers, etc.) specifically defining the approach.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2e2355a86e80">
<p local-id=3D"1a2b7a20796b"><strong>Fabrication:</strong> Fabricate the in=
dividual components and subassemblies of the system, as well as any compone=
nts necessary for testing the system or approach.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"46e0fc330c4c">
<p local-id=3D"b7e71bb40a8e"><strong>Assembly:</strong> Combine the individ=
ual components into a whole integrated system, and assemble whatever compon=
ents are necessary for testing the system or approach. Complete any program=
ming necessary for validating the system.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c048fbbca90b">
<p local-id=3D"4af1212f13fc"><strong>Test: </strong>Use the test protocol d=
etermined during the Engineering step to measure the system or approach aga=
inst the Requirements determined earlier.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c54a7d5f305b">
<p local-id=3D"2aaa44de1663"><strong>Iterate:</strong> If the system or app=
roach meets the requirements, the system is ready. If it does not meet the =
requirements, loop back as far as necessary (if there was an issue with tes=
ting, you might just need to loop back to Test, if the results raise ideas =
for alternative approaches or questions about whether this approach is feas=
ible, it may be necessary to loop back to the start of the process and rest=
art or cancel the project as appropriate). Don=E2=80=99t fall for the sunk =
cost fallacy! No matter how much time or effort has been spent, it always m=
ight be wise to scrap everything and change the approach or abandon the pro=
ject entirely.</p></li>
</ul>
