<p local-id=3D"b35d0a9e13ad">HOPPER/FLIP: I need to redesign the vibration =
isolator; having SHCSs bolt through the urethane is not structurally sound =
enough to hold up in operation with the load applied by the hose. It would =
work for a while, but it would not seal well over time. I need to redesign =
the isolator, both for the hopper=E2=80=99s vibrating hat and also the cart=
ridge dock. I also have an idea for casting the dosing roller from urethane=
 for the cartridge; I think that would hold up better over time while maint=
aining a better seal as compare with a 3D printed part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e02b9e7379b">I created a new design, printed the components=
 necessary, and did a pour, although I=E2=80=99m not sure how well it will =
come out; I am concerned about bubbles in the pour. I modified the aluminum=
 brackets that interface the Edmund stages to the Y plate and received the =
new low-profile M3 screws then bolted it all up, and it seems OK. I had to =
do some adjustment to get everything square but that=E2=80=99s not a surpri=
se.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3ad98d6c6a94">2026-02-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"b6be15ae85cc">PILOT: I have all the parts in for assembling =
the brass X axis cable tower hose fitting.</p>
<p local-id=3D"58d991603a8d">I assembled the fittings and the 3D printed pa=
rts for 2X hose fitting assemblies.</p>
<p local-id=3D"236b12055600">Since flexing of the 3D printed endstop mounts=
 on the 3 stage Z axis mount is a critical issue, I have designed a sheet m=
etal mount and sent the quote into our ordering system. I would also like t=
o look at how fast the stages are moving when they are homing, because the =
force required to trigger the endstops=E2=80=A6 So I looked up the force re=
quired to trigger the endstops, and it is 1N (102g on Earth) at a minimum s=
peed of 50mm/min. 100g is actually not insignificant for a relatively thin =
3D printed part, even one that I oriented to be as strong as possible. It i=
s printed from PETG-CF, which is fairly strong and more rigid than PETG, bu=
t not as rigid as .125=E2=80=9D thick 304SS (which is what I plan to replac=
e it with).</p>
<p local-id=3D"bb4ab49c12b8">HOPPER/FLIP: The new vibrating hat has been pr=
inted; this one is adapted for the new vibration isolator. I removed the mo=
ld walls from the vibration isolator I cast yesterday. There were a few voi=
ds as expected, and the urethane that had overflowed the pour points had pl=
ugged many of the printed holes that are necessary for passing through scre=
ws and bolting on the vibrating hat, but the urethane adhesion to the 3D pr=
inted parts seems good; When removing the mold walls I had to slice off the=
 urethane because it adhered so well, and those were just flat surfaces wit=
hout the dovetails that I had printed into the permanently attached compone=
nts. I am printing new top and bottoms parts as well as mold walls, and I p=
lan to print some funnels that will help to avoid the voids and eliminate t=
he overflow. I also plan to spray the mold walls with mold release to hopef=
ully make it easier to remove them with the next pour.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a674c4edf8c0">I assembled a new set of top and bottom parts =
and mold walls and did another pour with Shore 44A urethane. I had two funn=
els on top to theoretically pour into one funnel, have the urethane flow do=
wn that side of the mold, and flow up the other side, preventing the format=
ion of voids within the casting, but even with the mold tilted to one side,=
 the urethane started flowing down both sides of the mold, potentially trap=
ping air within. I did a couple cycle of vacuum degassing to hopefully clea=
r out any voids. For the next mold, I plan to have a removable barrier bloc=
king flow from one side to the other at the top of the mold so that the ure=
thane will be forced to travel down one side and up the other.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8821a6033478">I bolted down the new linear gate hopper yeste=
rday, but I then removed it to make the gate more visible as I develop macr=
os for opening and closing the gate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f5cbfd0b1cc1">I have finished macros for opening and closing=
 the gate, called GATEOPEN and GATECLOSE. They each work by moving the gate=
 until it hits the appropriate limit switch. Because the printer is wired t=
o use only one limit switch connection for the hopper axis, the limit switc=
hes are wired in parallel, so the Duet board cannot distinguish between the=
 two switches. This means that the limit switches need to be wired as norma=
lly open, so I had to rewire my switches, which I had originally wired as n=
ormally closed per standard 3D printer practice. If I had wanted to stick w=
ith the normally closed switches, I could have rewired the board so that th=
e switches were instead wired in parallel, and pressing either switch would=
 break the connection. Since the duet board has no way to distinguish betwe=
en the two switches when one of them is pressed, when starting either macro=
 it is impossible for the board to tell whether the gate is already open or=
 closed. I designed the macros so that they will first ignore the limit swi=
tches and travel in the appropriate direction for the macro called, assumin=
g they are in the right spot, at a slow speed for a short distance; if they=
 are in the expected position this will take them off the limit switch and =
the next command is to move them faster until they hit the other limit swit=
ch. If they are not in the expected position (i.e. you run the GATEOPEN mac=
ro when the gate is already open) the gate will ignore the limit switches, =
crash into the hard stop for a short time, then will start paying attention=
 to the limit switches and stop. By having it start out at a slower speed, =
the likelihood of damage is reduced, but it would still be good to avoid ca=
lling the wrong macro.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e485adef07c2">PILOT 8 and 9 Brainstorming Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"3e699fbd39f6">Goal is to make 2 more Pilots that are similar=
 to 6 and 7 with minor upgrades. One will be dedicated to copper and one wi=
ll be for general use and R&amp;D. Per Marcus, these machines will not be d=
edicated to laser use.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8af903d33af5">Epson upgrade requests: Touch sensor for level=
ing build plate (?) and closed-loop stepper motors.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f93193af9d2a">Timeline is 4 months for these 2 and a quarter=
ly cadence afterwards.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f07dd658a530">I recommend making a larger control cabinet th=
at can fit all the additional subsystems, such as the Mest controller.</p>
<p local-id=3D"62a91b60ca45">For the copper machine, it will need robust cl=
imate control. Alex recommends switching from the THC to the system that wa=
s implemented on the copper Saffron because it has been proven to work with=
 printing copper. The machine will also need improved sealing and inertabil=
ity. It may be better to put all the copper machines in one tent instead of=
 split between multiple tents as they are right now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"442bb0847f9e">Goal is to minimize impact on engineering time=
 and resources. Week 1/2: gather requirements, clean up BOM, take inventory=
 of parts in-house, assess possible machine changes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"54c3494e722e">Week 3: Begin design of new parts, source/orde=
r long lead time items.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3aa937d1fd84">Labor to build pilots to TBD, Marcus looking t=
o hire another technician but has no candidates yet. Marcus is also open to=
 the possibility of hiring interns, which Mason suggested.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1fd41c9290eb">Weekly R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"cb97d30eb3d6">No Depowering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8933e9579932">Made a list of desired refurb tasks for Pilot =
1 while it is not being used. Made a plan to make the AEM NDP part in coppe=
r, but has not been reviewed yet. Printed a 2 layer AEM print in copper OK,=
 but when a 10 layer print was attempted about 30% of features were picked =
up by the vacuum. Pourya plans to adjust binder volume, vacuum power, and d=
rying time to resolve this issue. Finished moving the Mest unit from Pilot =
1 to Pilot 6. Mohsen will work on improving base layer adhesion, after whic=
h test printing will begin. Still having issues with vacuum power, and the =
dry air curing process tends to lower the chamber humidity to unacceptable =
levels. Mohsen will look into adding a humidifier to add humidity. Corner o=
f the cake had been breaking on Pilot 1, this was not resolved due to time =
spent on base layer development. Larger end mills for facing the print afte=
r filler application were ordered but have not yet been tested. Mohsen note=
s that dry air curing can cause the base layer to detach from the build pla=
te.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d1ce04de0130">I pitched a concept of pre-building a layer on=
 a removable build plate using a slurry or a pre-coated powder. Pourya did =
an experiment with spraying a build plate with hairspray which helped for t=
he first layer but subsequent layers did not adhere well. Another approach =
might be to apply PEG or another water-soluble polymer as a binder to the b=
ase plate, which should help our water-based binder attach well.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f4cc93d16fca">Hopper:</p>
