<p local-id=3D"707eeb11cbee">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"5cf95de949ff">Demolded the new vibration isolator; the mold =
release applied to the 3D printed walls made it massively easier to remove =
them; I highly recommend it in the future. I counterbored the relevant hole=
s for screwing it to the hopper body, and it fit well. I also installed all=
 the necessary nyloc nuts in the vibrating hat, so I just need to make the =
vibrating sheet and complete final assembly for the system to be fully func=
tional.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5c167361784b">I made the vibrating sheet, but the holes I dr=
illed were in the wrong place because I changed the hole positions for the =
new vibration isolator, so I superglued the sheet into the vibrating hat fo=
r Mohsen=E2=80=99s initial testing. I also had to tweak the macros a bit to=
 get it working robustly; if you send it to one end of travel and then send=
 it to the same end again, it will overtravel enough that it needs to trave=
l farther back the other way to clear the limit switch when it is commanded=
 to go the other direction. This has the downside of stalling for a longer =
period of time if it is commanded to go to the position in which it current=
ly is. With the vibration motor currently installed, the vibration isolator=
 seems to be working quite well. The vibrating hat noticeably vibrates when=
 the motor is turned on and seems structurally rigid enough to mount the ho=
se to, but there is no noticeable vibration when you touch the machined hop=
per. The only open question, then, is how much vibration is actually requir=
ed to liquifact the powder, and whether the metal sheet I have made and ins=
talled can couple the appropriate amplitude and frequency of vibration to l=
iquifact the powder so it flows well out of the hopper onto the layer. This=
 will be determined by Mohsen=E2=80=99s testing (hopefully tomorrow). One t=
hing to note as well is that the vibration motor cover cracked as I was tig=
htening it down; it is intentionally designed to clamp tightly onto the mot=
or, but this particular version seems to be overly tight, leading to the pa=
rt becoming overstressed when the mounting bolts were tightened. I believe =
I have already corrected this in the CAD, but it will need to be tested whe=
n I make the benchtop dummy hopper for loading testing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e6a7f67ddc5b">Mohsen expressed concerns with the low vibrati=
on on the new hopper, so I have ordered several vibration motors of differe=
nt sizes (all larger than the one currently installed) that I will then int=
egrate into the design if necessary. It may be necessary to eliminate the t=
wo mounting holes one either side of the vibration motor that are used to s=
crew the vibrating hat onto the vibration isolator upper plate, but that wi=
ll leave 6 mounting holes so the mount should be strong enough. Also, since=
 the new vibration isolator design has rigid mounts on the top and bottom, =
it is less critical for there to be a large number of mounting screws (alth=
ough to be fair, the 10 mounting screws on the last design were still insuf=
ficient to clamp a counterbored unreinforced rubber vibration isolator even=
ly anyway).</p>
<p local-id=3D"37de8182a81e">I am making a 3D printed positive mold to make=
 a silicone mold to cast urethane doser balls. It is my hope that these wil=
l provide improved sealing and wear capabilities as compared with PETG-CF o=
r SLA resin doser balls.</p>
