for future SLA printed doser balls I will adjust the splined cutout dimensi=
ons.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c76bbb7ac4c1">I completed the design for the new vibration i=
solator for the cartridge dock based on the design I made for the vibrating=
 hat vibration isolator, printed out the components, sealed the outside wit=
h hot glue, and attempted a pour of 744 soft urethane. Even with a funnel o=
n top the pressure of the poured urethane was clearly insufficient to force=
 it down one side of the mold and up the other within the pot life of the u=
rethane. I also used a blocking tab to force it to flow down one side only =
first instead of down both sides so that there would be no voids trapped at=
 the bottom of the mold, which mostly seemed to work, although there was a =
little leakage past it from the pressure of the urethane in the funnel. The=
re are two possible alternative approaches I have thought of so far:</p>
<p local-id=3D"774047f470f3">First, I could reprint the mold with just an i=
njection hole in one side and an air outlet hole in the other side, then us=
e a syringe to inject urethane in while the mold is oriented so the injecti=
on hole faces down. This replaces the gravity-fed injection of urethane wit=
h a pressure-fed approach, and the bottom-up injection will let the mold fi=
ll both sides at once instead of needing to go down one side and up the oth=
er. However, it would require filling a syringe, likely more than once, and=
 managing bubbles in the syringe which would naturally tend to rise into th=
e mold during injection. I am also not sure just how much pressure would be=
 required to inject the urethane, which could make it physically difficult =
to inject and extend the time to an unreasonable level.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1088a20429cc">Second, I could lay the mold flat with one of =
the permanently attached sides (upper or lower) removed, fill the mold, the=
n press the upper of lower side into the mold. This would result in urethan=
e flowing out around the mold, which would of course adhere to everything a=
nd would be somewhat of a pain to remove, but the pouring aspect would be v=
ery easy since all of the mold is exposed and it would make it unnecessary =
to add any cutouts into the mold sides.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7965c01e7154">I=E2=80=99ve talked myself into it; I=E2=80=99=
m going to try the second approach. I=E2=80=99ll douse all the external sur=
faces with mold release to reduce the tendency of urethane to stick and red=
esign the mold so it does not have any features that are no longer necessar=
y for this process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"80f7084122c5">Mohsen noted that there is a line appearing on=
 the cake with the new hopper design, and asked for the spreader bar thickn=
ess (10mm, for reference, also 140mm long) to try and track down the root c=
ause.</p>
<p local-id=3D"18c3845faabd">I assembled a powder cartridge with the unders=
ize doser ball I printed previously. Even with the many possible engagement=
