<p local-id=3D"4c4fb18fe9b0">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"938585375e43">Scaling the V block I previously made for Dong=
 for Keyence measurement of the sintered IHI thruster; the scaled version i=
s needed for measuring the green part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"61b63f324da5">The scaled V block has been printed and cleane=
d with IPA, it just needs to be cured and have the supports removed to be r=
eady for testing. I suspect that its dimensions will need to be modified at=
 least once more, but that=E2=80=99s easy enough to do.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1b43e85e988c">Marcus noted concerns with the amount of vibra=
tion seen when Pilot 7 was doing repeated small cuts while printing an AEM =
part. In particular, he is concerned about loss of cutting accuracy (possib=
le but not necessarily the case) and the fact that customers may not like h=
ow much the machine vibrates in operation. Machine vibration is a well-know=
n side effect of two major design choices: using a lightweight frame and ba=
se plate, and having all components move together on the same gantry while =
cutting. To the first point, using a heavier welded frame and/or a thicker =
stainless steel base plate would help, while a granite base plate would be =
even more accurate, precise, and reduce vibrations. To the second point, wi=
th the current hardware vibration could be reduced by adjusting acceleratio=
n settings and reducing cutting speed, or to have a really significant impa=
ct, have the cutting components on their own more compact precision gantry =
and mount the spreader bar, mist box, heater, and hopper on their own singl=
e-axis less precise gantry. One issue with this is if we want to use the th=
ree-stage two axis/Mest unit assembly, there would still be a fair amount o=
f weight on the precision gantry, necessitating a larger gantry than if the=
re was only a single spindle mounted.</p>
<p local-id=3D"690f7d54796f">Devin noted that print edges curling up from t=
he edge of the build plate is a known failure mode due to low humidity, as =
seen in the pod back when we first moved here from Gardena and is the reaso=
n why there were humidifiers in the pod. Devin recommends warming the air b=
efore injecting it into the chamber. He also notes that when a green part i=
s cured with dry air instead of heat it becomes more hygroscopic. He recomm=
ends that maintaining control of the temperature and moisture content of th=
e cake is important to print quality and repeatability. He mainly pays atte=
ntion to telemetry, such as temperature, humidity, and layer time.</p>
<p local-id=3D"751dca4cac79">Are we using different firmware on the Pilot s=
pray driver vs the Saffron spray drivers?</p>
<p local-id=3D"4da96c2eeceb">I sent out my Inkscape version of the patent d=
iagram, and Marcus stated that he was more interested in one that is less s=
tylized, closer to a CAD model, and specifically requested the addition of =
a hopper to show that we have multiple ways of applying powder to the build=
, so I am going to try to create a CAD screenshot showing this. I personall=
y do not think that makes sense for a patent application, but to be fair, m=
ost of the patents I have looked at have been from several years old to man=
y years old, for the most part not within the last few years, so I am not s=
ure what is currently in vogue for patent applications.</p>
