<p local-id=3D"f526267c0337">Finished hopper post-processing, installed and=
 wired up the vibrator motors, installed all the necessary heat-set inserts=
, cast a new hopper vibration isolator, and began wiring up the control box=
. I tested the the new vibrator motors, and I feel pretty good about the vi=
brator on the vibrating hat but I am a little concerned that the motor on t=
he cartridge dock may not be powerful enough (it is the same motor, but the=
 mass it is vibrating is greater). I may need to upgrade to the larger vibr=
ator motor, and likely mount it in a vertical orientation (perpendicular to=
 the current motor=E2=80=99s orientation).</p>
<p local-id=3D"380f40a271a5">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e122e425e73a">The sheet metal parts for the more rigid 3-sta=
ge endstop design were delivered today, but they need some 3D printed parts=
 to be designed and made before they can be installed (the current 3D print=
ed parts on the machine are designed for a 3D printed endstop mount and so =
are not tall enough for the thinner sheet metal part).</p>
<p local-id=3D"5b026cbb5ab2">I have redesigned the 3D printed parts and sta=
rted them printing on one of the Bambu printers. I also measured the sheet =
metal part to make sure that the hole sizes are correct.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fad071fce43c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"2a480a6daa22"><strong>Laser meeting (Definitive Screening Te=
st Design):</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"ac4c5e39fc9e">Understand effects of different factors on las=
er =E2=80=9Cablation=E2=80=9D behavior. Seems like the goal is more to esta=
blish which primary variables are important, and quantify how important the=
y are, under baseline conditions.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"9e3ffebb6464">
<li local-id=3D"e84f7f626322">
<p local-id=3D"33a021b6ddcb">Per Payman, objective is not to determine fund=
amental mechanism of observed behavior</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"102e3bce563f">Controlled parameters: laser power, scan rate,=
 frequency, pulse width, number of passes, binder volume</p>
<p local-id=3D"fc70539f06d1">Covariates: environmental (temp, humidity), ne=
bulizer flow rate, powder batch</p>
<p local-id=3D"5702bbdb1bbd">Blocking factor: Day</p>
<p local-id=3D"7ffd319a6188">Payman suggested replacing number of passes wi=
th binder concentration, which I think is a significant variation from our =
current process and is more suited to an optimization experiment rather tha=
n a baseline experiment.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ff9f4325f78f">30 brix is max concentration the nebulizers ca=
n handle, but sugar builds up rapidly.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2f81f9950acf">Marcus noted that it is possible for pulses to=
 overlap, depending on spot size, scan rate, pulse frequency, and pulse wid=
th (note that if the spot size is infinitesimally small, it would not be po=
ssible to overlap as long as the dot is being moved continuously, but with =
a finite spot size it is possible to overlap).</p>
<p local-id=3D"daaf112aa7f9">Procedure is:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"915afbe94b7d">
<li local-id=3D"3c1ce7b1db0f">
<p local-id=3D"8ee65f941d1a">Create base layer</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a0893a0005a1">
<p local-id=3D"96305311e76b">Do three layers with set laser parameters</p><=
/li>
<li local-id=3D"d738cb102249">
<p local-id=3D"17d93cbd3052">Remove print and measure results at layer 3</p=
></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"fdab31fa2600">Mason pointed out that he has had trouble cutt=
ing through a bound layer to reach loose powder, which Payman pointed out i=
s necessary for our printing process to work at all. My recommendation is t=
o first determine whether it is possible for the laser to cut through a bou=
nd layer to reach loose powder cleanly at all, because if that is not possi=
ble then the process is not viable.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7e209fe6c757">IDEA: Maybe measure binder penetration by repe=
atedly firing short pulses at the same spot and monitoring the laser spot i=
n some way (visible light camera? IR camera?) and seeing how many pulses it=
 takes for the laser spot appearance to change.</p>
<p local-id=3D"74052319cce0">Marcus pointed out that it may not be necessar=
y to cleanly cut, we might just need to locally debind the layer (denaturin=
g the binder without kicking out the powder).</p>
<p local-id=3D"f1a1c01244af">IDEA: What if we intentionally sintered the pa=
rt dimensions, while also binding some layers to use that as a support to e=
nable us to directly print metal parts with a lower overhang angle than ave=
rage LPBF processes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1d74f3435189">Laser spot diameter is estimated to be ~50 mic=
rons.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f8632f8a872a">I pointed out that it may be necessary to cut =
into loose powder for this process to be viable, which it seems that it is =
unable to do as it stands. Sean pointed out that we don=E2=80=99t necessari=
ly need to cut into into the dry powder, we just need to cut TO the dry pow=
der, but it does need to be able to do so without damaging the dry powder (=
Mason sees =E2=80=9Csintering=E2=80=9D of the powder when the laser hits dr=
y powder using current settings, and so only gets clean cuts when cutting t=
hrough bound powder).</p>
<p local-id=3D"6254b8e78a4d">Payman has noted behavior on the Pilot ultra p=
latform where he would intentionally =E2=80=9Csinter=E2=80=9D features whic=
h would curl up and be easy to blow away.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5e656f7b7970">Payman wants to run the test cutting into one =
layer on top of loose powder, while Mason wants to run 3 layers on top of e=
ach other. Payman=E2=80=99s point is that the behavior will change with mul=
tiple layers, while Mason wants to run a test that is more representative o=
f actual prints.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4e6891b4895d">Data Collection: Kerf width/depth, environment=
al conditions (temp, humidity), debris field width, nebulizer flow rate, po=
wder batch (also recommend recording time of day that test is run)</p>
<p local-id=3D"efc574a40192">Payman pointed out that using the LVP may cure=
 the layer and affect the results, but Mason pointed out that results are l=
ess reliable when the LVP is not used.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1705d18001ed">2026-02-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"08407da5935d">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"fdd322c32cf5">Demolded the hopper vibration isolator I made =
yesterday. Since I failed to spray the walls with mold release before casti=
ng, it was difficult to remove them. I found that the cleanest way to remov=
e them was by slicing them off with a scalpel instead of pulling them off; =
with mold release I think pulling them off works fine but without it the ur=
ethane adheres to the printed part strongly enough that the urethane rips a=
s I am removing the walls. I think from a functional perspective the rips a=
ren=E2=80=99t important, but they look bad cosmetically.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f78ff23b9856">After setting up the dummy hopper on the hoppe=
r testbed, I realized that it will be very difficult to empty out the dummy=
 hopper once it is full of powder, so I am redesigning it with a sliding ga=
te on the bottom and threaded holes to connect it to a funnel that can be c=
onnected directly to a Nalgene bottle.</p>
<p local-id=3D"716d03420248">I designed a new dummy hopper with provisions =
for a manually removable gate as well as an attached funnel that connects t=
o a nalgene bottle for dropping down powder build up in the dummy hopper.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"ce9fb64c2f9a">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"4591fc15b34c">I finished a CAD-style patent drawing for the =
no-depowdering patent by making a new SolidWorks assembly, and I also added=
 the hopper and heater to the standard patent drawing style drawing I have =
been working on in Inkscape. Per Claude, it seems that there are restrictio=
ns on the style of drawings on patent applications; they generally need to =
be black-and-white line drawings so the patent can be printed easily. It is=
 possible to get permission to use color or grayscale drawings, but it requ=
ires special authorization that is only granted when the color is critical =
to the patent, such as for certain biological assays and whatnot, generally=
 not for style reasons alone.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wr=
