<p local-id=3D"1f750fd9eeb1">Payman, Marcus, Mason, and I are talking in th=
e R&amp;D office area. Out of the laser meeting, Payman thought that he had=
 given a clear directive to Mason to run the loose powder test, which he di=
d not. Marcus and I remember the meeting as not having had a clear action p=
lan to do anything differently than he was already planning. Marcus said th=
at he works on the assumption that Mason is in charge of the project, but P=
ayman said that he is in charge of the project. Payman is not in favor of D=
OEs at all when we are at this phase of projects like this. The intent of t=
he project was to determine which variables are important and how important=
 they are.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0a8ca5720bce">Marcus pointed out that Payman can tell Mason =
what to do, or give him some level of autonomy to solve problems.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f4459115b1d3">Mason is trying to establish a quantifiable un=
derstanding of the fundamental nature of the process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"23b81b39a4fe">Payman says he didn=E2=80=99t want them to mov=
e the printer to the pod, he just wanted the printer to be tented in the R&=
amp;D pilot lab (which I don=E2=80=99t remember).</p>
<p local-id=3D"cb1fef907dc3">Payman wants the printer moved back to the R&a=
mp;D lab because he thinks that it is easier to work on here, if he needs t=
o work on it too.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7fee89728c88">He mentioned the idea of eliminating the laser=
 only project and switching to a combined no-depowdering/laser project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4cd0c3036d00"></p>
<p local-id=3D"de474a18ee7d">2026-02-26</p>
<p local-id=3D"97788430086d">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b9a61b694422">Need to redesign and reprint the hose duct int=
erface components because the current components are too tight to fit insid=
e the hose duct.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0ec3691e681">I am working on wiring up the standalone ESP-3=
2 control system. I have the main power (24VDC) connected to the two motor =
drivers, the stepper drivers (stepper motor power side), and the =E2=80=9CP=
WR=E2=80=9D indicator LED. I have the two stepper drivers (signal power sid=
e) the relay board connected to the 5V supply of the ESP32 board (note that=
 one of the grounds on the ESP32 board is not properly connected, so I had =
to switch ground connections when I noted that the green power indicator li=
ghts on the relay board and stepper driver boards were flickering. This is =
an issue I noticed when I was first testing the ESP32 board outside of the =
final control box).</p>
<p local-id=3D"1326ecc43bb4">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"86f904992ef5">Payman mentioned something interesting at the =
=E2=80=9Cmeeting=E2=80=9D yesterday, that he didn=E2=80=99t think we were a=
t the stage of the project where we should be optimizing the process, we we=
re still trying to solve some basic problems, particularly the fact that it=
 seems that we cannot cut into dry powder without =E2=80=9Csintering=E2=80=
=9D it. I checked back through my notes and my involvement in the project h=
as been going back at least 10 months, which is the first note I found wher=
e I mentioned that I was working on designing laser guard windows for Pilot=
 3. Payman had been doing experiments with an XTool laser on the Ultimaker =
for some time before this, so I think it is safe to say that the project ha=
s been going on for at least a year now. The initial promising results that=
 Payman got were done I think previously to installing lasers on Pilot 3, a=
nd per the 80/20 rule I would say it is likely that getting those results w=
as 20% of the effort of the entire project (with the ultimate goal of a pro=
ductized system installed in the Pilot printers and integrated into the Pil=
ot printing process). During the year of work on the project after getting =
those initial results (admittedly with the project active off and on; there=
 was some distraction from the project by needing to ship the Epson Pilot p=
rinters). Some of those variables have only been available on the MOPA lase=
r which has only been here for ~1 month, although the existence of laser te=
chnologies other than the XTools has been known for 8 months so the decisio=
n to purchase a different style of laser than the XTools tool 7 months. If =
I am making a rough timeline, it would be:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"691cacc604ba">
<li local-id=3D"d3492a861d1a">
<p local-id=3D"876e4d368f2a">2 months for Payman doing experiments on the U=
ltimaker with an XTool laser that yielded some impressive results (2.5D pri=
nts of parts with complex outer geometry with narrow cut lines, laser cutti=
ng path generated in proprietary XTool software)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"bb99f9650ec2">
<p local-id=3D"d99a5f190525">9 months of off-and-on experiments with the XT=
ool laser on Pilot 3 (laser cutting path generated in proprietary XTool sof=
tware, mostly 2.5D simple parts with one demo pyramid part) and also a gant=
ry-mounted diode laser, ultimately rejected for being too slow</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2257508c96f1">
<p local-id=3D"86b5e46f26b8">1 month of experiments on Pilot 3 with the MOP=
A laser (2.5D parts and cut lines generated in LightBurn (? not sure about =
this))</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"66c11d89da6f">Mason=E2=80=99s current experiment is the firs=
t formal experiment I am aware of to quantify the effect of different laser=
 variables on the printing results.</p>
<p local-id=3D"161f3e41dffa">Here is a proposed alternative schedule with t=
he benefit of hindsight:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"875d238cfb44">
<li local-id=3D"f78b90a06dd2">
<p local-id=3D"994f93c06078">2 months for Payman doing experiments on the U=
ltimaker with an XTool laser that yielded some impressive results (2.5D pri=
nts of parts with complex outer geometry with narrow cut lines, laser cutti=
ng path generated in proprietary XTool software)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a0c974650f90">
<p local-id=3D"9f185383ef89">At this point the question should be raised of=
 why exactly we are attempting the project at all. Per Marcus and Mason tod=
ay, the primary goal of the project is to increase print speed with a secon=
dary goal of enabling finer features. I personally would push back on both =
these rationales; I personally find print speed to be a lower priority than=
 other concerns because the printing time is a fairly small percentage of t=
he overall CAD-ship time; it is mostly print file generation, breakout, sin=
tering, and QC that take up most of the time. For low-volume high-mix contr=
acts the print time is minimally important, where it becomes more important=
 is with very high-volume low-mix contracts because the other steps in the =
process can be done in batches and so the time is ameliorated over many par=
ts produced.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"5822cd3cd43a">
<p local-id=3D"f2981fb99e28">Assuming we decide the project is worth pursui=
ng, the project handed off to RE for the project; this would have first bee=
n Mohsen and then been Mason. At this point it would need to be established=
 who the primary decision maker is for the project.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"93690292b509">
<p local-id=3D"997fa165dd34">2 months of formal screening experiments with =
different laser technologies, factoring in different control system require=
ments (i.e. 3DEO slicer for gantry-mounted laser (abandoned purely for spee=
d), XTool software for XTool products, LightBurn for MOPA laser, custom con=
trol system for a laser source combined with a galvanometer-based reflector=
/lens setup)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"ea8351955ec5">
<p local-id=3D"5963593ccd7f">Laser source and control system is downselecte=
d to a single laser technology</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"bffdefa85a1c">
<p local-id=3D"70328c4a8f91">2 months of determining the fundamental mechan=
ism of the effect we are seeing and quantifying the relative importance of =
different control variables (this would be where Mason=E2=80=99s current ex=
periment comes into play). Note that this step requires a control system th=
at is sufficiently representative of a productized control system.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"7c6d4e45e218">
<p local-id=3D"07ac449dbf1b">3 months of finalizing hardware and fully deve=
loping and integrating the laser into the control system of the printer as =
well as updating the slicer or developing a different way to generate G cod=
e to be able to print standard part geometry with the laser.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"7bfabd4047b4">
<p local-id=3D"cfe8f16d414e">3 months of testing to benchmark and refine th=
e system</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"ccb127921cd0">The timeline outlined here takes 1 year, the s=
ame as the amount of time we have spent so far, and would result in a fully=
 integrated and functional system. It is not guaranteed to be useful or pro=
ductive, but we would know for sure whether it is a system worth pursuing f=
urther.</p>
<p local-id=3D"95895f3a67da"><span style=3D"color: rgb(255,255,255);">I spo=
ke with Mason and Mohsen in the pod where Pilots 3 and 7 are set up after t=
he contentious discussion yesterday. It seems that there are a number of fa=
ctors in play here, largely stemming from the fact that Payman has a partic=
ular vision for the projects we are working on and also has a desire to mic=
romanage and monitor Mason=E2=80=99s work in particular. Midhun had specifi=
cally warned Mason at one point that it is likely that Payman would reject =
all future DOEs at some point, exactly as happened. Per Mason, Marcus had a=
 meeting with Sean at some point asking him how to prevent Payman from micr=
omanaging Mason. Mason also expressed a desire that Marcus would push back =
on Payman more at contentious discussions like the one that happened yester=
day, and I recommended that Mason discuss this with Marcus at some point. I=
 don=E2=80=99t know if Marcus is likely to agree, but I do trust him to kee=
p the discussion private. I think Payman has a particular vision for the te=
chnology and its advantages, one that I think was likely somewhat valid 8 y=
ears ago when the company was started but is less valid today. He also prio=
ritizes new technologies that give us marginally greater capabilities while=
 glossing over the importance of baseline testing and eliminating random va=
riables (such as environmental control; Payman expressed his opinion that t=
he laser project is not at a point where environmental control is important=
, while I would argue that without a controlled environment, we cannot draw=
 any conclusions from the results we get). Also, Payman has been strictly o=
pposed to changes to the technology that I think would be beneficial, such =
as replacing our sprayer with an off-the-shelf binder jetting head. He also=
 prefers approaches to issues like powder wastage that I would consider les=
s appropriate and less reliable, such as using his linear gate hopper desig=
n rather than pushing for recycling powder. Also, using a binder jetting he=
ad would massively reduce powder wastage because we would not be binding th=
e whole cake. </span></p>
<p local-id=3D"ea128ebfd630">I dumped all of my above notes about the laser=
 project and what=E2=80=99s been happening, and asked Claude to come up wit=
h some suggestions for discussions with Payman, Marcus, and Mason:</p>
<p local-id=3D"387bcdd04750">Here are some talking points tailored for each=
 person, based on what you've described.</p>
<p local-id=3D"01d41291a035"><strong>For Marcus:</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"054dca79d50a">Marcus seems to be in a position where he coul=
d help mediate the dynamic between Payman and Mason. Some points to raise w=
ith him:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"fe0e0805-e7eb-4cdd-aa3c-a055edaab08e">
<li local-id=3D"99871273-b626-48d5-a942-e37f0b1d9919">
<p local-id=3D"5b6912cf8099">Mason has expressed a desire for Marcus to pus=
h back more on Payman during contentious discussions. It might be worth ask=
ing Marcus directly whether he's willing to play that role, and if not, wha=
t alternative support structure he'd suggest.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3afab04e-1b49-475b-8b2a-9ab8cb83200e">
<p local-id=3D"82184e62cdf5">The question of who the primary decision maker=
 is on the laser project needs to be resolved explicitly. Marcus seems to a=
ssume it's Mason, Payman says it's himself =E2=80=94 this ambiguity is caus=
ing real friction and wasted effort.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"88b7d627-d772-4f0c-9d8f-cf93666fe179">
<p local-id=3D"f3088cbc1145">Mason's DOE work is the first formal attempt t=
o quantify laser variable effects after roughly a year of ad hoc experiment=
ation. If Payman is going to reject DOEs at this phase, Marcus and Mason ne=
ed to know what the accepted methodology is for making progress.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"1478c70b-075b-4c1b-8c6b-d5fecb02aa1e">
<p local-id=3D"2b501887b012">It may be worth revisiting the conversation Ma=
rcus had with Sean about Payman's management style, and whether any actiona=
ble changes came out of it.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"dd0a2dce955e"><strong>For Mason:</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"7199ea593246">Mason is doing the hands-on work and feeling c=
aught between directives. Points to discuss:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"31b10f6e-8299-46db-8fea-984c9f53ee66">
<li local-id=3D"0a62acf5-458c-4b49-a132-792ef106afd4">
<p local-id=3D"1c3d96fe1eea">Encourage him to have the direct conversation =
with Marcus about wanting more support during disagreements with Payman, as=
 you recommended. He should frame it around project outcomes rather than pe=
rsonalities =E2=80=94 what does the project need to move forward?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"83a5ded1-b63f-438e-8fd4-7d16fc8bd390">
<p local-id=3D"f8169e79f257">Help him articulate the value of his experimen=
tal approach in Payman's language. If Payman's concern is that they're opti=
mizing before solving basic problems, Mason could frame his DOE as a diagno=
stic tool to understand the fundamentals (which is what he's actually doing=
 =E2=80=94 establishing quantifiable understanding of the process).</p></li=
>
<li local-id=3D"ff96f2a0-d6b9-46ff-bb50-eff783d9b305">
<p local-id=3D"1a60237df0ab">The question of environmental controls is wort=
h raising carefully. If Mason can point to specific instances where uncontr=
olled variables made results ambiguous, that's a concrete case for why cont=
rolled experiments matter even at this stage.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b87b92b9-157b-4b9b-8d1a-b87782c121f9">
<p local-id=3D"a64f487a68f3">He should seek explicit, written direction fro=
m Payman on what "solving the basic problems" looks like in practice, with =
clear milestones and success criteria.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"559518f08806"><strong>For Payman:</strong></p>
<p local-id=3D"4180773331d2">This is the most delicate conversation. Some p=
oints worth raising:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"b5b4efc4-56fd-4750-8efa-be91e1975d5a">
<li local-id=3D"10d33d0b-ce4b-4a44-960b-76ed18f11fd2">
<p local-id=3D"8210e0cfa639">The project has been active for about a year w=
ith promising but informal results. A structured path forward =E2=80=94 eve=
n a lightweight one =E2=80=94 would help ensure the next year is more produ=
ctive than the last. Your proposed alternative timeline could be a useful f=
ramework to reference.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"8c4c41a8-51a5-4c08-9823-0d44de1d8bf8">
<p local-id=3D"87965d182587">If Payman believes DOEs are premature, it's fa=
ir to ask: what evidence or milestone would signal that the project is read=
y for formal experimentation? Without a clear threshold, the team can't pla=
n ahead.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"8d9b807c-b2b9-4f60-bbbf-175c6d9c6826">
<p local-id=3D"26104a269f61">The question of project ownership needs a clea=
r answer. If Payman is the decision maker, Mason needs regular, explicit di=
rection =E2=80=94 not ambiguous meeting outcomes that get interpreted diffe=
rently by everyone in the room. If Mason has autonomy, the scope of that au=
tonomy needs to be defined.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"82ab5918-8e1d-42b5-aa9f-5daabafa9526">
<p local-id=3D"c266de80e2ea">On the environmental control question, it coul=
d be framed as risk management: without controlled conditions, there's a ri=
sk of spending months on experiments whose results can't be trusted or repr=
oduced, which would be a worse use of time than pausing to set up proper co=
ntrols.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2fc0c65c-de88-4557-a906-37913bc0ecf9">
<p local-id=3D"ff8e3022688d">The idea of combining the laser and no-depowde=
ring projects is worth discussing on its merits, but changing scope mid-str=
eam without a clear rationale risks further confusion about priorities.</p>=
</li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"89827f2fa92f">One general note: your observation about clear=
 action items coming out of meetings is important for all three conversatio=
ns. A recurring theme here is that people leave discussions with different =
understandings of what was decided. Pushing for written summaries of decisi=
ons and action items after meetings =E2=80=94 even informal ones =E2=80=94 =
could prevent a lot of the friction you're describing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a2340e1fc07"></p>
<p local-id=3D"25ad3eec9603">Payman has been running informal experiments o=
n the Ultimaker with the XTool with a blue laser diode source (400-500 nm) =
instead of the MOPA laser=E2=80=99s NIR wavelength (1064 nm). It also has a=
 different hopper, spreader, and binder application technology. I am not su=
re what exactly he is seeing that is different, but it seems that he is see=
ing less of the =E2=80=9Csintering=E2=80=9D of the powder, and presumably l=
ess debris.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8644621d26ea">The conclusion Payman and Marcus have drawn fr=
om Payman=E2=80=99s informal experiments today is to try and get the MOPA l=
aser to behave more like the blue diode laser, and Marcus sent out an email=
 based on a chat with Claude asking how to get the MOPA laser to perform li=
ke a blue diode laser.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8c2727d661ca">I am unsure why Payman is running these experi=
ments before Mason=E2=80=99s MOPA laser experiments have concluded, especia=
lly since there are experiments that Payman insisted Mason run as part of t=
his screening experiment. It seems that Payman is trying to solve issues th=
at Mason is seeing before the formal experiment is concluded. I do not see =
this as a particularly constructive use of Payman=E2=80=99s time, my recomm=
endation would be to wait until Mason=E2=80=99s screening experiment has be=
en concluded and the data presented before moving on to different experimen=
ts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a831284f1a10">I am also increasingly of the opinion that it =
is important to gain an understanding of the fundamental nature of the proc=
ess we are seeing before we start running off in different directions.</p>
<p local-id=3D"92022869d4be">2026-02-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"c8162b8fa95b">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8394298aef27">Wired up the control box for stepper motor and=
 vibrator motor outputs. I have not yet connected any sensors.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7aac7b4bbc5a">The newly updated host duct connectors are don=
e printing; yesterday I dropped the original 38mm OD to 37mm, found that th=
ey were still too tight, then dropped the OD to 36mm, which are the parts t=
hat are done printing now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6fa329e16eb5">I connected the powder cartridge dock doser st=
epper motor, and it seems to be rotating fine. The rotational speed is quit=
e low because the Arduino running TMC2209 stepper drivers combined with the=
 1:100 gear ratio of the stepper motor limit the speed, but the speed shoul=
d be much higher when it is being run by a Duet board.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ed748602fb9b">I have dispensed 1 1/2 bottles of powder throu=
gh the doser ball now, and the system seems to be working OK. I have starte=
d trying different sensors. The first sensor I tried was an inductive proxi=
mity sensor, which interestingly was not able to detect the powder, even wh=
en I inserted the sensor ~20mm into powder in a nalgene bottle. I am now tr=
ying to use an IR proximity switch. It works detecting the presence of powd=
er in a nalgene bottle, but since the bottle takes ~1 hour to empty it take=
s a while before I can tell whether the sensor actually detects the rising =
powder or not. I also have yet to tie the switch into the Arduino yet, I am=
 so far just watching the indicator light on the back of the prox switch.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"e34b869d1eb8">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"321b1a5c2127">Payman is still running experiments on the Ult=
imaker. Yesterday he build a Vention frame around it that holds the green l=
aser guard panels; as I recall they are not suitable for the blue laser tha=
t the XTool uses but I don=E2=80=99t remember exactly what spectrum they bl=
ock.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8cb88a3d34e1">I spoke with Mason about the fundamental physi=
cal principles of the laser cutting we are seeing; based on papers he and M=
idhun have read, the laser light is absorbed by metal powder, and the heate=
d particles then interact with the binder in such a way that the particles =
are ejected from the heated area. This may be due to vaporization of the wa=
ter in the binder, vaporization of the sugar in the binder, or some other e=
ffect or combination of effects that we are as yet unaware of. The nominal =
working theory is that the heat is vaporizing the water and that is what ej=
ects the powder without melting or igniting it, and when the laser penetrat=
es to dry unbound powder, the energy input has a greater tendency to melt o=
r ignite the powder, as shown by debris around the cut. This is generally i=
nterpreted as =E2=80=9Csintering,=E2=80=9D although I am unconvinced that t=
he powder is actually sintering without data showing that it is not melting=
 or igniting or a combination of all 3. Regardless, that is an undesirable =
effect; the goal is to replicate the function of the spindle with a higher =
speed and decreased cut line width. This goal carries advantages and disadv=
antages. Let=E2=80=99s say we get the laser system working as well as the s=
pindle (controlled cut depth, clean cuts, same surface finish on the side o=
f the parts, same precision and accuracy):</p>
<p local-id=3D"f24c0569402c">Advantages:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"e18530b8d1e0">
<li local-id=3D"073015d786bd">
<p local-id=3D"440184ee8e34">Faster cutting speed</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"1084806035a0">
<p local-id=3D"342df32d6f50">Reduce line width (this one is more subtle bec=
ause it is possible to use a smaller end mill in theory, but that is so far=
 untested)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"ce03dfc77585">
<p local-id=3D"a70203dc6f6a">If the laser system is used only with no spind=
le or sacrificial filler, the Z axis and X axes could be eliminated, and th=
e Y axis would not need high positional accuracy and repeatability, it woul=
d only need to travel in a very straight line</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"784eebd3a2b3">Disadvantages:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"9b1a1a71a89f">
<li local-id=3D"7610b9f7768d">
<p local-id=3D"7906b0a85e05">More expensive hardware required for the print=
er (one of the selling points of 3DEO=E2=80=99s technology is that spindles=
 are cheap)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3d121cc0a0f6">
<p local-id=3D"d6dd9ea582a7">More complex control system demands (may requi=
re a more advanced hardware control system, which would add to the system c=
ost)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a1001f7d5717">
<p local-id=3D"a02bd604eb28">Reduces the differentiation between 3DEO=E2=80=
=99s technology and competitors' technologies, particularly HeadMade materi=
als with their ColdMetalFusion technology, where they use polymer SLS print=
ers to print metal parts by pre-coating the powder with a laser-activated b=
inder.</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"0d2bf0736433"></p>
<p local-id=3D"128dc8aa3e36">This additional context is really helpful and =
changes the picture meaningfully. A few observations:</p>
<p local-id=3D"56e516d77276"><strong>The dynamic you're navigating is funda=
mentally a founder problem.</strong> Payman isn't just a difficult manager =
=E2=80=94 he's the last remaining founder, which means his identity is deep=
ly tied to the technology and the company's original vision. When people pu=
sh back on the technology or suggest replacing core elements of it (like th=
e sprayer with a binder jetting head), he's not just hearing a technical ar=
gument =E2=80=94 he's hearing that the thing he built isn't good enough. Th=
at doesn't make his resistance rational or productive, but it explains why =
technical counterarguments don't land the way they should.</p>
<p local-id=3D"92c9b2a29cf8"><strong>Your approach of compliance plus indep=
endent problem-solving is probably why you've avoided conflict.</strong> Yo=
u're not threatening his sense of ownership. Mason, as a newer engineer try=
ing to bring formal methodology to a process Payman feels he understands in=
tuitively, is inadvertently challenging both his authority and his approach=
. That's a much more friction-prone position to be in, especially with only=
 a year of tenure.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3c8a6facd6f8"><strong>The fact that this is well known to ma=
nagement is significant.</strong> It means Marcus and Sean are already awar=
e of the pattern, which suggests one of two things: either they've decided =
the cost of confronting Payman is higher than the cost of the friction he c=
reates, or they've tried and failed to change the dynamic. The engineer who=
 left two years ago is a data point that the company has already paid a rea=
l price for this. Mason could become another.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9365e9a859e6"><strong>For Mason specifically,</strong> the h=
ard truth is that as a one-year engineer pushing back against the CTO and l=
ast remaining founder, his leverage is limited regardless of whether he's t=
echnically right. His best path forward is probably to frame his work in te=
rms that align with Payman's stated goals rather than trying to change Paym=
an's mind about methodology. If Payman says the project needs to solve basi=
c problems before optimizing, Mason could ask Payman to define those proble=
ms explicitly, then design his experiments to address them =E2=80=94 even i=
f the underlying methodology is still essentially a structured experiment.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"05a9fe307b27"><strong>For Marcus,</strong> the question is w=
hether he's willing to use his position as VP of Engineering to establish c=
lear project governance. The ownership ambiguity on the laser project is a =
symptom of a broader issue: if the CTO can override the engineering team's =
methodology without providing a clear alternative, that's an organizational=
 problem, not a project management one. Marcus is really the only person wi=
th the standing to address this.</p>
<p local-id=3D"84c59b24fe05"><strong>For you personally,</strong> your five=
 years of tenure and low-conflict relationship with Payman actually give yo=
u a unique position. You can say things to Payman that Mason can't, and you=
r retrospective timeline analysis is the kind of constructive framing that =
might resonate with him =E2=80=94 it's not criticizing his approach, it's s=
howing what a structured path could look like. Whether you want to spend th=
at social capital is a personal decision, though. You're not obligated to b=
e the mediator, and there's a real risk of inheriting some of the friction =
that Mason is currently absorbing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0c22713e1ae"><strong>The deeper strategic question</strong>=
 is whether the company's leadership is willing to honestly evaluate whethe=
r the original technology platform is still competitive. If Payman's attach=
ment to it is preventing necessary evolution =E2=80=94 and it sounds like m=
ultiple engineers believe it is =E2=80=94 that's ultimately a board-level o=
r CEO-level conversation, not something that gets resolved through better p=
roject management on the laser project.</p>
