<p local-id=3D"bcbb61a8ec2f">2026-03-02</p>
<p local-id=3D"e29fdc86ff21">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"44f594899f16">Connected the IR prox switch to the ESP-32 boa=
rd. The way the output signal of the prox switch works is that it drives a =
load at 300 mA that is placed across the signal line (black) and the ground=
 wire (blue), presumably at the same voltage the sensor is supplied with (o=
riginally 12V, but I switched out the 12V power supply for a 24V power supp=
ly, so I have the option of increasing the voltage to the vibrator motors i=
f necessary, although so far it has not seemed to be necessary. I tried con=
necting the signal wire directly to an input on the ESP-32 board (which was=
 really stupid, I don=E2=80=99t know why I thought that might work. To be f=
air, I did try it before I had a complete understanding of the sensor outpu=
t architecture, but in a way that makes me look even worse.) I considered c=
onnecting a resistor from the signal wire to ground to drop 7V when it was =
using a 12V power supply, and connecting the ESP-32 board to the point wher=
e the voltage would nominally be 5V, but the resistance value wound up bein=
g 23.3 ohms, which would need to dissipate ~2.4W when the sensor is trigger=
ed. Since most resistors are, I believe, 1/4W, I would need to get a high-p=
ower resistor (probably either 22 or 24 ohm), and I would need to deal with=
 heat generation from the resistor. What I wound up doing was to connect th=
e sensor to a 24V PC board relay that I desoldered from one of those little=
 relay control boards. I started off trying to use the whole board with its=
 associated bits of electronics that make it possible to trigger it with a =
low voltage signal, but for some reason wiring it to the sensor fed power b=
ack into the sensor and caused the indicator light on the sensor to trigger=
. This setup seems to work; I have the sensor powering the coil of the rela=
y when it is activated and the NO/COM lines connected to switch 5VDC to the=
 ESP-32 input. There is something very worrisome where when I move around s=
ome of the wires and the newly added relay the 4 relays on the relay board =
that the ESP-32 board is connected to will turn on and off randomly (or it =
may just be the new relay that clicks quickly enough that it sounds like it=
 is switching a bunch of relays, but I am not totally sure.) It seems to wo=
rk nominally when I am not touching it. I rewrote the Arduino code so that =
it is reading the prox switch input; if the prox switch is not triggered, b=
oth vibrators and the powder feed stepper motor will run, and once the prox=
 switch has been triggered it will turn everything off and send a single se=
rial text line to the Arduino IDE serial monitor saying =E2=80=9CHOPPER FUL=
L :)". The prox switch seems to function OK even though its optics are pret=
ty well covered with powder (when the hopper vibrates everything inside it =
will get covered by powder that is kicked up). If this sensor works robustl=
y then it could be a very simple solution to detecting and adjusting the po=
wder level (there is a small set screw on the prox switch that can be turne=
d to change the triggering distance of the switch). One concern I have is a=
bout the repeatability of the switch; it may trigger at slightly different =
distances, which would cause the powder fill level to vary over time. I per=
sonally doubt that this will affect the print quality and part density sign=
ificantly, but it is one variable to consider. If this does turn out to be =
an issue, it may be possible to switch to one of the more advanced TOF sens=
ors (potentially more accurate, but are more difficult to interface to from=
 a software perspective and with a much smaller sensor window may be more s=
ensitive to powder buildup). I am confident that the prox switch can be int=
erfaced to one of the limit switch connection points of the Duet expansion =
board, but I am not sure if it is possible to interface an I2C sensor to th=
e Duet board, particularly one that requires a pretty complex library to ta=
lk to it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dd8fe9b857e1">Since the TOF sensor will hopefully not be nec=
essary and the opening for mounting is is allowing powder to be released, I=
 am going to print a new vibrating hopper hat without the TOF sensor cutout=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"94c27fd9d40c">The updated vibrating hat design is complete a=
nd I am printing it out on Anvil, the Bambu X1E, so it should be ready for =
post-processing and heat-set insert installation tomorrow.</p>
<p local-id=3D"684e332209f8">I made a new vibrating sheet from 301 stainles=
s steel that is .010=E2=80=9D thick, as opposed to the previous .020=E2=80=
=9D thick sheet. This new sheet also has holes in the correct positions so =
it will not need to be glued into place.</p>
<p local-id=3D"69b9a41d8fb3">I have continued testing of the powder loading=
 system with the ESP-32-based control system, and the IR proximity switch s=
eems to be working quite robustly to detect powder height. It does not appe=
ar to have any built-in hysteresis, so it has a tendency to switch between =
detecting the powder and not detecting it at a frequency of ~10 Hz. I wrote=
 a hysteresis function into the Arduino IDE code the ESP-32 is running, but=
 for the final Duet-board-run (G code) program I will likely just run it un=
til it first detects the powder and call the hopper filled at that point. A=
ctually, I take that back; since I will need to design some closed-loop fun=
ctionality into the macro anyway, I might as well design in a function that=
 detects the prox switch, dispenses for a while longer, checks the prox swi=
tch again, maybe dispenses for longer, etc.</p>
