<p local-id=3D"fffd3cb0014c">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2bc077a0067">The new vibrating hat with the dedicated openi=
ng for mounting a TOF sensor board removed has been printed and post-proces=
sed (supports removed) and just needs heat set inserts installed to be read=
y to install on Pilot 2.</p>
<p local-id=3D"91d7bf722ebc">I noted that the doser roller has a very low f=
low rate on the benchtop testing rig (although this is in part due to the s=
peed limitation of the stepper motor because I am using an Arduino to drive=
 it) and it also dispenses in discrete pulses due to the nature of the desi=
gn of the doser ball with discrete openings. I am going to redesign it with=
 a larger number of helical openings, which should give a continuous dispen=
se and also dispense faster because there are more openings. I am also goin=
g to increase the size of the doser ball slightly because powder leaks arou=
nd it when the cartridge is inverted but the doser ball is not rotated. I a=
m hoping to avoid the need to add some sort of seal to the cartridge cap, b=
ut they may become necessary if I am unable to prevent powder leakage by re=
designing the doser ball.</p>
<p local-id=3D"47ec3ed03e63">I populated the vibrating hat with heat set in=
serts, and now I am working on hose routing for the powder feed hose. This =
is going to be one of the trickier parts of the design; at the very least I=
 anticipate that the cartridge dock will only be able to be installed at on=
e location on the top of the machine, which likely will not be the most con=
venient location to reach, and it is highly likely that I will need to chan=
ge the hose duct interface on the vibrating hat that freely rotates using a=
 ball bearing; I suspect I will need to design it to be an angled elbow, an=
d restrict the range of rotational motion so the hose does not impact the m=
ist box or the X axis cable carrier. I also redesigned the doser ball with =
a spiral pattern; for some reason the swept cuts (I made two spiral paths i=
n opposite directions out of the center plane and swept the same profile al=
ong each to make sure that the spiral path is centered on the doser ball) w=
ere not forming evenly, even with the exact same spiral dimensions and swee=
p settings. One of them always twisted more than the other, and I=E2=80=99m=
 not sure why. I did try restarting SolidWorks to see if that resolved the =
issue, which it did not. Possibly restarting my computer entirely would res=
olve it. I ended up manually setting a fixed twist value for each sweep ope=
ration (0 degrees) which made them match each other (previously they were b=
oth set to =E2=80=9Cminimum twist=E2=80=9D).</p>
<p local-id=3D"4598e4b7ea21">In disassembling the benchtop dummy hopper tes=
t setup to move some of the components over to Pilot 2, I discovered that t=
