<p local-id=3D"8612e8e98dcf">Laser Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1e74921a7d52">Reviewing results of Mason/Midhun=E2=80=99s DO=
E</p>
<p local-id=3D"e07ac5e798ca">Different laser parameters adjusted for a seri=
es of prints, with a particular focus on cut depth, width, debris productio=
n, and whether cuts and hatches are connected between layers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ea1624bae6ce">In hatch areas, the section of the hatch where=
 the laser starts cutting tends to be deeper. The depth variation tends to =
be 100-200 microns. The scan pattern was a raster scan with X direction lin=
es stacked on each other in Y direction.</p>
<p local-id=3D"032c2d398e3e">Cut depth not strongly correlated with cut con=
nection. Strongest correlation was between number of passes and whether cut=
s connected.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1cfb49b6e08c">Debris area was strongly correlated with the m=
odel (cut debris R^2 98.53%, hatch debris R^2 of 95.97%).</p>
<p local-id=3D"d751830f8f07">Payman asked about one data point with a 40 mi=
cron cut that was connected. Mason said that the cut depth measurement was =
determined as a rough average of measured cut depth.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2aa56bbe27f4">Debris is considered to be melted/sintered/oxi=
dized/etc material within the cut.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6f9ed3f39a0a">Nonlinear correlation seen between input param=
eters and debris area output. The graphs of the Minitab predicted models sh=
ow negative values of debris area in some regions of the models.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7bff845203d3">Most combinations resulted in a 100 micron cut=
 width.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d15ed89c0df3">All variables had statistically significant ef=
fects on the response variables, but varied in how strong the correlation w=
as. Pulse width had the weakest correlation.</p>
<p local-id=3D"99d49bd7247d">Optimal parameters were calculated based on th=
e Minitab model, so the test protocol was repeated with those parameters. T=
he results of this test did not exactly match predicted results.</p>
<p local-id=3D"618e2b96fd42">Mason noted that the layer tends to be quite w=
et; even the powder that can be brushed out tends to form clumps on the bru=
sh. On the AEM-style demo part, unbound powder was loose before curing but =
after curing was more bound. The =E2=80=9Cunbound=E2=80=9D powder was easie=
r to remove than the green part bound powder after curing but was not compl=
etely loose powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"aeeada72eb08">Midhun=E2=80=99s answers to Q=E2=80=99s from y=
esterday. I asked how closely the model predicts the results seen, and he s=
aid that the model fits well. The graphs that show negative values are not =
showing the output variables, they are showing the effect on the mean of th=
e response variable for a given input variable.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2ac5c4a6dbad">Cut depth only measured at the third layer. Ma=
son noted that sometimes he did see that the cuts went all the way to the g=
lass. He also noted that the nominal layer thickness in the test was 100 mi=
crons.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a1fdeec5739b">Mason has recommendations for improving the ex=
perimental protocol in the future: having a hatch that is cut only on the t=
op layer and collecting data on kerf angle and surface roughness.</p>
<p local-id=3D"28a322cf3a53">For next steps, Mason recommends comparing the=
 MOPA fiber laser with the blue diode laser before moving forward with furt=
her MOPA laser experiments.</p>
<p local-id=3D"060cd5db537c">He also recommends multi-material testing on 3=
16L, 17-4PH, and copper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a5ec4c59294f">Marcus and Payman recommend sticking with 17-4=
