<p local-id=3D"cd90fcda3d11">Heat Exchanger:</p>
<p local-id=3D"af3f1ce7cfe1">Looked at a new heat exchanger design Sean has=
 generated with Pourya and Marcus, and discussed minimum feature size with =
Devin. There is a hard-coded limit on how close tool paths can be in the V1=
 slicer (whose value is unknown) so it is impossible to make a single cutte=
r-width cut, you always need at least two tool paths that are that unknown =
with apart. It is possible that this limit is reduced or eliminated with ZZ=
=E2=80=99s V2 slicer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2909972f66a">Management learned about a company recently ca=
lled Alloyed Enterprises based in Boston (of course) that makes heat exchan=
gers for the same market we are pursuing (processors in data centers). Thei=
r process involves starting with a roll of thin sheet metal (either copper =
or aluminum), laser cutting outlines into sections of the sheet for each la=
yer of the part (the part is sliced into layers the same way it is done wit=
h 3D printing), filling in the gaps with some sacrificial filler material, =
stacking the layers together with precise registration (I would probably cu=
t holes into each layer and use pins to align the layers together, although=
 they may have a more clever solution that involves vision-based inspection=
 of each layer and precision positioning of the layer to match the ones bel=
ow) and they then use diffusion bonding to join the layers into a single pa=
rt, and then remove the sacrificial filler material. There will be some pri=
nt limitations based on the force that needs to be applied in the Z directi=
on when diffusion bonding, as well as the fact that the sacrificial filler =
material cannot be removed from fully enclosed spaces.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5c1f1f99cb56">I installed WSL so that I can run the full Ope=
nFOAM package for heat exchanger simulation. the Unix user account I set up=
 is admin-jhumphreys and the password is =E2=80=9Cguest=E2=80=9D .</p>
