<p local-id=3D"10e44a1b2453">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"0ad3baf67728">I printed the new hopper duct hose mount and b=
earing mount with rotation limits so it nominally points straight back away=
 from the operator (-X direction, I think) is blocked from rotating towards=
 the cable carrier (+Y direction) and can only rotate 30 degrees towards th=
e mist box (-Y direction). This should prevent the hose duct from hitting a=
nything while still giving it enough motion to reduce the load applied to t=
he vibrating hat and get the hose angle correct when the gantry is at maxim=
um Y direction (this is the point at which the hose duct has a continuous p=
ath downwards to the hopper, so is likely the optimal gantry position for l=
oading the hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c3816d403f82">I demolded the cast urethane spiral doser ball=
 and it looks good.</p>
<p local-id=3D"31186387ac37">I wired up the stepper motor and vibrator moto=
r on the cartridge dock to the Duet expansion board, so it should be possib=
le to control them with G code now. I also demolded the scrap piece of hose=
 that I had cast a urethane end on to, and it came out really well. There a=
re a few voids here and there on the outside of the hose, but the inside lo=
oks OK. The castellations on the bottom which were intended to allow flow b=
etween the OD and ID areas of the mold actually had an interesting differen=
t effect. They collected bubbles that would otherwise have led to voids in =
the seal, so when I cut off the bits of urethane that formed in the castell=
ations there was a nice flush surface on the bottom of the duct. Overall, i=
t seems like a very promising technique for forming a reliable seal between=
 the hose duct and 3D printed parts.</p>
