<p local-id=3D"836d4cea5ec1">(The custom quote includes subpanels)</p>
<p local-id=3D"0989b2e7d5a8">This means that for the COTS parts with no cut=
outs, total cost per cabinet is $993, while the cost per custom cabinet is =
$1,942.28. This is almost exactly $1000 more per cabinet, or roughly double=
 the cost. Assuming that Ning can modify the COTS cabinet with cutouts of e=
xactly the right size in exactly the right location, the quality of the cab=
inets will be similar; the custom one would be powder coated RAL 7035, whic=
h would cover the cut area so there would be no exposed steel where Ning=E2=
=80=99s cutouts were made, but the only real advantage here is that the pri=
nter would look more professional. There is also a disadvantage that the le=
ad time for the custom cabinet is 5-6 weeks, and since we (presumably) need=
 to build the printers ASAP, this may not be acceptable. The average cost t=
o the company for one person=E2=80=99s labor is ~$1000/day, so assuming tha=
t Ning takes exactly one day to customize the cabinets, the company will ha=
ve saved $1000, and also lost 1 day of Ning=E2=80=99s time that could have =
been spent on EE work. If he takes 2 days, the company breaks even, althoug=
h with a lower quality cabinet (exposed steel, rough cut edges, ANSI 61 gra=
y instead of RAL 7035 powder coating) and every day Ning spends working on =
it beyond 2 days loses the company $1000. On the other hand, Ning is confid=
ent that he can completely customize both cabinets in 1 day, which would sa=
ve the company $1000 vs. the custom cabinet and would reduce the cabinet le=
ad time.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dead5c0486a8">One thing I don=E2=80=99t like is that having =
a cabinet customized in-house by an engineer is not particularly scalable. =
When building Epsons 1 and 2, I made the cabinet cutouts by hand but the na=
rrative was that we would document the cabinet customization and order cust=
om ones for Pilots 6 and 7. When we were given the green light to build 6 a=
nd 7, I created drawings for custom cabinets and worked with a custom cabin=
et manufacturer, but we needed the printers built ASAP so we instead cut co=
rners and Al customized the cabinets in-house. The narrative then was that =
for the next 2 printers we would definitely order the next cabinets to be c=
ustom made, and I pushed for that, but it looks like we=E2=80=99re probably=
 not going to.</p>
<p local-id=3D"37f493e90e4e"></p>
<p local-id=3D"f1c2d70e22fe">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"c6255bbee0e4">I have the powder dispenser doser stepper moto=
r and vibrator motor completely wired up and operation from the G code. Nex=
t step is to connect the IR prox switches to the expansion board. Because o=
f how the IR prox switches are designed, I have it set up to energize a 24V=
 relay when the prox switch is activated, and I will use the NO contacts on=
 the relay to trigger one of the Duet expansion board inputs. I am also cas=
ting urethane around the other end of the duct hose; I cast one end last Fr=
iday so it should be ready for installation once this pour of urethane is c=
ured. I printed an extended hose duct mount for the powder cartridge dock o=
ver the weekend as well as a new hose duct mount for the hopper with update=
d clearances so it is easier to assemble and install and also moves more sm=
oothly, and I post-processed and installed both those parts today. I noted =
that running the cartridge dock vibrator with the cartridge empty has a ten=
dency to vibrate the whole top window on the printer; this is not unexpecte=
d behavior but is worth consideration. It is possible that it will be less =
of an issue when there is powder in the bottle, and I also may be able to t=
urn down the vibrator motor further (the test today was run with it set to =
a power level of 50/255). If it continues to be an issue, I will make a new=
 vibration isolator that isolates vibration from the window better. It is a=
lso possible to couple the base of the vibration isolator to the printer fr=
ame, which would help to prevent vibration of the window, but would require=
 additional work to make the appropriate modifications to the upper window =
and install drop-in nuts etc.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9e3ef70bad7a">I have the sensor wired up and it is detected =
by conditional G code, so I can make a macro that will do stuff while the s=
ensor is not triggered then exit the code once it has been triggered.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3b35a26d49b6">I just started a print of 2X rotation indicato=
rs that will be glued to the end of the stepper motor shaft to show its rot=
ation.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6313aee643df">I started testing loading powder into the hopp=
er, and as expected there are some issues with powder flowing. The speed of=
 dispense is still quite low even with the Duet board=E2=80=99s increased s=
tepper driver speed as compared with the Arduino, so that may require some =
optimization. I am printing a new rotating duct mount for the cartridge doc=
k with a slight angle to it, which, along with shortening the duct hose, sh=
ould help with powder flowing into the hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d233aadcddc4">One thing that also may be worth considering i=
s redesigning the vibrating hat so that the opening is closer to the cartri=
dge dock. This would have the additional benefit of letting me move the IR =
prox switch closer to the center of the hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ef078a3fed18">2026-03-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"dcb87f83dc24">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"ad0f166895a8">The estimated cost per Pilot build without mos=
t of the custom machined parts sounded high to me (Senai said $50K but summ=
ing the BOM came out to $54K), so I went through and noticed some high-cost=
 items that I think can be removed. The big one is $12K worth of stages tha=
t we recycle from the Betas and so won=E2=80=99t need to be included in the=
 Pilot price.</p>
<p local-id=3D"df63a75e9705">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"cfcd81d03554">I am designing a new vibrating hat with the fe=
ed duct closer to the powder cartridge to get a sharper angle from the cart=
ridge to the hopper to resolve some of the issues I was seeing yesterday wi=
th powder flowing into the hopper. This also lets me make a more elegant CA=
D model because it doesn=E2=80=99t have various features that were added an=
d removed over time. I can also move the prox switch to the center of the h=
opper. The downside is that since the powder inlet is offset from the cente=
r, there is a risk of powder not being level within the hopper. Hopefully t=
he vibration applied by the sheet will help to level it out, but it is some=
thing that will need to be confirmed not to be an issue in testing (I suspe=
ct it will show up in different density in the X direction if it does turn =
out to be an issue at all).</p>
<p local-id=3D"b2d3b499a5dd">I have redesigned the vibrating hat and it is =
currently printing on Anvil, the Bambu X1E.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5b650ac05488">I have a concept for an improved way to seal t=
he ends of the hose duct without needing to cast urethane onto the hose per=
manently (which takes time and makes it difficult to adjust the length of t=
he hose):</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-w=
rapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"confluence-embedded-i=
mage image-center" alt=3D"image-20260310-215319.png" width=3D"349" loading=
