>
<p local-id=3D"84069e9768ef">This is a cast urethane cap that slides onto t=
he hose duct and seals it to the 3D printed part when it is clamped down. I=
 made the inner wall 1mm thick, but this may still be too much to be able t=
o slide on to the current 3D printed parts. I am currently printing a mold =
to make a silicone mold to cast these parts (yep, urethane casting is a two=
-step process, but the parts are much more durable than silicone).</p>
<p local-id=3D"d6d25bf45055">I did pours of Mold Star 30 for 2X end cap mol=
ds, and will test those out tomorrow. The new vibrating hat is done printin=
g, so I will install the heat set inserts and swap out the hats on the hopp=
er tomorrow. I also ran some tests filling the hopper and emptying it, and =
there are indeed some issues with the powder getting to the hopper with the=
 current shallow angle setup so I think it is definitely beneficial to swit=
ch to the steeper setup. I also put together a new cartridge cap with the n=
ewer spiral doser balls. The seal still isn=E2=80=99t perfect so I think I =
need to print a new cap with a smaller doser ball cavity, but the dispense =
is much smoother and closer to continuous with the spiral doser ball.</p>
<p local-id=3D"69bce13b25ed">2026-03-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"f21105c61adc">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"f77d5aaa7b74">I populated the new vibrating hat with heat se=
t inserts and installed it on the hopper on Pilot 2. I noticed that it is b=
eneficial for the vibrating hat duct hose connection to be at an angle perm=
anently rather than swiveling, because it forces the hose away from the cab=
le carrier when the gantry goes back to the Y 0 position (as I learned at A=
MP, hose rubbing is the enemy! Granted, we=E2=80=99re abusing the hose way =
less here, but it=E2=80=99s still good general practice). I tried loading t=
he hopper with the new hose setup, and it feeds better, although it still c=
an build up powder in the hose when the hopper gets full (my working theory=
 is that as the weight of the hopper increases, the vibrating hat vibrates =
less, and since part of the powder flow through the duct hose comes from th=
e duct hose being vibrated by the vibrating hat, that has a tendency to red=
uce liquefaction of the powder in the duct hose.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d8d628654973">I taught Mohsen and Ben about using the new ho=
pper feeder and showed them the macro I had written to run it, and handed o=
ff Pilot 2 to them to run a test with the new setup. Before the test, Mohse=
n pointed out that he has had difficulty in the past with starting prints w=
ith the linear gate hopper design because it has a tendency to push off the=
 cake, even when it is glued down to the build plate. This makes sense; one=
 inherent issue with this design of hopper is that since the powder column =
is always contacting the cake, there will be a large shear force applied to=
 the cake when the hopper is moving. Payman=E2=80=99s theory is that this i=
s also an advantage, because the weight of the powder is helping to compres=
s the layer of dry powder being applied, thereby increasing the density of =
the layer, and the design of the hopper also naturally regulates the flow o=
f powder onto the layer, minimizing powder wastage. I would argue that the =
effect of the weight of powder on the layer is unknown and may or may not e=
xist, and powder overflow doesn=E2=80=99t matter if we set up a powder recy=
cling/reconditioning line, which would have the knock-on benefit of letting=
 us condition all of our powder before use to make sure that it has very re=
liable properties, and also the powder wastage due to overflow is minimal c=
ompared with the powder wasted in binding the whole cake (that may or may n=
ot be true; the amount of powder wasted in binding the cake does dwarf the =
amount of powder that winds up in the parts, but depending on the amount of=
 extra powder spread the overflow may be comparable to or even greater than=
 the amount of powder spread onto the layer proper).</p>
<p local-id=3D"605f2cfb45e1"></p>
