<p local-id=3D"d1308eb0b4a9">2026-03-12</p>
<p local-id=3D"35fd52b21706">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"36dcfbe49cc3">Meeting about fault handling for the Pilots in=
 general and an issue being seen on pilot 7 where the Duet board disconnect=
s from the Raspberry Pi.</p>
<p local-id=3D"42090f1fa3cb">There is a fault that has been seen on the Pil=
ots where the spindle, heater, or vacuum have been left on overnight. Ning =
has installed a timer relay to prevent the vacuum from running too long (ti=
me setting?). There is currently no safety system on the spindle.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e33f4a3b362">Marcus wants a more robust error/fault handlin=
g system that will deenergize all active systems (especially heat-producing=
 systems) in a detected fault condition. ZZ does not have an immediate answ=
er for architecting that system. Ning pointed out that if the safety contro=
ller trips, it will erase the error logs. ZZ pointed out that this is not t=
he case, since the safety controller only cuts power to the Duet, so the er=
ror log in the Duet Web Interface will retain the error log.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3a8563916c2c">I would recommend setting up the safety system=
 to detect whether components have been left on for an extended period of t=
ime, as well as a separate python code running on the Raspberry pi separate=
 from the DSF program to monitor nominal operation and reset the system if =
necessary.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c50463554f91">Marcus pointed out that it would be good gener=
al practice to have a regular handshake protocol between components to shut=
 down the system if an off-nominal condition is detected.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7c1a7163e075">ZZ stated that what we are seeing is a brief f=
ailure of the SPI connection between the Duet board and the Raspberry Pi.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"7791d86e67af">It is worth investigating when the heater was =
left on overnight. The temperature sensor was replaced about a month ago. T=
esting of the current system has not been done. Senai recommends adding a t=
ime delay system to the heater so that if the heater is left on too long th=
en it will turn off, although Marcus pointed out that this is only a stopga=
p system. I would recommend that this system be connected to the control sy=
stem in some way so that if the heater is shut off the Raspberry Pi will be=
 aware of it to pause the print.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a979200175cb">Is this kind of delay function programmable in=
 the safety relay? Is it possible to set up the Duet so that it shuts off r=
elay power if it is disconnected from the Raspberry Pi?</p>
<p local-id=3D"b48eda99bbaf">Philipp and Senai requested the drawings gener=
ated for the machined parts for Pilots 8 and 9. Since some of the drawings =
seem to have gotten messed up, I am going through and fixing them and gener=
ating PDFs I am putting on the Z drive for now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4607ea413300">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b0fb72685239">Mohsen noted a failure mode where the IR senso=
r is not detecting the powder, causing the loading system to run indefinite=
ly until the hopper and hose are completely full of powder. On the upside, =
the hose seems robust to that much weight, but the sensor needs to be more =
robust. I have not yet tried capacitive prox switches, so I will try that n=
ext to see if it works better.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2ffa15a38ce7">Mohsen is currently working on forming a good =
base layer with the hopper, which can be done without the powder reloading =
system.</p>
<p local-id=3D"55a98a029bea">Mohsen noted an issue with the gate not fully =
opening when the hopper was filled with powder all the way to the top, and =
he found that it worked fine when the hopper was emptied. He also pointed o=
ut that he had disable the limit switch that detects whether the gate is cl=
osed, and is only using the limit switch that detects when the gate is full=
y open. I am unsure exactly why he did this; the macros should be able to o=
perate fine with both limit switches connected, but there are issues that c=
an arise because it was necessary to wire both limit switches in series due=
 to the Duet=E2=80=99s boards limitation that it can only use one input as =
a homing switch for an axis. On the other hand, now that I know how to use =
conditional statements in Duet G code, I could write a few lines that would=
 rotate the stepper motor until it hits the limit switch, and have the limi=
t switch attached to any of the input ports on the Duet expansion board.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"386e591799c8">I have entered an order request for a material=
-agnostic (capacitive) proximity switch, since the inductive proximity swit=
ch I tested in the past was not able to detect metal powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0a1e7fda9c6f">I designed a new prox switch holder for the ca=
pacitive prox switch for which I entered the order request. Since the curre=
nt design is too small to fit the nuts that the prox switch comes with, I d=
esigned M18X1 threads into the printed part. It would be better to print th=
e hole undersize and tap it, but taps of the right size are ~$80 from McMas=
ter so I am going to try printing the threads first. My plan is to screw th=
e prox switch into the holder then use a nut on the top to lock it in place=
.</p>
