<p local-id=3D"cba0522e2ded">2026-03-13</p>
<p local-id=3D"7b181d51d47a">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b0c110a94a4">Marcus stated at the morning standup that conc=
erns about the morning standup had been expressed to him, so he went over s=
ome of the reasons we have it: primarily to keep him informed of everyone=
=E2=80=99s tasks for that day, to keep everyone on the team generally infor=
med about what others are working on, for slight plan changes if necessary,=
 and to ensure that everyone shows up to work at a reasonably early time. H=
e also stated that if anyone has concerns about meetings we do they should =
come to him. I personally tend to show up to work a little before standup s=
o I don=E2=80=99t really start tasks until after it, but those who show up =
an hour or two beforehand do have their tasks interrupted by the standup, w=
hich I can imagine may be frustrating. I am unsure who specifically raised =
concerns and what those concerns are, so I can only guess the specifics.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"e5314f41be67">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b945559b6294">Received the capacitive sensor from McMaster t=
hat should be able to detect conductive or non-conductive surfaces so it sh=
ould be compatible with metal powder while inductive prox switches are not.=
 The critical issue with IR prox switches is that once the powder gets too =
close, they stop detecting it, so if the hopper overfills then it will keep=
 overfilling. Since the powder loads from the side of the hopper and the se=
nsor is in the middle, I suspect that the sensor tends to trip during the i=
nitial powder loading cycle with the powder pile at an angle, and with furt=
her vibration applied during the spread, the powder pile will level out and=
 the powder in the center will rise even without the addition of more powde=
r. If the level rises above the IR prox switch=E2=80=99s detection threshol=
d, it will stop detecting the powder level, and will keep loading powder in=
definitely.</p>
<p local-id=3D"00c05490780b">I installed the new prox switch in the printed=
 bayonet connector that had M18X1 threads printed into it, and the fit was =
perfect: tight enough that it should hold in place even without a locking n=
ut, which is good because I lost the nuts that it came with. It is importan=
t to note that I printed the bayonet connector with integrated threads from=
 PETG-CF with a layer height of 0.08mm; with other materials and thicker la=
yers the threads may not form as well. I attached an appropriate Molex Micr=
o-Fit 3.0 connector to the sensor to interface it with the cable present in=
 the machine, inserted the bayonet connector into the new vibrating hat, an=
d will now install heat set inserts (I also ordered M4 heat set inserts alo=
ng with the capacitive sensor from McMaster. I will transfer the stainless =
steel sheet to the new vibrating hat, transfer the vibrator motor over, and=
 switch the hose duct. This vibrating hat has some improvements over the pr=
evious model; since I designed it without using SolidWorks' loft feature I =
was able to add fillets that should reduce the stress concentration of corn=
ers, and I lowered the hose duct interface as much as possible to make the =
angle of the hose as steep as possible to increase powder flowability.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ba2fdb294de3">I found the plastic nuts that came with the ca=
pacitive prox switch and installed one to lock the switch height. I also fo=
und that the sensor was sensitive enough to be triggered by the walls of th=
e hopper once it was installed, so I turned down the sensitivity with the a=
djustment screw. Even with the increased hose duct angle with the new vibra=
ting hat, the powder flow in the hose was insufficient, so I increased the =
vibrating hat voltage from 9V to 11V to help liquifact the powder, and that=
 seemed to work.</p>
<p local-id=3D"27e3173aa473">I noticed that the current design of the vibra=
ting hat actually has the vibrating sheet in contact with the wall of the h=
opper, so I redesigned it to be 2mm away from the wall (a larger gap would =
necessitate moving around some other components, which I would like to avoi=
d if possible).</p>
<p local-id=3D"a70165c5958b">Discussing the current design of the hopper, M=
ohsen requested there be height adjustability added to enable the target po=
wder height to be set to different levels, and a way to trigger the sensor =
continuously so that the hopper can be emptied towards the end of the print=
. For the latter, I recommended adding a feature on the vibrating hat that =
you can insert the sensor into to trigger it continuously. This does carry =
with it the risk that powder will get kicked up through the now-empty senso=
r hole, so I should also print a plug connected to a lanyard to close it up=
 when the sensor is slotted into the holder. As for adjusting powder height=
, I do not have a design yet. Adjusting the sensor sensitivity could achiev=
e this goal to some extent, but it would likely not give a range more than =
a few millimeters, since if it set too high it will be triggered by the wal=
ls, and the range at the highest functional sensitivity is only ~5mm (I thi=
nk, I have not yet measured it with powder, only various metal, plastic, an=
d flesh/bone objects available in the print chamber).</p>
<p local-id=3D"28034f1a108f">R&amp;D Weekly Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"ecb3b2d46497">No-Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b92634e23434">6 prints on P6 w/17-4PH -</p>
<ul local-id=3D"6c3bc5a8a9c5">
<li local-id=3D"9456884b4d64">
<p local-id=3D"dedcd161c8bb">AEM part, air dry, corner curling.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"666503e9ca20">
<p local-id=3D"fbdea8f0681c">Repeated, screw fell from dispenser and fell i=
nto V tank, repaired damage.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9d1723d0056a">
<p local-id=3D"c955e50481f1">Switched to microchannel air dry, 1st print fa=
iled, nozzle cooled too much.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"42595e864021">
<p local-id=3D"1527fd532ee0">Repeated, added 60s delay to ensure dispenser =
reached temperature, same issue seen.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"22be1ab3763d">
<p local-id=3D"de4952555044">Started new print w/silicone rubber sheet insu=
lating syringe heater and nozzle, solved problem, may be able to remove 60s=
 delay, saw chipping and layer curling but was able to complete print.</p><=
/li>
<li local-id=3D"ce2ccd8bf8a6">
<p local-id=3D"bde893f2a971">Repeated test with fan drying which resolved c=
urling, but a single cut was not clean possibly due to insufficient drying,=
 so Pourya switched to 2 cuts.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"44623b1b58a8">
<p local-id=3D"c9bb4f38b597">Started another print today increasing the hum=
idity setting by 5% points and temperature by 2C on THC. Print is in progre=
ss, but chipping has already been seen</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"a802eaeadcd4">Mason noted that different end mills (# of flu=
tes, diameter) require different feeds and speeds to achieve comparable res=
ults (trying to reduce chipping seen).</p>
<p local-id=3D"f97c55c0929e">Next week plans to repeat tests with different=
 feeds and speeds to reduce chipping. Section printed sample to inspect cha=
nnels.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7375530c87d7">For copper, printed features detached during t=
he dewaxing process. Cake was dewaxed in Keith furnace with a peak temperat=
ure of 200C. Ran another print without sacrificial filler, using a 1 flute =
end mill and a 4 flute end mill cutting at the same time. With the part mad=
e with the single flute end mill, features detached. Repeated this test 3 t=
imes to confirm that the root cause is the number of flutes on the end mill=
. The severity of missing features varied from test to test but features we=
re always missing with the 1 flute end mill. Pourya pointed out that having=
 switched to a 1/8=E2=80=9D collet may have changed the runout of the end m=
ill.</p>
<p local-id=3D"29ef798f5938">Plan next week is adjust feed rate to reduce c=
hipping issues without adding sacrificial filler.</p>
<p local-id=3D"57f4c8636b9f">Marcus, Payman, Alex, and Pourya plan to meet =
on Monday to plan experiments for the week.</p>
<p local-id=3D"74378ba902f6">Air drying:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e121033d658">Ning and Mohsen worked on increasing the humid=
ity in the printer to reduce curling by adding a humidifier. Humidity was k=
ept within nominal range during print. This suggests to Mohsen that the iss=
ue is airflow and not humidity. Hardware for a new airflow system will arri=
ve on Monday. Printed new dry airflow nozzles. Goal was to reduce air flow.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"4d189235307e">I asked Mohsen whether he has ever seen a succ=
essful print with air drying, and he said he has not; air drying has always=
 caused layers to curl up after layer 10.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0f3a2414983">Payman and Marcus say to pause work on air dry=
ing in favor of hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3c1d128bd8a8">Payman, Marcus, and Alex had a meeting before =
this R&amp;D meeting, and one of their conclusions was to deprioritize redu=
cing layer speed in favor of optimizing for part quality.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4a05c78b401e">Mohsen questioned how the green part strength =
varies between 17-4PH and copper parts, suggesting that issues we are seein=
g may be due to decreased green strength. Mason pointed out that they use m=
uch more binder for each layer on copper than is used on 17-4PH.</p>
<p local-id=3D"eb72f968d9b3">Laser:</p>
<p local-id=3D"3963787a8685">Mason did some tests to study the effect of ch=
anging the number of passes. No matter what parameters were used, it did no=
t seem that going from 2 to 3 passes yielded the same beneficial returns as=
 going from 1 to 2, and more melting was seen after 3 passes, so he conclud=
es that it is better to focus on 2 passes. Experimental design focused on d=
epth control is still in progress. Main motivation for depth control is for=
 3D parts. He pointed out that depth consistency is more valuable at this p=
oint than absolute depth value. Tried to get the MOPA laser to perform more=
 like a diode CW laser by changing pulse width and frequency settings. Cut =
a gear using the previous optimized settings and a box outline and hatched =
box with the Claude-recommended settings. The new settings were found to yi=
eld inferior results (powder melting/sintering in areas where energy was ap=
plied) to previous optimized settings, and questions whether it is worth pu=
rsing these alternative settings. The diode-like settings performed fine on=
 the first layer, but melted/sintered the powder on every later layer. Paym=
an stated that he did not see powder sintering/melting using the XTool on t=
he Ultimaker, even when testing with up to 20 layers. Marcus raised points =
about the motivation behind the test. Mason has scrap copper green parts to=
 test given to him by Pourya, but has not had the chance to test them yet. =
Needs to get scrap 316L parts from Dong if possible.</p>
<p local-id=3D"741c39c7f141">Meeting on Monday to do more planning on laser=
 experiments. Marcus asked whether there is any testing he is interested in=
 doing. Mason asked Payman about whether he had made any 3D parts on the Ul=
timaker, and he said that he had stopped working on it. Mason has been work=
ing with Claude to make code to support making 3D parts by interfacing betw=
een the Raspberry Pi, his laptop running LightBurn, and the Duet. He asked =
whether he can continue working on this, and Payman provisionally said no (=
=E2=80=9Cnot yet=E2=80=9D haha), but it will be discussed further at Monday=
=E2=80=99s meeting. Mason also ran some experiments with adjusting curing t=
ime after the cut, which did not yield significant results. Noticed in prev=
ious prints, as layers built up the sintering/melting behavior would get wo=
rse, but curing the layers randomized the sintering/melting results. Procur=
ed an IR thermometer to install on the LVP to measure the layer (will be di=
scussed further on Monday at meeting whether he can proceed with this). Mas=
on did experiments with changing focus to adjust cut width (roughly corresp=
onding to spot size). Noted that cut order is significant; there is more de=
bris seen on the later cuts than the first cuts. Total cut time when this p=
henomenon was seen is ~40s.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2d9cf3c39c01">Next week:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8320b364e064">Meeting on Monday to discuss project planning.=
 Mason proposes working on installing an IR temperature sensor in the time =
before they have their planning meeting. Right now the IR camera has been m=
oved off-center to make room for the laser, which means it has difficulty g=
etting a good temperature value of the layer. Payman is pushing back on Mas=
on working on it because he wants to be able to record thermal images of th=
e layer. Mason will work on doing a proof of concept test with cutting copp=
er on Monday morning.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a2625fd3c5d8">Payman is concerned that multiple projects are=
 being rolled up in each project (i.e. no depowdering and spindle selection=
), and wants to have a clearer plan about deliverables for the project, eac=
h week, each month, etc.</p>
