<li local-id=3D"d8ffb70fb00b">
<p local-id=3D"e7c8b61f3b4e">Look into issue with gate not opening - DONE</=
p>
<ul local-id=3D"e07628a32d3d">
<li local-id=3D"c7a5210aa49a">
<p local-id=3D"2e82d28c7110">Due to the nature of limit switch wiring and t=
he Duet board=E2=80=99s limitation to a single endstop per axis</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9f0db1e04064">
<p local-id=3D"7c6f7ede8f92">With my recently acquired knowledge about writ=
ing conditional loops in Duet macros, it should be possible to write a gate=
 open/close macro that does not use defined endstops. Instead, it will use =
generic Duet Expansion Board I/Os for the open and close switches.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b05bab7b1b35">
<p local-id=3D"3e768eeec4cb">As a temporary patch, I increased the distance=
 the stepper motor moves to open the gate in open-loop mode before it start=
s paying attention to the limit switch. This fixed the problem at the momen=
t, but it is not sufficiently robust and also means that if the gate open m=
acro is called when the gate is already at the open position, it will drive=
 into the gate open hard stops until it starts detecting the limit switch. =
This will cause increased stress on the cam roller and the stepper motor, p=
ossibly leading to mechanical failure over time, and is therefore unaccepta=
ble on a production system.</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"a18e433b10f9">2026-03-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"bbdba8a5a255">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d42390b0b2a0">Several project replanning meetings. My questi=
on to Claude based on my limited understanding of the goals was:</p>
<p local-id=3D"64a5e8427ec0">What do you call it when an engineering projec=
t that has been going on for a year is replanned to change the deliverables=
 and the way progress is measured (i.e. adding goals for each week, month, =
etc.)?</p>
<p local-id=3D"7b0249c850a8">What you're describing is commonly called a <s=
trong>rebaseline</strong> (or <strong>re-baseline</strong>).</p>
<p local-id=3D"6947923a5ade">More specifically, it involves a few overlappi=
ng concepts depending on context:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8f6c50bcee71"><strong>Rebaseline</strong> is the primary ter=
m =E2=80=94 it means formally resetting the project's performance measureme=
nt baseline (PMB) to reflect new scope, schedule, or cost targets. It's wid=
ely used in defense and aerospace program management (EVM/EVMS contexts esp=
ecially).</p>
<p local-id=3D"08e8ea156fc7"><strong>Replanning</strong> is the broader act=
ivity that leads to a rebaseline =E2=80=94 restructuring the work, schedule=
, and metrics going forward. The two terms are often used together: "replan=
ning and rebaselinig."</p>
<p local-id=3D"690c9ae4a814"><strong>Restructuring</strong> is sometimes us=
ed when the changes are significant enough to alter the fundamental organiz=
ation of the work breakdown structure (WBS) or reporting hierarchy.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cff382cbc229">In more formal project management frameworks (=
like <strong>Earned Value Management</strong>, which is common in aerospace=
/defense), the specific actions involved would include:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"f579aa39-9009-446b-9680-fa388ba4ba8a">
<li local-id=3D"237b8446-83bd-443c-8b85-ccdb2b48b16f">
<p local-id=3D"9f1eefb212e6">Redefining the <strong>Work Breakdown Structur=
e (WBS)</strong></p></li>
<li local-id=3D"4e60f16d-c219-4798-873b-445f2a4f375e">
<p local-id=3D"e0d543618782">Resetting <strong>Control Account Plans (CAPs)=
</strong></p></li>
<li local-id=3D"5711b926-3570-4c7b-b10a-c953422fa418">
<p local-id=3D"2feae8a7b454">Establishing new <strong>Planned Value (PV)</s=
trong> curves</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"074d4db7-fdb9-451e-b8a4-7b6b6a54aed5">
<p local-id=3D"1a9aebc55de4">Adding time-phased <strong>budgets</strong> ti=
ed to weekly/monthly milestones</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"26b44462f7ba">The addition of structured weekly/monthly goal=
s specifically is often called <strong>time-phasing the budget</strong> or =
adding <strong>rolling wave planning</strong> detail to near-term work pack=
ages.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d93392f9259f">So the short answer: <strong>rebaseline</stron=
g> is the term most engineers and program managers would reach for in that =
situation.</p>
