<p local-id=3D"f30317d24c00">Mohsen and Ben encountered an issue with the h=
opper. The symptom was that there was a deep indentation in the build tank =
powder (roughly 4mm hemispherical) and the Y axis gantry was unable to home=
. It was jamming when the hopper gate opening was right above the -Y edge o=
f the build tank. Ben heard the stepper motors stalling, and hit the e-stop=
. The high-level root cause of the issue was an M4X10mm blue-dyed zinc SHCS=
 that had become jammed in the gate, partially protruding from the gate ope=
ning. This dented the gate and may have damaged the edge of the build tank,=
 but does not seem to have harmed the spreader bar. I worked with Mohsen to=
 hammer out the dent at the edge of the gate and polished it with a Hard Bl=
ack Arkansas Oil Sharpening Stone. The damage is visible but should not imp=
air the functionality of the hopper. The low-level root cause of the issue =
was FOD control; since Mohsen reduced the number of screws used to mount th=
e hopper vibrating hat, he had screws left over, and without careful accoun=
ting of all the screws removed it was easy to drop one in the hopper and no=
t notice it. There are a few ways to prevent this in the future: a FOD scre=
en in the hopper would prevent screws from reaching the gate but could poss=
ibly cause issues with powder flow, a standard procedure of emptying out an=
d inspecting the inside of the hopper any time the vibrating hat is removed=
 or replaced, a procedure of carefully accounting for all the screws presen=
t in the machine, or captive fasteners used for any components that may nee=
d to be detached in the machine would each prevent this issue.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b4d468cb7b0c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"5aae83900c18"></p>
