<p local-id=3D"47d73cfedc94">There are some possible temperature read error=
s with the current IR camera that concerns Midhun because the experimental =
results and predictions are dependent on accurate measurement. I would reco=
mmend the following:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"97b9aba3d016">
<li local-id=3D"f1c2115e6186">
<p local-id=3D"bebb45339796">Determine what parameters need to be measured =
(ambient temp and humidity, layer temp (either an image or single-point), e=
tc.)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"f96874986eb7">
<p local-id=3D"ecd6a6cf4b69">Select properly calibrated sensors</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"49c2882ed67c">
<p local-id=3D"eb1446f56992">Install the sensors in a way that supports acc=
urate measurements</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2ecbbaea0ef6">
<p local-id=3D"8447c5df05a5">Test the sensors in-situ</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"606c36042f8b">
<p local-id=3D"36560833c098">Ensure that the sensor values are properly rec=
orded during the print</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"f0671ed94b0d">Midhun recommends inerting the machine to impr=
ove the quality of laser interaction based on results he has seen in papers=
. He pointed out that there are significant safety hazards of nitrogen use =
that we should pay attention to, including wearing nitrogen sensors and ens=
uring that no one sticks their head in the machine when it is inerted.</p>
<p local-id=3D"23cbfada5f5f">Midhun asked about the current status of the n=
o-depowdering project, and Mason pointed out concerns about visual part qua=
lity and the fact there have been no tests done on quality of parts after s=
intering, such as porosity and conductivity and sinter density. There has a=
lso been minimal formal experimentation, data collection, and documentation=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"953c7cf1986b">Cisco has raised concerns about changes to the=
 copper pod, including adding humidifiers for some unclear reason.</p>
<p local-id=3D"48eaf9d2c7fb">Mason recommends we use a binder jetting head =
to apply the sacrificial filler.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f3529f13dccf">Midhun has been told that he will be supportin=
g the no-depowdering process with copper. He said that he is not going to d=
o anything he doesn=E2=80=99t understand, which I like.</p>
<p local-id=3D"32719c100122"></p>
<p local-id=3D"ec66954bf671">2026-03-17</p>
<p local-id=3D"414e884ed613">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"69caa4a41634">Issues with the hopper binding and the hopper =
open/close stepper motor shaft bending were seen yesterday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7b8cba723ff8">I put together a mold for the new higher-flow =
doser ball, but I did not have enough Mold Star 30 silicone to completely f=
ill the mold, so I need to order some more and complete the pour after it a=
rrives.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b8f20d44beb">I took apart the hopper gate system and replac=
ed the bronze/graphite bushings with linear bearings. The gate actually see=
ms more wobbly now (the bearings don=E2=80=99t keep its motion exactly line=
ar, they allow some rotation of the gate) but this is not necessarily criti=
cal to the function.</p>
<p local-id=3D"67a58cdf4183">I also hammered the stepper motor shaft back t=
o being roughly straight yesterday, and I am going to redesign the machined=
 cam component so that it extends to the base of the shaft so it is less li=
kely to bend.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a55ff43deacc">If these linear bearings do not work robustly,=
 I am considering switching to low-profile linear carriages.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0e870d257699">I entered an order request for another trial s=
ize package of Mold Star 30 from Reynolds Advanced Materials. This should g=
ive me more than enough to finish off this current mold, and make several m=
ore molds of a comparable size or maybe 1 or 2 larger molds (which I will n=
eed to make to mass-produce vibration isolators if we roll out to hoppers t=
o all the machines).</p>
<p local-id=3D"8291b9a8dd0d">I completed designing mounts to retrofit HOPPE=
R V5 for using linear carriages instead of linear rods. For production use,=
 they will need to be machined from aluminum but for initial testing I plan=
 to print them. I started a printing in PAHT-CF, but the surface finish was=
 quite poor so I cancelled the print, removed the roll of filament, and pla=
ced it in the filament dryer to dry overnight. This design will also only b=
e used if necessary; if the linear bearings and rods work OK then they woul=
d be simpler and cheaper to implement.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8a45b79ec3d7">Mohsen was trying to tilt the hopper farther f=
orward than it had been tilted before, so I looked at the side mounts and t=
here were two main problems: first, the adjustment stages were not contacti=
ng the dowel pins and so were tilted (dowel pins will be replaced with mach=
ined slots in V5.1), and second, the dovetail gib in the stages was loose t=
owards the bottom of the adjustment stage so it was possible for the mount =
to rotate slightly. I adjusted the gib (tightened the screw towards the bot=
tom of the hopper, loosened the screw towards the top) to tighten it up. Th=
e adjusting screws seemed pretty tight, but I would not be surprised if the=
 vibration being applied coupled with the torque from the stepper motor wei=
ght hanging off the side had loosened it. He also noted that the gate seeme=
d to be wobbly and not closing at one corner. I initially thought this was =
due to the linear bearings (which, admittedly, are somewhat loose), but the=
 gate should lock up when it is pressed forward until it is close to the ha=
rd stops. It turns out that the track roller on the cam had come loose, als=
o possible due to vibration as well as massive loading (the motor shaft had=
 become bent again, so clearly it=E2=80=99s being heavily loaded). I tighte=
ned the track roller, and I am also looking into replacing it with somethin=
g a little more robust, possibly a pair of bearings riding on a shoulder sc=
rew.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a04f8c740481">Mohsen called me over to look at the Hopper V5=
 prototype he is running on Pilot 2. He called me to look at powder buildup=
 in from of the gate when it is traversing in the -Y direction (dispensing =
powder and spreading a layer). The phenomenon did not seem to repeat when h=
e ran the spreading macro, which I suspect may be due to less weight on the=
 gate (he said the hopper is roughly 1/4 full right now). He also noted tha=
t when he had the vibrating hat held down by only 2 screws, there was visib=
le ripples on the layer, but when he used all 7 screws, the ripples went aw=
ay. It seems that the attachment between the vibrating hat and the vibratio=
n isolator directly affects the spread layer quality.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5a4e24ac78e0">Mohsen showed me a video of the hopper spreadi=
ng, and it seemed that the gate was shaving off the entire layer. It seems =
to me that it is doing a spread with the build at exactly the same height a=
s a previous spread. Mohsen said it is semi-random; he sees it happen maybe=
 once in 5 spreads. I suspect it is a macro issue, that for some reason the=
 macro is not lowering the V plate properly, but Mohsen seems confident in =
the macro being used (supposedly it is the same macro being called). I aske=
d him to keep an eye out for it and see if he can figure out a pattern.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7fc574ff3be7">Ben pointed out a possible issue: because the =
heater is currently sandwiched between the mist box and the hopper (if the =
heater is on the far side of the mist box, the gantry cannot travel far eno=
ugh for the heater to access the entire build plate), there is a risk of th=
e cables used to connect to the limit switches melting. The cable is a IGUS=
 continuous-flex cable, with an outer jacket of gray PVC and inner wire ins=
ulation of black TPE</p>
