<p local-id=3D"8f708261ee05">HOPPER/FLIP:<br>
Mohsen noted that the gate open stepper motor shaft was bent again, so I be=
nt it back into place and added some washers underneath it to help reinforc=
e it. I also removed the track roller, cleaned the threads on the cam and t=
he track roller with IPA, added some red loctite, and reinstalled it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"30580be8796f">Mohsen ran some spreads, and is still encounte=
ring issues with the stepper motor shaft bending. In HOPPER V5.1, I have ad=
ded a ball bearing installed in the stepper motor mount to provide support =
to the cam mount directly to try to keep the load off the stepper motor sha=
ft, but in the meantime I asked Mohsen to run the hopper with a reduced pow=
der load to try to decrease the load on the stepper motor cam.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3a749f6e7b41"></p>
<p local-id=3D"915353841fdd">R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e422a35ec879">No-Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"6aa663626a19">Broke out and sectioned a part from last week =
using dry air drying, significant chipping. Ran another print with adjusted=
 temp and humidity settings, with no change seen. Noted that there was low =
ambient humidity that day. Replace the tool bit, ran another print, saw chi=
pping. Dropped the feed rate with no noticeable change. Discovered that ali=
gnment between Mest unit and end mill have shifted after replacing the end =
mill. After adjusting the alignment by 100 micron Y and 50 micron X, and in=
ternal chipping was largely eliminated. Final conclusion was that the fix w=
as replacing the end mill and adjusting offset. Chipping can be caused by e=
ither a dull end mill or incorrect offset between the sacrificial filler di=
spenser and the spindle.</p>
<p local-id=3D"826ca44b04d2">After resolving the chipping issue, focus beca=
me improving the side wall quality. He increased sacrificial filler dispens=
er frequency from 54 to 74 Hz.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ab3d87979308">Marcus noted that experimenting with carbide e=
nd mill coatings may be beneficial, since we are seeing significant tool we=
ar with the single flute end mill. &lt;Suspect that tool sharpness is parti=
cularly critical for the no-depowdering process because we need to cut very=
 soft materials cleanly.&gt; After replacing the tool bit with a sharper on=
e, they ran some AEM-style prints with copper with better results. Pourya t=
ried melting out the sacrificial filler with a heat gun, and the part bulge=
d upward when heated and some of the small fin features were detached. Moti=
vation for using the heat gun was to rapidly remove the sacrificial filler.=
 &lt;If you skip a step, you will find 5 extra steps waiting for you.&gt; H=
e plans to repeat the test and instead use an oven cycle to melt out the sa=
crificial filler.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f6d9716130f6">Next week:</p>
<p local-id=3D"ad16d790828f">Removed sacrificial filler with Keith furnace.=
 Will try to print another part. Plans to start printing microchannel parts=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6a1f11b0efac">Hopper:</p>
<p local-id=3D"bf60fb600857">Powder loading found not to refill quickly</p>
<p local-id=3D"7f3a3e289069">Powder refill position blocks the fan, so it c=
an not load during layer cooling. Move refilling location to Y min position=
.</p>
