<p local-id=3D"939ed114732c">Laser Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"48c278c28163">Mason has fleshed out his laser comparison tes=
ting plan to the resolution of days and hours. After this meeting with me a=
nd Midhun (who is back from India), Mason is going to have a meeting with P=
ayman to go over this plan.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e3dbe0c3661f">Goal of laser project is to reduce cut time wi=
th a secondary goal of reduced cut size.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3bfd1da2e9f5">Mason wants to test different laser with diffe=
rent materials: 17-4PH, 316L, Cu.</p>
<p local-id=3D"45913b2dda0a">There is a spec limit of 250 micron maximum cu=
t width, which may be an issue on the diode laser due to previously seen re=
sults.</p>
<p local-id=3D"746d45e15f8a"></p>
<p local-id=3D"49bec2cf4002">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"095638e85535">The motor shaft had become bent again, so I be=
nt it back into place and machined the head of an M6 bolt into a spacer tha=
t I welded to the stainless steel cam mount to help move the bending moment=
 application point towards the base of the shaft, where it is better suppor=
ted. I also machined some new linear rod guides from a wear-resistant polym=
er rod I had around, making them tight to the shafts so that there is less =
slop in the gate. This also adds load to the motor because there is more dr=
ag in the new bushings, but hopefully they will break in over time.</p>
<p local-id=3D"212ed9740451">I prepared the doser ball V3 mold and did a po=
ur of 770 urethane (shore 70).</p>
<p local-id=3D"1de28b13284d">Mohsen was seeing an issue with the Duet board=
 not detecting the end stop limit switches, so I first tested the limit swi=
tch with a multimeter then checked the plugs, and they were not plugged int=
o the wire harness properly. There is a Y splitter that connects the two li=
mit switches in series to one 2-wire cable running to the Duet board, and i=
t was disconnected where the Y splitter connects to the 2-wire cable runnin=
g to the Duet board.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5b36c487cb03">I also did something that I should have done w=
eeks ago; I dropped the current of the gate close/open motor (A axis) from =
600 to 300 mA, so it is unable to bend the shaft even when it hits the hard=
 stops.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c03d7c02b65c">2026-03-24</p>
<p local-id=3D"3f11297340fc">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"feb7f8104226">Printed a new vibrating hat with the duct hose=
 connection rotated to 30 degrees towards +Y instead of 30 degrees -Y as it=
 has been. This is to be able to do hopper filling when the gantry is at th=
e -Y position, while the system is waiting for the layer to cool.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0f10dc877d78">I demolded the new high-flow doser ball, and i=
t mostly looks pretty good. There was some parting line residue at one poin=
t towards the top of the doser ball, but I shaved it off with a scalpel.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"3f6c1af1348d">I am continuing to work on the design of HOPPE=
R V5.1.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e5edd50ada0c">After printing 6 layers, the gate open/close s=
tepper motor shaft snapped, so I disassembled the gear box, welded the shaf=
t back together, machined the shaft on the lathe to be fairly straight, and=
 reassembled the planetary gear box. The shaft is slightly shorter than it =
was before because I machined off some of it to give flat surfaces and to e=
liminate the retaining ring groove (which is where it broke).</p>
<p local-id=3D"259e20ece0c0">Mohsen has only been filling the hopper about =
1/3 full by setting the powder dispenser to run for a set time each layer (=
I think he said 60 seconds, which seems long to me). I need to redesign the=
 capacitive sensor mount so that it is lower down, so the hopper can be fil=
led to precisely the same height each time.</p>
<p local-id=3D"14707a61d218">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"104210c1c0ae">Dong asked me for the link to the band saw bla=
de for cutting stainless steel and nickel alloys, and said that it needed t=
o be replaced. I asked whether the current blade was unable to cut some of =
our inconel samples right after being replaced, which I remember, and he ad=
mitted that it was possible. This will be the third identical blade install=
ed on the band saw; I installed the same model of blade when I first built =
the machine, it ran fine with minimal wear for years and several thousand p=
arts, but was unable to cut certain Inconel parts post-sintering (most part=
s it was fine with, but some would cause the blade to overheat and vaporize=
 the coolant and the linear stage jammed), so Dong had me replace the blade=
, which also worked fine for most Inconel parts but overheated on certain p=
arts, so now Dong is asking me to replace the blade again.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ac8b9c250514">For reference, the link to the blade is here: =
<a href=3D"https://www.mcmaster.com/4200A33/" local-id=3D"7bf9f5d060bf" dat=
a-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https=
://www.mcmaster.com/4200A33/</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"3205cc660d33">This obviously won=E2=80=99t solve the root ca=
use because none of the blades have shown significant wear and also worked =
fine on most parts, there were just some Inconel parts that caused issues. =
I would guess that those parts are case-hardened near the edge, because the=
 hardness reading towards the center of the part seemed fine. Inconel famou=
sly causes large heat buildup, so it could be that those parts just have a =
slightly different microstructure or formula than the rest of the parts.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"fd70ed9ecc2c">We can keep buying as many blades as Dong want=
s, but if we want to solve the problem we have several options:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"6eca02a08414">
<li local-id=3D"db0ab6a7c01e">
<p local-id=3D"96e0a56c3f87">If a sample does not cut well, just don=E2=80=
=99t section it</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a3c81a433f63">
<p local-id=3D"330f57afd61c">If a sample does not cut well, section it usin=
g an abrasive saw instead of the band saw</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"58d928751618">
<p local-id=3D"0ea969bc04c3">Get a sectioning saw (Claude claims that a dia=
mond saw is unsuitable because the diamond reacts with nickel superalloys, =
so we would want to use a different kind of abrasive saw blade).</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"65fd6828be4a">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"4bfa1c6ad35d">ZZ sent out an email regarding preparing Pilot=
 7 for the baseline test</p>
<p local-id=3D"cd52192caca6">2026-03-25</p>
<p local-id=3D"80d7c7303f55">PILOT: Ran a test by loading 110mm of powder i=
nto the U tank of Pilot 7, then ran the powder compression macro (which mov=
es the U tank down 10mm, then up 10mm, then repeats that cycle to compress =
the powder). The macro sets the speed at 3000 mm/min, but the max speed in =
the config file was set to 1000 mm/min initially, so that is the speed at w=
hich it would travel. I ran the macro at both 1000 and 750 mm/min, and it s=
eemed to work fine without losing a significant number of steps or making a=
ny noise. I ended up setting the max speed to 750 mm/min in the config file=
 to be safer, which may compress the powder less per cycle but Mohsen feels=
 that we can increase the number of cycles to achieve the same compression.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"8fab32ee754d">&lt;I would recommend compressing the powder w=
ith a weight on top of a UHMW sheet as was tested several years ago. This w=
ould both compress the powder more while also resulting in a more even comp=
ression that would be easier to level out before printing (when using the c=
urrent powder compression macro, the powder compresses more where it contac=
ts the sides than the center, and compresses still more at the corners.&gt;=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"f215a15878d0">The print of the newly designed mist box that =
has cutouts for glass windows on both sides and is being printed on the Bam=
bu X1E (Anvil) in PAHT-CF ran out of filament, so I loaded a new roll. This=
 new roll has not been dried overnight, so I am unsure what its print quali=
ty will be. The print quality of the previous roll, however, looks quite go=
od. Not quite as good as PETG-CF, but way better than when the roll had not=
 been dried overnight. I think the key takeaway is that the PAHT-CF (CF-rei=
nforced nylon) needs to be dried overnight at 60-65C before starting each p=
rint.</p>
<p local-id=3D"96d630737ce6">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"6e39f6ac4a7a">The longer prox switch holder that was printed=
 last night did not come out with good threads, and also did not have a hol=
e for accessing the sensitivity adjustment screw when the capacitive sensor=
 is installed, and was also not designed with an optimal length that would =
place the sensor at the right position when it has all threads engaged but =
is protruding from the holder as much as possible while still having the th=
reads engaged.</p>
<p local-id=3D"36c480cb37bb">The hopper installed in Pilot 2 is not closing=
 fully. I increased the current from 300 to 400 mA and it still did not clo=
se completely, so I asked Mohsen to remove it for further inspection. I sus=
pect that the cam is grinding into the gate, causing it to lock up even whe=
n the hopper is empty.</p>
<p local-id=3D"87195d1c6a65">That did not turn out to be the issue. I think=
 the gate may have been getting stopped by the area where a wedge feature o=
n the gate contacts a wedge feature on the aluminum frame, so I removed the=
 gate and ground down the stainless steel a bit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7961b78a4911">I tried to print a new mist box body from PAHT=
-CF last night, but the print failed because it became detached from the bu=
ild plate on one side because the part curled up, even with the heated cham=
ber. I am reprinting the body from PETG-CF, and I also realized that if I p=
rint it inverted it takes less support material and, more importantly, the =
section of the mist box that contacts the powder build will be flatter beca=
use it is printed last and not attached to the build plate, where it is lik=
ely to distort during the FDM print.</p>
<p local-id=3D"15e462b95665">Continued to refine the design of HOPPER V5.1.=
 I figured out a way to move away from the wedge contact surface entirely, =
by extending the leading edge of the sides of the gate and adding a flat fe=
ature that is parallel to the (now) flat bottom of the side aluminum pieces=
 comprising the hopper body. I will install felt to act as a sliding seal b=
etween these surfaces to allow them to move freely while blocking powder fl=
ow.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper=
