<p local-id=3D"0c151b3cf4ea">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"c88f73d9f6b0">Pourya asked me about the Offset Tables used o=
n the Saffron printers, so I asked Devin about them, read through the Confl=
uence documentation, and asked Devin about them again to confirm what it se=
emed they were. It turns out that it is referring to a single X Y offset va=
lue unique to each machine; having an offset defined by each machine=E2=80=
=99s slightly unique hardware makes it possible to run the same sliced G co=
de on different printers. The reason it is called an Offset Table is becaus=
e it is using a Trak function that provides for multiple coordinate offset =
values, likely for multiple tools that might be used with a tool changer, b=
ut in our case we are only using one offset value in the table to compensat=
e for slightly different spindle positions relative to the cake (I suspect =
this is mainly due to variations in the position of the homing sensors on t=
he glass scales, but it could also be due to dimensional variations in the =
machined parts or similar).</p>
<p local-id=3D"446ee43cfe66"></p>
<p local-id=3D"e9b6f24d6d8e">2026-03-26</p>
<p local-id=3D"db4435b915b2">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2cd65a086966">I cast another doser ball of the newer many-gr=
oove design. I am still having difficult filling the mold; there is a fairl=
y small annular opening at the top around the splined shaft, so I have been=
 filling the funnel-shaped cavity above the doser ball and squeezing the mo=
ld to squeeze out a bubble. This works, but runs the risk of urethane flowi=
ng into the parting line cuts in the mold, which requires more post-process=
ing of the doser ball and increases the risk of FOD in the print if any rem=
aining urethane bits break off. I haven=E2=80=99t come up with a really ele=
gant solution to filling the mold; increasing the annulus size would work b=
ut would make cutting off the cast funnel feature more difficult to do prec=
isely, and the precision of that cut defines the seal of the doser ball tow=
ards its edge. I could inject the urethane into the mold at one point, but =
this would burn up a syringe for each pour and would be difficult to fit in=
; I would likely need to redesign and reprint the upper 3D printed splined =
shaft guide. I could hold the mold at an angle and pour the urethane toward=
s the lower end of the annulus, but I would need to keep the pour flow rate=
 low to prevent urethane from completely sealing the annulus.</p>
<p local-id=3D"177b82c73ba0">I am printing a PAHT-CF gate on the Bambu X1E =
right now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9141264d1faa">My plan is to go over the design of HOPPER V5.=
1 at the R&amp;D meeting tomorrow to get the design signed off so I can ord=
er the machined parts, then while waiting for the machined parts, I will ma=
ke a 3D printed version to prove out the general design and get any issues =
with COTS parts ironed out.</p>
<p local-id=3D"851a29f6f0df">I am working on designing a guard to protect t=
he linear carriage from powder. It will not give it an airtight seal, but i=
t should help to keep the powder off it for longer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e0efcc3b6441">There are issues being seen with powder buildu=
p in the feed hose, so I am exploring alternative hose duct options to try.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"c256f4ee8211">The motor was experiencing issues, so Mohsen r=
emoved it from the hopper. It seemed that it was jamming up at a point in i=
ts rotation, even with the cam removed. I took it apart and noted that the =
shaft was bent, and was also beginning to crack at the joint where I welded=
 it (although it had not snapped off yet, which I=E2=80=99m pretty proud of=
). I removed it, bent everything back into place as best I could, ground a =
groove into the crack, rewelded it, and turned it down to be a loose fit in=
 the motor gearbox=E2=80=99s main output bearing. When I assembled the gear=
box last time the shaft was a press fit, which I suspect may have forced so=
me gears to start grinding into the shaft-mounted gear carrier (as evidence=
d by bare metal exposed at two points where two of the the gears on the gea=
r carrier would contact the carrier). With the loose fit of the shaft, I am=
 hoping that there will be less of a tendency for components to grind into =
each other, although there will be some loss of support from the bearing. I=
t is actually possible that the two gears were cutting into the gear carrie=
r because of a bending moment applied to the shaft that was not properly su=
pported by the bearing.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a2194039a62e">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b228877adbb9">The printed mist box looks like it came out we=
ll, and the glass windows I need for the new mist box arrived last night, s=
o I can put it together by gluing the glass windows to the printed mist box=
 with SLA resin injected through a needle attached to a syringe.</p>
<p local-id=3D"75591355c199">I assembled the mist box by gluing on the thre=
e glass panels with SLA resin and coated the inside surfaces with SLA resin=
 to waterproof them (the SLA resin used is an old ABS-like gray resin from =
Amazon that=E2=80=99s been around for a few years). Since the mist box is t=
oo large to fit in the FormLabs curing chamber, and I cannot find any of th=
e gray UV lights, I am using the cheap cylindrical curing chamber I brought=
 in by holding it and pointing it at the outsides, and to cure the inside o=
f the mist box I set the cylinder on top of the mist box so it exposes the =
inside pretty well to UV light. I will need to ream out the heat set insert=
 holes to .199=E2=80=9D because there was some SLA resin that wound up in t=
he holes which would interfere with the process of installing the inserts.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"85ca724b22e7">I cleaned the mist box with 99% IPA, reamed ou=
t the holes, and installed M3 heat set inserts, then gave the mist box to B=
en to install.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a486529305df">2026-03-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"ff802eae735a">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e28d297e5e0e">The gate seems to be opening and closing OK af=
ter my repairs yesterday. Mohsen screwed the capacitive prox switch farther=
 into the mount so that the end is flush with the bottom of the mount, and =
the indicator light and sensitivity adjusting screw are exposed at the top.=
 I had to adjust the sensitivity to compensate; I suspect that the sensor w=
as detecting the 3D printed material surrounding its end. Mohsen noted that=
 powder was still building up in the hose duct during the refill cycle, so =
I shortened the hose by ~12mm and made sure that the gantry was traveling t=
o the maximum Y position. This seems to get the hose duct angled sufficient=
ly that the powder flows through it, although the higher tension on the hos=
e duct due to shortening it does increase the risk of the hose duct failing=
 or becoming detached from either the vibrating hat or the cartridge dock h=
ose connection point.</p>
<p local-id=3D"656f2a20967f">I received the industrial limit switches from =
automation direct, and they seem to perform as advertised.</p>
<p local-id=3D"216b23229b8b">I printed out the parts for casting a hopper v=
ibration isolator using the same procedure as last time (which involves hot=
 gluing sacrificial FDM parts to the outside of the top and bottom vibratio=
n isolator panels) and did a pour with PMC 744 that I mixed with the flexib=
ilizer; I used 50g B, 100g A, and mixed 15g of flexibilizer with B for 1 mi=
nute before mixing A and B. This was clearly way too much; I was unable to =
fully degass the urethane in the mixing cup I was using so I had to do a de=
gass-pour-degass-pour etc in the vibration isolator and doser ball molds I =
had filled (I was also making a V3 doser ball from the same urethane, as lo=
ng as I had extra urethane to spare).</p>
<p local-id=3D"ef720c375361">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"55b85a67cca8">The R&amp;D meeting was moved to Monday, so I =
will not be able to order the machined parts today as I had hoped.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3d5997715c53">Running another print for Payman=E2=80=99s pri=
nter, which is a kind of most box/guide. I have run several of them over th=
e past few days. As far as I can tell, the intent is to uniformly apply bin=
der to a layer by having a line of dispensed mist that travels along the bu=
ild area. To get this line of mist, he has been exploring designs that use =
the current nebulizer to spray mist into a chamber with complex geometry to=
 transition the shape of spray from a circular cone to a line. As you might=
 expect, the vast majority of the mist condenses on the tortuous channels. =
His informal experiments do seem to be yielding uniform binder coverage, bu=
t I am unaware of any quantification he has done so the results are current=
ly anecdotal. I am also unsure why he is doing these experiments and develo=
ping his hardware at all; it functionally overlaps with what we are develop=
ing (my hopper and pilot printer, Mason=E2=80=99s laser) so it seems like a=
 duplication of effort.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e1cb1ff189ff">2026-03-30</p>
