e</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"c4dd744dd492">
<p local-id=3D"b5ca4bde62d0">Powder cartridge splined shaft shifts</p>
<ul local-id=3D"9afc1fce32f5">
<li local-id=3D"257867488602">
<p local-id=3D"69a04ca0f0bc">Need to design a more robust splined shaft sha=
ft collar</p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"db7d37e597dd"></p>
<p local-id=3D"d78702e65c64">Senai had some very interesting ideas for the =
hopper; he suggested having a cam on each side driven by a single shaft who=
se track rollers engage with each carriage, more or less aligned with each =
carriage rail (OK, I fleshed out some of the ideas there, but the core conc=
ept of two cams to minimize torque was his).</p>
<p local-id=3D"51c186893deb">Weekly R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"bef82add0942">No-Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2141ac7c05a1">Goal of week was to increase the quality of pr=
inted part side walls. Underfilling of the cuts seems to be a major contrib=
utor to poor side wall surface finish. Increasing the frequency (increasing=
 flow) does not seem to improve quality because additional sacrificial fill=
er does not stay in the cut, it will travel to one side or the other. Layer=
s where a whole layer facing operation was completed had improved quality a=
s compared with targeted machining of the cut only. The quality of east/wes=
t walls was better than north/south walls. The facing operation travels nor=
th/south. The larger end mill causes marks on the layer when changing direc=
tion (marks look like the ones you would see with a chipped tooth, but this=
 end mill only has one flute).</p>
<p local-id=3D"f3cecab45f6b">Ran another test where the facing operation fo=
llowed the same path as the original cuts (the facing operation is only don=
e with the large diameter end mill), and did not see any significant change=
s. There does not seem to be any difference between using different diamete=
rs of end mill or different paths.</p>
<p local-id=3D"580cff1ed99d">No heat pass is currently used so the layer te=
mperature matches ambient temperature in the machine. Pourya notes that the=
 temperature of the sacrificial filler does not impact its flow into the cu=
ts because it cools rapidly when it contacts the cake, so it is the cake te=
mperature that has more of an impact on the flow properties of the filler a=
gent when droplets hit the cut. &lt;I would recommend ensuring that the cak=
e temperature is controlled when applying the filler agent by using a heate=
r as necessary, and possibly switch to a higher temperature sacrificial fil=
ler if necessary.&gt; Mason noted and Pourya agreed that there is currently=
 no quantification of surface finish, it is only measured visually. &lt;Thi=
s makes it difficult to quantifiably compare different settings and approac=
hes.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"73d3179924ad">Pourya tried to redo a test, but the Festool f=
ailed, so he reran the test with the StoneVac instead. When starting a prin=
t today, he noted that the humidity in the machine was 11%, likely because =
the THC failed to turn off the dry air injection valve.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1caebb6e7dc7">&lt;Claude suggested that sugar is somewhat so=
luble in PEG, so I believe it is possible that when the sacrificial filler =
is melted out it debinds some of the powder and carries it with it.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"63778251170b">&lt;It seems clear that the root cause of the =
issue, however, is voids in the cuts after filling, facing, and spreading f=
resh powder. Pourya also noted several times that there are voids in the sa=
crificial filler after the facing operation. I would recommend resolving th=
is issue before doing anything else because it seems unlikely that it is po=
ssible to have a good print without completely filling cuts with sacrificia=
l filler.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"0200484d6c52">Pourya is trying another test by applying a mu=
ch larger amount of PEG than the previous tests, to ensure that there are n=
o gaps between the wall and the sacrificial filler that will then be filled=
 with dry powder on the next powder spread.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e256890e21b8">&lt;First note of the no-depowdering project i=
n my notes was 2025-01-14, so the no-depowdering project following the gene=
ral approach of filling the cuts with a sacrificial filler has been going o=
n for at least 14 months. Since this is only a few days after I started tak=
ing notes here, and based on what I said in my notes here, I suspect that i=
t was already in progress at that point.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"f704092436bf">Pourya also removed the sacrificial filler fro=
m the latest copper part, and the process of removing the filler caused som=
e of the printed features to detach. Payman and Pourya decided to switch to=
 printing a microchannel design instead of the more complex AEM-style part =
where the sacrificial filler thermal removal process damaged the part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"45005c63917b">There are some difficulties with making functi=
onal G-code for the print; the original sliced file needs to be adjusted ma=
nually (one reason for this is the Offset Table X/Y offset value that is un=
ique to each Saffron printer).</p>
<p local-id=3D"fd906b0d14b8">Marcus recommends trying a geometry that enabl=
es the sacrificial filler to drain out when melted instead of needing to fl=
ow through the porous part. Pourya thinks that the liquid PEG is too viscou=
s to flow out through holes, it soaks into the porous part and cake and is =
decomposed during the thermal sacrificial filler removal procedure.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c7ed54d4bc79">Laser Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"f9eca9fc8495">New types of lasers were ordered and arrived. =
There was a planning meeting conducted to determine experimental protocol a=
nd short term and long term goals. Surface roughness of cut walls is a prio=
rity.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e6c38b28518">Mason sealed Pilot 3 to inert it, injected nit=
rogen to reduce the oxygen concentration to 3%, and cut/hatched some square=
s. He noted that he is unable to run the LVP at the same time as inerting b=
ecause the LVP is based on using dry air injection and a non-recirculating =
vacuum. He is trying to run tests to compare the atmospheric cutting proces=
s with the inerted process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1dfd16a07b15">This week he plans to spend a few days working=
 on Payman=E2=80=99s Pilot Ultra &lt;hmm=E2=80=A6&gt; to get a =E2=80=9Cfee=
l=E2=80=9D for it, continue benchmarking the MOPA laser on Pilot 3, and eve=
ntually transfer a diode laser to Pilot 3 to compare that against other las=
er technologies. Mason has also been measuring the surface roughness of the=
 sides of green parts using the Keyence microscope=E2=80=99s 3D measurement=
 function.</p>
<p local-id=3D"dfa2119ba361">Mason is also researching a way to incorporate=
 vacuum functionality into the inerted system.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f6221957df49">He asked whether he should make slides for thi=
s weekly meeting the way that they were done at some point. Payman wants th=
is meeting to be brief &lt;also hmm=E2=80=A6&gt; but Marcus notes that it i=
s important that results are documented.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a124a933ea52">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"eb67c164fd4f">Add angle cuts on the gate</p>
<p local-id=3D"09e5767b482c">TO DO:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"9db0c5d63513">
<li local-id=3D"bdc0f4f1b475">
<p local-id=3D"dc120f3c0931"><strong>Finalize design</strong></p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e34822a70604">
