<p local-id=3D"34d3e383a585">PILOT ULTRA:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7575f0e462b6">Made prototype of simplified hopper that works=
 to make thin layers. Working on mist box.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f525b561db9a"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a2ad63bd299c">Marcus recommends setting up regular meetings =
for copper no-depowdering experiments on the Saffron printers as well as th=
e Pilot Baselining project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ee57971f1b77">2026-03-31</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b56b2b93c38">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"aa61880ee33e">Redesigning the cam system per Senai=E2=80=99s=
 suggestions. The core concept is that there are two cams each aligned with=
 the linear carriage rail to minimize the bending moment applied to the car=
riage by the cams, and the two cams are mounted to a shaft that is driven b=
y the stepper motor. An alternative approach would have been to use two ste=
pper motors, one on each side, which would be mechanically simpler (at leas=
t from my perspective, from a total system level there would be more moving=
 parts because of doubling the number of planetary gearboxes, but from the =
custom mechanicals would be simpler) and would allow for each side to be mo=
ved somewhat independently to ensure that both sides of the gate close comp=
letely, but keeping them adequately synchronized would be trickier than wit=
h a single stepper motor using mechanical synchronization. Also, the shaft-=
driven cam approach allows me to gear down the stepper motor output further=
, so there is even more force to close the gate without bending the stepper=
 motor shaft</p>
<p local-id=3D"41d91a2098da">LASER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"5ec1a42feef2">Meeting w/ Mason and Midhun about laser testin=
g. Spending this week on the MOPA laser on Pilot 3. When cutting multiple l=
ayers with a small part, the temperature change of the layer is fairly smal=
l. &lt;Can the total energy input per layer be calculated based on laser pa=
th and power settings, and the predicted layer temperature rise be calculat=
ed from that?&gt; Next week plans to run tests on the blue diode laser, whi=
ch should be easier because there are fewer parameters to adjust. Plans to =
do tests with and without inerting. Midhun recommends redesigning the LVP s=
o that the vacuum inlet is larger; apparently it has a very small cross-sec=
tional area right now. Currently he is finding the side walls have a surfac=
e finish of ~12 microns Sa, compared with ~6 microns Sa for spindle-cut gre=
en part side walls.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f1175d746235">Midhun is setting up the UV and CO2 lasers to =
conduct experiments with multiple powder materials on the benchtop instead =
of in a printer. The plan is to test laser cutting 17-4, 316L, Inconel 625,=
 and pure copper cakes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5997ca8a6115">They also brought up the possibility of trying=
 argon to see if its behavior is different from nitrogen.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3d74565d5b5d">2026-04-01</p>
<p local-id=3D"7d9d87004bd8">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7894be23bd05">Continuing to work on the updated version of t=
he hopper. I switched to a miter gear setup today because the available mit=
er gears are smaller diameter than the available spur gears, so I won=E2=80=
=99t need to make a cutout in the hopper body plate. I modified the carriag=
e mounts to be symmetrical and adjusted them to match the raised cam shaft,=
 which I had to raise from 12mm to 15mm away from the hopper body due to th=
e size of the stepper motor. Also, switching to miter gears let me change t=
he stepper motor to a vertical orientation, which makes it possible to moun=
t some limit switches to the hopper body next to the stepper motor. I plan =
to trigger these limit switches with clamping machined cams mounted the 8mm=
 camshaft.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b13c71e5f16a">I rewired the connection from the Duet expansi=
on board to the hopper vibrator motor to make it possible to control motor =
speed with G code. It was originally wired with the Duet output triggering =
a solid state relay that controlled power to an adjustable DC power supply,=
 so I instead wired the motor directly to the output (the output is a heate=
r output so its current capability is high enough to drive a DC motor). Moh=
sen wanted the capability to adjust vibrator motor power from G code so tha=
t he could increase vibration during the powder fill cycle to try to level =
out the powder in the hopper. He noted that the top surface of the powder w=
as not level because the powder loading hose is connected off-center (I did=
 this intentionally to decrease the hose angle between the hopper and the p=
owder dispenser to enable powder to flow down the hose freely). I am unconv=
inced that the powder level is critical to part quality, which can be verif=
ied by measuring the density of TRS coupons produced; if there is a variati=
on in density seen in the X direction then it will be confirmed that the po=
wder in the hopper needs to be level. In this case, the best option is to m=
ove the powder injection point back to the center and modify the powder loa=
ding system so that it can still flow into the hopper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b36be9e03baf">I initially planned to send out parts to be ma=
chined today, but Mohsen asked me to hold off until he gives me his latest =
feedback.</p>
<p local-id=3D"51e0f40f26ae">Mohsen suggested I increase the diameter of th=
e sensor holder on the vibrating hat to match the outer diameter of the new=
 sensor mount (the sensor holder is a cylindrical feature on the outside of=
 the vibrating hat that you can rest the sensor in so it is continually tri=
ggered regardless of powder level, which prevents the hopper from refilling=
 when you are trying to use up all the powder in the hopper). He didn=E2=80=
=99t have any suggestions for changes to machined parts, so I have begun th=
e process of submitting parts to Xometry, creating drawings as necessary to=
 call out threaded holes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"804197974ab9">2026-04-02</p>
<p local-id=3D"a6808bcd3e4c">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"97b844148e25">Going through and submitting all the machined =
parts to Xometry in preparation for submitting the order for approval and o=
rdering.</p>
<p local-id=3D"510306899668">Finished entering in and configuring all the m=
achined costs. The spreader bar I submitted through ProtoLabs because they =
have an option to Type III hard-anodize the aluminum which Xometry does not=
, and also has a way to select threads so a drawing doesn=E2=80=99t need to=
 be generated for each part, but the cost is much lower on Xometry so I sub=
mitted the rest of the parts through Xometry. The total cost for machined p=
arts is ~$3K, assuming a one-off first article. The cost per hopper would r=
educe as the quantity increases, although I would like to reduce the cost m=
uch further than that.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b4fecefd7a59">After submitting all the parts, I caught a sig=
nificant oversight in my design: There is no good feature that holds the ca=
mshaft in place in the axial direction. The two cams will prevent axial mot=
ions by crashing into the gate mounts if the shaft is pushed over too far, =
but this would cause increased friction and wear and so should be avoided. =
Since the shaft is being driven by a 90 degree miter gear, there are forces=
 transmitted along the axis of the camshaft. There are three components of =
force when bevel gears interact: axial forces along the shaft, radial force=
s perpendicular to the shaft axis, and tangential forces that cause rotatio=
n of the shaft. The radial loads are handled by the two permanently lubrica=
ted and sealed ball bearings in the custom-designed machined aluminum pillo=
w blocks that support the camshaft, and the tangential loads are what we wa=
nt to rotate the shaft, but I did not include a provision for handling the =
radial loads. I could add some thrust washers where the cam bearings intera=
ct with the gate mount slots (actually, I=E2=80=99m pretty sure that the ax=
ial force will always be applied in the same direction regardless of the di=
rection of rotation of the motor, in which case I would only need thrust wa=
shers on one side (I think the -X side, assuming that with straight-cut bev=
el gears the gears will always be pushed apart (Claude claims that it is di=
fferent with helical-cut gear sets, that in one direction they will push ap=
art and in the other they will pull together, which is possible, but I woul=
d like independent confirmation))). Regardless, I plan to have the shaft su=
pported against loads in both directions to support the axial load introduc=
ed by the bevel gears (in this case they are the special case of bevel gear=
s called miter gears, meaning they have the same number of teeth, but the m=
ore generic name for a conical gear set is bevel gears) in one direction an=
d ensure that the bevel gears stay meshed properly in the other direction i=
f there is some outside force introduced. Since I failed to catch this earl=
ier, I am somewhat limited by my pillow block design. I could cancel the or=
der and redesign the pillow block, but that would risk delaying the order a=
nd/or introduce mistakes, so I will make the current design work. I have al=
ready added retaining rings to the camshaft on both sides of each bearing, =
locking into grooves that I will machine into the keyed shaft. If the two s=
haft-supporting bearings are held in the pillow blocks axially, either due =
to the designed press fit (I designed the pillow blocks to match the outsid=
e of the bearings so they are an interference fit if they are machined exac=
tly correctly, but with tolerances of +- 0.005=E2=80=9D the fit could be lo=
oser) or green loctite then the shaft will be retained axially as-is, but I=
 do not want to depend on that being the case. It is possible that the bear=
ing could start loose or loosen over time, so I don=E2=80=99t want to depen=
d on a press fit or glue. One way that would have been reasonably elegant t=
o locate the bearing would be to machine the pillow block with a pair of in=
ternal retaining ring grooves, or possibly with a pair of set screws at 90 =
degrees apart, but this adds complexity to the machining and also cannot be=
 done at this point since the design is locked in. I could rework the pillo=
w blocks by adding the set screw threads after they are received, but that =
would not be a very elegant solution, and I am also concerned about how wel=
l set screws will retain a standard ball bearing, particularly because I am=
 using an aluminum housing. Also, these designs depend on the ball bearing =
handling thrust loads as well as radial loads.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a52a296e6587">My plan now is to add thrust bushings that con=
tact the pillow block around the bearing to avoid applying load to the bear=
ing itself. If I was able to change the design of the pillow block to accom=
modate this design change, I would add a counterbore around the bearing bor=
e to support a standard thrust bushing, but A. I won=E2=80=99t change the d=
esign now and B. the standard sizes of thrust bushings on McMaster are eith=
er smaller than the OD of the bearing so they wouldn=E2=80=99t engage with =
the housing regardless or are too large to fit within a reasonably sized co=
unterbore, so I will instead use a large thrust bushing (probably a dry-run=
ning polymer style to handle the dirty environment) and grind down one side=
 of it to make a flat that allows for the fillet on the pillow block that b=
locks a fully circular thrust bushing.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedde=
d-file-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img c=
lass=3D"confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260402-22595=
