ile" height=3D"262"></span>
<p local-id=3D"bc956b1d7ab2">I ended up designing some custom parts that ma=
tch the bearing and pillow block flat surface. I plan to machine them from =
glass-filled PTFE or have them laser cut.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9fcb3d3905fe">Instead, I now plan to have those parts laser =
cut from stainless steel and stack some standard thrust bushings on top of =
them.</p>
<p local-id=3D"960040a74fc0">I realized that the laser cut parts will likel=
y interfere with the screws used to mount down the pillow blocks, and I am =
concerned about getting good alignment with machined snap ring grooves cons=
idering the tolerance stackup in all the components on the camshaft, so I a=
m switching to using 2 piece clamping shaft collars instead of retaining ri=
ngs and switching to using modified washers instead of the laser cut stainl=
ess steel parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3e87de95fb82">2026-04-03</p>
<p local-id=3D"99ae326698ad">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"86b3674ae0d4">There are issues with two of the Pilot printer=
s not homing the U and V tank properly, which was seen previously because t=
he limit switch is recessed into the mount enough that if the mount bends s=
lightly the limit switch will not be tripped. I designed a new mount where =
the limit switch protrudes from the mount farther, so I plan to print some =
more from clear material on the FormLabs because it is the most dimensional=
ly accurate and rigid SLA material we are set up to print.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ca5849b1574e">R&amp;D Weekly Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"216df54110fe">No Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"4b8ca68dc9bd">Repeated experiment without removing sacrifici=
al filler, and the print failed at layer 20, but he broke it out anyway. He=
 found that it may be possible to break out the part without removing the s=
acrificial filler. When he removed the PEG he found that there was powder e=
mbedded in the PEG</p>
<p local-id=3D"f5cc9a1dd744">Marcus: the spirit of this request was to exam=
ine whether damage to walls was present before removing the sacrificial fil=
ler. Recommends inspecting a printed part with a CT scanner through Intrepi=
d, but has not heard back from them yet.</p>
<p local-id=3D"00a09758522f">Pourya increased the sacrificial filler dispen=
sed by increasing open time from .2 to 1.2 ms, and performed the facing ope=
ration every layer. After breaking it out, the wall quality was improved bu=
t still does not match spindle-only surface finish. He noticed voids in the=
 +Y side of the part where the spindle travels during the facing operation,=
 possibly due to dry powder being vacuumed up. He decreased vacuum power fr=
om 9 to 6, with no visible effect.</p>
<p local-id=3D"85a3dbd5f33a">Pourya printed another part with a drain path =
for the sacrificial filler instead of being completely dependent on it soak=
ing through the porous part only. He ran the thermal filler removal cycle w=
ith the part inverted, not with the openings pointing downwards. After remo=
ving the sacrificial filler, he found that the +X side had visible issues, =
but the other 3 sides seemed good. One issue Pourya noted is that the holes=
 on the side of the part, so the entire cake would have to be rotated 90 de=
grees to allow the filler to flow out, unless the part is sliced out of the=
 cake before the thermal sacrificial removal process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4af6371edf66">I raised the possibility of a higher temperatu=
re sacrificial filler to enable us to use the heat pass, and Mason raised t=
he idea of trying microcrystalline wax again. Pourya noted that the issue w=
ith microcrystalline wax was that it was difficult to face cleanly. Mason n=
oted that we are using a new end mill that may not have this issue. Marcus =
raised the question of what we are trying to solve, to which I responded fo=
r the sake of process standardization, matching the way our printing proces=
s is usually conducted. &lt;Could we switch to a sacrificial filler that is=
 soluble in IPA so we can remove it without the thermal removal process? Ac=
tually, per Claude, lower molecular weight PEG is somewhat soluble in IPA, =
especially when warm.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"bc96770f04be">Marcus raised the idea of sectioning a green c=
opper part and trying to sinter another one. Payman raised the point that w=
e don=E2=80=99t have a process for completely debinding and sintering coppe=
r parts that are heavily contaminated with PEG (I believe Dong has raised c=
oncerns previously with contamination of the copper debinding oven due to t=
he PEG-filled parts having such high binder content.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e9b11fbc1f45">Marcus recommends trying to fix the issue with=
 poor wall quality in 17-4 PH before trying to improve it in copper prints.=
 He also wants to try packing more parts into a print to reduce powder wast=
age &lt;ha ha!&gt;.</p>
<p local-id=3D"181f9a45f06d">Mason recommended measuring the surface finish=
 of the side walls using the VHX microscope, which is able to measure area =
surface finish visually.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7dbe0f08219e">Alex raised the point that there were issues w=
ith features in copper printed parts detaching during the thermal sacrifici=
al filler removal process, and Marcus stated that our response was to switc=
h to a simpler geometry. He also pointed out that there was a task to test =
green strength at some point, but it was =E2=80=9Cdeferred=E2=80=9D at some=
 point. He also said that green strength improvement would likely require a=
 binder improvement program &lt;ha ha!&gt;. Marcus said that we could just =
have design rules the prevent features that might detach, and Alex pointed =
out that the ability to print thin walls in copper is critical to the busin=
ess.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ee255b58e4bd">Laser:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2a2b09348fa1">Mason presented results from testing the MOPA =
and UV lasers. He showed some pictures of cuts using the UV laser. The UV l=
aser is a Q switched laser, so a lower frequency gives a higher peak power =
(Mason claims). He tried laser machining cakes made from 17-4, Inconel 625,=
 and pure copper. The cuts in Inconel creates =E2=80=9Cfiber=E2=80=9D-like =
objects above a certain peak power setting. Each test entailed laser machin=
ing 9 squares with a different energy density in each square. The temperatu=
re rise of the square with the highest energy density was about 12 degrees =
C for 17-4 and about 6 degrees C with copper.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e8565c3d02b">Mason noted that the UV laser cut width tends =
to be higher than other lasers and the laser tends to be slower.</p>
<p local-id=3D"27586fded96f">He finished the MOPA laser testing, but did in=
itial UV laser testing instead of moving the blue diode laser to Pilot 3 be=
cause Payman was still using it on his gantry.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9b0d38e3a828">Spread/Spray:</p>
<p local-id=3D"4a3fa305fca5">Marcus requested that Payman report on his exp=
eriments. Payman thinks he is close to finalizing the design of the hopper =
and mist box. He is continuing to encounter issues with binder dripping fro=
m mist that contacts the surfaces of the mist box. He ran a 50 layer print =
yesterday without binder dripping or other issues with a layer thickness of=
 100 microns. He plans to continue experiments and try printing with an arg=
on atmosphere. He notes that the hopper uses a monolithically printed livin=
g hinge as the door, but uses a fixed opening width. I asked whether the li=
ving hinge is necessary or if a fixed gap could be used, and Payman stated =
that the door needs some flexibility to be vibrated (it is not so much a do=
or as a flexible vibration application sheet).</p>
<p local-id=3D"0fc13b86ab2f">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d2449cdcea6d">I am continuing to work on ensuring the keyed =
shaft is located properly in the axial direction so that the two end cams w=
ith the track rollers do not rub on the gate mounts due to the axial loadin=
g of the bevel gears being used to rotate the shaft.</p>
<p local-id=3D"77e5ddcabd99">The stacked thrust bushing design was getting =
out of hand, so I am moving back to retaining the support ball bearings in =
the pillow block and using those to support the side load. I am leaning tow=
ards using shaft collars to provide an adjustable contact surface and havin=
g shims with a smaller OD than the shaft collars to interface between the s=
haft collars and the bearings to ensure that contact is only at the inner r=
ace of the bearing, not rubbing on the seal or the outer race. The shaft co=
llars will need to be machined to be slightly narrower than they are stock,=
 which is fine for me to do for the first article build, but they will like=
ly need to either be custom made or externally modified for full-rate produ=
ction. I am using set screw shaft collars instead of my preferred clamping =
collars because the diameter of the clamping shaft collar is too large to a=
ccommodate standard or button head socket head cap screws mounting the pill=
ow block to the hopper body. One approach to resolving this issue is to int=
egrate the pillow block into a monolithic machined back panel.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a3cdafd50781">I=E2=80=99m scrapping the whole design and mak=
ing a new one; I found out that I can combine parts to save cost; it turns =
out that Xometry=E2=80=99s pricing isn=E2=80=99t super dependent on materia=
l cost, so if I make a gate with the side mounts integrated that gets hogge=
d out from a block of stainless so 90% of the raw material is wasted, it=E2=
=80=99s still cheaper than having the gate and the side mounts machined sep=
arately, and I can make the design much more compact, robust, and precise. =
I also realized that there is no reason for me to design the spreader bar a=
s tall as I did, since that design was based on the assumption that it woul=
d be machined from a 30mm OD bar of stock, but here it is going to be machi=
ned from regular billet and be Type III Hard-Anodized afterwards.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fc4807c5aad7">Fortunately, the machined parts were never ord=
ered because I forwarded the quote to Adriana=E2=80=99s email instead of en=
tering it through the ordering form. She also noted that for this large of =
an order I should request a PO for it so that it goes through Gloria for ap=
proval.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0e85a490129b">For the sake of expediency, I am unscrapping t=
he V5.1 design because I want all the orders to be entered today. I am chan=
ging the design of the bearing pillow blocks to have threads for set screws=
 to lock in the bearing as well as thickening the ring around the bearing t=
o give more meat for the threads, but other than that I will leave the desi=
gn unchanged. I submitted the Xometry quote with only the bearing pillow bl=
ock changed, the ProtoLabs quote the same as before, and an order request f=
or the necessary parts from McMaster. Now I am going to start working on a =
3D printed prototype of HOPPER V5.1 to prove out the design, to refine the =
cam design, and organize all the hardware.</p>
<p local-id=3D"968b57640906">I have started prints on the Bambu printers to=
 print most of the necessary parts for assembling a HOPPER V5.1 prototype. =
One important thing to note is that the parts were designed to be machined =
and not 3D printed, so all the threaded holes will have to be tapped into t=
he printed parts instead of made using threaded inserts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c29b29d90e2b">2026-04-06</p>
