<p local-id=3D"1cefbf24710a">So it=E2=80=99s definitely going to be dumping=
 a lot of filament into the purge tower and out the poop chute, but it is a=
 worthwhile sacrifice to make to make a high-quality part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"995bc594297c">I machined the ends of the two linear rails so=
 they are 50mm long; the standard size they come in is 55mm so I had to tak=
e 2.5mm off each end. I then used a rotary tool to add a chamfer around eac=
h machined edge; they come with a chamfer to prevent a burr from damaging o=
r dislodging the linear bearing balls (I think) which the machining op I ra=
n removed, so I needed to add a chamfer back by hand to make sure that I ca=
n install the linear bearing carriages safely.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f498041a18aa">The parts came out fairly well, although the s=
upport material was hard to remove. I suspect it may need to be dried to be=
 more easily removable. I am printing a pair of bearing mounts using the sa=
me support material as an interface between the supports and the part; thes=
e parts are also hard to print in an optimal orientation without quality is=
sues from supports.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fd1ec4d0935f">I reamed out the bore on one of the bevel gear=
s to 8mm (I am using an 8mm keyed shaft as the main camshaft but the steppe=
r motor uses a 6mm output shaft, and the gear I needed to use was not avail=
able with 8mm and 6mm bores, so it was easiest to just buy 2X 6mm bore gear=
s and bore out one of them). I also machined one of the shaft collars to re=
duce its width along the axis of the shaft; most of the shaft collars will =
fit in the stack OK stock, but the one closest to the bevel gear needs to b=
e much narrower than stock so it will not interfere with the bevel gear.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"30aa0d0cc3f3">I stupidly designed the limit switches to be m=
ounted to the back panel with M5 screws for some reason, although the count=
erbores are sized for M4 screws (technically I think they=E2=80=99re design=
ed for some Imperial equivalent, but I=E2=80=99m going to use metric hardwa=
re regardless).</p>
<p local-id=3D"b0aa4d5062ee">I drilled and tapped the threaded holes in all=
 the 3D printed parts that have been printed so far. I found that IPA is a =
highly effective coolant/lubricant for tapping plastic; historically when I=
 have tapped plastic the tap heats up quickly and can melt the threads inst=
ead of cutting them.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e7edb20ddd8a">I cut down the 300mm shaft with the intent of =
making a 155mm shaft, but I accidentally cut in the wrong place, yielding a=
 150mm and a 145mm shaft. If it was time critical, it is possible to machin=
e the ends of the two sections of shaft, weld them together, and turn down =
the weld to the nominal diameter, but since is is possible to wait a day fo=
r the shaft I have entered it in the latest McMaster order request (which a=
lso included two sheets of 301 vibration-resistant sheet metal in .005=E2=
=80=9D and .003=E2=80=9D thickness, as compared with the current .010=E2=80=
=9D thickness (Mohsen asked for thinner sheet metal in the hopes that it wi=
ll yield improved layer quality)).</p>
<p local-id=3D"8da87c1f3af4">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"3a42840ed1c3">U and V tank limit switch mounts finished prin=
ting for Pilot 6 or 7 (I don=E2=80=99t remember exactly). I am currently wa=
shing them in the IPA filled washing unit.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e0e427d9fdf">Senai asked whether I am interested in changin=
g the material or thickness of the base plate for Pilot 8. I asked him whet=
her they want commonality between the printers and whether it is acceptable=
 for the new base plate to be a different color than the older base plates =
(silver if it is made from stainless steel vs. the current black anodized a=
luminum).</p>
<p local-id=3D"4ac33c6dc9c2">I also asked: Do you know what the main applic=
ation of Pilot 8 is planned to be? Is it cycle/stress testing that applies =
to the other Pilot printers, is it dedicated to production printing of 17-4=
 PH, is it dedicated to other materials (copper, inconel), is it dedicated =
to integrating and testing R&amp;D subsystems, is it intended to go to a cu=
stomer, or is it intended to be a first article for production of this styl=
e of printer?</p>
<p local-id=3D"7af760bbd2aa">Senai=E2=80=99s response was as expected: we d=
on=E2=80=99t know what the printer is for. Probably R&amp;D nonsense, but m=
aybe copper. Regardless, nothing that changes the printer requirements from=
 Pilots 6 and 7, and actually looser requirements because those printers we=
re nominally intended to match the Epson printers for troubleshooting purpo=
ses (although they did not; the electrical systems, software, and safety sy=
stems have been changed radically as compared with the Epson printers due t=
o changes driven by Ning and ZZ).</p>
<p local-id=3D"0896293aa564">The ultimate conclusion of our conversation wa=
s that he will get prices for 1/2=E2=80=9D and 1=E2=80=9D aluminum and stai=
nless steel ground sheets to compare the cost; if the cost of stainless ste=
el is much higher then changing material will be a non-starter. If we do de=
cide to go with a thicker aluminum plate, some slight design changes will n=
eed to be made to accommodate the extra thickness. Also, any changes to the=
 plate that make it heavier will necessitate adding lifting eyes to lift it=
 into place.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c023c336d730">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d7e64417bdea">I started another print of parts for Payman=E2=
=80=99s gantry on the Bambu P2S; should take about 13 hours to finish.</p>
<p local-id=3D"aaeaaebb2f3d">As I understand it, the goal of Payman=E2=80=
=99s gantry is to achieve the same level of functionality as the laser mach=
ine Mason is working on. Payman has developed his own hopper that he seems =
happy with and his own mist box that he seems happy with. The hopper is ess=
entially a shaker-style hopper that shakes a door with a living hinge and h=
as an always-open aperture, so it is dependent on the powder being non-flow=
able enough when not vibrated and flowable enough when it is vibrated. His =
mist box is essentially an occluded variant of the current mist box, that c=
ollects most of the mist on its internal passages and allows a line of mist=
 to flow through, that he then sweeps across the surface to apply binder. I=
t essentially replicates the functionality of a binder jetting head, but wi=
thout the dot-matrix control. Fortunately our binder is cheap and non-toxic=
, or a design that wastes most of the binder by collecting it on walls woul=
d not be usable. It then uses a laser to cut the outline of the parts. Inst=
ead of spreading on a base plate that drops into square walls, the hopper j=
ust travels on top of a pile of powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b3ee5c7df8a">My question is why is he pursuing all this stu=
ff in parallel? Does he think his approach is superior to ours? And if so, =
why are we doing all the work we=E2=80=99re doing?</p>
<p local-id=3D"c19d3b0b38c3">2026-04-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"1f3e1497adec">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"46530fe07441">Most of the 3D printed parts that need to be t=
apped have been tapped, so I can start screwing stuff together. I am still =
missing a full-length shaft, so that will be a limiting factor, but there=
=E2=80=99s plenty of stuff to work on before that gets here from McMaster. =
I also lost the limit switches, which need to be counterbored for M5 screws=
 because I accidentally designed the hopper to use M5 screws instead of the=
 M3 screws that the limit switches come sized for.</p>
<p local-id=3D"28d22da2fa8d">I dried the support filament overnight, and I =
am running another print of the bearing mounts. I am hoping that after dryi=
ng the support filament the supports will be easier to remove instead of ad=
hering to the PETG-CF somewhat as before.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d17c55a0a83b">I removed the new bearing holders, and it does=
n=E2=80=99t look like they=E2=80=99re much easier to remove than the previo=
us parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"66f493188b73">Mohsen noted issues with the gate not opening =
on Hopper V5.0. After removing the stepper motor, I found that the gate was=
 binding and was difficult to move. I tried adjusting the gate and linear b=
ushing mount positions, as well as cleaning out the polymer bushings, with =
little success. I wound up applying some MagnaLube grease to the polymer bu=
shings, which shouldn=E2=80=99t be necessary for these bushings and is risk=
y because the grease may collect powder which could chew up the bushings or=
 the shaft, but it seems to be the only way to get the gate actuating prope=
rly, and the hopper only needs to function for another week or two before H=
OPPER V5.1 comes online.</p>
<p local-id=3D"41b72cbee33e">I assembled the body of the hopper, installed =
the limit switches, installed the bearing mounts after pressing the bearing=
s into them, and installed the Edmund height/level adjustment stages. I rea=
lized that I need to redesign the cams to mount differently; right now they=
 need to be slid onto the end of the shaft, which will make replacing them =
very difficult if they need to be swapped out for wear or to adjust the ang=
le at which either of the limit switches trips.</p>
<p local-id=3D"550b0a3e3b17">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"12cf08f5d319">Issues have been encountered on Pilot 6 and 7 =
with communication between the Pi and the Arduino used for environmental co=
ntrol, which for some reason kills the print. ZZ and Ning claimed it was du=
e to interference, and after going back and forth a bunch of times over an =
email thread Ning said that one of the motors is causing interference that =
causes the Arduino to disconnect. Apparently the Arduino loses connection t=
o the BME280 temperature and humidity sensors, which makes the Arduino rest=
art, which interrupts communication with the Pi, which kills the print. I d=
on=E2=80=99t buy anything in the whole chain of reasoning or root cause ana=
lysis; it doesn=E2=80=99t make sense to me that it is an interference issue=
 unique to those machines when the hardware is nominally identical to the o=
ther machines. Ning really wants to eliminate the Arduino and run everythin=
g through the Pi, which to be fair is the right way to architect the system=
, but Ning seems to be mostly driven by an incorrect root cause analysis an=
d a bigotry for using industrial components; he has been pushing heavily to=
 not use non-industrial components like the Pi, the Arduino, or the Duet bo=
ard, and has gone so far as to design a custom control board to use instead=
, which seems bonkers insane. Designing and testing a robust industrial-gra=
de control board would take a huge amount of time and expense, even more so=
 than switching to COTS industrial-grade hardware, which would already take=
 significant money and time. To be fair, I do agree that if we are trying t=
o make an industrial-grade machine using 3DEO=E2=80=99s process, much like =
the Saffrons, and are willing to accept the increase in cost, then we shoul=
d use COTS industrial-grade hardware and build the system around that. If, =
however, we want to keep costs much lower than the Saffrons, then our optio=
ns are limited to making the printers with advanced hobbyist-grade componen=
ts where possible, or contracting a machine manufacturer to develop a custo=
m low-cost machine, which would require a significant order volume to reach=
 low costs (10s to 100s of thousands of units). When Ning brought up the fa=
ct that they had traced the interference to a particular motor, I recommend=
ed that we should fix that in addition to eliminating the Arduino.</p>
<p local-id=3D"82e101172af1">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"85c6a0ae448a">Mason also has the same concerns that I do abo=
ut Payman=E2=80=99s gantry printer; namely, why are we working on anything =
else if he is happy with the performance of his setup? Particularly when, a=
s far as I am aware, we have roughly 3 weeks of operating capital left?</p>
<p local-id=3D"37abd53c307e">2026-04-09</p>
<p local-id=3D"ae4bce240c22">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7eccc6fef10d">Working on the new cam design. I=E2=80=99m piv=
oting to a standard adjustable design that has a cam with a cutout so it ca=
n be slotted onto the shaft and an attached two-piece clamping feature to h=
old it to the shaft. This design has the advantage of being adjustable to e=
nable fine setting of the trigger point of the limit switches, but therefor=
e carries the disadvantage of adjustability, that it is more likely to slip=
 than if it had a key printed into it, and it is possible for operators to =
adjust it to temporarily compensate for a more fundamental issue, instead o=
f pursing a permanent fix to the root cause.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f57572927bbd">I finished the design of the new cams and am p=
rinting 2X sets. I also cut a shaft to 155mm and polished it with some Scot=
ch-Brite. I had intended to blue the shaft to give it some minor corrosion =
resistance, but it turns out that the bottle of bluing solution had complet=
ely evaporated, despite being tightly closed. Any rust on the shaft should =
be cosmetic and be unlikely to hinder the functionality of the hopper, but =
it may still be worth sourcing a stainless steel shaft for future hoppers f=
or aesthetic reasons (I used a steel shaft for this version because that wa=
s the only 8mm keyed shaft available on McMaster). I also post-processed th=
e new gate that had been printed last night using the removable support mat=
erial, and it looks pretty good. The surface finish of the part where the s=
upport material interfaced is quite good, and it was not tremendously diffi=
cult to remove the support material. This gave me a chance to test out the =
strength of the previously printed gate, and it did quite well; I bent it t=
o ~45 degrees and it did not break, and it was also possible to bend it bac=
k to its original shape so it was quite flat. The force it took to bend it =
that much was also quite high.</p>
<p local-id=3D"14f84b3ad6de">The set screws that came with the shaft collar=
s I am using are too long; they extend past the OD of the shaft collars eve=
n when they are tightened into the keyway (they are cone point set screws s=
o they extend into the keyway). Because of how much they extend out, they i=
nterfere with the socket head cap screws I am using to screw down the beari=
ng mounts. They also have the issue that, since they are M4 set screws, the=
y will cut into the edges of the keyway (which is 2mm wide), which will mak=
e it difficult to slide out the shaft through the bearings if it needs to b=
e removed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fb755015ac95">PILOT:</p>
