 us about pulling together a file of all the 3D printed parts necessary for=
 the Pilot printers that Epson has requested; they finally got a resin prin=
ter so they want the files in case they need to print replacement parts.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"a24f26b71835">I generated STEP files for all the parts that =
Epson asked for and handed them off to Senai. I also made two small bottle =
cap files even though they only asked for one; there are two versions of th=
e small bottle cap CAD, one with the word =E2=80=9Cflush=E2=80=9D and one w=
ith the word =E2=80=9Cbinder.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p local-id=3D"fe6138659d7a">2026-04-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"cf68dd5bd9ef">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"780621f746be">I have the core mechanicals of HOPPER V5.1 wor=
king, so now I need to connect the stepper motor to control it electrically=
 and start trying out the limit switch cams. The risk there is that they ar=
e indirectly measuring the gate position; they trigger with the camshaft th=
at then moves the gate forward and back, so if there is variation in the tr=
ack roller position or if there is variation in the positioning of the limi=
t switch cams (which is possible since they are clamping but not coupled to=
 the keyway) then the trigger point relative to the gate position could shi=
ft.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4d49acf5c11b">Mohsen noted some issues with vibration on the=
 hopper; when the vibrating hat vibration is turned up too high, the hopper=
 body itself can start to vibrate, and vibration applied to the print by th=
e spreader bar can dislodge parts in the cake.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a8ed5695b108">Weekly R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"9eb89b5d399f">No-Depowdering:</p>
<p local-id=3D"b3edabe7fd96">Goal was to compare different orientations of =
parts during the thermal sacrificial filler removal process. 80 layer recta=
ngle part prints were attempted: 2X with hatch facing and 2X with toolpath =
facing. The second print with hatch facing failed, as did another print whe=
re Pourya tried printing 3 rectangles per layer. The failed prints did comp=
lete, but there are anomalous cavities in the cake.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c04dfdc572bd">Broke out 4 copper parts and sectioned one. Th=
ey will be given to Alex for sintering. There seem to be some issues with m=
isalignment between the Mest unit and the cutting spindle, and a shift of o=
nly 30 microns can result in print failure. The copper machine currently us=
es a 3D printed mount for the Mest unit, which was known to result in the M=
est shifting on the Pilot printers. Marcus recommended making a machined mo=
unt for the Mest unit, and I agree that that would be beneficial.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c0d396539db2">Next week, Pourya plans to troubleshoot the is=
sues seen and print more parts from 17-4PH. For copper, he plans to run pri=
nts with 4 parts in each print. Currently printed parts will be debound and=
 sintered.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2fb0db6c4511">The thermal sacrificial removal cycle is progr=
ammed in the Keith furnace for copper parts, but the cycle for 17-4PH parts=
 is done manually by leaving the parts in the 100C oven overnight and movin=
g it to the 200C furnace for 2 hours.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cbf223374fc3">Laser:</p>
<p local-id=3D"de2176758485">This week, Mason ran informal screening tests =
on the UV and CO2 lasers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1227eea0221b">CO2 laser: Even optimized cut parameters produ=
ced a rounded cut profile; the smallest width achieved was 570 microns, and=
 kerf angle was found to be roughly 66 degrees from vertical. On 316L, copp=
er, and 17-4, adding water to the layer yielded clean cuts, although the cu=
ts were still =E2=80=9Cu-shaped.=E2=80=9D The exact formulation of water wa=
s unknown (tap, DI, etc.) His theory is that the cuts were always V shaped =
because of the mechanism of interaction between the laser and the layer; th=
e wavelength of CO2 lasers (~10000 nm) is supposed to be absorbed by water =
only and not the metal powder.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cfba6b1b631c">UV laser: Able to create connected cuts with a=
 depth of 230 microns and a width of 130 microns, and a kerf angle of 12 de=
grees. When using the UV laser on dry powder, less =E2=80=9Csintering=E2=80=
=9D was seen than is usually seen on a bound layer. Misting powder with wat=
er cause more =E2=80=9Csintering=E2=80=9D than is seen with a bound layer.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"b54c10c2f88e">Marcus brought up pictures of parts that Payma=
n has made on his gantry with a blue diode laser, and the results seem prom=
ising. Mason noted that the method of powder and binder application is very=
 different on the Pilot printers than it is on Payman=E2=80=99s gantry.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b930c7b1eacb">Mason believes that the CO2 laser is least lik=
ely to be usable with our process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a7e375d5115a">Mason is going to try running some of the same=
 tests with the MOPA laser, especially testing laser interaction with an un=
cured layer.</p>
<p local-id=3D"21ce19808554">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"62cddd0857e8">Mohsen made 4 prints of TRS coupons, but none =
of them came out well. First print failed due to broken limit switch. After=
 replacing the limit switch, he was unable to get a good spread, so he trie=
d 3 different bar cleaning systems, tried pre-drying the powder, tried trip=
ling the hopper vibration intensity. Noted that increasing the vibration be=
yond a certain intensity cause poor layer spreads. The hopper does not reta=
in level position overnight, so the hopper needs to be releveled before eac=
h print, necessitating that the spindle cut depth and the mist box level be=
 adjusted. &lt;Are the print quality issues due to the off-center powder lo=
ading?&gt; &lt;Also, recommend angling the metal sheet so it does not conta=
ct the side of the hopper.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"9c2ee84933b8">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"6d558da3838e">Pourya pointed out that we are seeing issues t=
hat may be due to poor powder quality. We apparently still are not using a =
penetration depth test procedure for the Pilot printers. Devin recommends r=
unning a penetration depth test before each print and adjusting the binder =
volume accordingly. He noted that penetration values tend to shift during t=
he year roughly following a sine wave. Increased porosity can also be resol=
ved with increasing binder volume.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6355988bed68">Mohsen is pushing back on that procedure, sayi=
ng that there are fluctuations in penetration values and it is therefore no=
t worth trying to chase that. It is true that it would be better to determi=
ne the root cause of penetration variation.</p>
