<p local-id=3D"d74524f2ea5c">I removed the supports from the latest guard a=
nd cams, and they seem good so far. I printed an SLA gate with a cutout for=
 epoxying in a steel reinforcing strip, cleaned off the residual resin with=
 the Form Wash and a manual post-cleaning with sprayed IPA and compressed a=
ir, and it is currently curing. Between the SLA material and the steel stri=
p, I think it should be stiff enough for actual printing. I also designed a=
nd am printing a positive mold for making the silicone mold that I will the=
n use to cast urethane seals for the gate. For initial testing, I printed F=
DM parts with the same dimensions as the urethane seals and super glued the=
m to the FDM steel-reinforced gate. I am concerned about the steel reinforc=
ing strip; I chose to use pre-hardened 4140 because it is available from Mc=
Master in a precision ground state and provides a very stiff reinforcement =
to the gate as long as it is properly attached (hence my careful preparatio=
n for epoxying it with DP420 epoxy, as well as the choice of DP420 since it=
 is a very strong industrial-grade epoxy (although now that I think about i=
t, it is possible that DP420 is not compatible with plastics, and therefore=
 may not form a sufficiently strong bond between the steel strip and the pr=
inted gate. It seems OK so far, but it bears further inspection and researc=
h)), but it has the disadvantage of being susceptible to rust. Looking up t=
he specs for DP420, it is supposed to adhere to =E2=80=9Csome plastics,=E2=
=80=9D but clearly is not guaranteed to adhere to all plastics. With FDM PE=
TG-CF and clear SLA material I have yet to see any issues with adhesive adh=
esion, but it is a risk, particularly because the consequences of the bond =
failure are severe (the steel strip with drop off the hopper, becoming FOD =
in the path of the hopper, heater, mist box, and spreader bar, and if angle=
d up sufficiently could damage the X or Z axes, or possibly damage the Y ax=
es by jamming gantry motion.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5fd84008639e">I cleaned up the support attachment points on =
the SLA gate using a scalpel, then cut a new reinforcing strip and epoxied =
it in place. It is now curing, and should be ready to have seals installed =
tomorrow, however, the seal casting mold will not be done until tomorrow so=
 seals will not be ready until the weekend or Monday.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6f9fcb508db4">Did a pour of silicone into a 3D printed mold =
to make a silicone mold for making the urethane gate side seals. Installed =
and adjusted the newly designed cams, and the new ones still cracked. I fou=
nd that the tabs on the -Y side guard that the +Y side guard slots into int=
erfere with the linear carriage mounts; it is still possible to install the=
 guard but it takes some bending and will likely eventually result in the t=
ab features failing. I connected hopper V5.1 to Pilot 2, and it was able to=
 open and close, but I found that it is only responding to the limit switch=
 when the gate is closing, even though pressing either limit switch causes =
the A axis endstop switch indicator to light up. My theory is that this is =
due to the Duet only being able to home in 1 direction, so even with the H1=
 modifier on the G1 command, it will not respond to the limit switch being =
pressed. My plan is to wire up the limit switches separately; I will set th=
e gate closed limit switch as the homing switch for the A axis and wire up =
the gate open limit switch to a spare output and make a conditional loop th=
at opens the gate until it detects that switch. This will also give me a ch=
ance to clean up the wiring; there are a bunch of wires and connectors and =
nonsense that have caused issues in the past when things were disconnected =
or connected to the wrong device.</p>
