<p local-id=3D"e9f43dfc3eda">2026-04-22</p>
<p local-id=3D"349de27319f3">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7e7b7a482f6d">Pourya is having issues with his visa, so he i=
s going to be offsite for an unknown amount of time. In the meantime, he is=
 transferring knowledge to Mohsen to take over the no-depowdering experimen=
ts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a012bc58c293">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"88ae697730f3">The HOPPER V5.1 was installed on Pilot 2, but =
had issues with leveling, and when Mohsen tried to run some layers it crash=
ed into something, breaking the SLA spreader bar, the cover, the SLA gate, =
and the 3D printed mounts. The cover can be omitted for now, I gave Ben the=
 FDM printed gate I had made with a steel reinforcing strip epoxied to it, =
and the mounts are being swapped out for the machined ones from the previou=
s hopper, and I am printing an FDM spreader bar to use temporarily. I am de=
signing a monolithic -Y panel that will be printed from high-temperature SL=
A material to enable the hopper to be used with the heater. I am experiment=
ing with incorporating an isogrid pattern into the wall to minimize resin u=
sage and weight while maintaining strength.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4db19e6be3f5">The downside to the isogrid pattern is that it=
 takes a long time to set up (although I did finally figure out how to crea=
te and insert blocks, so that helps a lot) and a lot of processing power fo=
r SolidWorks to generate the geometry, so it takes a lot of time to create =
the features, about 1/3 of which is taken up by waiting.</p>
<p local-id=3D"196cadac5b19">I designed a new back panel and spreader bar t=
o be 3D printed with the same mounting locations but as much material remov=
ed as possible by applying isogrid cutouts, and I am printing them along wi=
th two new bearing holders and two new cam cam/clamp pairs. This should mak=
e most of the hardware facing the heater fairly heat-resistant.</p>
<p local-id=3D"85868381c7e6">2026-04-23</p>
<p local-id=3D"75de06787ef5">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"852b73d05bd2">The repaired Hopper V5.1 is ready to be put ba=
ck together (the newly FDM printed spreader bar needs to be installed) and =
installed in the machine. It should function OK, although the FDM parts mea=
n that it is likely not heat resistant enough to be operated next to the he=
ater. The FormLabs printer ran out of clear resin last night 13 minutes bef=
ore the print was complete, so I installed the other resin cartridge we hav=
e around to finish the print. Since the cartridge is only partly full, I en=
tered an order request for another one. I couldn=E2=80=99t fit the limit sw=
itch mount and motor mount on the FormLabs print, so I need to print those =
next after this print has been removed from the build plate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9531ce8f56bd">I am working on the design of HOPPER V5.2 with=
 the intent of finishing the design and getting the parts ordered today.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"4f423e77740f">It occurred to me that since the hopper length=
 is defined by the position of the cam shaft and since the position of the =
cam shaft is set by the motor width, I could move the shaft closer to the b=
ody of the hopper by either sinking the motor into the body (which would re=
quire a redesign of the vibrating hat, which already has enough issues with=
out adding more constraints to design around (although sometimes adding des=
ign constraints can help if it forces you to change your thinking of the de=
sign)) or by angling the stepper motor outwards. This would make machining =
the hopper back panel from a single piece of aluminum more difficult, but I=
=E2=80=99m not sure if that would be reflected in the price because I=E2=80=
=99m pretty sure they use standard blocks of aluminum in a 5 axis mill anyw=
ay. Either way it would add design time without adding significant value, b=
ut it is worth considering for a future version if space becomes a greater =
constraint.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b5bef4898901">I realized that since I had moved the motor ov=
er to shrink the central mount structure as much as possible, the motor now=
 interfered with the vibrator motor, so I had to move it over ~7mm, which m=
eans that it is now farther from the center, assuming a two-weight motor is=
 used. If a single-weight ERM (eccentric rotating mass) motor is used, its =
weight will actually now be closer to the center of the vibrating hat than =
it was before.</p>
<p local-id=3D"99754a416d5d">I did some research on vibration isolation and=
 damping vibrations with tuned mass dampers (those big moving weights used =
in buildings, they=E2=80=99re also apparently used all over cars and compou=
nd bows. Tuned mass dampers are focused on damping a narrow frequency range=
, which could be useful if the vibrator motor is going to be run at a known=
 RPM. I could mount a custom TMD to the hopper body that would help to damp=
 vibrations at that frequency.</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-file-w=
rapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img class=3D"=
confluence-embedded-image image-center" alt=3D"image-20260423-235708.png" w=
