<p local-id=3D"02cb744dcc54">Tried installing the assembly, and the dovetai=
l positioning is off (spindle spacing is different from the CAD model I was=
 working off of). I first tried adding some spacers to one of the dovetails=
, but the precision required to get the positioning exactly right in such a=
 way that it does not cause the spindles to fight each other is high enough=
 that I am concerned about the amount of time it will take to get it dialed=
 in. There is also an issue with poor access to the SHCS that attach the sh=
eet metal to the dovetails; it is very difficult to tighten them in place, =
which would be very helpful for getting alignment exactly correct.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7c3ffb9f53b7">Instead of the rigid setup I have right now, I=
 mounted down one of the aluminum dovetails permanently as well aligned wit=
h the two brackets as possible (when screwing together the brackets, I rest=
ed them on a granite surface plate, and I also rested the assembly on the s=
ame surface when screwing down the dovetail to get it aligned as well as po=
ssible). I used lock washers and red Loctite to hold this dovetail in place=
 permanently. The dovetail I chose to mount permanently was the one closer =
to the center of gravity of the Mest unit. For the other dovetail, I am pri=
nting a new dovetail from PETG-CF with increased height to eliminate the ne=
ed for spacers, and since it is FDM printed it will have some compliance in=
 it to prevent the stages from fighting each other, but it should help to t=
ake some of the torque load off of the stage attached to the aluminum dovet=
ail.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f97afc580277">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"903e8d7e5c5e">The parts for the new vibration isolator finis=
hed printing , so I should be able to do a cast this afternoon after sprayi=
ng the walls heavily with mold release and hot gluing the bottom plate to t=
he mold sides. The procedure will be to pour in urethane with the top panel=
 off so there is full access to the entire mold, then press the top panel i=
nto the top of the mold, squeezing out any excess urethane. This approach i=
s much easier, faster, and less likely to introduce bubbles in the vibratio=
n isolation rubber, but increases the difficulty of cleanup because of the =
urethane squeezeout.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a21059193ba1">I finished modifying the metal spreader bar fr=
om HOPPER V5.0 to be compatible with HOPPER V5.1. The weld plugging I did d=
id not penetrate far on one side, so the hole I tried to make to tap M3 thr=
eads broke into another hole that was present before weld plugging, which m=
ade it impossible to tap good threads. The other three holes, though, got a=
bout 3-4mm of solid metal to tap into, which should be OK for holding the s=
preader bar in shear. The thread tearout strength will be sub-optimal; the =
rule of thumb is at least two diameters worth of threads, which in this cas=
e would be 6mm of threaded depth for an M3 screw.</p>
<p local-id=3D"35f409cbda55">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"f49f2099a0ee">Ning is continuing to work on the electronics =
of Pilots 6 and 7. He is trying to make a completely isolated environmental=
 control system using an Inkbird controller from Amazon, which he claims is=
 =E2=80=9Cindustrial.=E2=80=9D He is also recommending eliminating the I2C =
BME280 sensors in favor of RS485 sensors, which I agree is a good idea, alt=
hough he also recommended Amazon sensors, which I have little faith in the =
accuracy of.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0f52b3826078">We really should be figuring out the goal arch=
itecture of the Pilots before digging into any of this nonsense. Here are a=
 few possibilities I can think of:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"18a8958377b7">
<li local-id=3D"3f804fbbcfef">
<p local-id=3D"a31f13cedc0b">Open-source, low-cost R&amp;D platform</p>
<ul local-id=3D"04c12346c731">
<li local-id=3D"d8d8491c2eba">
<p local-id=3D"f75698e58eb0">The need to reduce costs forces us to use hobb=
yist COTS control systems and components</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"4dcbbbb24726">
<p local-id=3D"f97b81143979">This is the motivation for using a Raspberry P=
i and Duet board in SBC mode</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"667151f18511">
<p local-id=3D"b2cc1c3568c5">All control should be done with the Pi and Due=
t, to simplify the system, reduce part count, hence reducing cost, and prov=
ide centralized control</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"957ed4a8f1eb">
<p local-id=3D"63da92ab94c2">Productized low-cost system</p>
<ul local-id=3D"539b7617be9b">
<li local-id=3D"20873b32343e">
<p local-id=3D"93b0637240cc">Same architecture, with wiring polished up a b=
it to mitigate noise and make assembly and wiring easier</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"1eaac6163a6e">
<p local-id=3D"a48c05ae2b6a">Productized high-cost system</p>
<ul local-id=3D"73d3e0cda3b9">
<li local-id=3D"b5df622a49f1">
<p local-id=3D"749cfdcc6b6b">Change to an industrial grade control/electron=
ics architecture</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a120e1e93fd6">
<p local-id=3D"363349b19574">Essentially a simplified duplicate of the Saff=
ron printers</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"dd641578e7fa">
<p local-id=3D"805ffc4caced">Hire an outside dedicated machine manufacturer=
 for consulting on the design</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e14306330a06">
<p local-id=3D"6e8fbdae64f7">Hire necessary experts for electrical design a=
nd programming</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"1b2b73390181">
<p local-id=3D"cb79b475265a">Hire industrial designer to make an aesthetica=
lly pleasing and functional exterior</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"98b9445f7302">
<p local-id=3D"348111fbd6a0">Inerted with gas recycling system and environm=
ental controls (easiest way is to contract an outside company to make a dro=
p-in system or integrate a system into our printer)</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li local-id=3D"c4ceb354be75">
<p local-id=3D"77dc998fb65b">Productized low-cost system</p>
<ul local-id=3D"03e6f5bebad6">
<li local-id=3D"8db506ff937d">
<p local-id=3D"618d68da2ccc">All the electronics will need to be designed f=
rom scratch, closer to a Bambu printer than the Saffrons</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"64146f866de5">
<p local-id=3D"10831f733f6a">Work with a Chinese machine manufacturer to de=
sign and mass-produce an inexpensive and reasonably reliable system</p></li=
>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"c1a1de1e7444">The open-source and hobbyist-grade systems are=
 most suitable for internal R&amp;D because they are easy to modify and rep=
rogram, while the industrial grade or Bambu grade printers are better suite=
d for production because they will need to be a large order of identical ma=
chines with stabilized electronics and software.</p>
