=3D"inline" class=3D"external-link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.tzonetempe=
rature.com/warehouse-monitor-temperature-and-humidity-transmitter-rs485-gre=
enhouse-temperature-and-humidity-sensor</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"434791bda6d1">This uses an SHT30 chip:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8e4199436cef"><a href=3D"https://www.adafruit.com/product/50=
64?srsltid=3DAfmBOor2q_Pejelam27hK-GgueGfnxdIKG8iu2V-359owF5GNewf2ycl" loca=
l-id=3D"bde3f9ce1ff5" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-lin=
k" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.adafruit.com/product/5064?srsltid=3DAfmBOor=
2q_Pejelam27hK-GgueGfnxdIKG8iu2V-359owF5GNewf2ycl</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"5560ac6b1515">Which natively uses the I2C communication prot=
ocol, but in the case it has a chip to communicate over RS485.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e0b9dcfab46f">&lt;My recommendation would be to monitor and =
control everything through a central control system (likely the Raspberry P=
i) so that the environmental setpoints can be adjusted through the main use=
r interface and the RPi can record the environmental data. Any failures wil=
l be easier to debug since there is only one system in control of everythin=
g. If there are issues seen, first establish the root cause. Are there othe=
r known good machines that the machine with the issues can be compared with=
? What are the differences? How can you prove that a theory about the root =
cause is correct? With noise, it=E2=80=99s an easy test; connect an oscillo=
scope to the suspect lines and look for noise. With my architecture where a=
ll systems are tied together instead of completely isolated, any noise issu=
es would need to be resolved with traditional noise mitigation approaches (=
star grounding, cable shielding, inductive voltage spike suppression diodes=
, noise filters). Star grounding should always be implemented regardless ju=
st as good practice, but the other measures should only be implemented as n=
ecessary; if there are issues seen, noise needs to be established with an o=
scilloscope, mitigated with the correct approach, measured again with an os=
cilloscope to demonstrate that it has been mitigated, and have that data re=
corded and published so it is accessible to the whole company. If noise is =
detected and all standard noise mitigation approaches fail, then it may be =
worth isolating components. If isolation fails, then it may be worth redesi=
gning the system to prevent the root cause. My general process followed wit=
h the first 5 pilots, then is: build system following standard best practic=
es (DC negatives tied together, star grounding) =E2=86=92 Issue seen =E2=86=
=92 find root cause =E2=86=92 if noise, prove with oscilloscope =E2=86=92 t=
ake measures to mitigate noise =E2=86=92 if that fails, isolate systems =E2=
=86=92 if that fails, replace subsystems while maintaining necessary functi=
onality. Bear in mind that Pilots 1-5 had their DC negatives tied together,=
 as do all the Beta machines and the Saffron printers as best I can tell, b=
ut OK, maybe noise is a new issue on 6 and 7 for some reason, I don=E2=80=
=99t know. In that case, I would recommend following the procedure above. A=
s far as I can tell, Ning=E2=80=99s approach has been: Issue seen =E2=86=92=
 isolate systems and replace subsystems regardless of lost functionality.&g=
t;</p>
<p local-id=3D"2d19e5288d3c">Payman recommends running a print to test dens=
ity.</p>
