<p local-id=3D"0ec465d15933">Pilot Conversion for Copper:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e1fc3b368907">How did we convert the Saffron to run copper? =
Requirements?</p>
<p local-id=3D"f90570012f45">OSHA PEL (Permissible Emission Limit), keep fi=
ne powder contained into machine as much as possible</p>
<p local-id=3D"ebe3e2cb5835">Our grade of copper powder is non-combustible<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"75d901bd82dd">Because copper is so sensitive to oxidation fr=
om oxygen and humidity, it needs to printed at low humidity, low heat, pref=
erably low oxygen.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8c6a40c7b236">Tents were first implemented to keep out conta=
mination from 17-4 PH present elsewhere in the room; per Midhun, if we dedi=
cated a room to printing copper only, then we would not need a tent.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0ee538a03de4">Midhun notes that we see issues in final part =
quality because of poor green strength. Things that would lead to increased=
 green part strength:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"4f5547a6f95d">
<li local-id=3D"84ae2ad7a2de">
<p local-id=3D"8befef31a06c">Reduced layer thickness</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b2b20500ee44">
<p local-id=3D"cab82166cac4">Reduced oxygen (Midhun=E2=80=99s target: 2-8%)=
</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"cf7f1d1464f1">
<p local-id=3D"1327be990a94">Non-aqueous binder</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"e80e45c98ec6"></p>
<p local-id=3D"752732c794c1">2026-05-05</p>
<p local-id=3D"6652427bfcbf">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7d800bdb5e2f">Curing the SLA parts for making a mold for the=
 urethane wiper seals for HOPPER V5.2</p>
<p local-id=3D"905632f4633e">Beginning to design a hopper for the copper Pi=
lots. Per Midhun, the flowability (as measured by angle repose) of our copp=
er powder is similar to that of treated 17-4 PH stainless powder, even thou=
gh the 17-4 powder has a flow aid added (fumed silica of some formulation).=
 Technically, it is likely possible to duplicate HOPPER V5.2 and install it=
 in the copper printer, assuming that it is usable at all (as will hopefull=
y be determined by today=E2=80=99s hopper tests), but since our copper gree=
n parts are chronically weak and insufficiently dense, it would be wise to =
incorporate some more advanced design features, namely an active roller spr=
eader bar.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f2fb84d7fd4d">The main design challenge of an active roller =
spreader bar is keeping the axis of rotation aligned with the central axis =
of the outer surface of the spreader bar; misalignment will cause waviness =
in the layer, resulting in variations in layer thickness and possibly in la=
yer density.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2f35e874b9ec">I need to add a quick release mechanism for th=
e hopper so that it can be installed and removed easily while still being s=
ufficiently rigid.</p>
<p local-id=3D"49e7ebf1faae">I am working on adding the active spreader bar=
 to a copy of HOPPER V5.2 (the copy is named V5.3, naturally, although the =
assembly model has a different label: LINEAR GATE ACTIVE ROLLER HOPPER). I =
am hoping to recycle most of V5.2, so it will still have a fixed spreader b=
ar that is used as a surface for the gate to seal against and as a pre-spre=
ader, with an active spreader bar roller slightly downstream of that. I am =
designing it that way instead of replacing the current fixed spreader bar w=
ith an active one because of packaging issues with the side gate support ca=
rriages and rails. I also need to incorporate a mechanism to make it possib=
le to adjust the active spreader bar up and down and tilt it to align it re=
lative to the fixed spreader bar</p>
<p local-id=3D"931e26b7db68">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"c735ba8b979b">I talked with Devin about copper powder recycl=
ing/reconditioning. He was reluctant to commit to it, with good reason, but=
 I think he agrees with what I=E2=80=99m thinking, at least at a high level=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c0325e0e8aa8">So 3DEO has a problem with powder wastage: onl=
y a small amount of the copper powder that comes in the loading dock winds =
up in parts that are shipped to the customer (numbers?). Most of it is lost=
 to:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"71887ece040b">
<li local-id=3D"fd96b7510392">
<p local-id=3D"69b231be10a4">Penetration tests and other powder benchmarkin=
g procedures</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b5b828ca465a">
<p local-id=3D"54e0a26cc601">Failed prints (including pushoffs)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"3ac1b396b319">
<p local-id=3D"39fa50973ab3">Powder that is vacuumed up (leftover powder in=
 U tank, powder in cleaning downdraft table, etc.)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"bb3a20100ddd">
<p local-id=3D"fa8fba122239">Bound cakes</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2108e4530697">
<p local-id=3D"45441edf2e6b">Scrap green parts</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0b9e272f3fec">
<p local-id=3D"ee41204d1859">Overflow</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"471379be8a04">We are currently approaching this from the fro=
nt end, trying to reduce wastage at some of these steps with the following =
projects:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"ed30d434332a">
<li local-id=3D"a20f781e4764">
<p local-id=3D"f1cbd560b8e7">Hopper</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"6fc73f884b2c">
<p local-id=3D"32d4e242bfad">Using Pilot printers with a smaller build area=
 when possible</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"d9485a2dec8f">
<p local-id=3D"2f47567977a4">No-depowdering may reduce powder usage</p></li=
>
<li local-id=3D"20b7a03e4307">
<p local-id=3D"77ca3925f0b6">Optimization of powder feeding strategy to min=
imize wastage</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"d26653b84f71">An alternative to this approach would be to go=
 at it from the back end; instead of trying to reduce wastage at each of th=
ese steps, accept powder contamination and recycle and recondition it to ma=
tch the properties of virgin powder (or get close enough that a blend of re=
cycled and virgin powder can be used). This would require significant upfro=
nt capital investment and carries risks, but would also reduce powder wasta=
ge to zero if it can be proven to work. The R&amp;D team could instead work=
 on optimizing for part quality, throughput, and part capabilities.</p>
<p local-id=3D"36f05df2fffd"></p>
<p local-id=3D"5fa593e34d5d">2026-05-06</p>
