ecause the felt seal came partially detached.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cb1cc3f0f05d">Removed the faulty felt seal and glued on a ur=
ethane seal. I then reconnected the hopper to Pilot 2 and tested opening an=
d closing the gate a few times, and it seemed to work fine. It is an open q=
uestion whether the urethane seal will provide a good seal to prevent powde=
r leakage, but hopefully it will at least stay in place because it=E2=80=99=
s super glued on.</p>
<p local-id=3D"38ae817b5b19">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"6465ffbf8ec4">Payman asked for access to the latest PILOT ca=
d, but he does not have access to the vault so I cleaned up the SolidWorks =
assembly and am am creating a pack and go folder for him. Created the Pack =
and Go folder, Zipped it, and sent it to Payman over Slack.</p>
<p local-id=3D"849d27ff080a">Heat Exchangers:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1ccfc2122a03">Need to add M3 threads to two of the sample Cl=
eanPrint(tm) parts I have to connect push-to-connect fittings to test them =
for leaks.</p>
<p local-id=3D"612a335df3b4">Now that I have a CAD model of the heat exchan=
gers, I have a better understanding of how to machine them to see the inter=
nal channels. Instead of the stepped milling, I think a better approach is =
to face the top and bottom surfaces until I hit the important features.</p>
<p local-id=3D"33e74f5ef732">I drilled and tapped three parts and milled do=
wn one of those three to expose the internal features (I just tapped it fir=
st because it=E2=80=99s cool to see the threads cross sectioned.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a64540f719b8">GENERAL R&amp;D:</p>
<p local-id=3D"65e400fd3fbc">Turns out I was somewhat wrong about the safet=
y of hydrotesting. My points were technically correct but incomplete; argua=
bly pressurizing a test article with gas is more dangerous than liquid at t=
he same pressure, although there are also risks associated with hydrotestin=
g that are not present with gas testing. At the same pressure, there is mor=
e energy stored in compressed gas than there is in compressed liquid, so an=
 isolated tank filled with water at 1000 PSI is much safer than an isolated=
 tank filled with air at 1000 PSI. On the other hand, if the test article i=
s connected to an active pump and there is a small breach, a jet of water c=
an cause high pressure fluid injection injuries that a jet of gas would not=
 cause.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ab94a0809681">I think that 99% of the time it is correct to =
say that testing with pressurized liquid is safer than testing with pressur=
ized gas, but it is important to be aware of the exact testing regime, hard=
ware, and failure modes, and recognize that there are dangers that are uniq=
ue to hydrotesting that can be quite severe.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2e568c202dbf">Also of note: high pressure fluid injection in=
juries can occur with little initial pain or visible damage with worsening =
symptoms over 8-24 hours, and result in fairly high amputation rates, so if=
 you do have an injury like that, go to the hospital ASAGDMFP.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a57d0da1b2cb">MOSP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1098df06c42e">I designed a new metal mount for the Mest unit=
 on the copper Saffron with two major changes: it uses two spindle stages t=
o either side of the front feature cutting spindle, and I don=E2=80=99t rem=
ember the other change. With this design, the nozzle of the Mest unit is al=
igned with a front and rear spindle in Y, to maximize the area that any of =
the three can reach in X (since X travel is more limited than Y travel in t=
he Saffron printers).</p>
<p local-id=3D"cb019aba28d0"></p>
