<p local-id=3D"2c74a1b3c12c">HOPPER/FLIP:</p>
<p local-id=3D"0ac9d493323a">The machined parts for Hopper V5.2 were suppos=
ed to ship today, but they have been delayed. Fortunately, the 3D printed V=
5.1 hopper seems to still be functioning well enough to get some preliminar=
y results.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f14835d95e34">HEAT EXCHANGERS:</p>
<p local-id=3D"437116a480dd">I did some research on the impact of surface r=
oughness of internal channels on the performance of liquid cooled heat exch=
angers. Surface roughness increases the convective heat transfer coefficien=
t but also increases the friction factor of the channels. This results in a=
 higher pressure drop for a given flow rate, so the pumping power per unit =
of heat transfer is equivalent or slightly worse, but it lets you get more =
heat transfer for a given heat exchanger volume. Depending on what you are =
optimizing for and how much of the system you are allowed to change, a roug=
her surface can actually be beneficial for certain applications of heat exc=
hangers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b81912addc83">For rocket engines, it tends not to be desirab=
le to have rough channels; the rough surface can trap contaminants, increas=
es pressure drop, and can cause unpredictable pressure drop depending on ho=
w rough the surface is and where it is rougher or smoother. Typically rocke=
t engine channels are polished in some way, whether that=E2=80=99s electrop=
olishing or abrasive flow machining or similar.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bf4f4fee682d"></p>
