<p local-id=3D"384cd68d0fa3">For reference, the maximum TIR allowed is 25 m=
icrons (this may be a circular runout measurement instead of TIR; circular =
runout is measurement at one ring around the surface being measured while T=
otal Indicated Runout is a measurement of the entire surface, so both aroun=
d the part and along it in the axial direction. TIR can catch issues that c=
ircular runout cannot, such as tapering of the part and axial angle misalig=
nment vs distance between parallel axes).</p>
<p local-id=3D"f64e9107f0e8">Talked with Adriana, Ning, Mason, and Ben in t=
he parking lot after work.</p>
<p local-id=3D"21c8fe5effc9"></p>
<p local-id=3D"21572160daed">2026-05-27</p>
<p local-id=3D"1b0a56c79da8">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"46b3777b4c63">Spoke with Senai about the test bench. He is o=
f the opinion that we should test a heat exchanger by mounting it to a GPU =
to compare it with a COTS equivalent. This makes for a much simpler, althou=
gh much less flexible, test bench. At its simplest, we would only need a co=
olant reservoir held at a constant temperature and a pump with controlled f=
low to control the input to the heat exchanger, then mount an exchanger to =
the GPU and run the GPU at different loads while measuring the GPU temperat=
ure with its internal temperature sensors.</p>
<p local-id=3D"714aaa189391">This makes my job easier because the test benc=
h design is much simpler, and it creates a much simpler data set: at its si=
mplest, there would be one graph of GPU temperature vs load with different =
curves for different flow rates and the 3DEO design and competitor=E2=80=99=
s design shown on the same graph.</p>
<p local-id=3D"57c34006db09">It also would be a compelling demo to stupid e=
xecutives, because we would be showing that a drop-in product made by 3DEO =
performs better than a commercially available product.</p>
<p local-id=3D"668f8a8b5429">The downside is that we would need to design a=
nd print a complete product heat exchanger that is optimized for the GPU, o=
r is just generally so high performance that it beats commercial products e=
ven when not optimized for the exact application. Senai is confident that t=
his can be done within the necessary timeframe (my SWAG is that we will nee=
d a heat exchanger ready for final testing within 6 weeks, assuming that we=
 get funding to do this 30-60-90 plan).</p>
<p local-id=3D"ff1acf7b1eea">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1d4556a01046">Ning sent out an email saying that he wants to=
 use shielded twisted-pair cables instead of the unshielded cables we have =
been using that run out of the control cabinet to connect to various compon=
ents. His goal is to reduce electrical interference, which was not an issue=
 on Pilots 1-5. It did not become an issue until Pilots 6 and 7 had electri=
cal and software changes as compared with 4 and 5 (Epsons 1 and 2). Shielde=
d cables are about twice as expensive as unshielded cables, a pain to work =
with because the shielding needs to be removed when stripping insulation to=
 attach electrical connectors, and the cables are thicker so they may neces=
sitate different IcoTek blocks to pass them through the control cabinet wal=
l (which is not very difficult but is a design change that needs to be moni=
tored and recorded) and larger cable carriers (which frankly we should swit=
ch to anyway because of how overcrowded the current ones are from the extra=
 cables for the three spindles and the Mest unit).</p>
<p local-id=3D"f5c0edf8638e">If I were communicating appropriately with peo=
ple, here=E2=80=99s what I would do:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"e98c92a1a3ea">
<li local-id=3D"065e2794a4e9">
<p local-id=3D"d0d89b70cd5b">Ask Ning why he wants to use shielded cables. =
Is there a reason they are necessary for this exact application, or does he=
 want to use them because it is standard practice for reducing interference=
?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9211c4df9d10">
<p local-id=3D"d596ceeb0e9d">Why was this not an issue on the previous Pilo=
ts?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"9f5043641a31">
<p local-id=3D"336a5986fb69">What data is present showing that interference=
 is causing the issues that have been seen (disconnection between Duet and =
Raspberry Pi, although there may be other issues of which I am unaware)? Is=
 there oscilloscope data showing the interference and evidence that that is=
 correlated with disconnection, or is this just assumed to be an issue?</p>=
</li>
<li local-id=3D"b3ccc0d4ad70">
<p local-id=3D"85d68f988a46">What approaches has Ning taken to eliminate no=
ise in the control cabinet? Are all components star grounded as they were i=
n Pilots 1-5? Have other noise suppression techniques been used, such as ad=
ding a diode to the solenoid valve to dissipate the energy in the coil when=
 it is shut off?</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"c2e6827beaf7">
<p local-id=3D"b2650b20dfa2">Has noise on each ground been measured to show=
 that it is present when the grounds are star connected, and shown to be el=
iminated by isolating the grounds?</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"efe7942c5d69"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b255e5176b54">Started a print on the Bambu X1E of the cable =
carrier mounts that support the cable carrier as the Y gantry moves.</p>
<p local-id=3D"02441375ea48"></p>
<p local-id=3D"1c1e2f813ded">2026-05-28</p>
<p local-id=3D"8d9db9c0ed07">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"a45126d434c1">Ben asked for a new design of the vacuum hose =
splitter that is mounted to the X axis cable carrier to run one vacuum hose=
 to the standard cutting SVP and one vacuum hose to the facing SVP.</p>
<p local-id=3D"44dc0599d65a">Cleaning up the print files on the SD card tha=
t has been used on the Bambu X1E for printing parts for the Pilot printers.=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"8eec2bf3d9e9">Removed obviously unnecessary files (a bunch o=
f random files seem to have wound up on the SD card over time, either becau=
se they were manually loaded onto the SD card outside of the machine or bec=
ause the Bambu Labs slicer software was set to store the print file on the =
SD card).</p>
<p local-id=3D"29b255fe26f9">Need to send out a link to a newly created fol=
der containing STEP files for 3D printed files for Pilot 8. Also collecting=
 STEP files of all the necessary 3D printed parts in that folder. There are=
 a bunch of files in the folder that were used on the Epson printers but ar=
e now unnecessary because they will be machined for Pilot 8 (mostly various=
 endstop mounts).</p>
<p local-id=3D"734e8c38475b">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2c926f5ade9e">I want to get the design of the test bench to =
the point that I can do a PDR at the R&amp;D weekly meeting tomorrow.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3c409ed30223"></p>
<p local-id=3D"f0866b5a0296">2026-05-29</p>
<p local-id=3D"ad94013e4093">HOPPER:</p>
<p local-id=3D"9b7fb86955d1">The machined parts for Hopper V5.2 finally sho=
wed up from Xometry. At the morning standup, Marcus said to deprioritize th=
e hopper in favor of developing the CleanPrint(tm) process or, in my case, =
the heat exchanger test bench.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e9dc171dfc5c">Requested that Ben remove the hopper from Pilo=
t 2 so I can scavenge the various expensive components from it (bearings, e=
tc.) and assemble Hopper V5.2. I am confident that it will never be used, b=
ut I plan to put it together and have it on my desk as a cool looking show =
piece.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6ed2f344606e">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"998a5d2e2e19">Gave the modified stage side machined aluminum=
 Mest mount to Ben.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e1467a99da8f">R&amp;D Weekly Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"f61bfa9a3df8">CleanPrint(tm):</p>
<p local-id=3D"a2bad24cd87b">Main focus of this past week was learning abou=
t the process, knowledge transfer, and G-code optimization.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3b1960b931e2">Midhun wants to increase the air temperature o=
f the internal chamber of the printer to reduce the likelihood of the sacri=
ficial filler material cracking.</p>
<p local-id=3D"88d6c932eb38">ZZ has set up a Jira page for slicer work and =
is requesting that any change requests be formalized in Jira.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ff888ce0e3a1">Devin invited to meeting to answer questions a=
bout which machines had been used to print previous heat exchanger models.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"bb6399771d42">There have apparently been issues with the Mes=
t unit rubbing on the build or plate. The print was not supposed to use the=
 Mest unit at all, but for some reason the Mest unit dropped during the pri=
nt.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2cc8570048a8">Alex left the meeting after 30 minutes. He ret=
urned 30 minutes later. He left after 10 minutes.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a843a9d4d393">Meeting appears to be exclusively focused on m=
odifying the slicer to work with the current CleanPrint(tm) process.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b74493c6bf84">Midhun et al goals or next week:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d4bf94a1d920">Get the Mest operating on Pilot 7. Run an exte=
nded non-CleanPrint(tm) print first to ensure that it can print TRS coupons=
 in copper without the Mest dispenser.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3f1e4e8ab4b4">Run prints on Pilot 6 while getting good telem=
etry and adjust parameters to get good parts.</p>
<p local-id=3D"42708230f457">TO DO:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"244b53bdf687">
<li local-id=3D"65fc34ea332d">
<p local-id=3D"96e7dad43084">Finish generating STEP files for 3D printing p=
arts for Pilot 8</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"fed94adbf067">
<p local-id=3D"51599be1bb9a">Get LabJack T4 up and running integrated with =
LabView</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"25f3570a0cd6">
<p local-id=3D"6e54075f1649">Finish component selection</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"d7cb1c9f1d0d">
<p local-id=3D"6ea6580038cc">Finish detailed design</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"56fe9c0f1837">
<p local-id=3D"1f7f666d81fa">Order components</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"d84375f8f138">2026-06-02</p>
<p local-id=3D"554ca24aef63">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7a9c388ebf34">There was a visitor from Nippon Sanso. The Nip=
pon Sanso Holdings Corporation, a subsidiary of Mitsubishi Chemicals Group,=
 which is owned by the Mitsubishi Group. The Nippon Sanso Holdings Corporat=
ion owns Taiyo Nippon Sanso, a company that manufactures industrial gases. =
The NSHC also owns Thermos, strangely enough, which it turns out is both an=
 American company and not just a genericized trademark. I think the visitor=
 was from Taiyo Nippon Sanso based on the fact that they focused on the use=
 of inert gases in our printing process (per Mason). Per Marcus, they are i=
nterested in spooling up an AM facility (I assume in Japan, although Marcus=
 didn=E2=80=99t specify), and may be interested in setting up some of our p=
rinters there.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7b9af5e427b3">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"99c98c31dc53">I need the design of the heat exchanger flow t=
est bench to be at a point where I can present it to the team at the Heat S=
ync tomorrow (which Midhun and I have been added to).</p>
<p local-id=3D"78e80d062df2">I am dropping the maximum pump pressure from 1=
00 to 30 PSI because I realized that the max pump pressure only needs to ma=
tch the max heat exchanger delta P (probably about 15 PSI) with a buffer fo=
r blocked heat exchangers and extreme geometry. This expands the possibilit=
y space of pumps that can be used.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6f3466463a90">2026-06-03</p>
<p local-id=3D"56eddfea61dd">Heat Sync:</p>
<p local-id=3D"d7ab7cc6867a">Weekly meeting on heat exchangers w/ Marcus, A=
lex, Sean, Senai, and me. Midhun was invited but I guess he had a baby in t=
he last day or two.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f61ac1dbaad6">Marcus presented some results from CoolIT Syst=
ems Inc. about a 15 kW heat exchanger with a flow rate of 18 LPM of a water=
/glycol solution.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4b518c8b3bb3">For my test bench, I need a signoff on maximum=
 pressure drop across the heat exchanger (currently 36 PSI), maximum flow r=
ate (currently 8 LPM), and maximum power input into the heater block (curre=
ntly open, I was thinking in the region of 1kW but that may need to be bump=
ed up). I also need to know the input coolant temperature range. Chip based=
 heat application or generalized heater block?</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b98800a08e4">0-7 PSI diff press sens</p>
<p local-id=3D"bbcef445fd5b">Pump OK to 8 LPM</p>
<p local-id=3D"4b6b713b2fb2">2.5 kW min, 5 kW</p>
<p local-id=3D"e4d6d9ac0911">Coolant inlet T: Room temp to 45C, -10 to 60C =
optimum</p>
<p local-id=3D"efe612c093ba"></p>
<p local-id=3D"7f7750d5d335">Marcus is talking about the =E2=80=9Chero plat=
e=E2=80=9D design, which it sounds like nominally is a cooling plate design=
ed for a COTS Nvidia GPU (the modern model heat exchangers are being design=
ed for currently is the Nvidia Blackwell series, whose power is ~1200 W). T=
he actual commercial data center boards have multiple GPU and HBM (high ban=
dwidth memory) modules on an Interposer substrate (silicon substrate). The =
name Marcus is using for the assembly is a MCM (multi-chip module). One sti=
cking point is that the chips tend to be at different heights so the bottom=
 of our heat exchanger will need to have steps machined into the bottom. &l=
t;note: our heat exchanger will warp due to temperature changes and tempera=
ture gradients, and this effect may be more exaggerated than it would be wi=
th more traditional manufacturing processes because our printed parts have =
wacky material properties, which may be worse with the PEG added to parts&g=
t;.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fda38e023601">Based on a diagram Marcus showed, the Rubin GP=
U/HBM square has a layout of 47.5X47.5mm. Marcus' target for the demo HX is=
 a functional (chilled) layout of 65X65mm. There is a question of how non-u=
niform the heat application to our heat exchanger from the GPU will be in s=
ervice.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7b48b36fe2b2">Sean modified his simulations to use the actua=
l measured thermal conductivity of our printed parts: 367 w/mK for Sandvik =
122 HIP and 330 w/mK for Ultrafine 198 HIP. His results showed that the deg=
radation in max and average temperature in operation is less than 0.5%. &lt=
;note: this seems to counter the common assertion that 3D printing in pure =
copper doesn=E2=80=99t actually give a large advantage over alloys.</p>
<p local-id=3D"884713c53d8f">Sean has also been doing transient analyses in=
stead of his previous steady-state simulations, which is valuable for appli=
cations like AEM=E2=80=99s wafer testing where exchangers need to be heated=
 and cooled quickly.</p>
<p local-id=3D"fdf1965c3f07">He also did a structural analysis of the heat =
sink because Accretech asked if a 100X100mm heat sink could withstand 300 k=
gf of compressive force. The maximum stress was ~4 MPa, with a nominal yiel=
d stress of 43 MPa, which is good, but deflection was predicted to be about=
 50 microns, which sounds high to me, but the spec would need to be checked=
 with Accretech.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8646d1fd6eb6">Accretech uses Galden D02TS coolant.</p>
