<p local-id=3D"3c69e3e81c1b">Speaking with ZZ and Mason, Mason said that he=
 heard that this new company will in fact give us health insurance (which i=
s something Alex had to fight for, which is terrifying; it had never occurr=
ed to me that any kind of startup or engineering company would not offer he=
alth insurance. For them to even consider starting up a new company that do=
es not offer health insurance is already a HUGE red flag) and that they wil=
l offer =E2=80=9Ccompetitive=E2=80=9D wages. I would hope that this means t=
hat the wages will be a significant bump over what we are getting at 3DEO t=
o send the message that this new company will not just be a carbon copy of =
3DEO being used as a way to dodge debt but is instead a completely new comp=
any that will take 3DEO=E2=80=99s IP and get it done right this time, but I=
 suspect that they will be grudgingly matching the salaries we have at 3DEO=
 (which are already lower than average companies in the area). Alex did men=
tion a few weeks ago that he expects that this new company will offer great=
er employee ownership in the company (referring to stock options, not meani=
ngful ownership), which would be nice if true, although stock options are g=
enerally not worth anything for about 10-15 years after a company has been =
founded, and if the clock is reset with this new company then we=E2=80=99re=
 looking at a 10% likelihood of a significant payout (90% of startups fail)=
 no earlier than 2040, so that=E2=80=99s obviously not compelling to me in =
monetary terms, although it would be a nice gesture.</p>
<p local-id=3D"aea7c6ff6450">Mason also said that it is possible that someo=
ne may outbid Ken and Takeshi and whoever else for 3DEO=E2=80=99s IP, which=
 would mean that this new company concept would evaporate, although I find =
that highly unlikely.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a67f9f1d8b91">Let=E2=80=99s get some boundaries on the (like=
ly, I=E2=80=99m not going to go far outside the realm of likelihood here) b=
ell curve here:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2c7e97978715">Worst Case:</p>
<p local-id=3D"783f5f71bfd4">Everyone shows up on Monday to find the doors =
locked, and any personal stuff left in the building now belongs to the cred=
itors and the police are called to escort everyone off of the property. The=
y terminate everyone with cause to prevent anyone from claiming unemploymen=
t. An unpleasant situation, but the response is simple; I have a dozen cont=
ingencies in place for this eventuality.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cf9613ae48a5">Best Case:</p>
<p local-id=3D"9f7604799ecb">They start up this new company and rehire ever=
yone with the same or improved benefits. They are able to purchase all of 3=
DEO=E2=80=99s IP. There is some timeline to demonstrate manufacturing a hea=
t exchanger. What then? What would we need to do to be a long-term viable c=
ompany? First, we would need a 1 year, 5 year, and 10 year business plan sh=
owing that we have a clear and compelling path to profitability, that there=
 is a market for whatever we plan to sell, and that we are capable of meeti=
ng the needs of that market. Then we would need to execute on that plan.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"5b4e580a05ef">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"c007c957a809">I worked with Mohsen to remove the Mest spindl=
e stage from Pilot 6 (one of the SS M3 BHCS had been stripped so I had to u=
se a Torx bit to remove it) and I machined down one of the dovetails on the=
 Mest-side mount. As I expected before removing the dovetail, the root caus=
e seems to be that when the set screws used to retain the dovetail were ins=
talled it distorted the aluminum dovetail and because I had machined the do=
vetails with such a close fit that raised metal interfered with removing th=
e dovetails. There are a few ways to resolve this: one would be to switch t=
o set screws with a soft tip that clamp down but do not damage the aluminum=
, but what I ended up doing was machining down one of the Mest-side dovetai=
ls so that there is a flat surface the set screws will hit that is spaced o=
ff of the stage-side dovetail, so that even if there is material raised on =
the Mest-side dovetail, it will not interfere with installation or removal =
of the Mest unit. The downside is that there is no longer any force holding=
 the Mest-side dovetail against the flat face of the stage-side dovetail mo=
unt, so uninstalling and reinstalling the Mest unit will shift its position=
, but it should stay locked in position once the set screws are tightened.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"70e395fdc69b">CleanPrint(tm):</p>
<p local-id=3D"875b5bb3a37a">Marcus sent out an email talking about the con=
cept of reactivating the Beta printers, upgrading the electronics with Pilo=
t electronics, and installing the Mest unit on them to enable printing no-d=
epowdering copper heat exchangers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f6e547eddf31">His goal with this email seems to be raising s=
ome ideas of increasing throughput on the printers by using multiple spindl=
es, multiple Mest units, or some combination of the two. The inside dimensi=
ons of the mist box (which defines the maximum theoretical printable footpr=
int) are 7.625=E2=80=9D square (193.675mm) which, assuming a scaling factor=
 of 1.25, yields a theoretical sintered part footprint of about 155mm, or f=
our parts that are about 77.5mm square. Printing all the way up against the=
 mist box would probably not work reliably, so the real-world limits would =
be stricter. For the sake of clean numbers, call it 4 parts that are 75mm s=
quare. Still highly optimistic, but not impossible.</p>
<p local-id=3D"3b7df9ad3c9a">I find this line of discussion to be interesti=
ng but worrisome; we are far from having a reliable printing process for a =
single part at a time printing on the Pilots, and scaling production on a d=
ifferent machine would take a significant amount of extra work versus getti=
ng the process to work on Pilots (it might just be double the work, but bas=
ed on what we have learned from the F-35 program I=E2=80=99d say we are loo=
king at a minimum of tripling the amount of work required).</p>
