<p local-id=3D"c8a7c4e9ff29">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"24f7299aa010">There is only one machined aluminum carriage s=
pacer around, and 2 are needed for Pilot 8, so I am going to make some more=
. They are a super simple part so it should be easy enough for me to make s=
ome on one of the Trak mills.</p>
<p local-id=3D"296d18322bb7">Apparently the plan now is to sell both Trak m=
achines, both Haas machines, and the 2 op mill. Nick is cleaning up the Tra=
k machine that I have been using in preparation for selling it, so it is un=
likely that any of the mills will be an available resource.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8a9b5c6e4cc9">This is a very interesting case study on the v=
alues of having machine tools onsite and having engineers who are both trai=
ned and permitted to use them: if there are no machinists around, either so=
meone else runs the machines or you don=E2=80=99t get the parts you need. S=
ame thing with not having the appropriate tools: if you don=E2=80=99t have =
the tools, you don=E2=80=99t get the parts you need. If you have no interna=
l tools, everything needs to be farmed out to an external service provider,=
 which is either slow or expensive or both, and denies company employees th=
e chance to develop new skills and form a greater understanding of the manu=
facturing processes. This varies depending on the complexity of the part, o=
f course; if it is a very complex part requiring extensive programming and =
complex fixturing, then the calculus may change because of the amount of ti=
me it would take for an engineer to program it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1b1ddae5c5fd">I entered a purchase request for spacers to sh=
ift the position of the spindle probe on Pilot 2 to be flush with the base =
plate, matching Pilots 4-8. Alex C wants to experiment with Pilot 2 to get =
the end mill probing function working again on the Pilots.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8c20cec9504c">Both Mohsen and Mason encountered issues with =
cut depth uniformity on Pilots 6 and 7, respectively. When cut depth varies=
 linearly in the X direction it can be resolved with spreader bar adjustmen=
t, but linear variation in cut depth in the Y direction is trickier. Mohsen=
 was able to resolve it by printing multiple bound layers; as more layers w=
ere stacked up, the cuts became more uniform. Mason and Dong resolved it by=
 loosening the four screws on one Y stage carriage then retightening those =
screws to the specified torque of 11 Nm (this loosening/retightening step m=
ay be unnecessary but was the procedure they followed so I am noting it her=
e) then loosening all the screws mounting down that Y stage to the base pla=
te, jogging the gantry back and forth in the Y direction, then retightening=
 those screws to 11 Nm.</p>
