<p local-id=3D"7ea5a5556fa3">Heat Sync:</p>
<p local-id=3D"2a789105a79b">Senai did some simulations to determine the mi=
nimum allowable wall thickness of HXs, and recommends a thickness of 1.25mm=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d8ff646b86be">He also worked on a design with targeted cooli=
ng (essentially an HX for each chip, with all HXs integrated into one compo=
nent: 2.2 kW, 45C inlet, 150 psi, 2.64 lpm.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6e7c98f7ee99">The simulation he ran did not converge and the=
 results are nonsensical (~600C chip temp, .1m/s velocity, velocity of flui=
d injected in the center of polar fins did not change as it flows outward).=
</p>
<p local-id=3D"8ee36711f76d">Marcus mentioned the 30-60-90 day plan; what e=
xactly are the deliverables at each decision point? And who is driving thos=
e? And what will we get if we do meet them?</p>
<p local-id=3D"d469d1166567">Alex is talking about two-phase cooling (enclo=
sed wick exchangers and pumped two phase systems). Sean raised the idea of =
retrofitting single-phase liquid cooling systems with two-phase systems.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"0f42b3adce78">Senai pointed out that we don=E2=80=99t want t=
o lose focus from the =E2=80=9Chero plate=E2=80=9D concept if that is the g=
oal of the 30-60-90 plan.</p>
<p local-id=3D"cd5115f06acf">Marcus plans to print some sample wick structu=
res if there is bandwidth. Specifically he wants to recreate some research =
that Mohsen did in grad school with mixing powder with sacrificial material=
, except with copper. &lt;What is our value add? COTS heat pipes already us=
e sintered metal as wicks. Would we be printing it all in one shot with dif=
ferent powders in one print, to eliminate assembly and sealing steps?&gt;</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"c02fedf386ec">Sean focused mainly on FEA to determine the ma=
x allowable internal pressure of printed HXs. He also highlighted that our =
printed parts have anisotropic strength, sharp internal corners, and a tend=
ency for interlayer porosity to form when the cross sectional area of the p=
art changes significantly during the print, or even just randomly as a natu=
re of our process and process controls.</p>
<p local-id=3D"959f144d1bd9">His simulations showed that it could handle up=
 to 150 PSI (with a safety factor of 1.2 PSI haha), but a deformation of 14=
0 microns at 50 PSI, 258 at 100, 358 at 150. It is also important to note t=
hat this is with a wall thickness of .5mm. This is an interesting artifact =
of the the high level design architecture where it uses large empty plenum =
spaces that then feed into many microchannels. He found that adding a reinf=
orcing wall reduced the deformation by a factor of two, but increased the m=
ax stress. Increasing the roof thickness to 1mm reduced deformation and str=
ess, although the results are confusing and seem to show slightly more defo=
rmation with a thicker wall?</p>
<p local-id=3D"993fd1252be8">There is interest in testing the failure press=
ure of heat sinks along with the deformation of the part as pressure is var=
ied.</p>
<p local-id=3D"71a283478e82">Sean also ran some FEA on compressing the heat=
 exchanger, although the results came out a little weird; it looks like he =
applied a pressure to the top surface instead of a uniformly applied force =
so the deformation distance of the top surface varied depending on the unde=
rlying features.</p>
<p local-id=3D"761ad4ae6452">Sean also did a simulation to compare our heat=
 exchanger design with CoolIT=E2=80=99s design, based on the data they have=
 published for a heat exchanger running at 15 kW with a flow rate of 18 LPM=
 and an inlet temperature of 45C. He assumed PG25 coolant because it is a c=
ommonly used coolant in the industry, but CoolIT did not publish the exact =
coolant formulation they used. The pressure drop Sean found at those (extre=
mely high) flow rates was in the =E2=80=9Chigh double-digits=E2=80=9D of PS=
I. Our heat exchanger design=E2=80=99s performance is somewhat lower than C=
oolIT=E2=80=99s published numbers (for ours, temp rise of 48.67C and resist=
ance of .0032 C/W, and for theirs a temperature rise of 35C and a thermal r=
esistance of .0023 C/W).</p>
<p local-id=3D"69f66dffeefd"></p>
<p local-id=3D"2d651cb1b219">2026-06-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"0b59a1e50888">PILOT:</p>
<p local-id=3D"dc43b01fd50f">Ning theorizes that issues seen with the Pilot=
s are due to fluctuations in the city provided power ultimately due to grid=
 power shortages. Since this has not been measured and does not seem to hav=
e been an issue when we were running Saffrons, machining tools, and furnace=
s constantly it seems unlikely to me that our current facility and city pow=
er supply is unable to power the Pilots reliably, but it is possible that t=
hings have changed in the past year or two. Ning recommends installing powe=
r filters in the machines to filter out power surges.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d037417afc02">I asked Devin whether he has seen these kinds =
of power surge issues in the past, and he said that he had seen issues that=
 he theorized were due to power fluctuations (prints that stopped or faulte=
d out for no obvious reason) but there was no confirmation that this was th=
e case. If we do end up moving to a new facility, we will need a customized=
 facility power system to meet our power needs, so I would recommend integr=
ating power monitoring into that system when we build it.</p>
