<p local-id=3D"9b2dfe74e0a8">2026-06-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"045aa64c5ce5">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"1f970d0ead05">I am investigating using a PCB with surface-mo=
unt transistors as the thermal test vehicle instead of a copper block with =
cartridge heaters. This will reduce cost and also give greater resolution i=
n heat application and measurement.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f16a54022a02">After extensive research and design, it looks =
like my best option is to use linear-mode MOSFETs as the heat source, which=
 are designed to operate in the linear region instead of the typical on/off=
 behavior of MOSFETs. Usually you want MOSFETs to dissipate as little heat =
as possible because you typically use them for switching loads on and off, =
but in my case I am trying to use the MOSFET as the load and have it dissip=
ate all the heat, which runs the risk of destroying the components. The Lin=
ear 2 series of MOSFETs made by IXYS (now owned by LittelFuse) is designed =
to operate continuously within the linear analog region where it will be di=
ssipating large amounts of heat, so it is my best bet for a MOSFET that is =
also a heat source. The downside to these components is that they=E2=80=99r=
e pretty expensive; it=E2=80=99s ~$18 per component, and with my nominal or=
iginal design I would need 100 of them. I=E2=80=99m planning to reduce the =
number of MOSFETs and either reduce the power capability of the TTV, drive =
each MOSFET with more power, or more likely a combination of the two.</p>
<p local-id=3D"603395c3b2c3">2026-06-16</p>
<p local-id=3D"243b05ff28ff">HEALTH: Working on refining the design of the =
MOSFET-based heater.</p>
