<p local-id=3D"669f9a24779f">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"ecae63ee2064">I am working on a semi-modular approach to the=
 heat source PCB: each MOSFET will have an associated DAC for current contr=
ol, a shunt resistor and INA229 (NOT INA2290) for reading shunt resistor cu=
rrent (the INA229 also has an internal temperature sensor, so if you design=
 the PCB so that the INA229 and the shunt resistor are in contact you can m=
easure the shunt resistor temperature and use that to compensate for resist=
ance change as a result of temperature and get a more accurate current valu=
e), and a temperature sensor (that is connected to the copper heat transfer=
 plate), all of which communicate with an RP2040 (either a XIAO board solde=
red to the main board or possibly an RP2040 directly mounted to the board) =
using SPI. The way SPI works is that the 3 data lines from the MCU are wire=
d to each SPI device in parallel, and there needs to be an independently co=
ntrolled chip select pin for each device. This means that the MCU needs a m=
inimum of 48 digital outputs, but just in case I am going to design it with=
 a spare output for each node for a total of 64 outlets. Fortunately these =
outputs don=E2=80=99t need to have any fancy communication capabilities, so=
 I am going to use an array of 74HC595 serial-to-parallel shift registers (=
8X ICs, each of which has 8X digital outputs).</p>
<p local-id=3D"aba9649b25dd"></p>
<p local-id=3D"e1d4b8cf5d9f">2026-06-18</p>
<p local-id=3D"790455b16bfc">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"13efb38765f5">The homing switch on the U tank of Pilot 6 was=
n=E2=80=99t working, so I removed the 3D printed endstop mount, sanded down=
 the face of the mount so that the endstop switch is more exposed, and rein=
stalled it. I also checked the Molex connectors because people have a tende=
ncy to crimp the contacts incorrectly which can result in them breaking, bu=
t they seemed OK. Ben tested homing after reinstallation and it seems to be=
 working. For Pilots 8 forward the U and V tank endstop mounts are machined=
 from aluminum, which should help with reliability of the endstops because =
the 3D printed part can warp over time and shift the endstop to a position =
where it doesn=E2=80=99t contact the U or V tank plates, but the accessibil=
ity of the endstop mount is an issue. I think the endstop mount should bolt=
 directly to the sides of the powder tank module, with the screws moved clo=
ser to the bottom so that they can be reached more easily. Also, the powder=
 and build tank module needs to be redesigned so that it can be removed fro=
m the machine easily (using a rolling cart with extended arms like OneClick=
Metal=E2=80=99s design) and also needs to be easier to disassemble for clea=
ning and maintenance. I also think it should be redesigned with filleted co=
rners similar to EOS printer plates to make sealing easier and more robust.=
</p>
