<p local-id=3D"299e6bf30890">I asked about whether thermal performance is t=
he main value-add or the manufacturability is the goal. Marcus' opinion is =
there is a threshold thermal performance that needs to be hit, and as long =
as we hit that, then anything beyond that is a competitive advantage &lt;wh=
at do customers want that is beyond what they have in operation?&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"2aa7559d0df8">Per Marcus, the big performance characteristic=
s are thermal resistance and pressure drop (both a function of flow rate).<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"4f89cfeec1c2">Mohsen asked about if customers are happy with=
 the systems that are currently in operation and how saturated the market i=
s currently. We are currently targeting the previous generation GPUs (Black=
well) because there is more published data about their performance, where t=
he market is probably saturated. This case is more of a technology demonstr=
ator than a sellable product because of how saturated the market is. Long-t=
erm we are planning to focus on higher-performance cooling applications (po=
ssibly custom server designs). We are also targeting the semiconductor test=
 industry (Accretech, AEM, etc.). In that case, the design parameters would=
 need to be more customizable for low-volume high-mix parts. I asked about =
whether customers in this industry care about different performance specs t=
han data center applications. Per Alex, performance specs are fairly compar=
able with the GPU industry, although it sounds like thermal response time m=
ay be more valuable in that industry rather than optimized heat transfer at=
 steady-state conditions, as well as conductivity through the heat exchange=
r.</p>
