g during the Japan visit, along with some of their customers. Sean asked wh=
at the desired outcome/objective of the Japan meeting is.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ae2fce7a8451">Alex recommends testing some different sacrifi=
cial filler material in the same way that Payman et al had tried before by =
printing open boxes, filling them with filler material, capping off the box=
es, then melting/burning out the filler material. I agree with the spirit o=
f the request, namely investigating alternative materials for the CleanPrin=
t(tm) process that may have the strengths of PEG (machinability, higher mel=
ting point than paraffin, relatively clean burnout) without the weaknesses =
(degrades the green/brown part when it is being melted out), although I don=
=E2=80=99t believe that filling boxes again is the best way to do this. I w=
ould recommend:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"4c5ddaa856f5">
<li local-id=3D"b44b3ae8cfaa">
<p local-id=3D"a20f2eb0b48b">Based on previous results and the current stat=
e of the art, select a set of possible materials and material blends, along=
 with documented predictions of the reason they may be superior to PEG.</p>=
</li>
<li local-id=3D"fe8f6f9a3460">
<p local-id=3D"df838429bb69">Develop rapid blocking experiments to downsele=
ct: burnout tests (easy to do batches, and if a material doesn=E2=80=99t bu=
rn out cleanly then it is not usable), machinability tests (correlate with =
hardness if possible to make blocking even easier), dispensing tests.</p></=
li>
<li local-id=3D"6fa79462fde3">
<p local-id=3D"dc40ed2c7492">Once a subset of materials has been selected, =
print a simplified part with the CleanPrint(tm) process (maybe an enclosed =
box or similar)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"f29c10633abe">
<p local-id=3D"3c94c5504f1b">Debind and sinter these parts</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"e33961d7cd94"></p>
