<p local-id=3D"7e431d4884f3">I bought a 0-5VDC to 4-20 mA converter board w=
ith my own money that arrived today. This is to interface the LabJack analo=
g output with the VFD; The voltage control on the VFD is 0-10V while the ma=
x output of the LabJack is 5VDC, so if I just connected the analog output s=
traight we could only run the motor up to half its maximum speed.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2b6bcbea2217">Alex is working on getting the system to minim=
um viable operating status: if the pressure drop, absolute inlet pressure, =
delta T, flow rate, and temperature of the contact surface can be recorded =
then that should be enough for basic initial testing and benchmarking of he=
at exchangers. The temperature of the contact surface will initially be ass=
umed to be the displayed temperature of the hot plate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"814dba36db9b">For accurate and automatic testing, we will ne=
ed closed-loop control of the flow rate (which will require that the LabJac=
k can control the pump speed) and closed-loop control of the applied temper=
ature (which will require that the LabJack can control the cartridge heater=
s by PWMing an SSR), I will make a copper temperature measurement block to =
go between the heat source and the heat exchanger, and I will also make a h=
eater block with installed cartridge heaters and RTDs that are read and con=
trolled by the LabJack. This will enable automatic data collection at varyi=
ng flow rates and applied temperatures, as well as measuring the actual hea=
t flow into the heat exchanger by comparing RTD values along the direction =
of heat flow out of the heater block.</p>
