<p local-id=3D"4af5c1bff940">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b00a6c2c757">Resolved the fluctuations in inlet and outlet =
temperature readings. To diagnose the issue, I first removed one of the tem=
perature sensors from its manifold. I noted that the temperature reading wa=
s much more stable, and the fluctuations could be intentionally caused by t=
ouching the sensor threads to the manifold block. This suggested that the i=
ssue was due to a floating negative on the sensor side; the manifold and di=
fferential pressure sensor are earth grounded through the differential pres=
sure sensor, but the temperature sensors were only connected to the negativ=
e of the 24VDC power supply and the negative was isolated from ground. I in=
stalled a jumper wire between the earth ground terminal block stack and the=
 24VDC negative terminal block stack, and the temperature readings seem muc=
h more stable now.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4c949b018261">I also connected a Fluke 179 multimeter across=
 the heater connection point and measured the actual voltage (110.2 VAC) an=
d then measured the current through the cartridge heater block when it is p=
owered at 100% and it read 5.48A initially, and dropped to 5.45A when the H=
X surface was at 64.5C (the safety RTD system reads 69.1C). This correspond=
s to almost exactly 600W, so I think it is reasonable safe to assume that t=
he heater block is outputting 600W at full power.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b339d6db7b4">With this much power input, the coolant reserv=
oir temperature rises rapidly (as measured by the coolant inlet temperature=
) (~10C/10min) so it is necessary to run the radiator fan when the heater b=
lock is running.</p>
<p local-id=3D"483744675886">I ran a rough flow sensor calibration test whe=
re I set the DAC output to the pump at .9V which a previous test showed res=
ulted in a flow meter value of 1 LPM and set up a tube so the coolant was f=
lowing into a graduated container for 60s. The quantity of liquid dispensed=
 was almost exactly 1L after 1 minute, so I think the flow meter is reasona=
bly accurate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1baf223aba1e">I implemented a PI closed-loop flow control al=
gorithm that gets to the right value and holds it without significant oscil=
lations, but the rate at which it gets to the right value is fairly slow (~=
30s to ramp up or down 2 LPM). Increasing the P term higher than 1.2 yields=
 oscillations, and the derivative term did not help to cancel those out.</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"0c256e69d559">I did a full test on the Dracaena HX, and the =
results still don=E2=80=99t make sense, so I am now doing a calibration on =
the temperature sensors. I created an ice bath by freezing tap water in som=
e 3DEO cups and put one of those ice cubes in my 3DEO Yeti mug. I then boil=
ed some water and poured it into the Yeti mug, however, the voltage values =
dropped steadily even as I switched between temperature sensors and LabJack=
 ports, suggesting that the temperature of the bath was dropping significan=
tly between tests. Regardless, the calculated temperature using my existing=
 algorithm was ~82-88C, which is much lower than it should be (in theory 10=
0C, although I=E2=80=99d expect a little lower).</p>
<p local-id=3D"7057861ce83e">My current working theory is that the equation=
s I am using to calculate the temperature from the voltage values are close=
 enough to seem correct but are far enough from reality to give false power=
 readings (hence Qdiss reading greater than Qin).</p>
<p local-id=3D"009dca5f0a27"></p>
