<p local-id=3D"e07c4ee9ce63">The kinds of errors and fluctuations I=E2=80=
=99m seeing are way too large to trust the results, though. At a flow rate =
of 3 LPM, the delta T I am reading is 3C, yielding a thermal resistance of =
0.044. Drop that down by .8C, though, and you get 0.0601. Raise it by .8C, =
and you get 0.0348. Granted, the actual errors under most operating regimes=
 aren=E2=80=99t quite as bad because the delta T is much larger than that, =
but that is an unacceptable amount of error for getting actionable results.=
 One major contributing factor is that the temperature sensors I=E2=80=99m =
using have a range of 0-300F (-17.8 to 148.9C), mapping to 4-20 mA, mapping=
 to a LJTick Current Shunt voltage range of 0.472 to 2.360 V. Also, the NPT=
 threads on the temperature sensors make them a pain to remove and reinstal=
l for testing and calibration. The obvious solution is to replace them with=
 extended RTD probes that go into compression fittings and feed those into =
an RTD amplifier with a narrower range (20-50C would be fairly optimal)</p>
