<p local-id=3D"0d58b4cdbf5a">I really screwed up and failed to do the neces=
sary math when I was designing the test bench. What I need to figure out is=
 how much inaccuracy in inlet and outlet temperature readings will affect t=
he thermal resistance value. Let=E2=80=99s start with the final goal:</p>
<p local-id=3D"5a52c3680466">Need thermal resistance, calculated with the f=
ollowing equation:</p>
<p local-id=3D"979efa2c2057">(Tsurf-Tinlet)/Qin . The difference between th=
e HX surface temperature and the inlet temperature divided by the heat into=
 the surface of the HX. Surface temperature we estimate with a temperature =
reading from a sensor installed in the heater block stack that is as close =
to the surface as possible. The actual surface temperature of the HX will b=
e off from this due to the TIM between the heater block and the HX, which i=
s already a source of inaccuracy, but this can be compensated for in the si=
mulation if the thermal resistance of the TIM is known. Since we do not hav=
e the TIM and the joint completely characterized, we would need to estimate=
 the thermal resistance. This is one source of error in the thermal resista=
nce calculation. The inlet temperature is determined by a temperature senso=
r measuring the temperature of the coolant as close to the HX as possible.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"b4d4afa4ce1a">The Qin value, the actual heat going into the =
heat exchanger, is much more interesting to determine. There are three ways=
 to get it, with varying levels of accuracy. They are, starting with the le=
ast accurate and increasing in accuracy:</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"a94360ea22c3">
<li local-id=3D"4912bf82a6f8">
<p local-id=3D"36531288472f">The power going into the heater block can be m=
easured directly from the voltage across and the current through the heat s=
ource. Calculating the thermal resistance with this value will give the the=
rmal resistance value under perfect conditions, where all heat into the hea=
ter block goes into the HX. This is useful for bounding the thermal resista=
nce, but the real world thermal resistance will be higher due to heat loss =
from the system (heat lost from the sides and bottom of the heater block).<=
/p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2923aa916262">
<p local-id=3D"87d08b9f652c">Next, it is possible to determine heat flow th=
rough the heater block by installing temperature sensors at multiple points=
 in a region of the heater block above the actual heat source so that they =
are in line with the flow of heat. Since the thermal conductivity of solid =
copper is well known, the heat flux can be calculated easily from the therm=
al gradient at different points. The TIM between the temperature sensors an=
d the heater block only matters if the system is not at steady state; at st=
eady state the temperature of the sensors will exactly match the temperatur=
es of their surroundings. This is the method used to measure heat flow thro=
ugh standardized test setups used for characterizing TIM, and is an accurat=
e way to measure the Qin value, and is independent of the flow rate or type=
 of fluid being used in the HX. However, it does not measure the actual hea=
t added to the coolant.</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"8db4b2ff2e0b">
<p local-id=3D"efca80723100">By measuring the inlet and outlet temperatures=
 and flow rate of the coolant, as long as the density and specific heat of =
the coolant are known, it is possible to calculate the caloric heat actuall=
y added to the coolant. This is a less relevant metric for end users trying=
 to cool a heat source because they care about how much heat can be pulled =
from the heat source at what temperature, but this is a very useful metric =
for characterizing heat exchangers themselves because it is independent of =
the heat source or TIM.</p></li>
</ol>
<p local-id=3D"b9d4f9c3ff13">The best way to design and operate a test benc=
h is to determine Q with all three of these methods to generate three therm=
al resistance curves. In theory all three should match, so the variation be=
tween them yields information about errors in the system.</p>
<p local-id=3D"785f021da7c8">The LabJack has a 12 bit resolution over an in=
put range of 0-2.5V, meaning it can differentiate between 4096 steps in tha=
t range, or 0.0006V. The actual detected voltage from the LabJack current s=
hunt is 0.472 - 2.360, so the output of the temperature sensor sweeps over =
a 1.888V range, which is (1.888 / 2.5) of the 4096 step range. This comes o=
ut to about 3093 steps detectable over the temperature sensor=E2=80=99s ran=
ge. Even before factoring in any errors, there is a hard limit on the tempe=
rature change that the LabJack can discriminate. The temperature sensors th=
at are currently being used have a range of 0 - 300F, or ~ -17.8 - 148.9C, =
a range of ~166.7C. Divide this by 3093 and you get a minimum detectable te=
mperature change of ~0.054C. At 600W, a change in the delta T of 0.054C res=
ults in a change of 0.0000898 C/W in thermal resistance. Now let=E2=80=99s =
start breaking down some of the actual errors.</p>
<p local-id=3D"299ec1071776">The accuracy of the temperature sensors is sta=
ted in the technical specifications document as being 0.25K + 0.002 |T|, wh=
ere T (oddly enough in C, even though it adds on 0.25K) is the absolute val=
ue of the temperature reading. This is an interesting way to spec accuracy;=
 it suggests that accuracy is best at 0C and gets worse as you increase or =
decrease the temperature. My guess would be that this is because they calib=
rate it at 0C. At the low end of what I care about (25C) that comes out to =
0.3C, and at 45 that comes out to 0.34C. Doesn=E2=80=99t sound too bad, but=
 since we care about the delta T, the worst case error is if one is reading=
 high and the other low, resulting in an inaccuracy of 0.68C.</p>
<p local-id=3D"9d8c320ef97a"></p>
