<p local-id=3D"83946f6dc199">2026-08-28</p>
<p local-id=3D"467c538946dd">Per Midhun, sintering at DSH is still under de=
velopment. Their most recent sintering run resulted in some melting of the =
part.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f1acbb20a90b">Accretech Project Reboot Meeting (called by Se=
an):</p>
<p local-id=3D"51fa6b202d12">They want a 100X100mm cold plate assembly for =
die-level testing, potentially with heaters integrated into it.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c7f507aa171e">They have mentioned delivery around February 2=
027, but it is unclear how absolute that requirement is.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a9c16e6d6b18">They want a prototype they can test that is ma=
de with a design that is on a path to a production solution.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a3bff5213bf5">Per Alex, the production rate is likely ~15-30=
 per month.</p>
<p local-id=3D"7646939e1499">They want to minimize the thermal capacity of =
the base to optimize for heating and cooling rate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"b0338ae934e9">They are not currently interested in targeted =
heating and cooling, they want it to be as uniform as possible.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a470f5327c5c">Flow rate, coolant formulation, and burst pres=
sure need to be determined from Accretech.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ef2646017014">It sounds like the main priority of the projec=
t is to minimize response time of heating and cooling &lt;I assume the flow=
 rate and inlet pressure are fixed, otherwise they could just pump through =
a ton of coolant to get it to respond faster (maybe?)&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"7482b0d600c9">I pitched using two liquid loops that the syst=
em switches between, one hot loop and one cold loop, which Sean said they m=
ay be open to but Senai pushed back on it, based on concerns about difficul=
ty of implementing such a system with our timeline. I personally disagree; =
a hot side loop would be super easy to add to the heat exchanger test bench=
. It would just need an extra pump and motor, a heated coolant reservoir, a=
nd fast-acting valves to switch between them. Since I already have designs =
for fast-acting valves for my rocket project, that would be trivial for me =
to implement.</p>
<p local-id=3D"72cc06410f49">The coolant formulation is not known yet, but =
Senai believes that the one they discussed was quite viscous. We don=E2=80=
=99t have specs for required pressure drop and flow rate.</p>
<p local-id=3D"458bfd31c851">Senai asked whether it would be easier to prin=
t a larger part with microchannels only, or a smaller part with manifold, a=
nd Midhun=E2=80=99s response was that the larger part would be easier.</p>
<p local-id=3D"62e71490dc2e">Sean asked about design rules for the CleanPri=
nt(tm) process, and there aren=E2=80=99t any hard requirements, just intuit=
ive opinions on what is more or less difficult to print. My pitch is to mov=
e as much of the manifolding to the machined base as possible and remove th=
e runners that are filled with filler material.</p>
<p local-id=3D"f6817d22f857">We do not currently have the ability to sinter=
 17-4PH SS parts, and it is not something being pursued currently.</p>
<p local-id=3D"35c5290f5c3e">HEALTH:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"3d4435accc64">
<li local-id=3D"347c13ca0831">
<p local-id=3D"2c1a64704b81">Order a cold plate and heat source with specif=
ied thermal resistance</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"2a74a8fbb47d">
<p local-id=3D"2c708ea9fca3">Reinstall the 20X20mm cold plate and generate =
all three thermal resistance curves</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"45f7eadb7b6c">I switched out the Dracaena HX for the origina=
l 20X20mm 3D printed HX to get some results that I can reasonably trust. I =
installed a new RTD in the intermediate block to replace the one that had b=
een damaged for some reason. The results were initially strange; the interm=
ediate block results suggested that the power flow through the block was hi=
gher than the input power into the stack, which is impossible. I removed al=
l three RTDs, applied Arctic Silver MX-7 thermal paste to them, and reinsta=
lled them, and the results seem better.</p>
<p local-id=3D"99d0b731882b">I am also running it in the newer operating re=
gime; I keep the heater block on at full power constantly instead of regula=
ting it to maintain a constant HX temperature.</p>
<p local-id=3D"91487db2b2ab"></p>
<p local-id=3D"5a6dd2f09c71">2026-08-31</p>
<p local-id=3D"9baf9cc31b7e">R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"8aeea617908a">Mason working on printing 65X65mm heatsink on =
Pilot 8, ~45 minutes per layer. Tried some various experiments: dispensing =
on part boundary, increasing wall thickness, 2 others. Best results found b=
y cutting multiple boundary layers at once. Large delams seen in experiment=
 4 (cutting part boundary first). With experiment 4, the delam was found to=
 extend past the part into the cake, within the footprint of the facing ope=
ration.</p>
<p local-id=3D"ec1947c9ce77">Dong=E2=80=99s experiments: parts dewaxed on Z=
TA setters lost more mass than parts that were dewaxed in alumina ballast. =
Experiments 6, 7, 8, 3R2 need to be broken out and data needs to be collect=
ed. Experiment 1, spraying again after facing, needs to be repeated. Experi=
ment 9 needs to be conducted still.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d0305150ea66">Midhun presented results from experiments cond=
ucted by printing with paraffin sacrificial filler material. As dispensed, =
the paraffin extends above the top of the build, and after the facing op th=
ere is paraffin seen to extend beyond the nominal edges of the cuts, as if =
the end mill is smearing the paraffin. &lt;Maybe try facing with different =
feeds and speeds? Since the end mill is much larger in diameter than the us=
ual cutter, it should have a greatly reduced RPM or reduced &lt;?&gt; feed =
rate to match the usual surface speed. It also might be beneficial to use a=
 different filler material.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"f17860a81cfa">Alex asked about why exactly we need the flatt=
ening pass, which is a valid question although worrisomely fundamental to o=
ur process to be asking about at this point. Then again, it is always bette=
r to ask questions when you don=E2=80=99t know something, no matter when. B=
etter to look stupid and learn rather than pretend to know something and st=
ay ignorant.</p>
<p local-id=3D"199d572dc128">Upmilling =3D Conventional Cutting</p>
<p local-id=3D"52c991f3ab08">Downmilling =3D Climb Cutting</p>
<p local-id=3D"2dbecc75c8ef">I raised the concept of switching to a differe=
nt dispensing technology that would be faster and eliminate the issue being=
 seen with blobs forming when the Mest dispenser is started at the same tim=
e that the gantry starts moving. I didn=E2=80=99t get any traction since we=
 don=E2=80=99t have time to explore different options before we have to del=
iver this =E2=80=9Chero plate.=E2=80=9D</p>
<p local-id=3D"c1aaea554898">I also raised the idea of buying a dropwatcher=
 to calibrate the Mest units more easily and reliably rather than trying to=
 run lines of droplets and setting the parameters that way. This would also=
 let us benchmark the quality of droplets (i.e. whether micro droplets are =
being formed, etc.). I didn=E2=80=99t get any traction here either.</p>
<p local-id=3D"0153f1929ece">Midhun notes that using paraffin as the sacrif=
icial filler material has improved the dewaxing process as compared with PE=
G. This comes at the cost of reduced machinability compared with PEG and mo=
re blobs at the start of the dispense, which per Mohsen is less of a proble=
m with PEG than it is with paraffin.</p>
