<p local-id=3D"4f60611811c0">2026-09-04</p>
<p local-id=3D"7a963d831dee">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"dd964f13b917">Now that the hardware design is ready to go, I=
 need to figure out the instrumentation. This really has several parts:</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"1be755e2c9e7">
<li local-id=3D"e046fe1c5344">
<p local-id=3D"8f8f9dc55252">Determine what needs to be measured and how ac=
curately it needs to be measured</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"a54172afbb77">
<p local-id=3D"71404930e8a1">Determine how to measure and record the necess=
ary parameters</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e9a8ede54e6e">
<p local-id=3D"6fdd578d44ed">Determine how to confirm that the sensors are =
reading as accurately as required</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"0bdf89da1a14">
<p local-id=3D"821264a4e957">Build, wire, and program the system</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"633af2f8121a">
<p local-id=3D"d44a019806b2">Confirm and document that each sensor is calib=
rated correctly and put error bars on each sensor reading for each sensor i=
n isolation</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"23c0625f0166">
<p local-id=3D"6b2944cedb09"></p></li>
<li local-id=3D"92b2663acff0">
<p local-id=3D"afa7b169bea7">If possible, run a confirmation experiment to =
ensure that system-level accuracy is within spec. Depending on the applicat=
ion it may be impossible to confirm system-level accuracy (for example, if =
there is no component available with precisely known properties to test). I=
f possible, testing the system with a known part would be a good check to c=
onfirm that system-level accuracy matches what is predicted by component-le=
vel accuracy, but with documented calibration and validation of each sensor=
 you can be confident that each sensor works properly in isolation, and lik=
ely will work at a system level, depending on the system and sensor. The er=
ror bars for each sensor can be extrapolated to predict error bars for syst=
em level results.</p></li>
</ol>
<p local-id=3D"59f43dfd5e76">Discussed commercially available cold plates w=
ith Midhun. There are some commercially available cold plates on DigiKey th=
at list thermal resistances down to 0.005 C/W at high flow rates, a high pr=
essure drop, and with water as the coolant. The cold plate listed as 0.005 =
C/W is an Advanced Thermal Solutions ATS-TCP-1005 cold plate, surface area =
of roughly 610 X 178 mm operating at 2-8 LPM with a 400W power input and a =
20C coolant inlet temperature. Power density ~0.37 W/cm^2 (for reference, t=
he power density being tested with our 20X20mm heatsink is ~100 W/cm^2).</p=
>
<p local-id=3D"db6de0a74726">Alex informed me that Takeshi will be here on =
Tuesday and recommended that I be prepared to show him the functionality of=
 it and some of the results determined so far. Sean will discuss the simula=
tion results and how they relate to the test bench results. Since Monday is=
 Labor Day, I will need to ensure that the test bench is ready either today=
 or if I come in on the weekend.</p>
<p local-id=3D"465b3f88381b">Here is the ATS cold plate characterization te=
st setup available for sale:</p>
<p local-id=3D"39af09d5d64f"><a href=3D"https://www.digikey.com/en/products=
/detail/advanced-thermal-solutions-inc/IFLOW-200/10669152?utm_source=3Decia=
&amp;utm_medium=3Daggregator&amp;utm_campaign=3Dadvancedthermalsolutions" l=
ocal-id=3D"8ba79dae361b" data-card-appearance=3D"inline" class=3D"external-=
link" rel=3D"nofollow">https://www.digikey.com/en/products/detail/advanced-=
thermal-solutions-inc/IFLOW-200/10669152?utm_source=3Decia&amp;utm_medium=
=3Daggregator&amp;utm_campaign=3Dadvancedthermalsolutions</a></p>
<p local-id=3D"d03b3e591c5c">$40K, and limited in sensor accuracy and numbe=
r of sensors (2 K type thermocouples instead of 8 RTDs for the current desi=
gn of HEALTH), reduced pressure drop range, same LabView control system (al=
though they provide a premade program that makes it easy to benchmark a col=
d plate). It is much more compact than HEALTH, which is nice for space usag=
e but bites for coolant temperature stability and heat rejection capabiliti=
es and addition of sensors and components. It=E2=80=99s the classic make-vs=
-buy conundrum: Something COTS is more expensive upfront and has set capabi=
lities that are non-optimal for our needs and is not flexible and modifiabl=
e if requirements change, but it is faster and easier to implement if timel=
ine is the priority and cost and flexibility are lower priorities.</p>
<p local-id=3D"8b8f52466d06">Working on making a V5 version of the LabView =
code for running HEALTH that is cleaned up compared with V4. Added function=
ality to clear all the graphs with a click of a button and adjusted the X a=
xis time scales and time so that all the graphs have consistent labeling an=
d the slow-response parameters (temperature) have an X time display length =
of 60 seconds and the fast-response parameters (flow, pressure) have a disp=
lay duration of 10 seconds. This may or may not be the right approach; it m=
akes it easier to see the shape of curves when the X axis is scaled to the =
response times but it makes it harder to correlate response between differe=
nt parameters with different time scales.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c1ac5707d069"></p>
<p local-id=3D"a2aa8c71b5ab">2026-09-08</p>
<p local-id=3D"566b1f120ba7">Weekly R&amp;D Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e5bf4d192f8a">Dong presented experimental results. Ran a tes=
t with dispensing wax on the outer boundary of the part, which resulted in =
porous outer walls of the negative and lines were also present on the part.=
 Another test was conducted printing with thicker part walls, which also re=
sulted in porous surfaces where the filler material was dispensed. When tes=
ting with lower print temperatures in another experiment, small positive an=
d negative issues were seen but there did not seem to be large delams. Prin=
ting 3 layers of risers underneath the microchannels resulted in the flatte=
ning pass picking up a layer, and delam was observed across the entire cake=
. Lower temperatures tested in another print resulted in porous surfaces. D=
ouble flattening was tested with half of the parts on the print (two facing=
 ops at decreasing heights). A test without any filler material dispensing =
resulted in delams that were more prominent on the +X side than the -X side=
.</p>
<p local-id=3D"403ed6ebec7a">Dong ran/is running metallography inspection o=
f the sintered copper parts that came back from the second DSH sintering at=
tempt.</p>
<p local-id=3D"145cf46a7756">Midhun trying to run experiments with dispensi=
ng paraffin on P7 prints to minimize cut fill, but he is seeing MEST dispen=
se variations within the print. Adjusting the acceleration and jerk of the =
motion control system for the dispense cycles only helped with the blobs se=
en at the start of a dispense line.</p>
<p local-id=3D"2eb9369a33dd">&lt;Are we using pure paraffin still, and why?=
&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"4b2da9bafbd8">I raised the concept of testing other sacrific=
ial filler materials, and Midhun said that they have locked in pure paraffi=
n to minimize variables being tested and the melt out stage of the debind p=
rocess seemed to work OK when tested at some point. I pointed out that diff=
erent filler materials would help with machinability &lt;also springback, d=
ispensing reliability, burnout reliability (since paraffin is by its nature=
 a blended hydrocarbon so there may be some variation in formulation betwee=
n batches), although I did not point out these exact advantages&gt;. At som=
e point, they plan to test some crappy waxes, such as beeswax, carnauba wax=
, and machinable wax, which are tremendously unreliable in formulation, hav=
e lots of other junk added, and historically have not burned out as cleanly=
. It sounds like polymers have been abandoned completely for the foreseeabl=
e future. There are also some interesting materials available such as a hig=
h-wax UV-curable material designed for clean burnout (various SLA materials=
 that are designed for lost-wax casting have a wax phase that can be melted=
 out to soak into the part to allow for some part shrinkage as the remainin=
g filler material is pyrolyzed).</p>
<p local-id=3D"5b1a2d8fa4c2">It seems that there are three different resear=
ch directions: Midhun is working on printing microchannel cold plates from =
Sean=E2=80=99s arbitrary design in copper, Sean is also working on Accretec=
h-relevant designs to print, and Senai is working on a different design to =
print (Epson-relevant in some way?) Bound up in these are also some parts w=
ith an open top and some parts with an integrated manifold that has large v=
olumes of sacrificial filler material.</p>
<p local-id=3D"a81814d3ebc8">My recommendation would be to start with our e=
nd goal and timeline, and work backward from there. I=E2=80=99m not a big f=
an of having the =E2=80=9Chero plate,=E2=80=9D this Epson thing, and the Ac=
cretech project running at the same time. The issues I have with this appro=
ach are:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"e356fb8c8d8c">
<li local-id=3D"ca62da2b6926">
<p local-id=3D"72232795eb9c">We have limited people</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e0909caba7fa">
<p local-id=3D"eaaca7b063fe">Limited time</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"145aa836ac0d">
<p local-id=3D"682778ddf952">Limited feedstock</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"040476bdb9e5">
<p local-id=3D"a1a58131ee08">Limited machines</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"e98eeada55dc">
<p local-id=3D"3e054493e044">An experimental, unproven process</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"e545ab10055c"></p>
<p local-id=3D"296086dcc11c">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"0663c5d83975">There are five things required to complete any=
 project:</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"4ac981af961a">
<li local-id=3D"06a45782fcda">
<p local-id=3D"1b48c796c1c6">Space</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"ce521f906716">
<p local-id=3D"19a9a3a474a1">Time</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"f9742062f9b9">
<p local-id=3D"58af19b58727">Tools</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"58ff4db8c6ce">
<p local-id=3D"9363c3520d1e">Friends</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b9d5cd37e6e3">
<p local-id=3D"48ba897988ac">Feedstock (I use this term to apply to consuma=
bles, money, raw materials, etc.)</p></li>
</ol>
<p local-id=3D"d237517757d9">Any project is a finite-sum optimization exerc=
ise; the more of one category you have, the less you need in others (up to =
a point), and the total sum enables larger scope projects.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e92c7049293d">HEALTH:</p>
<ul local-id=3D"7df68f076fde">
