<p local-id=3D"02f888feaea4">Wired up the chassis resistor, connected fluid=
 lines to the cold plate, and Kapton taped RTD sensors to the edge of the c=
hassis resistor: one connects to the independent safety controller, while t=
he other is the surface side RTD connected to the LabJack.</p>
<p local-id=3D"48c85defb401">Increased the max VFD drive frequency to 30 Hz=
 to increase the max flow rate (previously it was capped at ~3.5LPM, while =
the cold plate I am testing is rated to 2 GPM, or 7.57 LPM.</p>
<p local-id=3D"5231d48c64a5"></p>
<p local-id=3D"fad827bc9b94">2026-09-10</p>
<p local-id=3D"7a7a3fcd98f7">GENERAL:</p>
<p local-id=3D"326f93048bb3">Meeting w/ Cerebras</p>
<p local-id=3D"56177bf8a967">Company that is working on making a compute ra=
ck with wafer-scale processing and high-speed memory (?). The idea is to ma=
ke a monolithic compute structure instead of a series of smaller GPU/memory=
 cards (Nvidia approach). As of 2-4 years ago, when Ken et al talked to the=
m, their main difficulty was with cooling. When they were first exploring l=
iquid cooling then, they were printing parts with PBF processes, and ran in=
to issues with depowdering small internal channels. When using the ECAM pro=
cess from Fabric8Labs, they ran into issues with part-to-part variation. Th=
is meeting involves Jim Golden, Ken, Alex N, Sean, Midhun, and me. Midhun c=
laims that Lumafield CT scanners have difficulty penetrating copper to a de=
pth of greater than 9mm. Ken is pushing for CT scanning of parts to show to=
 customers.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1da76a39d8c4">Midhun asked about printing more demo parts fr=
om clear SLA. I entered an order request for a liter of clear resin for the=
 Form 3 since recently we have been using the Tough 1500 resin instead, whi=
ch produces more flexible and tougher parts but is not translucent and has =
a greater tendency for prints to sag and round as compared with the clear m=
aterial.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e38d6fed6766">There are these small pucks that they=E2=80=99=
re using for something, which they need ~8000 of per month for full rate pr=
oduction. Jim Golden=E2=80=99s quote for selling them to Cerebras when he w=
as at Fabric8Labs was $75 per part at full rate production.</p>
<ul local-id=3D"8d75afafa461">
<li local-id=3D"9918e6227bd2">
<p local-id=3D"fdbb7fec2656">Get thermal performance graphs to Jim/Ken (? d=
on=E2=80=99t remember which one Midhun said)</p></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"a93e3a6c2c14"></p>
<p local-id=3D"78640e9073b3">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"e621893cba58">Finally got the programming cable for XT serie=
s sensors. Programmed the coolant temp sensors to read a range of 20-50C, s=
o there is much greater resolution for the range we are looking at. Running=
 the LabView code after modifying the voltage to degrees C conversion equat=
ion, the delta T is close to rock-solid without any correction.</p>
<p local-id=3D"e181f10ded8c">Demounting the 20X20mm HX for Midhun. Noticed =
that the SLA manifold design is a little weird; it doesn=E2=80=99t seem to =
direct the coolant straight into the microchannels. Presumably Sean ran the=
 appropriate CFD on it, but it looks weird to me. I=E2=80=99ll bring it up =
the next time we=E2=80=99re printing a manifold to attach to one of the cop=
per HXs. It turns out that Sean had a spare of the 20X20 HX assembly that I=
 had demounted for Midhun, so I now have another one to test to compare to =
the original results and also use as a similar sort of benchmark.</p>
