eat-transfer-fluid-5-gallon-pail-yellow?gad_source=3D1&amp;gad_campaignid=
=3D22805635358&amp;gclid=3DCjwKCAjwqonVBhA4EiwA9wYJ3TXgybpequtq1gM4nvGXPfsF=
bCCqkS8U3yKJmSHjO7yQ3pykAYTFDxoCtHQQAvD_BwE</a></p></li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<p local-id=3D"bb79866baf74"></p>
<p local-id=3D"b1885c588643">2026-09-11</p>
<p local-id=3D"dab35d03805e">R&amp;D Hardware Weekly Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"7fde0ed9704a">I went over the latest results from the test b=
ench with the Ohmite HX and chassis resistor with published thermal resista=
nce and pressure drop. Pressure drop values seem to follow a different curv=
e with lower pressure drop at low flow and higher pressure drop at high flo=
w.</p>
<p local-id=3D"c0dec152493a">The Mest unit has chronic issues with unreliab=
le dispensing during a print. Mohsen noted that this seems to be a bigger i=
ssue with paraffin than with PEG, but that has not been confirmed. Mason no=
ted that some competitors to Mest have a system to calibrate automatically =
during the print. I think there are a few ways to resolve this issue:</p>
<ol start=3D"1" local-id=3D"1172c9875920">
<li local-id=3D"38d836520d6a">
<p local-id=3D"8033f40b61b3">Modify our process to be tolerant to variation=
 (such as always overfilling with a filler material that can be machined cl=
eanly, so if it overfills a little bit or a lot the final part will not be =
affected)</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"854531c053bd">
<p local-id=3D"12167200ebe6">Change the sacrificial filler material to be o=
ne that is more reliable</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b0178f58eba8">
<p local-id=3D"75e13d09975e">Develop an in-process calibration procedure, s=
uch as a dropwatcher that the Mest unit will travel to to analyze the drop =
size, and the code will modify the parameters to match the nominal requirem=
ents</p></li>
<li local-id=3D"b5e9bd72a5d0">
<p local-id=3D"c3f12dab87ca">Change the dispenser hardware to one that is m=
ore reliable and/or has an automated calibration system</p></li>
</ol>
<p local-id=3D"9675304dd13e">ZZ noted that the firmware version on the Mest=
 unit does not match the firmware version in the manual we have, and he is =
seeing discrepancies between the behaviors of the registers discussed in th=
e manual and the behaviors of the registers in the actual controller.</p>
<p local-id=3D"db10500a3018">Devin discussed the layer strategy optimizatio=
n. He said that optimizing the travel moves reduces travel distance by 85-9=
5% on some layers. Travel move speed is roughly twice that of cutting speed=
, although it is an open question whether it is able to reach those speeds =
given the time required to accelerate to top speed and decelerate to the ta=
rget position.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d90f890cfd94">For software optimization, ZZ is working on co=
mmunicating with the Mest unit to adjust its parameters during the print. T=
hey also discussed the need for graceful shutdown of the machine, but that =
is not an active project.</p>
<p local-id=3D"74fa46200516">Senai is working on installing a Mest unit on =
a Beta machine. He has designed new brackets and also raised the point that=
 various components on the Beta machine will need to be moved around to ena=
ble full travel of the Mest unit without components interfering with each o=
ther.</p>
<p local-id=3D"d60f046c529b">Pilot PM and failure logging. Looks like they=
=E2=80=99re recording in Google Sheets instead of a more formal system.</p>
<p local-id=3D"1401ffe1646e">Mason raised the point that if we explore alte=
rnative filler material dispensing technologies, it would be better to use =
a technology that has significant advantages (such as binder jetting heads)=
 rather than exploring an incremental improvement to the Mest units (such a=
s switching to a Japanese brand that Ning sent out to some people).</p>
<p local-id=3D"4dda751ed002"></p>
<p local-id=3D"df2f4ac72d63">2026-09-14</p>
<p local-id=3D"9cfe56034661">R&amp;D Weekly Meeting:</p>
<p local-id=3D"694032dffb73">Dong discussed various delam experiments that =
have been run. Two different tests were run without filler material dispens=
ing but still with the facing op, and still saw delams and porosity. In ano=
ther test, saw a layer shift in y direction from the facing op.</p>
<p local-id=3D"4f59b3d389d5">There seems to be some issue with layer misali=
gnment on prints when the facing op is conducted. Midhun=E2=80=99s theory i=
s that this is somehow due to misalignment of the different Z axes, althoug=
h I have failed to understand how misalignment in the facing spindle axis r=
elative to the primary spindle causes a repeatable X/Y shift when the facin=
g spindle is not touching the surfaces that are in the wrong position. It a=
lso seems to only occur with outer surfaces and not the internal features.<=
/p>
<p local-id=3D"419b561094ad">Midhun will send out the CT scan results. It s=
ounds like they generate it in a proprietary format that can be opened with=
 a free viewer software package.</p>
<p local-id=3D"68f32eb83e4e">He is pursuing an approach to eliminate the fa=
cing op; it comprises filling each layer precisely instead of overfilling a=
nd facing (facing op adds time, possible dimensional errors, FOD contaminat=
ion from the chips produced).</p>
<p local-id=3D"5383b0cb0e78">&lt;Filling exactly to the surface would be be=
st if possible, although it requires very tight control over the amount of =
filler material dispense. I would recommend dodging this issue by always ov=
erfilling and figure out the issues seen with the facing op, which to me se=
em more easily solvable than the precision dispensing. For that level of pr=
ecision, it is likely necessary to either switch to a more repeatable dispe=
nser or measure the Mest dispensed drop size before each layer and modify t=
he parameters to get the drop size within spec. Frankly, integrating a drop=
 watcher into the printer would be a good thing to do regardless of this cu=
rrent issue to solve.&gt;</p>
<p local-id=3D"701898fa79dd">Mohsen presented some of the results from prin=
ts run last week. There is a chronic issue with banding seen on the outside=
 of the part, which seems to correlate with when filler material dispensing=
 and facing is started or stopped.</p>
<p local-id=3D"51bb204f00aa">Had a very interesting philosophical conversat=
ion with the whole team at the meeting (primarily Midhun, Sean, and Senai) =
and then continued it with Sean and Senai after the meeting. The crux of th=
e question is what to spend our remaining copper powder on. We have on the =
order of 15 prints worth of copper left (probably not exactly, but close en=
ough) and the question is what do you do when you are resource-limited like=
 that. Midhun was fairly insistent that it is not worth printing anything t=
hat is not closely representative of what we want to print for customers in=
 copper. I am of the opinion that we should dedicate at least some of those=
 prints to determining the limits of our process so that Sean has some gene=
ral design rules for designing heat exchangers that are more likely to be s=
uccessfully printed in copper. This would entail printing various geometrie=
s that are not directly representative of a customer part, but instead prov=
ide insight into our process itself. To Midhun=E2=80=99s point, this would =
reduce the number of prints we have to dedicate to printing parts that we c=
an show to customers, but it would also increase the likelihood that the pr=
ints we do run are successful, and would provide information about how to d=
esign parts in the future for the CleanPrint(tm) process that are distinct =
from this =E2=80=9Chero plate=E2=80=9D nonsense. It is a nontrivial enginee=
ring optimization exercise with no clear correct answer; you can dedicate m=
ore resources to understanding your process by breaking it down into pieces=
 that can be clearly understood and then assemble those pieces into the who=
le, or you can test the whole and try to learn from how it works. If the wh=
ole process does work then you=E2=80=99ve saved a huge amount of time and r=
esources, and if it fails in a way that is instructional for improving it t=
hen it still may have saved resources. If it fails in an ambiguous way, how=
ever, where it is not clear which variable exactly led to the failure, then=
 your experiment is worse than useless, because it gives you a false sense =
of confidence in the process if the process mostly works (remember that jus=
t because a process 99% works, that does not prove that it will 100% work),=
 and because people will interpret results in different ways then each pers=
on will take away a different root cause of the failure. In that situation,=
 you=E2=80=99ve spent time and resources and you have no actionable results=
, but everyone has a different view of the results so everyone will work in=
 different directions.</p>
<p local-id=3D"bb2f027281bb">Now, there are times when this is a necessary =
approach. Let=E2=80=99s say we have time to run 15 prints, but we do not ha=
ve time to run tests, analyze the results, and design prints based on those=
 results, and when we run out of time, there will be serious consequences. =
In that case, you do your best to come up with a design, try it, and if you=
 succeed you=E2=80=99re a hero and if you fail then the project=E2=80=99s d=
one. If you want to continue the project from this point, you need to reset=
 back to the start with whatever learnings you can glean from the results, =
but you can=E2=80=99t expect to keep running prints with a dozen variables =
and determine the correct parameters in a reasonable timeframe.</p>
<p local-id=3D"feba1a887adc"></p>
<p local-id=3D"50a4dfde3337">HEALTH:</p>
<p local-id=3D"c3b4668da0b3">Reran the test with the Ohmite reference setup=
 as long as it=E2=80=99s still hooked up, and it looks highly repeatable.</=
p>
<p local-id=3D"b1ad9f241a08">Received the big block of copper I need to mak=
e the larger intermediate heat flux measurement bar, and started preparing =
the mill for use.</p>
<p local-id=3D"66d2336e61f4">Cut two blanks off the big block (each roughly=
 3X3X1=E2=80=9D) to make into heat flux test blocks tomorrow.</p>
<p local-id=3D"6895fa78d174"></p>
<p local-id=3D"0338b12941ac">2026-09-15</p>
<p local-id=3D"e4c3ff460cdb">Ning brought in some generic cold plates he bo=
ught for ~$10 each, and he=E2=80=99s been showing them around and talking a=
bout how cheap they are, which, yeah, obviously. I calibrated my emotional =
reaction with some of the AIs I=E2=80=99ve made for this purpose, and my ge=
neral takeaway from writing out the situation and getting clanker feedback =
was that it is OK to be frustrated with criticism of our technology and app=
roach, but I should also be respectful of Ning=E2=80=99s points and not fal=
l into the trap of assuming that just because we have spent so much time an=
d money developing our process and approach, it is necessarily better. We c=
ertainly lose on cost and ease of mass manufacturability, but where we win =
is in tunable performance of monolithic cold plates (we can optimize for pr=
essure drop or for thermal resistance, and target hot spots specifically. T=
he basic CPU cold plates you can get for cheap are very good with pressure =
drop but have much poorer thermal resistance and flow control in different =
regions than our process is capable of).</p>
<p local-id=3D"98f3f0f027bc">I asked Sean what his simulations said for the=
 65X65mm heat sink he has designed:</p><span class=3D"confluence-embedded-f=
ile-wrapper image-center-wrapper confluence-embedded-manual-size"><img clas=
